# v6.7.16 (2026-10-21, Break 2 repair 6): no new entry — every rule is dissolved into its class; the 6.7.15 changes
# beyond the decisions' text are KEPT (6.7.16's D36 – D67) and their "filed for the 6.7.16 open" notes say so. Updated
# (6.7.16's D# unless named): sizeof_operator (an intrinsic statement refused — D11; type-argument lists), unions +
# struct_field_widths (fields need their `;` — D69), enum_namespacing (`Color.NOPE` named; `Color<..>` — D88; float
# pairs compared), ret2_rethi (`ret2` after a case label — 6.7.14's D42; its refusals after its arguments; a generic's
# wrong count once; a struct value element refused, a handle kept — D90; a chain destructures), slice_type_v58x (a generic
# instance element — D89; the subscript widths), relaxed_fn_ordering (`mod` / `use` / `pub` in a body, the refused names'
# uses, an impl above its struct — D6), tagged_unions_v58x (members need `;` / `,` — D12; type arguments by declaration —
# D88), typed_simd_value_type_abi (a vector method on any receiver; no vector `+` — D24 – D26; a vector call is no operand,
# no scalar return, no word argument, no destructure source — D100, D101; the top-level assignment), struct_byval_int_
# pair_return_abi (a `return` error alone — D105), ts_tsx_to_js_emitter + build_target_js (the whole command line, TL-9;
# arm64 macOS — D50), f64_named_type_and_operators (an untyped global from an f64 call — D98), closures_v637_v638 +
# nested_closure_capture_v6617 (a capture shadows a struct global — D96; a closure's parameter untyped; a captured f64 —
# D3; a narrow parameter's capture), generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310 (the bound contract's explicit spelling — D4,
# a captured f64 — D3, `**G<..T..>` carriers, the base's signature, no scalar instance — D1, `return &n` — D2, type
# arguments on a non-generic callee, multi-value elements by instance, D5 / D7 / D8 / D88 / D89 / D92 / D93, an untyped
# local of a T value — D102, a generic operator fn — D85 (a), chain lookaheads, `nofn<i64>(..)`), async_await_futures_
# v6311 (a narrow parameter — D72), reserved_word_class_toknames + closeout_language_changes_v6620 (an intrinsic
# statement — D11; a list on one), diagnostics_column_caret_multierror (one mistake, one error; what is filed),
# visibility_public_private_v650 (a per-item `private` in a body), stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555
# (a chain is a pair call; a default named `Dv.qd`), compile_time_assert_v6610 (a refused `#assert` ends at its line),
# struct_literals_generic_heads_and_nested_values_v6612 (tuple commas, enum members, a short positional literal, an
# undeclared name), field_chains_any_depth_and_call_result_fields_v6612 (an undefined callee after a plain call, lint on
# a here-defined `: *Foo`, a non-struct field), struct_field_arguments_and_toplevel_copy_init_v6612 (a small global from
# a source below), aarch64_xattr_..._v6612 (a fn-local const's warning once — TL-7), cx_tail_calls_and_cxvm_guest_traps_
# v6612 (errno, kill, F_GETFL — D29, getdents64 / mknodat / wait4 / poll / truncate), struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616
# (`*Str` sources and destinations — D19, D20, D71, D73, D74; a generic's `x: T` at T = Str), pointer_fields_returns_and_
# linked_lists_v6617 (D7 on a pointer field, a `**G<..>` field minted, `**B` below — D103), typed_pointer_step_is_
# sizeof_t_v6617 (`o.s = *pp`, `*(&x)` of a narrow / float / bool / inline `Str` — D21, D70, D71, D72; the
# typed-pointer warning — `(0)`, `: Str` values, the for step), method_chains_v6617 (an untyped `: Str` method local;
# field methods; names), nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617 (an instance element held to its bound — D10),
# traits_checked_v670 (D9 returns, the impl check reports every mismatch, `*fn` members — D91, D92, an impl body holds
# fns), trait_bounds_v671 (the contract's explicit spelling — D4, ~300 links, a third parameter — D8, an untyped local's
# bound call — D102), const_and_const_fn_v672 (a const has no subscript; case labels), declaration_zone_constant_
# initializers_v6714 (float and struct / tuple redeclarations — D13 (a), D14 (a); an integer right operand baked — D15
# (a); a list over a constant — D86 (a)), bool_true_false_not_v673 (`*(&b)`), struct_argument_type_checked_v673 (a
# pointer of another struct — D97), if_expression_v674 (braces; the top-level struct binding — D16 (a)), loop_and_do_
# while_v675 (braces), struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676 (`(pn)[0] = 1;` — D22; a `callptr` chain argument),
# tuples_v677 (float and bool tuple arguments — D23; generic multi-value elements; a chain capture), dyn_v678 (the pair
# check reads annotations; a chain forms), struct_handle_line_..._v6710 (top-level copies from `*GPP` / `P` — D17 (a),
# D18 (a); the source above at every size; a `*U` source — D87 (a)), struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711
# (the stub and `&n` — D1, D2; `+|` gives an integer — D104; a multi-value element — D90; a store through a scalar
# pointer — D99; the zone's untyped bindings — D16 (a); a chain's struct return). The 6.7.15 passages that went beyond
# their decisions' text now name the 6.7.16 decision that kept each (D36 – D66). 114 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.16).

[[entries]]
name = "byte_array_literal"
description = "var X: T[N] = { .. } — N elements of T baked into the image; the bare var b[N] = { .. } is a byte list (v5.11.51; typed and baked 6.6.16)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX (module scope):
    enum Op { ADD = 1; SUB = 2; }
    var table: u16[4] = {0xFFFF, Op.SUB, 1 << 8, -1};   # 0xFFFF, 2, 256, 0xFFFF
    var scale: f64[2] = {1.5, -0.25};                   # exact binary64 bits
    var half:  f32[2] = {0.1, 3.4};                     # rounded to f32, ties to even
    var wide:  u128[1] = {0xFF};                        # low 8 bytes; high 8 are 0
    var guid[2] = { 0x8B, 0xE4, 0xDF, 0x61, 0x93, 0xCA, 0x11, 0xD2,
                    0xAA, 0x0D, 0x00, 0xE0, 0x98, 0x03, 0x2B, 0x8C };   # the BARE form: bytes

SEMANTICS (6.6.16):
    `var X: T[N] = { e, .. }` is N elements of T. An element is an integer
    literal, an enum constant (`A` or `E.A`, declared on either side), or a
    constant expression of them (`1 << 4`, `E.A * 2`, `-5`); an f64 / f32
    element also takes a float literal, optionally negated. Elements past
    the list are 0. More than N elements is refused, naming N.
    RANGE: one window per width, -2^(8w-1) .. 2^(8w)-1 — `0xFF` into an i8
    and `-1` into a u8 are the same bits; a value that would be truncated is
    refused. i64 / u64 / bool / an enum / *T take any 64-bit value.
    The BARE `var b[N] = { .. }` is a list of BYTES in [0, 255], at most
    N * 8 of them at top level (its historical meaning — a bare top-level
    `var b[N]` is N * 8 bytes), and takes enum constants and constant
    expressions too. A shorter list was always legal; the rest is 0.
    REFUSED BY NAME: a call, variable or string element ("must be
    constants"); a float literal in an integer array; a struct, union,
    vector, slice, cstring, Str, Result / Option / Tagged or Vec element
    type ("store the elements explicitly").
    POSITION: the same declaration after the first top-level statement means
    the same thing. Inside a top-level block (while / if / elif / else / for
    and its init clause / a switch case or default arm / match / @unsafe / a
    bare { }) a list is refused — a baked list would hold its values once,
    where a scalar's initializer runs every time the block does.
    A fn-local array takes no list.
    REDECLARATION: a list is a constant initializer (6.6.6's value rule).
    Over an earlier `= 0` or computed initializer of the name it is the value
    from program start — `var a = f(); var a[1] = {0, 4};` runs f() and reads
    0x400; over an earlier scalar constant it keeps the bytes it does not list
    (`var a = 0x0506; var a[1] = {9};` reads 0x0509); two lists overlay in
    order; a constant scalar after a list wins whole.

CODEGEN (6.6.16):
    NONE on an image target. Pass 1 (PARSE_GVAR_ARR) checks the element type
    and the count; the replay (EMIT_GVAR_INITS -> _gai_replay) evaluates each
    element with the constant folder (_CF_TRY, enum arm on) into the slot's
    blob (_vgai_base); every image writer copies the blob into the var area
    (_EMIT_GVAR_STATIC_INITS -> _gai_bake), x86 ELF / aarch64 / PE / EFI /
    Mach-O x86 and arm64 alike. So the values hold from the first
    instruction — in a `kernel;` build too, where the old run-time stores ran
    after a top-level program that never returns (B read 0). cx too: a .cyx
    carries its var data after the bytecode and cxvm copies it into the
    guest, so main_cx.cyr bakes the same blob there. An initializer that runs
    earlier — even one reading the array before its declaration — sees the
    list. (Compiler side: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
    `a_guard_in_the_loop_implies_a_second_pass_somebody_has_to_write`.)

⚠ UNTIL 6.6.16 the list was a byte list WHATEVER T was:
    `var t: i64[3] = {1, 2, 42};` read t[0] == 0x2A0201, t[1] == t[2] == 0
    on every target; 256, -3, 1.5 and an enum constant were refused, and
    Pt[2] / bool[2] / i8v16[2] lists compiled silently into bytes. The old
    entry here also said "element count must equal N" (never true) and
    described a store8 sequence per byte (removed by the bake).

CONSUMER:
    gnoboot (sovereign UEFI bootloader, `kernel;` + CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1):
    29 bare lists — EFI GUIDs and UTF-16LE strings. Same bytes since 6.6.16,
    now in the image (the binary lost 2 KB of stores).

[[entries]]
name = "efi_main_entry_convention"
description = "fn efi_main(handle, st) — UEFI Application entry convention (v5.11.52)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    fn efi_main(handle, st): i64 {
        # handle = EFI_HANDLE (UEFI ImageHandle)
        # st     = *EFI_SYSTEM_TABLE
        # Return: EFI_STATUS (0 = EFI_SUCCESS).
        return 0;
    }

WHEN IT'S USED:
    Only when `CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1` is set AND the program
    declares a `fn efi_main(handle, st)`. cyrius emits a
    trampoline that:
      1. Reads firmware args saved at entry (R14 ← RCX =
         ImageHandle; R15 ← RDX = SystemTable).
      2. Allocates the MS x64 shadow space + 8-byte stack
         alignment.
      3. Calls efi_main(handle, st) directly.
      4. Restores stack; EEXIT under EFI mode emits a single
         `ret` — firmware reads rax as EFI_STATUS.

ERGONOMICS:
    Pre-v5.11.52, gnoboot had to hand-write the trampoline +
    grab args from registers manually. Post-.52, the entry
    convention is implicit: just declare `fn efi_main(handle,
    st)` and the compiler wires it up.

GOTCHA (resolved at v5.11.53): MR-form `mov r/m64, r64`
(opcode `89 /r`) puts dst in r/m field, src in reg field. To
extend dst to r14/r15 needs REX.B; v5.11.52's first cut set
REX.R instead — gnoboot saw `mov rsi, r9` decoded bytes
instead of `mov r14, rcx`. v5.11.53 hotfix corrected the REX
prefix `0x4C → 0x49` in `src/main.cyr:1273` save emit.
Restore (line ~1369) was correct as-is.

CROSS-REF: see field_notes/kernel.cyml for the full UEFI
Application emit arc (v5.11.47-.49 + .52-.53).

[[entries]]
name = "multi_width_types"
description = "Multi-width types (i8, i16, i32, i64, u128) with width-correct codegen"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var x: i8 = 42;       # 1-byte variable
    var y: i16 = 30000;   # 2-byte variable
    var z: i32 = 100000;  # 4-byte variable
    var w: i64 = 0;       # explicit 8-byte (same as untyped)
    var u: u128 = 0;      # 16-byte; + - and comparisons since 6.7.6, the rest via lib/bayan.cyr

SEMANTICS:
    Type annotations control BOTH storage size AND load/store width.
    var x: i8 = 256; → stores 0 (truncated to 1 byte).
    Reading x back gives 0 (movzx zero-extends to rax).

    Globals: var_sizes[idx] = 1/2/4/8/16 (actual byte allocation in data section).
    Locals: 8-byte stack slots, but loads/stores use correct width within the slot.

CODEGEN:
    EVLOAD_W(S, idx, width) / EVSTORE_W(S, idx, width) for globals.
    EFLLOAD_W(S, idx, width) / EFLSTORE_W(S, idx, width) for locals.
    Width 1: movzx rax, byte [addr] / mov byte [addr], al
    Width 2: movzx eax, word [addr] / mov word [addr], ax
    Width 4: mov eax, [addr]        / mov dword [addr], eax
    Width 8: mov rax, [addr]        / mov qword [addr], rax (default)

GOTCHA (resolved at v5.8.16 §8 layout-flip): pre-v5.8.16,
`var x: u128 = 0;` at fn scope only allocated an 8-byte slot —
type-annotated locals carried size on globals but not locals.
Two adjacent fn-local 16-byte vars (slices, u128) silently
shared a stack slot, with `slice_set` / `store64(&x+8, ...)`
overflowing into saved-rbp territory. Post-v5.8.16:
scalar_type=16 fn-locals reserve TWO 8-byte slots, with the
canonical (named) slot at the LOWER stack address — `&x + 8`
correctly addresses the high half. The pre-v5.8.16 workaround
(`var buf[16]; pass as pointer`) still works but is no longer
needed — `var x: u128 = 0;` is fully load-bearing today. See
slice_type_v58x for the same fix on slices.

⚠ EVERY STORE PATH IS WIDTH-CORRECT SINCE 6.6.12 — two were not. Narrow
GLOBALS are PACKED (`var a: u8 = 1; var b: u8 = 2;` puts `b` at `a + 1`),
so an 8-byte store to `a` writes over the next seven bytes of globals:
  - the classic-`for` STEP kept its own copy of the assignment store and
    it was always 8 bytes. On a u8 global,
    `for (a = 250; a != 252; a = a + 1)` zeroed the packed globals after
    `a` (a neighbour set to 7 read 0), and a step `a += 1` wrapping 255
    carried into the next global (7 became 8). On a u8 LOCAL stepped
    past 255 the 8-byte slot held 256 — the narrow read showed 0, so the
    loop looked right — until a compound op read the whole slot:
    `i >>= 1` gave 128, not 0;
  - a COMPOUND operator's load read 8 bytes and did not sign-extend:
    with packed u8 globals `a = 4` and `b = 1`, `a >>= 1` shifted b's byte
    into `a` (130, not 2), and `c /= 2` on an i8 local -8 gave 124, not -4.
Both now go through the statement's store (`_asg_store_slot`) and the
expression read's width-and-sign load, on x86, aarch64, PE, both Mach-O
backends and cx (whose narrow store/load emitters were 8 bytes wide as
well). The `for` step also draws the statement's narrowing warning now.
Every shape above was measured wrong on 6.6.11 and right on 6.6.12 (x86;
aarch64 under qemu and cx under cxvm right on 6.6.12 too). A plain
statement `a += 1` was already right: the statement arm always stored
at the slot's width.

⚠ STILL OPEN at 6.6.12 (backlog, not fixed by the above): in the leading
declaration block, a narrow global initialised to ZERO clobbers the narrow
global after it — `var a: u8 = 0; var b: u8 = 7;` reads `b` as 0 (the u8
pair on x86, aarch64 under qemu and PE under wine, and an i32 pair on x86;
cx reads 7; 6.6.11 the same). A non-zero initialiser,
the reverse order, or the pair declared after the first top-level
statement reads 7. Until it is fixed, give such a global a non-zero
initialiser or set it at run time.

⚠ 6.7.16 — A NARROW PARAMETER IS NARROWED IN THE FN, LIKE A NARROW LOCAL (the user's
D72, "Narrowed in the fn, like a narrow local"; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] SS-15, SS-16, SS-18):
a parameter typed `u8` / `i8` / `u16` / `i16` / `u32` / `i32` reads its width, sign- or
zero-extended, and `y = v` / `y OP= v` / `*(&y) = v` write its width, wrapping there:
`wid(0x1FF)` with `y: u8` reads 255 (it read 511 — the caller's whole word),
`w32(0x1FFFFFFFF)` -1, an `i16` 32767 `+= 1` -32768 — through a fn pointer, a stack-passed
parameter, an `#inline` replay, a capture, a method, a default, `#regalloc` and a
non-suspending `async fn`. The caller still passes the whole word (the ABI is unchanged):
the callee narrows. ⚠ A generic `x: T` bound to i8 / i16 / i32 is narrowed too (beyond
D72's text). ⚠ A SUSPENDING `async fn`'s narrow parameter keeps the word (98304 where D72
gives -32768; filed at the 6.7.16 close, unplaced). And a SIGNED narrow GLOBAL declared
after the first top-level statement reads sign-extended, as in the declaration zone
(SS-15; it read zero-extended).

BACKWARD COMPATIBLE: Untyped variables remain i64. The compiler
source uses no type annotations.

[[entries]]
name = "sizeof_operator"
description = "sizeof(Type) compile-time constant — every type name, matched whole (6.6.16)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var n = sizeof(i8);      # 1      sizeof(u8)  1
    var n = sizeof(i16);     # 2      sizeof(u16) 2
    var n = sizeof(i32);     # 4      sizeof(u32) 4
    var n = sizeof(i64);     # 8      sizeof(u64) 8
    var n = sizeof(u128);    # 16     (also in #assert since 6.6.16)
    var n = sizeof(f32);     # 4      sizeof(f64) 8
    var n = sizeof(bool);    # 8      cstring, Result, Option, Tagged, Vec, an enum: 8
    var n = sizeof(f64v2);   # 16     every 128-bit vector 16; f64v4 / f32v8 32
    var n = sizeof(Point);   # STRUCTSZ — unpadded, recursive
    fn g<T>(p: T): i64 { var k = sizeof(T); return k; }   # T's argument's size; the i64 base 8

IMPLEMENTATION (6.6.16):
    PARSE_FACTOR spots the IDENT `sizeof` (`_is_sizeof_word`, whole word —
    NOT a lexer keyword); `#assert` reads the same through
    `_EVAL_CONST_ATOM`. Both call `_tn_sizeof` (src/frontend/parse_types.cyr):
    a bound type parameter is its argument's size, else the one type-name
    resolver `_tn_resolve` (struct lookup FIRST, then the builtin names,
    matched WHOLE), and `_tn_bytes` gives the size — exactly what
    `var a: T[N]` reserves per element, so the array is N * sizeof(T) by
    construction. The vocabulary: language/core.cyml `type_names_v6616`.

⚠ UNTIL 6.6.16 sizeof knew only i8..i64, u128 and structs: `sizeof(f64)`,
    `sizeof(u8)`, `sizeof(bool)`, `sizeof(cstring)`, an enum and every vector
    type were `sizeof: unknown type`, and `#assert sizeof(u128)` was
    `expected ')', got u128`. A generic's `sizeof(T)` with T = f64 read 9
    (the f64 return code -9 taken as a width); under CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0 an
    unbound T was `unknown type`.

⚠ A NAME THAT IS NO TYPE is refused naming it: `unknown type 'i8zz' in
    sizeof` / `... in #assert sizeof` (plus `note: 'i8zz' is not 'i8' - a type
    name must match whole`), and an #assert whose operand was refused does
    not add a second `#assert failed`. `ptr` is not a cyrius type (`*T` is the
    pointer spelling). History: the word was matched by a 6-byte prefix until
    6.6.10 (`sizeofzz(P)` was sizeof; `mulh64x(a, b)` was the intrinsic until
    6.6.11); the TYPE by a prefix until 6.6.11 (`sizeof(i16v8)` was 2 — it is
    16 now, a vector).

⚠ `return sizeof(T);` AND `return mulh64(a, b);` WORK SINCE 6.6.11.
    Both are IDENT-spelled intrinsics lowered inline; the tail-call path
    used to jump to a fn of that name (`_is_ident_intrinsic` diverts it).

⚠ `sizeof` / `mulh64` ARE VALUES, NOT CALLS (6.7.16 — the user's D11, "Refuse it
    by name"; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] FE-3, FF-3, FF-32). A statement that is ONLY one —
    `mulh64(a, b);`, `sizeof(x);`, `sizeof(T) + 1;` — is one error at the name,
    "the value of 'mulh64(..)' is discarded - a statement is an assignment or a
    call" (`'sizeof(..)'`; 6.7.15's D60 words, as `x + 1;` and `(s).a;` get), in a
    fn, at top level and under `--syntax-only`, once in a generic. No binary even
    with `--allow-undef`: through 6.7.15 the statement was a call to an undefined
    fn, so `--allow-undef` built a binary that died with SIGILL (exit 132) at the
    line (`sizeof(H);` of a struct type was already refused, "undefined variable
    'H'"). Bind the value or delete the line. `fncall0` .. `fncall8(..);` stay
    calls; the value forms (`var x = mulh64(2, 3);`) are unchanged. A `.field`
    after one — `mulh64(2, 3).n = 5;`, `sizeof(H).n;`, `OP=`, a chain, a method — is
    "expected ';', got '.'" alone (FF-3; neither result has fields — it added two
    invented "undefined function" lines). And a type-argument list on an intrinsic
    or a builtin — `mulh64<i64>(2, 3)`, `sizeof<i64>(H)`, `load64<i64>(p)`,
    `syscall<i64>(..)`, `f64_sqrt<f64>(..)` — is "'mulh64' is not a generic fn: it
    takes no type arguments" at the name, the call parsed on (FF-32). ⚠ Another
    value builtin as a bare statement is not judged alike yet: `load8(p);`
    compiles while `load16(p);` / `load64(p);` / `f32_from(x);` are "unexpected"
    (filed at the 6.7.16 close, unplaced).

[[entries]]
name = "unions"
description = "union keyword — all fields at offset 0, size = max field"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    union Value {
        as_int;
        as_ptr;
    }

SEMANTICS:
    All fields share offset 0. sizeof(Value) = max(sizeof(field)).
    Field access: v.as_int and v.as_ptr read from the same memory.
    Init: var v = Value { 42, 42 }; (all fields, like struct init).
    Fields are separated by `;` (the last may omit it): `union U { a: i64
    b: i32 }`, or one field per line with no `;`, is "expected ';' between
    union fields" at the second field, once per declaration, since 6.7.16
    (the user's D69, FF-33 — as a struct's, struct_field_widths); it
    compiled through 6.7.15.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    Token 101 (UNION). Parsed by PARSE_UNION_DEF — same as
    PARSE_STRUCT_DEF but sets high bit (bit 63) of struct_fcounts
    as union flag. ISUNION(S, si) checks bit 63. FIELDOFF returns
    0 for all union fields. STRUCTSZ returns max field size
    instead of sum.

    ⚠ The flag rides in the field COUNT, so every reader of that
    count must mask it. Until 6.6.11 ADDFIELD / ADDFIELDTYPED /
    ADDFIELDFK used it raw: the count was negative, so the
    256-fields-per-struct cap never fired for a union (a 300-field
    union compiled rc 0), and the flag leaked into the field-pool
    top, so after ANY union the 8192-entry pool cap stopped firing
    for every later struct too (40 structs of 250 fields wrote past
    the pool and failed with an unrelated "passing integer literal
    8 to 'alloc'"). Both caps fire now ("too many struct fields
    (max 256)"); compiled output is unchanged.

[[entries]]
name = "bitfield_builtins"
description = "Bit manipulation builtins — bit_get/set/clear, popcount, ctz/clz"
category = "cyrius"
---
Bit-level operations as compiler builtins:

    bit_get(value, n)        — extract bit n (returns 0 or 1)
    bit_set(value, n)        — set bit n
    bit_clear(value, n)      — clear bit n
    bit_toggle(value, n)     — flip bit n
    popcount(value)          — count set bits (POPCNT instruction)
    ctz(value)               — count trailing zeros (BSF / TZCNT)
    clz(value)               — count leading zeros (BSR / LZCNT)

These compile to single x86 instructions where available (POPCNT
/ BSF / BSR via SSE4.2 / BMI1). Fallback paths for older CPUs
emit equivalent unrolled loops.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_field_widths"
description = "Per-field type annotations on structs — size and alignment correctness. Fields are PACKED (no padding): i8 / u8 1 B, i16 / u16 2 B, i32 / u32 / f32 4 B, the rest 8; u8..u32 zero-extend, i8..i32 sign-extend. Since cyrius 6.7.10 u8 / u16 / u32 / f32 are their widths (8 B each through 6.7.9) — a layout / ABI change"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    struct Header {
        magic:   i32;       # 4 bytes
        version: i16;       # 2 bytes
        flags:   i8;        # 1 byte
        _pad:    i8;        # 1 byte (manual padding)
        size:    i64;       # 8 bytes
    }
    struct C4 { r: u8; g: u8; b: u8; a: u8; }   # 4 bytes (32 through 6.7.9)
    struct UB { a: u8; b: i64; }                # 9 bytes, b at offset 1 (16, b at 8)
    struct F2 { x: f32; y: f32; }               # 8 bytes (16)
    union  UU { a: u8; b: u32; c: f32; }        # 4 bytes (8)

    T                       bytes   read as
    i8 / i16 / i32          1/2/4   sign-extended
    u8 / u16 / u32          1/2/4   zero-extended (6.7.10); a write or an OP= stores only the
                                    field's bytes, so it wraps there (`c.r = 250; c.r += 10;` is 4)
    f32                     4       its binary32 bits, typed single-precision arithmetic (6.7.10)
    i64 / u64 / f64 / bool / cstring / Result / Option / Tagged / an enum / Vec / *T   8
    a struct or union       its size, inline (declared ABOVE the struct using it)

SEMANTICS:
    Field types control individual field widths. STRUCTSZ sums them.
    FIELDOFF returns the byte offset of each field considering prior
    fields' widths.

    Unannotated fields default to i64 (8 bytes). Mix is fine:

    struct Mixed { tag: i32; payload; }   # 4 + 8 = 12 bytes

    Programmer is responsible for alignment-aware ordering. The
    compiler does NOT auto-pad — write padding fields explicitly
    if a downstream consumer needs C ABI compatibility.

⚠ 6.7.16 — FIELDS ARE SEPARATED BY `;` (the user's D69, "Refuse it by name";
    CHANGELOG [6.7.16] FF-33). A field that follows another with no `;` —
    `struct P { a b }`, `struct Q { x: i64 y: i64 }`, `union U { a: i64 b: i32 }`,
    one field per line with no `;` — is one error at the second field,
    "expected ';' between struct fields" ("... union fields"), once per
    declaration. The LAST field may omit its `;` (`struct P { a; b }` stays
    valid); a `,` is "expected identifier, got ','". Through 6.7.15 each compiled,
    every field at the offset the separated form gives — so adding the `;` changes
    no offset. D69 is 6.7.15's D1 (a) (struct-literal commas) and 6.7.16's D12
    (enum members) applied to the third brace list.

⚠ 6.6.16: `bool` is a field type — 8 bytes, a plain word like u64 (it was
    refused here while an array element took it). The field vocabulary is the
    one type-name resolver's (`_tn_resolve`, language/core.cyml
    `type_names_v6616`); the refusal note lists bool, Result, Option and
    Tagged, and a misspelt prefix gets the `'i8x' is not 'i8'` hint.

⚠ 6.7.10 — PACKED NARROW UNSIGNED AND f32 FIELDS (the user's layout decision, 2026-10-08).
    Through 6.7.9 u8 / u16 / u32 and f32 each took 8 bytes (an f32 in the low half), so
    a struct declaring one CHANGED SIZE AND OFFSETS. Everything keyed on the size follows
    the new one: `sizeof`, a `*C4` steps 4, `C4[2]` is 8 bytes, and the by-value class (a
    struct of 8 B or less travels in one register, 9-16 B in two) — so `dyn`'s `self: T`
    refusal now meets a struct that shrank to 8 B or less, and the refusals keyed on "over
    8 B" stop applying to it (an if-expression branch, an `async fn` by-value parameter, a
    top-level call result `var G: C4 = mk4(9);`). The fields, `sizeof` and the
    `#derive(accessors)` fns (u8..u32 getters zero-extend, an f32 getter returns the
    binary32 bits) follow the layout; code that reached such a struct BY HAND does not —
    re-check `load64(p + 8)` for a second field, a literal size, a wire / file format
    written with `sizeof`, and an 8-byte load at a narrow field's offset (`load64(&s + off)`
    now reads the next fields too). `&x.f` itself is NOT an expression: it is refused by name
    since 6.7.13 (D3 (b), ST-8) — write `load64(&s + off)` at the field's offset, or reach the
    field through a `*T` (`var p: *S = &s; p.f`). PACKED MEANS UNALIGNED: a `u32` / `f32` after a `u8` sits at offset 1. A
    plain field read or write is fine on every target; an atomic or a futex word needs its
    natural alignment — declare it first or pad explicitly (`_pad: u8; _pad2: u16;`). A
    struct redefined with another narrow KIND of the same width (`i8` vs `u8`, `i32` vs
    `f32`) warns, as one with another layout does. A declaration-zone literal with an f32
    field bakes the binary32 bits (only an x86 `kernel;` image could read the difference).
    An f32 SCALAR (`var x: f32`) keeps its 8-byte slot. Gates:
    tests/gates/frontend/narrow_fields_checked.sh, tests/tcyr/crossos/narrow_unsigned_f32_fields.tcyr.
    The guide: § Field types. CHANGELOG [6.7.10] (B7-2).

[[entries]]
name = "struct_storage_class_v665"
description = "Where a struct lives: inside a fn it is a PER-CALL, PER-THREAD frame object; at top level it is one shared object; `var p: T = <expr>` yielding an address is a POINTER to someone else's struct (v6.6.5)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX AND MEANING:
    fn f(n) {
        var a = P { n, n * 2 };   # per call, per thread
        var b: P;                 # per call, per thread
        var c: P = mk();          # mk(): *P or untyped — a HANDLE (pointer) to what it
                                  # returned; mk(): P (a struct value) — a COPY (6.6.6)
        var d: P = a;             # a COPY of a (all STRUCTSZ bytes)
        d = c;                    # since 6.6.16: a COPY of what c points at (c is a pointer)
        return a.x + c.y;
    }
    var G = P { 1, 2 };           # ONE shared object in the data section

WHAT CHANGED AT v6.6.5:
    A struct LITERAL written inside a fn used to be compiled as a GLOBAL —
    shared across recursion, shared across threads, and visible to every
    later reference of that name in ANY file. `var cfg = P { 1, 2 };` inside
    a library fn captured a consumer's top-level `var cfg = 5;`, silently.
    It is a frame object now, like `var p: T;` has always been.

    The compiler also RECORDS, at the declaration, whether a variable is the
    struct or a pointer to one. Before v6.6.5 it inferred that from the shape
    of the neighbouring stack slot and got it wrong for any pointer-mode
    struct declared after a closed `{ ... }` block or after a `callptr` /
    `bitset` / SIMD expression.

WHAT FOLLOWS FROM IT:
  - Operator overloading (`P_add`, `P_mul`, …) dispatches for a struct VALUE
    wherever it lives — literal local, `var p: P;`, global — and does NOT
    dispatch for a pointer-mode local (a handle), where `+` is pointer
    arithmetic. Before v6.6.5 a struct LOCAL silently did an integer add.
    ⚠ 6.7.10: `var p: P = 1` is a HANDLE to address 1 at every size (the handle
    line — struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710), so it no
    longer dispatches; declare it from a struct value (`P { 1 }`, `mk(1)`).
  - A closure capturing an enclosing struct or array copies it BY VALUE,
    including a stack array referenced as `&buf`.
  - `fn mk(): P { var p = P { 7, 8 }; return p; }` returns both fields.
  - A multi-word struct local inside a SUSPENDING `async fn` works (x86
    family). A coroutine's heap frame lays its slots out in the opposite
    direction to the stack, so every access bases the aggregate at its
    LOWEST-index slot (`_ECORO_AGG_OFF`, x86/emit.cyr) and its words are
    exactly the heap slots the block owns. (This line used to say such a
    local is refused: the first cut of 6.6.5 refused it, and that refusal
    was removed before 6.6.5 shipped as a capability regression.) A tuple
    local across an `await` works the same (6.7.7, tuple_checked.sh AS1 /
    AS2).

IF YOU RELIED ON THE OLD BEHAVIOUR (a literal that persisted across calls),
declare it at top level — that is what it actually was.

[[entries]]
name = "expression_type_propagation"
description = "Type annotations propagate through expressions for width-correct codegen"
category = "cyrius"
---
When operands have known widths, the compiler picks the correct
load/store size at codegen time. Mixing widths warns:

    var x: i32 = 100000;
    var y: i64 = 5;
    var z = x + y;        # warning: i32 + i64 — narrowing

    var x: i8 = 200;
    var n = x;            # n is i64; movzx zero-extends to 8 bytes

    var p: *i32 = ...;
    *p = 42;              # 4-byte store via mov dword [p], 42

NOTES:
- No implicit signed-vs-unsigned conversions; all integer types
  are signed under the `iN` annotation.
- Pointer arithmetic on typed pointers (`*i32`) scales by element
  size — `p + 1` advances 4 bytes for `*i32`, 8 bytes for `*i64`.

[[entries]]
name = "defer_statement"
description = "defer { body } — execute cleanup code at function return (LIFO)"
category = "cyrius"
---
defer { body } executes body when the enclosing function returns.
Multiple defers execute in LIFO order (last declared = first to run).
The WHOLE return convention survives the defer bodies (cycc 6.6.7): an
Ok/Err payload, a tuple / ret2 pair, a two-word struct, an f64, an
f64v2/f64v4 — on x86 rax/rdx/r8/xmm0/xmm1, on aarch64 x0/x2/x3/q0/q1,
on cx r0-r5. Before 6.6.7 only rax was kept, so a defer that called
anything could zero an Ok payload. Since 6.6.15 the 64-byte area those
registers are saved in is CLEARED before it is released (+74 B x86_64,
+16 B aarch64 per fn with a defer or secret var): it used to outlive a
`secret var` wipe, holding whatever the return registers held (the secret-var epilogue spill gap).

EVERY RETURN PATH (cycc 6.6.7):
  A defer (and every `secret var` zeroise) runs on every return, a
  tail-shaped `return f(x);` included — and `return Ok(v);` /
  `return Err(e);` are exactly that shape. So a fn with a defer or a
  secret var anywhere in its body NEVER tail-calls, and is never
  inlined (`#inline` warns and is ignored). Deep self-recursion in such
  a fn keeps a frame per level: loop instead, or move the defer into a
  wrapper around a defer-free helper. Before 6.6.7 the tail call jumped
  past every defer (a skipped unlock = a deadlock; a skipped secret
  zeroise = the tail-call secret-var wipe gap).

WHERE IT MAY APPEAR (cycc 6.6.7 — each used to compile silently wrong):
  - only inside a fn (closure and async fn included); top-level defer
    is refused, like `secret var` / `stack var`
  - a defer body cannot leave its fn: return / `?` / ret2 inside it are
    refused, as is a break/continue jumping out of it (a loop opened
    inside the body is fine); a break/continue with nothing to leave in
    the same fn is refused too (it used to jump into another fn)
  - a defer inside a closure belongs to the CLOSURE and runs when the
    closure returns (it used to register on the enclosing fn — the closure secret-var wipe gap
    for a closure's `secret var`)
  - in a coroutine async fn it runs once, at completion; an `await`
    inside a defer body is refused
  - not in a `#naked` fn (6.6.15, the naked-fn secret-var gap): a naked fn has no epilogue,
    so the block never ran and a `secret var` was never zeroised —
    both now refused by name, as `return` always was. On cx `#naked` is
    inert and the defer runs.
  The five ways a `secret var` wipe has been skipped or copied around:
  field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
  `secret_var_zeroisation_skipped_four_ways_and_copied_around_once_enumerate_every_exit_and_every_epilogue_store`.

USAGE:
  fn process_file(path) {
      var fd = file_open(path, O_RDONLY, 0);
      defer { file_close(fd); }
      defer { file_unlock(fd); }
      file_lock(fd);
      # ... work with file ...
      return result;  # defers run: unlock, then close
  }

EXIT-PATH SAFETY:
  Per-defer runtime flags (hidden locals). Each defer sets a flag
  to 1 when reached. At epilogue, flag is checked before running
  the block. Unreached defers are skipped — early return BEFORE
  a defer statement won't trigger that defer's cleanup (closing
  the resource-double-free class).

IMPLEMENTATION:
  Token 106. Deferred blocks compiled inline (jumped over during
  normal flow). At function epilogue, emit jmp→block→jmp→block→
  epilogue chain. Each block's end-jmp patched to return to the
  chain.

Max 8 defer blocks per function. Zig/Odin parity.

[[entries]]
name = "ifplat_directive"
description = "#ifplat PLAT / #endplat — sugar for #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_<UPPER>"
category = "cyrius"
---
`#ifplat PLAT` / `#endplat` is a cleaner spelling for arch-gated
blocks. Strictly additive — the old `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86` /
`#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` forms keep working.

USAGE:

    #ifplat x86
    asm { ... x86 bytes ... }
    #endplat
    #ifplat aarch64
    asm { ... aarch64 bytes ... }
    #endplat

    #ifplat aarch64
    asm { dmb ish; }      # D5033BBF — compiles since 6.7.15
    #endplat

The guide's own `#ifplat aarch64` / `asm { dmb ish }` example did not compile through
6.7.14 ("unknown aarch64 asm mnemonic 'ish'": a barrier took no option). Since 6.7.15 (the
user's D37; CHANGELOG [6.7.15] IT-7, IT-8) `dmb` / `dsb` take sy, st, ld, ish, ishst,
ishld, nsh, nshst, nshld, osh, oshst or oshld, `isb` takes sy, and a bare barrier is the
full-system one (sy) — through 6.7.14 bare `dsb` was SSBB and `wfe` an MSR that faulted at
EL0. A block closes with `#endplat` or `#endif`.

SUPPORTED PLATFORMS:
- x86      → resolves to CYRIUS_ARCH_X86
- aarch64  → resolves to CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
- Unknown tokens (e.g. riscv64) fail closed — the body is
  skipped unconditionally.

SEMANTICS:
Identical to `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_<UPPER>`. The evaluator
hashes the token to the canonical compiler-builtin symbol
and looks it up in the same flag table as #ifdef. No separate
state; no new scopes.

NESTED BLOCKS work (skip_depth tracking mirrors #ifdef).
`#ifplat` can be nested inside `#ifdef` and vice versa.

WHEN TO USE:
- New code that gates on architecture: prefer `#ifplat`.
- Existing code using `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_*`: no migration
  required. Both forms are first-class.

FUTURE: the `(arch, format)` tuple form — e.g.
`#ifplat aarch64-macho`, `#ifplat x86-pe` — is reserved.

[[entries]]
name = "enum_namespacing"
description = "Enum.VARIANT syntax — qualified enum access in all expression contexts"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    enum Color { RED; GREEN; BLUE; }
    var c = Color.RED;
    if (c == Color.BLUE) { ... }
    fn paint(color) { return color == Color.GREEN; }

SEMANTICS:
    Enum.VARIANT works everywhere an expression is valid:
    assignments, comparisons, function arguments, return values,
    array indices — and, since 6.6.10, `#assert` operands and
    array sizes (`var buf[Sz.BUF]`).

    ⚠ THE QUALIFIER IS CHECKED (cycc 6.6.11). The base must name an
    enum, and VARIANT must be a member of an enum of THAT name:

        enum E1 { EA = 1; EB = 2; }
        enum E2 { EC = 3; }
        Foo.EB     # error: 'Foo' is not an enum
        E2.EB      # error: 'EB' is not a variant of 'E2'

    The same resolver (`_enum_qual_resolve`, parse.cyr) serves
    expressions, `#assert` and array sizes, and each mistake is
    reported once. An enum NAME may be declared more than once
    (every lib/syscalls*.cyr declares `Signal`); the variant is
    looked up across all enums of that name.

    Two enums may share a variant name, and the qualified form is
    how you pick one: with `enum A { X = 1; }` and
    `enum B { X = 2; }`, `A.X` is 1 and `B.X` is 2. The BARE `X`
    keeps "last definition wins" (2) and warns
    `duplicate symbol 'X' redefined with conflicting value`.
    A prior constant ZERO counts since 6.7.12 (D30): `var a = 0;
    var a = 5;` warns "duplicate symbol 'a' redefined with
    conflicting value (last definition wins)" as every differing
    pair does (through 6.7.11 a recorded 0 read as "no constant",
    so two files' `var _shared = 0;` / `= 5;` became one cell
    unseen). A same value stays silent and a computed prior is not
    compared. The new value was compared only as an exact
    `= NUM ;` literal through 6.7.13; since 6.7.14 any later
    initializer that FOLDS to a constant is (`= 2 + 1`, `= -5`,
    `= (7)`, `true` / `false` as 1 / 0 — FE-18); since 6.7.15 a
    NAMED constant on either side is too (`var a = 3; var a = C;`,
    `var a = C; var a = 3;`, `= E.B`, `var a = D; var a = C;` —
    each later constant against the last constant before it,
    FE-14; through 6.7.14 those were silent) —
    declaration_zone_constant_initializers_v6714. CHANGELOG
    [6.7.12] FX-6, [6.7.15] FE-14.

    Since 6.7.16 a FLOAT constant redeclaration is compared too, bit
    for bit (a differing pair warns at the later `=`, a same-bits pair
    is silent), and it is the global's value from program start (D13
    (a), ZN-2 — declaration_zone_constant_initializers_v6714).

    A MEMBER DECLARED NOWHERE (6.7.16, FF-15): `Color.NOPE` with
    `Color` a declared enum is "'NOPE' is not a variant of 'Color'" at
    NOPE in every context — an expression, a const, a case label, an
    array size, `#assert`, an assignment, `OP=`, `&`, a statement
    (through 6.7.15 "undefined variable 'Color'" / "unknown name
    'Color' in a const context"). A type-argument list after an enum
    is judged by what the enum declares (6.7.16's D88, FF-49): `Color<i64>`
    is "'Color' takes no type arguments" — tagged_unions_v58x.

    BEFORE 6.6.11 THE QUALIFIER WAS IGNORED. The parser only asked
    whether the name after the dot was a global, so `Foo.EB` and
    `E2.EB` compiled to EB's value, `Nope.gz` loaded a plain global
    `gz`, and `A.X` read the LAST enum's X (A.X * 10 + B.X was 22,
    not 12). Code that compiled only because of that now fails —
    at the 6.6.11 cut, four consumers carried stale kavach
    spellings (`Backend.PROCESS` for the renamed `KavachBackend`,
    `KavachError.OK` for `KavachError.KAVACH_ERR_OK`, which had
    been reading whichever global named `OK` came last). Across
    1,655 ecosystem builds nothing else changed.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    Enum variants are still compiled as global constants; the
    qualifier is resolved through the enum table (`_enum_id_of`,
    `_enum_variant_in`) rather than by treating `Enum.` as noise.
    A base that is a VARIABLE is a field access, not an enum
    access (`p.x` never consults the enum table).

⚠ AN ENUM CONSTANT IS NOT AN LVALUE (cycc 6.7.3). With
    `enum E { A = 5; }`, these are refused by name:
    `A = 6;`, `A += 1;` (every OP=), `&A`, `E.A = 6;`, `E.A += 1;`,
    `&E.A`, and a for step that writes one.

        error: cannot assign to enum constant 'A' - an enum constant is
               a fixed value, not a variable (declare a `var` to change it)

    `&Ctor` of a payload variant is refused the same way (it was the
    variant's slot, never the constructor's address). The check uses
    the slot the name RESOLVES to, so a local `var A`, a closure's
    capture of one, and a later top-level `var A` (last definition
    wins) stay variables.
    BEFORE 6.7.3 the bare forms compiled silently. Every read folds
    to 5, but the write landed in the variant's 8-byte slot and `&A`
    returned that slot: `A = 6; A * 10 + load64(&A)` was 56. The
    qualified write was refused only as "undefined variable 'E'".
    Gate: tests/gates/frontend/enum_const_not_lvalue.sh.

[[entries]]
name = "ref_directive"
description = "#ref directive — compile-time TOML loading"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    #ref "config.toml"
    # If config.toml contains:
    #   width = 800
    #   height = 600
    # Then after preprocessing, the source contains:
    #   var width = 800;
    #   var height = 600;

SEMANTICS:
    #ref "file.toml" loads a TOML file at compile time and emits
    var declarations for each key-value pair. Skips comments (#),
    section headers ([section]), and blank lines. String values
    become string literals, numeric values become integer literals.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    PP_REF_PASS runs as a separate preprocessing pass BEFORE PP_PASS.
    This ordering is critical — #ref must expand before #ifdef and
    other preprocessor directives see the generated var declarations.

    ISREF() detects the #ref directive. The pass reads the TOML
    file, parses key = value lines, and emits `var key = value;`
    for each valid entry.

[[entries]]
name = "ret2_rethi"
description = "Return two values via rax:rdx register pair; since 6.7.13 `ret2(a, b)` is `return (a, b);` spelled as a builtin, judged by every rule the literal is; since 6.7.14 a ret2 fn is a 2-value fn to every count, and `ret2` is refused in a vector-returning fn; since 6.7.16 a `ret2` after a case label counts, a struct value over 8 B is no multi-value element (D90), and a chain's method destructures"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    fn divmod(a, b) {
        var q = a / b;
        var r = a % b;
        ret2(q, r);        # return two values: rax=q, rdx=r
    }
    var result = divmod(17, 5);   # rax = 3 (quotient)
    var remainder = rethi();      # rdx = 2 (remainder)

SEMANTICS:
    ret2(a, b) packs two values into the rax:rdx register pair and
    returns. The first argument goes to rax (normal return), the
    second to rdx. rethi() retrieves the second return value from
    rdx (mov rax, rdx).

    Follows the System V AMD64 ABI convention where functions can
    return two 64-bit values in rax and rdx.

ALTERNATIVE — NATIVE MULTI-RETURN (v3.7.2):
    fn divmod(a, b) { return (a / b, a % b); }   # PARENS REQUIRED
    var q, r = divmod(10, 3);                    # targets are NOT parenthesised

⚠ THE PARENS ARE MANDATORY ON THE RETURN AND FORBIDDEN ON THE BIND.
This entry documented `return a / b, a % b;` — WITHOUT parens — from
v3.7.2 until v6.5.21. That form is a parse error
(`expected ';', got ','`), and `var (q, r) = f();` is another
(`expected identifier, got '('`). The cost was not theoretical: an
abaco consumer filed a proposal in 2026-08 asking for multi-value
returns as a NEW feature, its "verified against 6.5.20" table listing
exactly these two spellings as unsupported — because vidya, the place
CLAUDE.md tells you to search before reimplementing, had the working
syntax written down wrong. The feature had shipped four majors earlier.

DECLARED FORM (v6.5.21) — arity and element types in the signature:
    fn two_product(a, b): (f64, f64) { return (p, err); }
    var prod, e = two_product(x, y);   # both bindings typed f64

Arity 2 or 3. Declaring the return type is what lets the compiler
arity-check a FORWARD call and type the bindings; an undeclared
`return (a, b);` still works and stays untyped.

Two values come back via rax + rdx (x0/x2 on aarch64, r0/r4 on cx);
a third uses r8 / x3 / r5. ret2/rethi remain the lower-level primitive
and share the same register pair.

SINCE 6.7.7 (tuples, B4) — keep every value, or re-assign:
    var t: (i64, i64) = divmod(10, 3);   # a tuple captures BOTH: t.0 = 3, t.1 = 1
    q, r = divmod(20, 3);                # re-assigns existing variables: 6, 2
    fn bounds(n): (i64, i64) { var t = (n - 1, n + 1); return t; }
`var x = f();` is still the FIRST value (the ret2 / rethi() idiom); the
capture and `a, b = f();` take what the destructure takes (one whole
call) and refuse a struct-returning callee. See tuples_v677.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    Tokens: ret2 = 97, rethi = 98. Handled in PARSE_SIMD_EXT.

    ret2(a, b):
      1. Evaluate b → rax
      2. mov rdx, rax    # second value in rdx
      3. Evaluate a → rax  # first value in rax
      4. Emit function return (epilogue + ret)

    rethi():
      1. mov rax, rdx    # move second return value to rax

SINCE 6.7.11 (RET-1 .. RET-4) — the value counts are judged on the PRODUCER side too:
  - THE ONE-VALUE PROOF (6.6.17) also covers a body ending in `loop { }`, `while (1) { }` or
    `do { } while (1);` whose block holds a `return` and has no `break` that leaves it:
    `var a, b = one(1);` from such a fn is "binds 2 names, but 'one' returns 1 value". A loop a
    `break` can leave is not judged. (`while (true)` / `do { } while (true);` were not judged either
    through 6.7.12; since 6.7.13 they count as the `1` spellings — see below.)
  - AN UNDECLARED fn whose every `return` is `(..)` of ONE arity, and whose body is left only
    through them, HAS that count: a destructure, a tuple capture or `a, b[, c] = f();` binding
    another count is refused — "binds 3 names, but 'dm' returns 2 values", "cannot capture 'dm'
    into tuple (i64, i64, i64) - it returns 2 values" — FEWER names included, as the declared
    spelling's arity check refuses. Mixed arities and a body that can fall off its end are not judged.
  - THE PRODUCER: in a fn declared `: (T1, T2[, T3])` every `return` is `return (a, b[, c]);` of the
    declared arity, `return t;` of a tuple, or a whole forwarded call returning as many values
    (declared, counted, or unjudgeable: a fn pointer, `callptr`). Anything else — one value,
    `return p;` of a struct, `return;`, `return (5);`, another arity, a one-value / struct / vector /
    dyn callee, the tail call too — is "'f' returns 2 values - return (a, b)": it filled only the
    first register and the caller's `b` read whatever an earlier call left. The rule is 6.7.7 B4's
    "a struct return is not multiple values", on the producer side. Since 6.7.13 it also covers an
    ASYNC forward: in an async fn not declared multi-value, `return h(..);` of a multi-value call is
    refused (6.7.13's D24, async_await_futures_v6311).
  - A STRUCT'S WORDS ARE ITS RETURN REGISTERS: `var a, b = f();` from a 9-16 B struct fn binds its two
    words; three names from 9-16 B ("whose 2 words are its return registers - bind 2 names"), a
    struct over 16 B ("which is not in registers - bind it to one name") and two names from 8 B or
    less or a `Str` ("returns 1 value") are refused, judged by the DECLARED return.
  - An `async fn` returning several values is refused (async_await_futures_v6311).
  Gates and the full table: the guide § Multi-value returns. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (RET-1 .. RET-4).

SINCE 6.7.13 (MV-9 .. MV-18; the user's D20 – D23, D27, D28) — `ret2` is the literal, and the edges are defined:
  - `while (true) { }` and `do { } while (true);` count as the `1` spellings (`true` is the bool literal 1, D27): a
    one-value fn ending in one is refused "binds 2 names, but 'one' returns 1 value" against `var a, b = one(1);`, and
    an undeclared `(a, b)` fn ending in one is counted. A loop a `break` leaves is still not judged.
  - `ret2(a, b)` IS `return (a, b);` SPELLED AS A BUILTIN, and every rule for the literal applies to it:
      outside a fn body (top level, a top-level block, a global's initializer)  "ret2 outside a fn body" (D20)
      in a fn declared `: (T1, T2, T3)`                                          "'f' returns 3 values - return (a, b, c)" (D21)
      in a fn declared `: S` (a named struct, any size)                          "a fn returning the struct `S` returns that
                                                                                  struct — `ret2` writes two values" (D22)
      in an async fn                                                             refused since 6.7.11
    Each was a silent wrong value or a hang (outside a fn x86_64 / PE ran on past it, aarch64 / cx hung; the third
    value was stale; a struct fn got 0s over 16 B). A closure's own `ret2` and an `#inline` fn's are THAT fn's (an
    `#inline` fn's `return ret2(..);` replayed inside a 9-16 B struct fn compiles — the callee returns, not the
    struct fn; kept by the user's 6.7.14 D48). A fn declared to return a VECTOR refuses it too since 6.7.14 (D43 —
    below); through 6.7.13 `ret2` there compiled and returned no vector.
  - THE FALL-OFF RULE (D28 C): a fn declared `: (T1, T2[, T3])` whose body reaches its `}` returns 0 for EVERY value,
    the pair to a bool fn's `false` (6.7.3). 6.7.11's D17 refuses a bare `return;`; D28 defines the fall-off. Before,
    the caller read stale registers. No diagnostic: a forgotten final `return` gives 0s. An `#inline` replay of such a
    fn falls off to 0s too.
  - A SYNC FN DECLARED ONE TYPE THAT RETURNS SEVERAL VALUES IS KEPT (D23 B): `fn g2(): i64 { return (1, 2); }` (or
    `ret2(1, 2);`) compiles — the declared type types EACH value (the v6.5.21 `: f64` tuple box, 6.7.3's bool element
    0, 6.7.7 R16i); pass 1 counts a `return (a, b)` body, so three names and a 3-tuple capture are refused; a one-name
    bind keeps the first value. `: (T1, T2)` is the recommended spelling. A `ret2` body was NOT counted through 6.7.13
    (`var a, b, c = g2();` read a stale third register); since 6.7.14 it is (D42 — below).
  - DESTRUCTURE: the "needs a call" list includes `callptr(fp, ..)` since 6.7.13 (MV-6; the count is not checked, as
    for `fncallN`). `var t: (i64, i64) = callptr(..)` was still refused "not a tuple" through 6.7.13; since 6.7.14 it
    captures (RC-19 — below).
  Field notes: field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml multi_value_returns_since_6_7_13;
  field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml multi_value_fall_off_lands_at_the_fn_end_and_the_replay_end_v6713. CHANGELOG [6.7.13]
  (MV-1 .. MV-19).

SINCE 6.7.14 (the user's D42, D43, D45; CHANGELOG [6.7.14] RC-16, RC-17, RC-19, RC-20) — `ret2(a, b)` is `return (a, b);`
to every reader of the count:
  - A FN LEFT ONLY THROUGH `ret2(..);`, `return ret2(..);` and `return (a, b);` — a final one, or an if / elif / else
    whose every arm ends in one — RETURNS 2 VALUES to pass 1's count (D42, RC-16): `var a, b, c = g2();`, `a, b, c =
    g2();` and `var t: (i64, i64, i64) = g2();` are refused "multi-value destructure binds 3 names, but 'g2' returns 2
    values - bind 2 names" / "cannot capture 'g2' into tuple (i64, i64, i64) - it returns 2 values"; such a fn into a
    by-value struct parameter or a struct declaration is "a multi-value call 'g2' is not a struct 'P' argument" (it
    crashed, 139); a fn declared `: (T1, T2, T3)` forwarding it with `return g2(..);` is "'f' returns 3 values". Through
    6.7.13 the third name read a stale register (88 on every target). ⚠ The if / else end counts a fn with no `ret2`
    too: three names from a fn whose if / else arms each `return (a, b);` are refused (it compiled, 88 — beyond D42's
    question; kept by the user's 6.7.15 D62). Unchanged:
    two names (`var q, r = divmod_old(..)`), one name and `rethi()`, the 2-tuple, and a `ret2` inside an expression
    (`var z = ret2(1, 2);`, not counted).
  - SINCE 6.7.15 (RC-1, within 6.7.14's D42 and 6.7.15's D62; no new decision) A BODY ENDING IN AN INFINITE LOOP — `loop
    { }`, `while (1)` / `while (true)`, `do { } while (1);` / `(true)` — that no `break` leaves, whose only exits are
    `ret2(..);` statements, returns 2 values too; a `ret2(..);` in a nested `while` / `for` or a switch's case BLOCK
    inside the loop counts. `var a, b, c = g2(1);` and the 3-tuple capture are refused with the words above; through
    6.7.14 both compiled and the third value read a stale register (88 on every target). Pass 1 had looked for a
    `return` TOKEN to prove the loop's exits, and a `ret2(..);` statement has none. A loop a `break` leaves is still
    not counted. A `ret2(..);` written directly after a `case N:` / `default:` label counts since 6.7.16 (RV-12 — below).
  - `ret2(a, b);` IN A FN DECLARED TO RETURN A VECTOR (`: f64v2`, `f64v4`, `f32v4`, `i32v4`, …) is refused by the 6.6.6
    vector rule's text, once, at the ret2 (D43, RC-17): "a vector-returning fn carries its result in a fixed register
    pair, so `return` takes a local of that vector type or a call to a fn declared to return the same one — got
    `ret2`". It compiled and the caller's vector held the vector registers' leftovers (0 on x86_64, aarch64 and PE, 41
    on cx). `return ret2(..);` there keeps its one refusal ("… got `this expression`"); a `ret2` in a closure, or in an
    `#inline` fn replayed in the vector fn, is that fn's own and compiles. Store the lanes into a local of the vector
    type and `return` it.
  - So `ret2` is refused wherever the fn's declared return is not two words: outside a fn, in a 3-value fn, in a
    struct fn (6.7.13's D20 – D22) and in a vector fn (D43).
  - THE TUPLE CAPTURE TAKES `callptr(fp, ..)` (RC-19): `var t: (i64, i64) = callptr(fp, 5);`, `t = callptr(..);` (a for
    step too), `G = callptr(..);` at top level and a global in both top-level zones capture every value, as
    `fncall1(fp, ..)` did; the callee is unknown, so its count is never judged. `callptr(..) + 1` stays refused (R12),
    and a refused capture names the callee 'callptr'.
  - PARENTHESES AROUND THE WHOLE CALL ARE TRANSPARENT to the destructure and the capture (D45, RC-20): `var a, b =
    (f(7));`, `((..))`, `a, b = (f());`, a classic-for step, both top-level zones and `var t: (i64, i64) = (two(3));`
    compile exactly as the unwrapped spelling. Not one call — `(f(7)) + 1`, `(f(7) + 1)` — stays refused.
  The guide: § Multi-Return, § Capture by type. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] (RC-16 .. RC-20), [6.7.15] RC-1.

SINCE 6.7.16 (CHANGELOG [6.7.16] RV-12, RV-16, GS-17, GS-18, FF-46, FF-52, SB-1; the user's D90; no new decision for the
rest):
  - A `ret2(..);` STATEMENT STARTS after `;`, `{`, `}` AND DIRECTLY AFTER A `case N:` / `default:` LABEL (RV-12, within
    6.7.14's D42, kept by 6.7.15's D62): `fn g2(x) { switch (x) { case 1: ret2(1, 2); default: x = 1; } ret2(3, 4); }` is a
    2-value fn, so `var a, b, c = g2(1);` and the 3-tuple capture are refused with the words above — through 6.7.15 the
    label's `:` made it read as a `ret2` inside an expression, no count was recorded, and the third name read a stale
    register (88 on every target). Also an expression case value (`case 1 + 1:`) and a method (`P.dm`).
  - `ret2(a, b)`'s OWN REFUSALS COME AFTER ITS ARGUMENTS (RV-16): in a struct fn (6.7.13's D22), a vector fn (6.7.14's
    D43), a 3-value fn (6.7.13's D21) and outside a fn (6.7.13's D20) — so `ret2(zz, 1);` reports `zz` alone (it printed
    the refusal and "undefined variable 'zz'"); arguments that parse keep the refusal.
  - IN A GENERIC, a wrong-count `return` is ONE report naming the generic, `'gr' returns 2 values - return (a, b)`, however
    many instances re-parse the body, and none beside an error the statement already has (`return 1, 2;` is `expected
    ';'` alone) — it printed one line per instance under the mangled name (GS-17); a method's names it as written,
    `'P.mv'` (GS-18).
  - A STRUCT VALUE OVER 8 BYTES IS NO MULTI-VALUE ELEMENT (D90, "Refuse the element at the return"; FF-46): `return (b,
    5);` with a 16-byte `b` — in any multi-value fn, declared or not; also `(mk(), 5)`, a method / operator result, a
    by-value parameter or field, a value global, `*p` — is "'mv' returns struct 'Pt' (16 bytes) as a multi-value element
    - return its pointer, or its fields as elements", once, at the element. Through 6.7.15 the element handed back the
    struct's FIRST WORD silently (`b.y` gone; passed on as a `Pt` it faulted). Still elements: an 8-byte struct, a word,
    `&b`, a `*Pt`, `b.x`, a `Str`, and a HANDLE named whole (FF-52 — a struct local initialised from an address, a
    pointer-mode global, wrapped): its word IS the struct's address, so it returns the pointer, as it always did. The
    `(Pt, i64)` DECLARATION keeps compiling (D90's own text).
  - THE DESTRUCTURE'S "WHOLE CALL" INCLUDES A METHOD ENDING A CHAIN (SB-1; no decision — the single name's rules applied
    to every head): `var c, d = w.p.m2();`, `h.w.p.m2()`, `w.gp().m2()`, `mk().p.m2()`, `(*pw).p.m2()`, three names, a
    re-assignment, a for step, both top-level zones — through 6.7.15 "needs a call on the right-hand side" (the workaround
    `var p = w.p; var c, d = p.m2();` can go). A generic step is typed as the method arm types it (SB-1r, SB-4r, SB-4r2):
    `w.p.idv(q).m2()` / `w.p.idv<P>(q).m2()` with `fn idv<V>(self, x: V): V` continue through the struct V binds (a `: *V`
    return too), a `: G<V>` through that instance of G. A step whose argument is itself a generic call
    (`q.idv(q.idv(q)).m2()`) is still refused, as `q.idv(q.idv(q)).k` is — annotate the inner result first.

[[entries]]
name = "continue_in_for_loops"
description = "continue statement with forward-patch mechanism for for/while-loops"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    for (var i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) {
        if (i == 5) { continue; }
        print_i64(i);
    }

SEMANTICS:
    continue skips the rest of the loop body and jumps to the step
    expression (i = i + 1) in for-loops, or to the condition check
    in while-loops.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    Forward-patch mechanism. Array of up to 8 continue patches per
    loop scope.

    For-loop compilation:
    1. Compile init (var i = 0)
    2. Emit condition check (i < 10)
    3. Compile body — any `continue` emits a jmp with placeholder
       and records the patch location in the array
    4. After body, record current position as the step expression start
    5. Compile step (i = i + 1)
    6. Patch all continue jmps to point to step expression

[[entries]]
name = "derive_str_fields"
description = "#derive(Serialize) — auto-generate JSON serializer/deserializer for structs (i8..i64, u8/u16/u32, f64, f32, bool, Str, cstring, Vec<T>, nested; f64 v6.3.40; u8..u32 / f32 / cstring fields and a Str decode that unescapes everything into a fresh copy since 6.7.10; u64 unsupported) A nested derived struct field decodes in the scanner too since 6.7.11; a scalar field holding an object / array is walked whole since 6.7.13."
category = "cyrius"
---
`#derive(Serialize)` before a struct auto-generates
`Name_to_json(ptr, sb)` that writes JSON into a str_builder.

REQUIRED INCLUDES (cyrius doesn't auto-pull these):
    include "lib/syscalls.cyr"
    include "lib/string.cyr"
    include "lib/str.cyr"
    include "lib/alloc.cyr"
    include "lib/fmt.cyr"
    # serializer-only ↑    ↓ deserializer (round-trip) needs these too:
    include "lib/vec.cyr"
    include "lib/hashmap.cyr"
    include "lib/bayan.cyr"   # 6.1.x: json/toml/base64 bundled in bayan
    # 6.7.10: the guide's example (§ Derive Serialize on a struct) builds with
    # `include "lib/bayan.cyr"` alone; an f64 / f32 field needs bayan in scope.

USAGE:
    #derive(Serialize)
    struct Agent { id: i64; name: Str; }

    var a = Agent { 42, str_from("alice") };
    var sb = str_builder_new();
    Agent_to_json(&a, sb);
    var s = str_builder_build(sb);
    # s is a `Str` (16-byte heap header {ptr, len}).
    # To print:
    str_println(s);
    # NOT `println(s)` — `println` expects a cstring and will
    # interpret the Str header bytes as garbage (low addr +
    # length). Use `str_println(s)` for the Str-typed case;
    # it's the canonical helper (lib/str.cyr line 72,
    # `str_print` + `\n`). Surfaced by agnosys 1.1.12 re-file
    # 2026-05-07 where the consumer used `println(out)` and
    # reported "garbage output"; root cause was API misuse,
    # not codegen.

SEMANTICS:
    Untyped scalar fields (no annotation) → bare JSON numbers: 42
    `: i8` / `: i16` / `: i32` / `: i64`  → bare JSON numbers via
                                            str_builder_add_int +
                                            width-correct loadN
                                            (v5.9.30 i64; v5.9.36
                                            i8/i16/i32 added)
    `: f64` fields                        → bare JSON numbers (v6.3.40;
                                            codec rewired to bayan Grisu2
                                            at v6.4.69):
                                            `_to_json` renders via
                                            bayan_f64_to_json (shortest
                                            round-trip-correct output);
                                            `_from_json`/`_from_json_str`
                                            parse via bayan_f64_from_json
                                            (correctly-rounded, DoS-safe).
                                            An f64-deriving struct MUST have
                                            `lib/bayan.cyr` in scope.
                                            Fractional values round-trip
                                            bit-exactly — e.g. 1/3 no longer
                                            truncates to 6 decimals.
    `: Str` fields                        → quoted JSON strings: "alice"
    Nested struct fields (typed by another
    `#derive(Serialize)` struct name)     → recursive _to_json call
    `: u8` / `: u16` / `: u32` (6.7.10)   → bare JSON numbers, read ZERO-extended
                                            (4000000000, never negative);
                                            decoding stores at the field's width,
                                            so 300 into a u8 is 44 (no range
                                            check, as i8..i32)
    `: f32` (6.7.10)                      → written WIDENED to f64 through
                                            bayan's printer: 0.1f is
                                            0.10000000149011612, 1.5f is 1.5;
                                            decoded as f64 and rounded to the
                                            nearest f32 — an exact round trip
    `: cstring` (6.7.10)                  → `null` for NULL, else an escaped
                                            JSON string (the Str escaper:
                                            `"`, `\` and U+0000..U+001F
                                            escaped); see below
    `: bool`, `Vec<T>` of integers / f64 /
    a #derive'd struct                    → true / false; a JSON array
    `: u64`                               → NOT supported (the build fails
                                            naming `u64_to_json`); nor Vec<Str>

6.7.10 — cstring WIRE SHAPE (the user's decision 2, sigil's hand-rolled shape) AND THE
Str DECODE FIX (decision 9):
    #derive(Serialize)
    struct Px { r: u8; n: u32; w: f32; host: cstring; note: cstring; s: Str; }
    # p.r = 255; p.n = 4000000000; p.w = 0.1; p.host = "a\"b"; p.note = 0; p.s = str_from("x\ty");
    # Px_to_json(&p, sb) ->
    # {"r":255,"n":4000000000,"w":0.10000000149011612,"host":"a\"b","note":null,"s":"x\ty"}
  - a cstring decodes `null` to NULL and anything else to a FRESH `alloc`'d NUL-terminated
    copy (the field never points into the document): a JSON string unescaped per RFC 8259
    (`\uHHHH` written as UTF-8, a surrogate pair joined, a lone surrogate U+FFFD), any other
    value its text (`42` gives "42"). A quoted "null" is the 4-byte string `null` in both
    decoders, never NULL. A decoded `\u0000` ENDS the C string there.
  - a Str decodes EVERY escape the same way in both decoders, into a fresh Str whose length
    counts a decoded U+0000. Through 6.7.9 the scanner decoded only \n \t \r (`"\u0001\b"`
    read `u0001b`) and the pairs form kept every escape raw AND returned a BORROW into the
    parsed document — code that relied on either changes.
  - public in lib/str.cyr: str_builder_add_json_str(sb, s), str_builder_add_json_cstr(sb, p)
    (`null` for NULL), str_json_unescape(p, n) (a fresh Str), str_json_unescape_cstr(p, n)
    (a fresh C string), over the n bytes between a JSON string's quotes.
  6.7.11 (ST-21) — NESTED STRUCTS IN THE SCANNER: a field typed with a Serialize / Deserialize-derived
    struct is decoded through `<T>_from_json_str` (an OBJECT value only; `null` or a number leaves 0);
    unknown keys and nested values are walked whole (the shared depth- and string-aware skip); an
    accessors-only, plain or later-derived struct and a derived ENUM field are skipped. Through 6.7.10 the
    scanner did not decode a nested #derive'd struct field and lost every field after it (left 0).
  6.7.13 (ST-4) — A SCALAR FIELD'S VALUE IS WALKED WHOLE: in `T_from_json_str` a scalar field (integer, f64 /
    f32, bool, Str, cstring) holding an object, an array or a mismatched string gets what its type can take
    (0 for an object; a cstring its text) and the scan resumes AFTER the value — the scanner and the pairs
    form agree. Through 6.7.12 the scalar arms stopped at the object's `{` and the scan then read its inner
    keys as the outer struct's (the 6.7.11 advice "use the pairs form there" is withdrawn).
  Gates: tests/gates/frontend/derive_cstring_field.sh, tests/tcyr/derive/derive_serialize_narrow.tcyr,
  tests/tcyr/derive/derive_serialize_cstring.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.7.10] (B7-3, B7-4, B7-4b).

    Detection: preprocessor checks the type cstring's first
    bytes. `Str\0` → string path. `i8\0` / `i16\0` / `i32\0`
    / `i64\0` → primitive integer path (v5.9.36; narrow widths
    paired with parser-side struct-literal width-correct
    initializer fix in the same slot). `f64\0` → the float path
    (v6.3.40 — before that an f64 field emitted an undefined
    `f64_to_json` / `f64_from_json`, a hard "reachable undefined
    function" build error, same class as the i64 gap fixed v5.9.35).

GLOBAL-SCOPE Str-FIELD FIX (v6.3.44):
    A `var g = T { .., <str> };` at GLOBAL scope, where T has a Str
    field, used to fail to PARSE (`unexpected '}'`) — the in-fn init
    was fine. Root cause: EMIT_GVAR_INITS's pass-2 replay of a global
    struct literal is a PARALLEL COPY of PARSE_STRUCT_INIT's positional
    loop that never got the v5.10.7 single-8-byte-slot Str handling, so
    a Str field fell into the nested-struct FLATTEN branch and PCMPE'd a
    second (nonexistent) `len` expression, hitting `}`. Fixed by
    mirroring the IS_STR_FIELD branch into EMIT_GVAR_INITS. (The bug is
    `#derive`-INDEPENDENT — a plain global struct literal with a Str
    field reproduced it.)

DESERIALIZER COMPANION:
    #derive(Serialize) ALSO generates `_from_json(pairs)` (the
    `lib/json.cyr` json_get-based form) and `_from_json_str(json)`
    (single-pass byte scanner). Round-trip works for `Str` and
    `i64` fields:

        var p = Agent_from_json_str(str_data(s));
        var id = load64(p + 0);   # 42
        # p + 8 holds a Str header for "alice"

    Pre-v5.9.35 the deserializer emitted `<typename>_from_json(...)`
    for every typed-non-Str field — including primitives — so
    `i64` fields hit `i64_from_json` (undefined). v5.9.35
    (agnosys 1.1.12 re-file close) added i64 detection: typed
    i64 fields take the `str_to_int(v)` + `store64(ptr+offset, ...)`
    path mirroring the serializer side.

NARROW INTEGER WIDTHS (i8, i16, i32):
    Supported as of v5.9.36. Two compounding bugs were paired
    in that slot:
    (1) PP_DERIVE codegen — `prim_load` byte-gate detection
    extended with per-width outer-byte checks ('8'/'1'/'3'/'6')
    in `_to_json`, `_from_json`, `_from_json_str` AND
    `PP_PARSE_STRUCT_DEF`'s offset-table cumul.
    (2) Parser-side struct-literal initializer made width-
    correct via new `EMIT_STRUCT_FIELD_W(S, vcnt, boff, width)`
    (picks ESTORE8/16/32/STOC by width); `PARSE_STRUCT_INIT`
    positional + named + `EMIT_GVAR_INITS` all advance `boff`
    by `FIELDSZ(ft)` per field. `STRUCTSZ` semantics
    unchanged — `sizeof(Packet) == 15` (1+2+4+8 packed) still
    asserts. The fix is at the literal-write side, not the
    size-reporting side.

PLATFORM CAVEAT (Mach-O / macOS arm64) — RESOLVED v5.9.38/39:
    Pre-v5.9.38 the auto-generated `_to_json` body SIGSEGV'd
    at runtime on real macOS arm64 (ecb host). Two compounding
    bugs surfaced via lldb crash log:
    (1) **v5.9.38 Bug A** — `lib/fnptr.cyr` had no
        `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS` branches in `fncall0..8`;
        on Mach-O builds `fncallN` returned 0 → `alloc_via`
        returned NULL → `str_builder_new` returned NULL →
        SIGSEGV at `load64(NULL+8)` inside `_sb_grow_a`. Fix:
        macOS branches with x86_64 + aarch64 asm copies of
        the Linux block content (SysV-identical).
    (2) **v5.9.39 Bug B** — `src/backend/aarch64/fixup.cyr`
        ftype==3 emitted a static MOVZ/MOVK chain encoding
        the unslid file VA. Apple Silicon enforces MH_PIE;
        dyld slides the image at load time → `blr xN` jumped
        to (file_VA, no slide), landing in `__data` / `__got`
        instead of `__text`. Fix: dispatch to ADRP+ADD
        relative addressing on `_TARGET_MACHO == 2`,
        mirroring how ftype==1 (string addrs) and ftype==0
        (var addrs) already worked.

    Both fixes verified via `_macho_derive_serialize_gate`
    in programs/check.cyr — runs `point_to_json(&p, sb)` on
    ecb and asserts the byte-count exit code. Cross-host
    parity with Linux aarch64 (pi) confirmed at v5.9.39 ship.
    The agnosys 1.1.12 verbatim repro hash
    (`6425355b6147d5a674078794310ae2c1`) now executes through
    the build path on Mach-O — the residual SIGSEGV at the
    `println(strlen(out))` shape is a TYPE-SYSTEM gap pinned
    for v5.10.x, not a Mach-O codegen bug.

ALSO AVAILABLE — #derive(accessors):
    #derive(accessors) struct Point { x; y; }
    Generates: Point_x(p), Point_set_x(p, v), Point_y(p),
    Point_set_y(p, v)

    A derived struct NAME declared twice (6.6.11): the parser keeps
    the FIRST layout, so a second `#derive`d definition whose field
    names, order or size differ is refused —
        #derive: struct 'A' is defined again with different field
        names, order or size -- the FIRST definition is the layout
        used, so these accessors would read and write the wrong bytes
    Before 6.6.11 it built with warnings only: a larger second
    definition's setters wrote past the first one's sizeof, and
    `{ x; y; }` redefined as `{ y; x; }` had every setter write the
    OTHER field. The same names at the same offsets still build —
    one struct vendored by two libraries, or field TYPES that differ
    at the same width (`f64` in one copy, `i64` in the other).

PP_DERIVE handles both — same preprocessor codegen pattern.

[[entries]]
name = "secret_var_compound_ops"
description = "Cyrius syntax extras — struct init, &local arch dispatch, #regalloc, secret var (an array zeroised on every return path — and what it does NOT cover: callee frames, vector registers, the heap, timing; the sigil 3.13.8 / CYRIUS-2026-0023 and the ECDHE key zeroisation gap lessons, 6.6.15)"
category = "cyrius"
---
A grab-bag of syntactic additions consolidated for agent reference.

STRUCT INITIALIZER SYNTAX:
    var p = Point { x: 10, y: 20 };
Emits struct-shaped stores in declaration order. Pairs with
#derive(accessors) for accessor-fn generation.

NATIVE MULTI-RETURN:
    fn divmod(a, b) { return (a / b, a % b); }   # the parens are required
    var q, r = divmod(10, 3);
Two values come back via rax (ret1) + rdx (ret2). (This block showed
`return a / b, a % b;` — a parse error; see ret2_rethi.) Since 6.7.7 a
tuple captures them whole, `var t: (i64, i64) = divmod(10, 3);` —
tuples_v677. Since 6.7.11 a fn declared `: (i64, i64)` must `return (a, b);`
(or a tuple, or a forwarded call of the same arity), an undeclared fn whose
returns are all `(a, b)` is counted, and a 9-16 B struct's two words bind
as two names — ret2_rethi § SINCE 6.7.11.

&LOCAL ARCH DISPATCH:
`&local_var` — takes the address of a local variable.
Compiler dispatches per-arch in the emit: x86 uses
`lea rax, [rbp - N]`, aarch64 uses `sub x0, x29, #N`.

#regalloc DIRECTIVE:
    #regalloc 1
    fn hot_loop() { ... }
Opt-in per-fn flag. Routes the innermost loop's accumulator
local to rbx via a post-emit peephole with width-aware safety
abort (reverts to stack if it spots a width violation).
~12% speedup on nested accumulators.

SECRET VAR:
    secret var key[32];
    load_key(&key);
    if (ct_eq_bytes(&key, candidate, 32) == 1) { ... }
Declares a fn-local ARRAY that is ZEROISED on every return path —
the wipe is a synthetic `defer` the epilogue walker runs, so every
rule of `defer_statement` applies (owned by its closure, never
tail-called or inlined, refused at top level and, since 6.6.15, in a
`#naked` fn). Scalar form (`secret var x = 0;`) is rejected —
`secret var name[N]` only. The keyword does NOT make code
constant-time: comparisons go through `lib/ct.cyr` (below). Used
throughout sigil and by agnosys certpin.

WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER — the sigil 3.13.8 and native-TLS lessons
folded at cyrius 6.6.15. `secret var` wipes ITS array; key material
also lives in:
  - a CALLEE's dead frame. The HMAC_DRBG context sigil's ECDSA signer
    used sat ~43 KB down holding the nonce k (HMAC never wiped its
    context, and its final state IS the MAC); k plus one signature is
    the private key (CYRIUS-2026-0023 part B). Wipe a context in the code that
    owns it; to scrub a deep call tree, burn the stack from a PLAIN
    wrapper AFTER the secret callee has returned, so the burn also
    covers that callee's epilogue (sigil burns 128 / 160 KB).
  - vector registers. SHA-NI / AES-NI leave state in xmm0..; an asm
    block computing on secrets clears the registers it used (a later
    `secret var` epilogue used to spill them to dead stack — the secret-var epilogue spill gap).
  - the heap. Native TLS kept every connection's ephemeral key and
    shared secret in ctx buffers until 6.6.15 (the ECDHE key zeroisation gap): zero each at
    its last use AND on every exit of the driver.
  - TIMING. sigil's ECDSA signing (3.5.9 through 3.13.7) ran its
    ladder over field ops that branched on their operands — final
    subtracts, `if` carry fix-ups, early-exit compares, aarch64's
    software high-half multiply — a fixed-vs-random Welch t-test
    gave |t| = 6.3 at 400 samples, the Minerva / TPM-Fail lattice
    class (CYRIUS-2026-0023). Constant time is a property of EVERY primitive
    under the secret, not of the top-level algorithm: two earlier
    fixes had removed only the coarse leaks and audits had filed the
    arithmetic as INFO. sigil 3.13.8's engine (src/ec_ct.cyr:
    branch-free Montgomery arithmetic, complete formulas, fixed
    window with a full-table masked lookup) was verified two ways:
    |t| = 0.7 at 1200, and EVERY conditional branch of the compiled
    engine, x86_64 and aarch64, accounted for by disassembly as a
    loop bound, public exponent bit, public size or public verdict.
    Not claimed: operand-value timing via power / frequency
    (Hertzbleed class).
The compiler-side holes in the wipe itself: field_notes/compiler/
gotchas.cyml `secret_var_zeroisation_skipped_four_ways_...`.

Stdlib-side helpers live in `lib/ct.cyr`:
  - `ct_select(cond, a, b)`             — branchless select (v3.x)
  - `ct_eq_bytes(a, b, n)`              — branchless byte-array
                                          equality, equal-length
                                          (v5.9.18; canonical XOR-
                                          accumulate)
  - `ct_eq_bytes_lens(a, alen, b, blen)` — dual-length variant;
                                           returns 0 on length
                                           mismatch then runs the
                                           CT compare on the equal-
                                           length common prefix
                                           (v5.9.20; additive
                                           stdlib helper).
The single-length form is the workhorse — use it for fixed-
length compares where lengths are equal-by-construction (MAC
tags, signature components, SHA-256 digests). The dual-length
variant exists for cstring or variable-length compares where
the call site genuinely needs a length-mismatch fast path.

**Sigil's `ct_eq` family**: sigil ships its own
`fn ct_eq(a, a_len, b, b_len)` and `fn ct_eq_32(a, b)` in
`sigil/src/ct.cyr` (vendored into cyrius `lib/sigil.cyr`).
Those are sigil-public symbols downstream consumers (kavach,
hoosh, libro, majra, plus 11+ vendored-copy holders) call
directly. They remain in sigil; the cyrius-side
`ct_eq_bytes_lens` is an additive helper, NOT a replacement.
A consolidation slot would require coordinated downstream
migration + a sigil major-version bump, since removing
`fn ct_eq` from sigil's bundle is a breaking public-surface
change across the ecosystem.

**agnosys 1.1.2+ certpin** uses `ct_eq_bytes` (the
single-length form). The cstring variant (`certpin_ct_streq`)
remains hand-rolled in agnosys pending a separate
cstring-helper slot. Compiler builtins in this neighborhood:
`mulh64(a, b)` for constant-time multiplication.

EXPRESSION-POSITION COMPARISONS:
    var flag = (x == y);
Comparisons return 0/1 and can live anywhere an integer
expression does. PARSER LIMITATION: comparisons inside function
CALL ARGUMENTS are rejected (parser ambiguity with generic/
templated forms). Work around by assigning first:
    var eq = (x == y); foo(eq);

OVERFLOW-EXPLICIT OPERATORS (lib/overflow.cyr):
    +%  -%  *%      # wrapping (alias for bare + - *; 2's complement wrap)
    +|  -|  *|      # saturating (clamp to i64::MIN / i64::MAX)
    +?  -?  *?      # checked (panic on overflow with exit code 57)

Wrapping ops are bytes-identical to the bare operator — they
document intent, no runtime cost. Saturating and checked
variants compile to calls into lib/overflow.cyr helpers, and
their RESULT is an integer whatever the right operand (6.7.16's
D104, FB-4: with `x: f64`, `(n +| x) * 3` is an integer multiply —
it took the f64 arm; struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711).

ATTRIBUTES — #must_use, @unsafe, #deprecated:
    #must_use
    fn checked_op(x): i64 { return x * 2; }

    @unsafe { store64(some_raw_ptr, 0); }

    #deprecated("use sha256_init() — sha1 is collision-broken")
    fn sha1_init() { ... }

#must_use warns at expression-statement discard. @unsafe is
advisory only. #deprecated("why, and what to use instead")
warns ONCE per call, on that call's line, however the fn is
reached: a plain call, `return f(x);`, a call written BEFORE
the definition, `&f`, `o.m()` for a top-level `fn T_m`, an
overloaded operator, a struct-valued receive (`var q: P3 =
f()`), a generic instance (`g<i32>(..)`), or a call inside an
`#inline` / generic body (once, not once per expansion) — all
since 6.6.16; before, only three call paths warned. The
message is required: a bare `#deprecated` is refused by name.
It changes no code. Inside an `impl` body a `#deprecated` is
refused (deprecate the method with a top-level `fn T_m`).

#pure / #io / #alloc: a `#pure` fn that calls an `#io` or
`#alloc` fn warns ("#pure fn calls #io fn 'wr'"). Since 6.7.14
(FE-21) the check holds WHEREVER THE CALLEE IS DEFINED — above or
below the caller, a tail call `return wr();`, a method of an
`impl` further down (named `'A.m'`), a fn after the first
top-level statement or in a top-level block: pass 1 records the
two attributes as it records `#deprecated`. Through 6.7.13 a
callee defined below its caller warned nothing. A warning only.
Since 6.7.15 (FE-5) a call through a GENERIC INSTANCE warns too:
`h<i32>(1)` of an `#io fn h<T>` warns "#pure fn calls #io fn
'h<i32>'" (a non-i64 inferred instance, a tail call and a method
`p.m<i32>(..)` — 'P.m<i32>' — likewise). An instance is minted
with the pending attributes parked, so it never got the base's
`#io` / `#alloc` bits; through 6.7.14 only the all-i64 spelling,
which binds the base, warned. CHANGELOG [6.7.15] FE-5.

[[entries]]
name = "slice_type_v58x"
description = "slice<T> / [T] type — bounds-aware indexing s[i], dot-syntax field access s.ptr / s.len"
category = "cyrius"
---
Slices are a first-class type with the syntactic ergonomics of
any other primitive — not just a 16-byte fat-pointer convention.

== TYPE ANNOTATIONS ==

Two equivalent forms, on a fn-local `var` (and a top-level `var`
after the first statement). ⟳ 6.7.8: this said "everywhere a type
annotation goes (var, fn param, field)" — a parameter and a field
never parsed; since 6.7.8 every other position is refused BY NAME
(see SINCE 6.7.8 below):

    var s: [u8] = 0;        # bracket form
    var s: slice<u8> = 0;   # ident form

Element type may be any scalar (u8/i8/u16/i16/u32/i32/u64/i64/
u128) or pointer (*T). Element width is captured at PARSE_VAR
time and drives sized loads in indexing.

== ALLOCATION + LAYOUT ==

A slice fn-local reserves TWO 8-byte slots:
  - canonical slot (lower address) — holds the .ptr field
  - high-half slot (higher address) — holds the .len field

Layout puts the canonical slot at the LOWER stack address so
`&slice + 8` correctly addresses the .len field. `var s: [T] = 0;`
zero-inits BOTH halves via address-based memory stores.

== HEAP-FALLOC INTEROP ==

A slice value is byte-identical to:
  - Str (lib/str.cyr) — heap-allocated 16 bytes, data@0, len@8
  - vec (lib/vec.cyr) first 16 bytes — same shape

Consequence: a Str pointer or vec value can be passed directly to
slice_ptr / slice_len / slice_eq / slice_is_empty / slice_is_null
without conversion. The only difference is lifetime — Str/vec
live in heap; fn-local slices are frame-scoped.

== SUBSCRIPT ==

    var d[5];  store8(&d, 65);  store8(&d+1, 66);  ...
    var s: [u8] = 0;
    slice_set(&s, &d, 5);
    var b = s[0];      # = 65 — bounds-checked u8 load
    var c = s[2];      # = 67
    # OOB / negative idx → stderr "slice bounds violation\n", exit 134

`s[i]` lowers at parse time to `_slice_idx_get_W(&s, i)` where
W is the element byte-width (1/2/4/8/16). Width-16 returns a
POINTER to the u128 slot (cyrius's ABI can't return u128 by value).
Since 6.7.8 a SIGNED element (`[i8]` / `[i16]` / `[i32]`) is read
through `_slice_idx_addr(&s, i, w)` + a sign-extending load instead
(see SINCE 6.7.8).

For perf hot paths inside already-bounds-checked loops, the
unchecked variants `slice_unchecked_get_W(&s, i)` skip the
bounds check.

== DOT-SYNTAX FIELD ACCESS ==

    var s: [u8] = 0;
    slice_set(&s, &d, 5);
    var p = s.ptr;            # = &d (lower-half slot)
    var n = s.len;            # = 5  (higher-half slot via &s + 8)
    s.len = 3;                # truncate the view
    s.ptr = &d + 1;           # rewrite view start

Only `.ptr` and `.len` are accepted; other field names hard-error
with "slice has only .ptr and .len fields".

== POINTER-TO-STRUCT DOT SYNTAX ==

    var s: Str = str_from("hello");
    var n = s.len;           # = 5  (pointer-deref first)
    var p = s.data;          # = pointer to "hello" bytes

PARSE_FIELD_LOAD/STORE auto-detects pointer-vs-inline by checking
the slot above the named slot for a sentinel name `-1`:
  - sentinel present → inline mode (lea, slot IS the struct)
  - sentinel absent → pointer mode (mov, slot HOLDS a pointer)

`fn foo(s: Str) { return s.len; }` also works.

== SLICE-TYPED WRAPPER HELPERS ==

    fn sys_read_slice(fd, &s)     — sys_read into s.data, up to s.len bytes
    fn slice_copy_bytes(&dst, &src) — memcpy with min(dst.len, src.len) cap
    fn slice_eq_bytes(&a, &b)     — content equality; length-mismatch is unequal

Additive — the canonical (ptr, len) primitives in lib/string.cyr
/ lib/syscalls.cyr remain the building blocks.

== SINCE 6.7.8 (C2; user decisions 2026-10-10) ==

STORES. `s[i] = v`, `s[i] OP= v` (all eleven) and a for step store
the element at T's width (1/2/4/8 B; an f64 into an f32 element
rounds; a bool element takes a boolean; a pointer / enum / cstring
element is a word). The address comes from lib/slice.cyr's private
`_slice_idx_addr(&s, i, sizeof(T))`, checked as the getters are —
out of range or negative is "slice bounds violation", exit 134, in
EVERY build. Refused by name: a struct / u128 element store, `OP=`
on an f64 / f32 / bool element (the read is an untyped word: write
`s[i] = v`), and a slice subscripted inside a closure that captured
it. `store8(slice_ptr(&s) + i, v)` stays the unchecked spelling.

SIGNED READS (an approved value change). `s[i]` on an `[i8]` /
`[i16]` / `[i32]` is SIGN-extended, as an `i8[N]` element, `p[i]`
through a `*i8` and an `i8` field are: a stored -3 read 253 before
6.7.8 (the getters zero-extend), so `s[0] < 0` was false. `OP=`
loads the same way (`-7 /= 2` is -3, was 124). `[u8]` / `[u16]` /
`[u32]` still zero-extend; `s[i] & 0xFF` is the unsigned read;
`slice_unchecked_get_1/2/4` still zero-extend.

A SLICE IS A FN-LOCAL. A parameter, a return type, a struct / union
field, `*[T]`, a declaration-zone global, a slice of slices, a type
argument and a tuple element are each refused by name ("a slice
cannot be a parameter (6.7.8: slices are fn-locals) - pass &s and
read slice_ptr / slice_len, or the pointer and the length", and
siblings) — they were misleading parse errors. A top-level slice
after the first statement is a 16-byte global used through
`slice_ptr(&s)` / `slice_len(&s)` / `slice_set`. Pass `&s` to a fn.

UNTYPED PARAMETERS ARE NOT SLICES. A slot used to inherit the width
of whatever slice last sat in it, in ANY earlier fn, so an untyped
parameter `y` compiled `y[0]` as a slice read and `y.len` as a frame
read; refused by name since 6.7.8 (the slice width is trusted only
behind a self-clearing bit in the slot's depth word).

THE MODE. Under the bounds-checked mode a slice violation prints the
mode's line, `bounds: index N out of [0, LEN) at <file>:<line>`,
instead (same exit 134) — bounds_checked_mode_v678. A constant index
`s[-1]` warns in every build. CHANGELOG [6.7.8].

6.7.11 (GF-8, D10): `slice` is NOT reserved — a program's own `struct slice` / `struct slice<T>` wins in a fn-local
annotation too, as it already did for a parameter, a pointer target, a field, a zone global and a type argument.
The built-in is `[T]`. ⚠ With such a struct declared, `var s: slice<u8>` is the STRUCT, so the built-in's `.len` /
`.ptr` / `s[i]` on it are refused — spell the built-in `[u8]`.
6.7.14 (GN-9, D3 (a)): the built-in slice's WORD form is refused as a type argument as `[T]` is — `g<slice<u8>>(0)`,
`Box<slice<u8>>` — "a slice cannot be a type argument" (it fell through to the i64 base); a program's own `struct
slice<T>` (`g<slice<i64>>`) still takes the word. And the VECTOR type names are not reserved either (6.7.14, D41,
VC-16): a program's own `struct f32v4 { .. }` is that struct everywhere — typed_simd_value_type_abi.
6.7.11 (GF-15, D13): in a closure, a captured slice's `s.len` / `s.ptr` (and `slice_len(&s)` / `slice_ptr(&s)`) read
the captured COPY; any other field is refused as on a local; a store into the copy (`s.len = n`) is refused by name;
`s[i]` stays refused — declare the slice in the closure to subscript or re-point it.
6.7.16 (the user's D89, D7; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] FF-48, FF-48r, GS-11, GS-14): A GENERIC STRUCT INSTANCE IS AN ELEMENT —
`var e: [Box<i64>] = 0;`, `slice<Box<i64>>`, `[*Box<i64>]` declare, the element the instance at its size, as `[Pt]` does
(D89; it was "expected ']', got '<'"); a name in its list that is no type is refused as in any annotation. A struct
element of any width declares, but a subscript READ serves only 1-, 2-, 4-, 8- and 16-byte elements (the
`_slice_idx_get_W` getters): another width (`[Box<Pt>]` 24 B, `[W<i32>]` 12 B, `[P3]` 24 B) is "cannot subscript 'e': a
slice subscript reads a 1-, 2-, 4-, 8- or 16-byte element - this one is 24 bytes: read at slice_ptr(&s) + i * sizeof(T)"
(FF-48r; it was a false "requires include lib/slice.cyr"). A NON-generic element given a list (`[Pt<i64>]`,
`[*Pt<i64>]`, `slice<*i32<u8>>`) is refused at the name, "'Pt' is not a generic struct: it takes no type arguments" (D7).

[[entries]]
name = "relaxed_fn_ordering"
description = "fn definitions can appear after top-level statements; since 6.7.15 a forward call reads a signature naming a struct declared below the fn, and a declaration inside a body is one error; since 6.7.16 `mod` / `use` / `pub` there too, every use of a refused name stands down, and an impl above its struct types `self` (D6)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var x = 42;
    print_i64(x);      # top-level statement
    fn helper(a) {     # fn defined AFTER top-level code
        return a * 2;
    }

SEMANTICS:
    fn definitions can be freely interleaved with top-level code.
    Forward calls already worked (two-pass compilation), so this
    only affected fn definitions appearing physically after
    executed statements.

IMPLEMENTATION:
    PARSE_PROG handles `fn` tokens by:
    1. Emitting a jmp instruction to jump over the function body
    2. Compiling the function body normally (same as before)
    3. Patching the jmp to land after the function body

    Same jmp-over pattern used for if/else blocks.

WHAT MAY FOLLOW A STATEMENT, AND WHAT MAY NOT (6.7.14 — the user's D13 (a), D14 (a); CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-25):
    `fn`, `const fn`, an `#inline` / generic fn, `const`, `var` and — since 6.7.14 — `async fn` may follow top-level
    statements (`syscall(39); async fn af(): i64 { return 1; }` builds under `CYRIUS_ASYNC=1`; it was "expected
    identifier, got reserved keyword 'fn'"). A `struct`, `enum` (`enum E: stack` too), `union`, `trait`, `impl` (`impl
    Tr for T` too), `mod`, `use` or `pub` / `public` declaration after the first top-level statement is ONE error naming
    the rule, at the keyword:
        a `struct` declaration must come before the first top-level statement (line 2)
    (the keyword as spelled; the statement's file named when it is another). An `include` of a file that declares one is
    reported at the include's line: "an include of a file that declares a `struct` (lib/str.cyr:24) must come before the
    first top-level statement (line 1)". Through 6.7.13 each was "unexpected struct" (`union`: "expected ';', got union")
    plus up to three invented errors, and the include's failed inside the included file; calling a refused impl's
    method or a refused include's fn no longer adds "undefined function". Nothing that compiled changes.

INSIDE A FN BODY OR A BLOCK, AND THE REFUSED NAMES' USES (6.7.15 — CHANGELOG [6.7.15] FE-17, FE-18; diagnostics only):
    A `struct`, `enum`, `union`, `trait` or `impl` inside a fn body or a block is ONE error at its keyword, and the body
    parses on after it:
        a `struct` declaration belongs to the declaration section at top level, not inside a fn body
    ("... not inside a block" in a top-level `if` / `while` / `for` / `else` body, a `switch` case or `@unsafe`; "a fn
    body" in a closure, an impl method or a generic body — once, not once per instance). Through 6.7.14 it was
    "unexpected struct" plus two to four invented errors, often ending "return outside a fn body" (the resync took the
    fn's own `}`). And a USE of what a refused declaration names — after the first statement, in a refused include, or
    inside a body — adds no error of its own: a literal `Q { 5 }` / `G<i64> { 5 }`, `E.B`, an enum member, a `: Q` /
    `*Q` annotation and that variable's `.y` / `.m()`, `sizeof(Q)`, a payload constructor `Ok2(5)`, an impl's method,
    a refused include's fn. A name declared nowhere, an unknown type beside a refused one, and an untyped `q.y` in
    another fn are still reported. Nothing that compiled changes. The rest is 6.7.16's (below): `mod` / `use` / `pub`
    in a body, the uses in a const context, a refused trait as a bound, `var q: Q;` with no initializer and a use read
    before the refused declaration.

6.7.16 — THE REST OF THE RULE ONE LEVEL DOWN, AND EVERY USE STANDS DOWN (CHANGELOG [6.7.16] FE-10 – FE-13, FF-27, FF-28,
FF-28r, FF-29; diagnostics only — refused before and after):
    `mod`, `use` and `pub` / `public` inside a fn body or a block are the same one error (FE-10): "a `mod` declaration
    belongs to the declaration section at top level, not inside a fn body" ("... a block"; "a `use` / `pub`
    declaration ...") — they were "unexpected mod" / "unexpected pub'/'public" (the token table's two-spelling name),
    and a `pub fn` there added "fn 'g' is defined inside fn 'f'". A `pub var` / `pub const` there (and a late `pub var`
    at module scope after the first statement, FE-11) still declares its name; a `pub const fn` ends at its `}`. The
    refused names' uses now stand down in a CONST CONTEXT (a `case` label, a const, an array size — a top-level const's
    report no longer prints BEFORE the declaration's error), a trait BOUND, a no-initializer `var q: Q;` (and `var q:
    Foo;` after any refused annotation), a USE PARSED BEFORE the late declaration (an earlier fn's `q: *Q`) and a
    refused inclusion's by-name `s: *Str` parameter (FE-12); a use read before a declaration refused INSIDE a body —
    an earlier fn's `q: *Q`, `E.B`, a bound, `dyn Tr`, `impl Tr for P` — adds no second error (FF-28), and a zone const
    context naming a late (refused) enum prints no "unknown name" before the enum's own error (FF-29). A refused
    struct's / enum's derive step no longer takes the user's own fn on its closing line (FF-27, FF-27r). A name declared
    nowhere is still reported. ⚠ An inner declaration in a body pass 2 never parses (a trait default no impl inherits,
    an uninstantiated bounded generic's base) that nothing uses is never refused (filed at the 6.7.16 close, unplaced).

A FORWARD CALL OF A FN WHOSE SIGNATURE NAMES A STRUCT DECLARED BELOW IT (6.7.15 — CHANGELOG [6.7.15] ST-1; a fault and
false refusals fixed, no decision needed — 6.7.14's D41 A now holds above the fn too):
    Pass 1 records a fn's ABI facts as it meets the fn; a struct declared BELOW that fn was not registered yet, so a
    call parsed before the fn's own definition read a record with no struct. Since 6.7.15 every such signature is read
    again once all structs are registered, and the call reads what the definition reads — a by-value parameter at every
    size, `*S`, a method's parameter (by `.` and by name), a generic instance (`One<B16>`), a `: S` / `: *S` / `: **S` /
    `: *Box<S>` return, the untyped `var t = mk(10);`, a struct named like a vector (`f32v4`). Through 6.7.14 those
    faulted (139; cx 0 / 1 / 254, PE 5) or were refused ("pass &t for its address", "no struct type in scope for 't'",
    "vector return type does not match the declared type"). A program whose structs precede their fns compiles
    byte-identically.
    - Such a forward call is now argument-checked: a struct of ANOTHER type is refused "cannot pass 'p' to a parameter
      of a different struct type in a call to 'g'" (it compiled and passed the wrong layout).
    - Include order can put a library's struct below another library's fns (`lib/tls_native.cyr` before `lib/str.cyr`
      puts `struct Str` below bayan's fns): their forward `: Str` results are now `Str`-typed.
    - AN `impl S { fn m(self) }` ABOVE `struct S` (6.7.16, GS-13; the user's D6): the untyped `self` is a `*S` whichever
      comes first — the end of pass 1 re-reads such a method once every struct is registered, so `self.b` reads,
      `t.m()`, `S_m(t)` and `S_m(&t)` agree (a trait impl's method, direct and through a dyn, and a generic method too).
      Through 6.7.15 `self` stayed untyped ("no struct type in scope for 'self'") and the direct call `S_m(t)` passed t's
      VALUE instead of `&t` and faulted (139; cxvm "guest address out of range"). Arithmetic on that `self` (`self + 8`,
      `self[i]`) is refused as with the struct first (D6, "Refuse it, as with the struct declared first" — `load64(self +
      8)` compiled in this order).

[[entries]]
name = "tagged_unions_v58x"
description = "enum sum types — `enum Result<T, E> { Ok(T), Err(E) }` with multi-arg constructors + nullary tagged variants. DEFAULT form is a heap box; `enum Name: stack` (v6.5.55) is the VALUE FORM returning (tag, payload) in a register pair with ZERO allocation — what the stdlib uses since v6.6.0. Since 6.7.16 members need their `;` / `,` (D12) and a use's type arguments are held to what the enum declares (D88)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Tagged unions / sum types — first-class language feature shipped
across v5.8.21–v5.8.23 + v5.8.26. Replaces the hand-rolled
lib/tagged.cyr `tagged_new(tag, value)` pattern with
compiler-generated constructor fns.

== SYNTAX ==

The pinned canonical shape from the v5.8.21 sum-type slot:

    enum Result<T, E> {
        Ok(T),
        Err(E)
    }

All four pieces of this shape are independently optional:

    enum Maybe { Nothing; Just(v); }            # no generics, ;-sep
    enum Pair<T> { P(a, b); }                   # 1 type-param, multi-arg
    enum Tri<T, U, V> {
        Triple(a, b, c),                        # multi-arg
        Pair(x, y),                             # different arity OK
        Single(s),                              # single-arg
        Bare                                    # auto-incremented int
    }

Generic params (`<T, E>`) are syntactically accepted but not yet
bound semantically (mono-only, erasure semantics — full binding
arrives when monomorphization lands post-v5.8.x). Variant
separators are `;` OR `,` — mixed in same decl tolerated.

⚠ MEMBERS ARE SEPARATED (6.7.16 — the user's D12, "Refuse it by name";
CHANGELOG [6.7.16] FE-16). `;` or `,`, mixed, a trailing one allowed; a member
that follows another with NO separator is "expected ';' or ',' between enum
members" at it, once per enum (`{ A B C }` is one error; a second enum reports
its own), `--syntax-only` too. A LINE BREAK IS NO SEPARATOR: one member per line
needs its `,` / `;` (⚠ beyond D12's answer text, which named `{ A B }` and `{ A =
1 B = 5 }`). `enum E { A B }`, `{ A = 1 B = 5 }` and `: stack { Ok(x) Err(e) }`
compiled through 6.7.15 with the values the separated form gives — adding the
separators changes no value. `enum E { A 5 }` keeps "expected identifier, got
number 5". The same rule as struct-literal elements (6.7.15's D1 (a)) and struct /
union fields (6.7.16's D69).

TYPE ARGUMENTS BY DECLARATION (6.7.16 — the user's D88; FF-49). A USE's list
after an enum is held to what the enum declares: an enum written without `<..>`
(above or below the use) given a list is "'Color' takes no type arguments" at
the name, in every position; an enum declaring its own `<T, E>` has the list
READ — a name that is no type refused ("unknown type 'Nope' as a type
argument"), a third argument refused by D5's count ("'Result' takes 2 type
arguments, 3 given"). `Result` is two parameters (lib/result.cyr's `enum
Result<T, E>`, also when no enum declares it); `Option`, `Tagged` and `cstring`
take none. `fn g(): Result<i64, Pt>` compiles (it was a parse error, "expected
'{', got '<'"). Through 6.7.15 every reader stepped such a list unread
(`var e: Color<i64>`, `fn f(p: Result<Nope>)` compiled). ⚠ A read list also
refuses an argument the type-argument reader does not take — a pointer
(`Result<*Pt, i64>`), an enum, another handle, u8..u64 / bool — beyond D88's
text. Silent under `cyrius lint`, as D7's refusals are.

== CONSTRUCTOR CODEGEN ==

    var ok = Ok(42);
    # → alloc(8 + 8*1 = 16); store64(ptr, 0); store64(ptr+8, 42); return ptr

    var t = Triple(11, 22, 33);
    # → alloc(8 + 8*3 = 32); store64(ptr, 0); store64(ptr+8, 11);
    #   store64(ptr+16, 22); store64(ptr+24, 33); return ptr

Layout: tag at +0, payload[i] at +8 + 8*i. Frame size scales with
arity: `(arity + 1) * 16` bytes. Arity-1 produces byte-identical
output to the pre-v5.8.21 single-arg path (preserves the v5.5.2
single-arg corpus exactly).

== `: stack` — THE VALUE FORM (v6.5.55; the stdlib flipped at v6.6.0) ==

⚠ **Everything above describes the DEFAULT (boxed) form, and it is still correct
for it.** `: stack` is opt-in, and it is what `lib/result.cyr` and `lib/tagged.cyr`
now use:

    enum Result<T, E>: stack { Ok(v); Err(e); }
    enum Option: stack      { None(); Some(v); }

A **single-payload** variant of a `: stack` enum returns `(tag, payload)` in the
multi-return REGISTER PAIR (rax, rdx) — **no allocation at all**, so the boxed
codegen above is not emitted. Bind both halves:

    var t, v = Ok(42);      # t = 0 (the tag), v = 42

The measurement that forced the stdlib flip: 100 × `sock_send` grew the global
bump allocator by exactly 1600 B, unbounded — and that allocator's only reclaim
invalidates every pointer it ever returned, so a long-running server could never
call it. Now 0 B.

CONSEQUENCES that are easy to get wrong:
  * ⛔ rdx does NOT reach a parameter, so **no helper can take a pair in one
    argument.** This is why `payload()` was DELETED rather than re-implemented,
    and why `unwrap`/`result_unwrap`/`unwrap_or`/`err_code_of`/`*_print` all grew
    the tag as an argument. Tag-only predicates (`is_ok`, `is_none`, …) keep
    arity 1 because argument 1 receives rax, which IS the tag.
  * A variant with **two or more** payload fields cannot be a register pair, so it
    stays BOXED even under `: stack` — the layout above still applies to it.
  * ⛔ `return res;` on an Err path returns the payload ALONE — `Err(77)` then reads
    as tag=77 / is_err=0, an error that reads as SUCCESS. Return `(t, v)`. Mixed
    returns warn since v6.6.0.
  * Single-value consumption of a pair (`var r = f();`), `store64(&s, f())` and
    assignment of one are **hard errors** since v6.5.67 — they used to compile and
    silently drop the payload.

== EMPTY-PAREN NULLARY TAGGED VARIANTS (v5.8.23) ==

    enum Option { None(); Some(v); }
    var n = None();
    # → alloc(8); store64(ptr, 0); return ptr — TAG ONLY, no payload

Empty parens `Foo()` produces a true arity-0 nullary tagged
variant (8-byte alloc, tag at +0, no payload, no params consumed).
Pre-v5.8.23 it collapsed to arity-1 (16-byte alloc with garbage
payload) — a no-real-consumer landmine, fixed at v5.8.23.

== BARE-NAME VS PAREN'D ==

    enum Color { RED; GREEN; BLUE; }    # all bare
    var c = RED;                         # = 0 (auto-incremented int)

    enum Option { None(); Some(v); }    # all paren'd
    var n = None();                     # = 16-byte heap pointer, tag 0

In MIXED enums, bare variants stay int-typed and paren'd variants
heap-allocate — convention is paren-consistent. Bare-name
auto-tagging in mixed-enum decls (the v5.8.21 pin's
`enum Option { None; Some(v); }` prescriptive shape) was deferred;
the paren-consistent form works cleanly without it.

== STDLIB MIGRATION (v5.8.23, v5.8.26) ==

`lib/tagged.cyr` shipped 3 compiler-generated sum types
replacing 6 hand-rolled constructor fns:

    enum Option { None(); Some(v); }
    enum Result { Ok(v); Err(e); }
    enum Either { Left(v); Right(v); }

Helpers (`is_none`, `is_some`, `unwrap`, `unwrap_or`,
`result_unwrap`, `err_code_of`, `is_left`, `is_right`) reference
the lowercase variant names which fold via `enum_const_val` to
identical tag values. The primitives today: `is_tag(t, expected)`,
`option_print(t, v)` / `result_print(t, v)`. Re-aritied at v6.6.0
(value form). `payload` was deleted at 6.6.0 and `tag` at 6.6.2.
`tagged_new` is in lib/boxed.cyr.

`lib/hashmap.cyr` got a 6-site migration at v5.8.26:
`enum KeyType { KeyTypeCstr; KeyTypeStr; KeyTypeU64; }` replaces
raw 0/1/2 stored at map header offset 24.

== INTERNAL BOOKKEEPING ==

PARSE_ENUM_DEF in pass 1 writes 4 heap regions (added v5.8.22):
  - var_enum_id[8192] @ 0x204000 — per-variant parent eid
  - enum_count[8] @ 0x214000 — total enum decls (cap 1024)
  - enum_variant_count[1024] @ 0x214008 — variants per enum
  - enum_name[1024] @ 0x216008 — name offset for diagnostics

Enum cap: 1024 (bumped from 256 at v5.8.24 — stdlib alone has
299 enum decls, downstream multi-dep programs were 1-2 releases
from tripping). 8192 var-table cap is the variant-side cap;
no realistic consumer is anywhere near it.

[[entries]]
name = "exhaustive_match_v58x"
description = "exhaustive `match` coverage check — warns on missing variants + duplicate arms; opt out via `_ =>`"
category = "cyrius"
---
PARSE_MATCH coverage check shipped across v5.8.22 (coverage
diagnostic) + v5.8.25 (arm-tag dedup). **Warning**, not error,
per v5.8.x's incremental-adoption policy (same shape as
#must_use / #deprecated / #pure→#io). `cyrius lint --strict`
escalation reserved for a future slot.

== EXHAUSTIVE MATCH ==

    enum Status { PENDING; ACTIVE; DONE; }

    match s {
        PENDING => { ... }
        ACTIVE  => { ... }
        DONE    => { ... }
    }
    # → no warning (all 3 variants covered)

== NON-EXHAUSTIVE WITHOUT `_ =>` ==

    match s {
        PENDING => { ... }
        ACTIVE  => { ... }
    }
    # → warning:<file>:<line>: non-exhaustive match over enum
    #   'Status' — covers 2 of 3 variants; add `_ =>` to opt out

== `_ =>` OPT-OUT ==

    match s {
        PENDING => { ... }
        _       => { ... }
    }
    # → no warning (default arm present)

== MIXED-ENUM ARMS ==

    enum A { A1; A2; }
    enum B { B1; B2; }

    match A1 {
        A1 => { ... }
        B2 => { ... }   # arm references DIFFERENT enum
        _  => { ... }
    }
    # → warning:<file>:<line>: match arms span multiple enums
    #   (exhaustive-coverage check skipped)

== DUPLICATE ARM (v5.8.25) ==

    match s {
        PENDING => { ... }
        PENDING => { ... }
    }
    # → warning:<file>:<line>: duplicate match arm 'PENDING'
    # → warning:<file>:<line>: non-exhaustive match over enum
    #   'Status' — covers 1 of 3 variants; ...

Pre-v5.8.25, duplicate arms counted as 2-of-3 covered (false-
clean — adding a third arm made it 3-of-3 even though PENDING was
duplicated and the others were missing). v5.8.25's `seen_vcnt[256]`
stack-allocated dedup array fixes the math.

== CODEGEN UNCHANGED ==

The coverage check is metadata-only. Runtime cmp/jcc-skip cascade
still picks the FIRST matching arm — duplicate arms are dead code
at runtime (first wins). Tested in `tests/tcyr/match_dedup.tcyr`.

== WHAT FIRES THE CHECK ==

The check fires only when at least one arm references a variant
of an enum (PARSE_MATCH peeks the arm's first ident pre-PCMPE,
looks up `var_enum_id[arm_vi]`). Literal-int matches
(`match n { 0 => ..., 1 => ... }`) skip the check entirely —
treated as non-enum match. Non-variant idents (regular const
vars, complex expressions) also skip.

== INTEGRATION WITH PATTERN-PAYLOAD MATCHING ==

This slot ships the COVERAGE check only. Pattern destructuring
(`match opt { Some(v) => use(v) }` extracting the payload into
`v`) is NOT shipped — it would need:
  1. Auto-deref of the matchee from heap pointer to tag
  2. Variable binding from pattern slot offsets
That's a future slot. Today's idiom for tagged values:

    match load64(opt) {
        None => { ... }
        Some => { var v = load64(opt + 8); ... }
    }

Or use the `lib/tagged.cyr` helper API (`is_some`, `unwrap_or`,
etc.) which encapsulates the load64 pattern.

[[entries]]
name = "char_literals_v59x"
description = "Char literals — `'X'` syntax in lex.cyr emits NUMBER token (v5.9.37)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "syntax", "v5.9.37", "char-literal"]
---
v5.9.37 added char-literal lexing. `'X'` produces a NUMBER
token (token type 1) carrying the byte value of the character
between the single quotes.

USAGE:
    str_builder_putc(sb, '[');           # 91
    str_builder_putc(sb, '\\n');          # 10
    if (c == 'a') { ... }                # c == 97
    var space = ' ';                      # 32

ESCAPES SUPPORTED (mirror string-literal escapes):
    \\n  → 10  (LF)
    \\t  → 9   (TAB)
    \\r  → 13  (CR)
    \\\\  → 92  (backslash itself)
    \\'  → 39  (single quote)
    \\0  → 0   (NUL)

PARSING SHAPE:
    `'` opens; one byte (or 2-char escape) follows; `'` closes.
    No multi-byte / unicode in char literals — single ASCII byte.
    Mismatched closing quote → lexer error at the line.

WHY IT LANDED:
    agnosys 1.1.12 verbatim repro at v5.9.37 ship used
    `str_builder_putc(sb, '[')` and `str_builder_putc(sb, ']')`.
    Pre-v5.9.37 cyrius had no char-literal token; consumers
    encoded byte values as `91` etc. with a comment naming
    the char. Adding `'X'` is consumer-ergonomic + standard
    lexer feature.

PAIRED ADDITION (lib/str.cyr):
    `str_builder_putc(sb, ch)` — append single byte to a
    str_builder. Mirrors the existing `str_builder_add`
    (Str), `str_builder_add_cstr` (cstring), `str_builder_add_int`
    helpers; pairs with the new char literal for byte-at-a-
    time append.

[[entries]]
name = "auto_call_main_v59x"
description = "Auto-call `main()` if defined — compiler emits ECALLTO at exit epilogue (v5.9.37)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["compiler", "behavior", "v5.9.37", "auto-call-main"]
---
v5.9.37 added auto-call-main behavior. If a source defines
`fn main() { ... }` but doesn't explicitly call it via
`var ec = main(); syscall(60, ec);` (the long-standing
manual wiring), the compiler now walks the fn table for
the byte-match `"main\\0"` and emits `ECALLTO(_main_off)`
at the exit epilogue, AFTER the GLVAR-load.

ORDER MATTERS:
    Exit epilogue:
      1. `if (lv >= 0) { EVLOAD(S, lv); }`   ; load last expr
      2. **auto-call-main** (if `fn main` defined)            ← v5.9.37
      3. `EEXIT(S);`                                          ; ret syscall

    Auto-call AFTER GLVAR-load preserves the manual-wiring
    case: a source with explicit `var ec = main(); syscall(60, ec);`
    short-circuits at step 2 because syscall(60, ec) terminates
    the process before EEXIT runs. Auto-call only fires when
    the user didn't manually wire it.

DCE EXEMPTION:
    Cyrius's dead-code-elimination bitmap stubs unreferenced
    fns to `xor eax, eax; ret`. With auto-call-main, `main`
    is now reachable from the exit epilogue — but the DCE
    bitmap doesn't know that (the ECALLTO is emitted AFTER
    DCE marks the fn). Workaround: a name-byte-match
    exemption (`m`/`a`/`i`/`n`/`\\0` at start of name) sets
    `dce_is_main = 1` and bypasses the bitmap check.

CROSS-ARCH:
    Same shape in `src/main.cyr` and `src/main_aarch64.cyr`.
    aarch64 doesn't have a DCE pass so no exemption needed
    on that side.

WHY IT LANDED:
    agnosys 1.1.12 verbatim repro had `fn main() { ... }`
    without the manual `var ec = main(); syscall(60, ec);`
    wiring. Pre-v5.9.37 the compiler ran gvar inits + the
    parse-prog walk and exited with `rax=junk` — `main` was
    never invoked. Auto-call closes the consumer-friendliness
    gap. Long-standing `var ec = main(); syscall(60, ec);`
    pattern still works — it short-circuits the auto-call.

6.7.11 (GF-19, D16): EVERY target calls `fn main` from the entry epilogue — cx included, which never did before (the
cx exit was the last global's value; the fork lacked v5.9.37's epilogue block, as PE did until 6.0.54). The three
correct shapes are unchanged (an explicit `syscall(60, main())`, a `main` the auto-call runs, no `main`). A cx program
that defines `main` AND calls it at top level without an explicit exit now runs it twice, as on every other target.
Turning it on exposed seven .tcyr that had never run on cxvm (their asserts lived in `main`).

[[entries]]
name = "cx_phase2c_v59x"
description = "cyrius-x bytecode (cx) backend Phase 2c parity — sub-byte fields, struct-byval return, 7+-arg fns (v5.9.40)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cx-backend", "bytecode", "v5.9.40", "stdlib", "cxvm"]
---
v5.9.40 closed the three Phase 2c sites that v5.9.26 +
v5.9.27 narrowed but didn't fix in the cyrius-x (cx)
bytecode backend. End-to-end: a cyrius source exercising
struct-by-value return, sub-8-byte struct field loads, and
7+-arg fn calls now round-trips cleanly through cycc_cx →
.cyx bytecode → cxvm interpreter.

THREE COORDINATED CHANGES:

(1) Sub-byte struct field load — new per-backend
    `EFIELD_LOAD_W(S, width)` helper. cx version emits new
    cxvm `load16` (0x44) / `load32` (0x45) opcodes; aarch64
    version emits `ldrb` / `ldrh` / `ldr w0`; x86 emits
    `movzx rax, byte [rcx]` etc. parse_decl.cyr's
    `PARSE_FIELD_LOAD` switched from inline emit to the
    helper.

(2) Struct return by value — new per-backend
    `ESTRUCT_BYVAL_COPY(S, src_disp, stash_disp, aligned)`
    helper. cx emits an explicit byte-loop (load8 / store8 +
    add / sub + jnz) since cxvm has no `rep movsb` / post-
    incr semantics. aarch64 emits LDRB/STRB-with-post-incr.
    x86 emits `rep movsb` block.

(3) ESTORESTACKPARM — real impl in cx (was TODO stub).
    cxvm has 32 registers so 7+-arg fn calls just pop into
    r3..r9+ via the existing `ECALLPOPS` loop;
    `ESTORESTACKPARM` mirrors `ESTOREPARM`'s r-to-frame
    body shape using the call-site `disp` directly.

CXVM OPCODE EXTENSIONS:
    0x44 — load16 ra, [rb]   (zero-extend halfword)
    0x45 — load32 ra, [rb]   (zero-extend word)
    0x46 — store16 [ra], rb  (low halfword)
    0x47 — store32 [ra], rb  (low word)

    Composed from cyrius's native load8 / load64 + bit-shift
    primitives (the language has no native sub-8/sub-64
    multi-byte memory ops).

CX-SIDE ABI COMPLETION (required to make the chain compile):
    Pre-v5.9.40 these were stubs:
    - `EFLADDR(S, lli)` — was stub returning 0
    - `EFLADDR_X1(S, lli)` — didn't exist
    - `ESTOREREGPARM(S, pidx, disp)` — didn't exist
    Without them, cycc_cx SIGILL'd at compile-time on any
    sub-byte struct-field store or `fn f(): S` declaration.
    All three got real impls this slot mirroring the existing
    `ESTOREPARM` r-to-frame body shape.

CONSUMER NOTE:
    cx is x86-shaped (mostly) but has 32 registers vs SysV's
    8 GP-arg / 6 reg-arg convention. ECALLPOPS pops into
    r3..r9+ regardless of arg count — no separate
    "stack-arg" path is actually needed; cxvm's ESTOREPARM
    handles arbitrary arity via `r(3+pidx)`.

[[entries]]
name = "regression_module_v59x"
description = "lib/regression.cyr — testing-stdlib carved from programs/check.cyr at v5.9.42"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["stdlib", "testing", "v5.9.42", "regression"]
---
v5.9.42 carved out 22 reusable testing primitives from
`programs/check.cyr` into a new stdlib module
`lib/regression.cyr`. Downstream test runners (yantra,
agnosys/mabda smoke suites, future runners) can reach for
the same shapes via `include "lib/regression.cyr"` instead
of re-rolling fork/exec/SSH/timeout boilerplate.

PUBLIC SURFACE — 22 verbs across 5 groups:

  Display (4):
    regression_print(s)
    regression_section(name)
    regression_check(name, exit_code)
    REGRESSION_ANSI_RED / _GREEN / _RESET (3 const vars)

  Buffer scan (3):
    regression_buf_eq(p, s, n)
    regression_count_substr(buf, n, needle)
    regression_file_contains_substr(path, substr)

  Process (8):
    regression_pipe_to_bin / _capture
    regression_run_with_timeout (poll-based wall-clock)
    regression_exec_capture / _exec_run
    regression_exec_with_arg_capture / _both
    regression_exec_in_dir3 / _env

  Network (1):
    regression_network_probe(addr_ipv4, port, timeout_ms)
        non-blocking connect + poll(POLLOUT) + SO_ERROR
        readback. Replaces bash `/dev/tcp` trick.

  SSH cluster (6):
    regression_ssh_target / _ssh_skip_check / _scp_to /
    _ssh_remote_exit / _ssh_remote_exec_capture /
    _codesign_remote

CONVENTIONS:
- Every fork+exec verb takes explicit `envp` arg (no
  implicit env). Caller passes their own; pass `0`-
  terminated 1-elem array for empty.
- Exit-code convention: 0..127 = normal exit; 128+sig
  on signal; -1 on fork/exec/syscall failure; -2 for
  timeout (`_run_with_timeout` only).
- `regression_pipe_to_bin*` use 1 MB src cap. Cyrius
  self-host's `_self_host_pipe` uses 2 MB + raw status —
  not interchangeable. Compile-shaped helpers stay in
  `programs/check.cyr` for that reason.

WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVIDE:
- Test framework with assert macros / fixture discovery /
  parallel runners — that's `lib/test.cyr` material;
  decision (memory pin 2026-04-30): keep narrow + earn
  on consumer ask.
- cyrius-specific gate dispatchers (`_tcyr_relay_gate`,
  `_expected_output_gate`) — depend on audit-internal
  accounting, stay in `programs/check.cyr`.

CONSUMER NOTE — sandhi:
sandhi's `_sandhi_conn_connect_nb` shares the exact
non-blocking-connect + poll + SO_ERROR mechanics. If a
future sandhi minor (1.2.0+) wants to factor down to a
shared primitive, the natural target is a `lib/net.cyr`
extension `net_connect_nb(fd, addr, port, timeout_ms)`
that both modules compose-use. Default per sandhi's
1.2.0 roadmap entry: leave parallel impls intentional
unless profiling surfaces a hot-path reason.


[[entries]]
name = "f64v_simd_primitives"
description = "Flat-array SIMD packed-double builtins. f64v_add/sub/mul/div/sqrt/abs/fmadd (v1.9.0; cross-arch close v5.10.16) + f64v_dot/scale/axpy (v5.10.17 — keystone for hisab gap-close). x86 SSE2 + aarch64 NEON .2D + cx per-lane scalar loops (v6.4.32)."
category = "cyrius"
---
SHAPE:
    f64v_add(dst, a, b, n);    # dst[i] = a[i] + b[i]
    f64v_sub(dst, a, b, n);    # dst[i] = a[i] - b[i]
    f64v_mul(dst, a, b, n);    # dst[i] = a[i] * b[i]
    f64v_div(dst, a, b, n);    # dst[i] = a[i] / b[i]
    f64v_sqrt(dst, src, n);    # dst[i] = sqrt(src[i])
    f64v_abs(dst, src, n);     # dst[i] = |src[i]|
    f64v_fmadd(dst, a, b, c, n); # dst[i] = a[i]*b[i] + c[i]
    var s = f64v_dot(a, b, n); # f64 = sum(a[i]*b[i])
    f64v_scale(dst, a, s, n);  # dst[i] = a[i] * s (scalar)
    f64v_axpy(y, x, alpha, n); # y[i] += alpha * x[i]

All take RAW POINTERS (i64 addresses) and i64 element
counts (in DOUBLES, not bytes). f64 args / returns are
i64 bit-pattern representations — use f64_from(x) /
f64_to(x) to convert int <-> f64.

PAIR-STEP:
    Loop processes 2 doubles per iteration (SSE2 packed-
    double has 2 lanes; aarch64 NEON .2D similarly).
    For odd n the last iteration touches one element
    past the array (16-byte load/store starting at
    index n-1). Caller contract: over-allocate by one
    slot or pass even n. Pre-existing footgun preserved
    cross-arch for parity.

CROSS-ARCH ENCODINGS:
    x86 SSE2: addpd / subpd / mulpd / divpd / sqrtpd /
    andpd (sign-mask abs) / mulpd+addpd (fmadd) / haddpd
    (dot reduce) / unpcklpd (broadcast).

    aarch64 NEON .2D: fadd / fsub / fmul / fdiv / fsqrt
    / fabs / fmla (fmadd, dot accumulate, axpy) / faddp
    (dot reduce) / dup (broadcast). FMLA gives axpy a
    fused single-instruction y += x*alpha — measurable
    win over the x86 mulpd+addpd two-instruction shape.

    cx: per-lane scalar loops (v6.4.32 — EMIT_F64V_LOOP/
    UNARY/FMADD/DOT/SCALE/AXPY, with the fsqrt opcode 0x68).
    The cx f64 pipeline is no longer no-op/TBD.

CONSUMER COMPOSITION (hisab Vec3/Vec4/Mat4):
- |v|^2: f64v_dot(v, v, 3 or 4)
- normalize(v): f64v_dot then 1/sqrt then f64v_scale
- lerp(a, b, t): copy a → dst; f64v_axpy(dst, b-a, t, n)
  (or compose with f64v_sub + f64v_scale)
- mat4_mul row · col: f64v_dot per output cell
- ray_sphere intersect: dot products dominate the cost

PARSER DISPATCH:
- Tokens: 89-91 (add/sub/mul), 93 (div), 94 (sqrt), 95
  (abs), 96 (fmadd), 128 (dot), 129 (scale), 130 (axpy).
- 89-91 inlined in PARSE_TERM; 93-96 + 128-130 dispatch
  to PARSE_SIMD_EXT in src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr.
- Statement-level dispatch in parse.cyr extended at
  v5.10.17 to cover typ 128-130 (pre-fix bare
  `f64v_dot(...);` statements gave "unexpected unknown").

LATENT BUG HISTORY:
- Pre-v5.10.16 the existing primitives 89-96 were
  aarch64-stubbed (no-op) AND had an x86 codegen bug
  under auto-regalloc (40+ byte frame-disp mismatch
  vs EFLSTORE). Both surfaced via v5.10.16 premise
  check. See gotchas.cyml f64v_simd_frame_disp_under_
  regalloc.
- programs/simd_expand_test.cyr was the only consumer
  for ~3.5 years and was never wired into check.sh —
  that is why both bugs hid. v5.10.16 added
  tests/tcyr/simd.tcyr as the regression floor.

[[entries]]
name = "type_system_real_phase_arc"
description = "Real type system: annotations + call-site checks + registry-based overload dispatch (v5.10.1 - v5.10.26 5-phase arc)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX (CALL-SITE CHECKING):
    fn add(a: i64, b: i64): i64 { return a + b; }

    var x: i64 = 5;
    var y: i64 = 7;
    var r = add(x, y);     # types match → pass
    var s = add(x, "hi");  # type mismatch at parse-time → error

WHAT v5.10.x ADDED:
    - Type-annotation registry per fn: param_types[fi][pi]
      stored at parse-time. Call-site argument expressions
      get their result-type tagged; mismatch raises a parse
      error before codegen.
    - Overload dispatch: fns with the same base name but
      different param-type fingerprints register independently.
      Call sites pick the matching variant based on the actual
      argument types observed.
    - Type-rewriting for sum types / structs (consumed by
      derive). The compiler can now answer "what is the type
      of this expression" at every point in parse, not just
      "what is the slot width".

ARC PHASES:
    Phase 1 (v5.10.1)  — annotations parsed + stored in registry.
    Phase 2 (v5.10.13) — call-site arg-type checking.
    Phase 3 (v5.10.18) — overload dispatch (same-name disambig).
    Phase 4 (v5.10.22) — return-type recording + cross-call flow.
    Phase 5 (v5.10.26) — sum-type rewriting + derive-friendly.

WHY:
    Pre-v5.10.x, all fn args were i64-shaped at the codegen
    layer; type annotations existed only as documentation.
    Real typed-simd ABI (f64v2/f64v4 value-form params) couldn't
    work without compile-time knowledge of param types — needed
    to pick the right register class (GPR vs XMM/V) per param.
    The type system arc was the unblock for the typed-simd
    ABI arc (v5.10.28 - .39).

CONSUMERS:
    - Typed-simd value-form fns (lib/simd.cyr, value-form half).
    - Sum-type derive (agnosys serialize/deserialize).
    - Pointer-vs-value overload dispatch (v5.10.39).

[[entries]]
name = "typed_simd_value_type_abi"
description = "f64v2 and f64v4 as primitive value types — XMM/V0 register-pair param + return ABI across all backends; Win64 passes a value-form parameter by address with a copy-in, and since 6.7.14 takes exactly the SysV sources (a local, a call, a wrap, a capture, a `*p`); since 6.7.15 a vector call is the vector at every receive (a field-chain method, a wrap) and refused into anything that is not its vector (a non-integer annotation, a global, a top-level declaration); since 6.7.16 it is never a word — a method on any receiver is the vector, and a vector call is no operand, no scalar return, no word argument and no destructure source (D24 – D26, D100, D101)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    fn add(a: f64v2, b: f64v2): f64v2 {
        return f64v2_add(a, b);
    }

    var x: f64v2 = f64v2_make(0x4000000000000000, 0x4008000000000000);
    var y: f64v2 = f64v2_make(0x4010000000000000, 0x4014000000000000);
    var r: f64v2 = add(x, y);   # passes via XMM0/XMM1, returns via XMM0

ABI BY BACKEND:
    x86 SysV (Linux/macOS/BSD):
        f64v2 → 1 XMM register (XMM0..XMM7 for params, XMM0 for return).
        f64v4 → 2 XMM register pair (XMM0+XMM1, XMM2+XMM3, …; return
            in XMM0+XMM1).

    aarch64 AAPCS (Linux/macOS):
        f64v2 → 1 V register (V0..V7 for params, V0 for return).
        f64v4 → 2 V register pair (V0+V1, …; return in V0+V1).

    cx bytecode:
        f64v2 → register pair (r0+r1, r2+r3, …).
        f64v4 → 4 registers (r0..r3, r4..r7, …; return in r0..r3).

    Win64 PE (value-form params and returns since v6.4.31):
        A value-form parameter is passed BY ADDRESS, and the callee
        copies it in (16 bytes; 32 for f64v4 / f32v8 — 6.7.13 VC-2);
        a vector return goes through the hidden result pointer
        (retptr). ⟳ This block said "Value-form params NOT SUPPORTED
        on PE (deliberate gap)" — wrong since v6.4.31. The by-address
        ABI let Windows accept sources the other targets refuse; since
        6.7.14 (VC-2, VC-6) Win64 takes EXACTLY the SysV sources —
        a local of the type, a call declared to return it, either
        wrapped in parentheses, a captured vector, a `*p` — and
        refuses the rest with the other targets' words (`&w`, a struct
        value, another vector class, an expression led by a name).
        `f64v2_add(&a, &b)` still means `f64v2_add_ptr` there, as on
        every target. See 6.7.14 below.

CYRIUS-INTERNAL SPLIT-COUNTER (SysV/AAPCS):
    A SIMD ordinal counts XMM0..XMM7 (or V0..V7) consumption
    independently of the int ordinal (RDI/RSI/RDX/RCX/R8/R9
    or X0..X7). A fn signature like `fn f(a: i64, b: f64v2,
    c: i64, d: f64v2)` places a→RDI, b→XMM0, c→RSI, d→XMM1
    (NOT b→RSI/RDX, d→R8/R9 as would happen if everything
    shared one counter).

WHEN TO USE:
    - Math / numeric kernels (hisab Vec3/Vec4/Mat4, geometry).
    - Reducing call overhead in hot loops vs pointer-passing.
    - Value-form is FASTER than pointer-form when chaining
      ops (no stack write+read round-trip between calls).

WHEN NOT TO USE:
    - ⟳ This said "PE-targeted code (use pointer-form)" and "gate
      value-form definitions with `#ifdef CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS`".
      Both stale: Win64 has value-form params since v6.4.31, and the
      macro is predefined on EVERY target (native and cross) since
      v6.4.81, so the gate is a no-op. Use the pointer form (`p: *V`)
      when the callee should write the caller's vector, or a C /
      `fncallN` caller needs one integer word per argument.

ARC PHASES (v5.10.28 - .39):
    Phase 1  (v5.10.28) — f64v2 ABI shape design (XMM0 single).
    Phase 2  (v5.10.29) — x86 SysV f64v2 return path.
    Phase 3  (v5.10.30) — aarch64 NEON f64v2 return (V0).
    Phase 4  (v5.10.31) — Win64 PE retptr fallback (return only).
    Phase 5  (v5.10.32) — cx bytecode pair (r0+r1).
    Phase 6  (v5.10.33) — macho Mach-O inherits SysV; full pair return.
    Phase 7  (v5.10.36) — aarch64 single V0 NEON (was X0+X1 GPR pair).
    Phase 8  (v5.10.37) — f64v4 quad-register return.
    Phase 9  (v5.10.38) — VALUE-FORM PARAMS (the unlock).
    Phase 10 (v5.10.39) — pointer-vs-value overload dispatch.

6.7.11 — A VALUE-FORM VECTOR LOCAL TAKES A VECTOR, OR NOTHING (D32): the accepted sources are a local of its
type, a by-name call declared to return it (also parenthesised), and `fncallN(&f, ..)` / `callptr(&f, ..)`
with `f` declared to return it — received whole on every target (PE: the local as the hidden pointer).
Everything else is refused by name: a callee in a variable, `= 0` / `= 5`, `load64(..)`, `&w`, a scalar
local or call, a method call, `*p`. So `var v: f64v2 = 0;` inside a fn is refused (it zeroed one lane and
left the other stale) — declare it bare; a vector GLOBAL still takes `= 0`. A vector-returning callptr now
HAS a working spelling: `callptr(&f, ..)`.
A `p: *V` PARAMETER IS A POINTER (D33): one integer word (the C ABI's pointer — an ABI change from the
value-form arrival it had through 6.7.10), `p + 1` steps one whole vector, `rd(&b)` compiles, `rd(b)` with a
vector local passes `&b` (as a struct into `*T` does since 6.6.20), and a vector of another type (the same
size included) is refused by name; `p[1]` on it is refused as on a `*V` local. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (ARG-2,
ARG-4). (6.7.11's open items — `rd(mkv(..))` / `rd(*pv)` passing lane 0, the Win64 discarded vector result —
are closed in 6.7.12, below.)

6.7.12 — A VECTOR VALUE INTO `*V` IS AN ADDRESS (D27, fork B): a call declared to return the vector — by name,
wrapped in parentheses, `fncallN(&f, ..)` / `callptr(&f, ..)`, an inferred generic, a method's argument, a tail
position — passes a frame temporary's address (Win64: the call's hidden result pointer), so `rd(mkv(1.0, 2.0))`
works; `rd(*pv)` with `pv` a `*V` (local, parameter, capture, field, global, `: *V` call, `pv + n`) passes `pv`.
Another vector type is refused with ARG-4's words. At top level there is no frame: "'mkv' returns a vector by
value, and a vector result needs storage in a fn's frame - call it inside a fn".
A VECTOR LOCAL INTO ANOTHER POINTER KIND IS REFUSED (D27): `p: *Pt`, `*i64`, `*u8`, `**T` — "cannot pass 'b' to
a parameter that is not a pointer to its vector type in a call to 'rp' - pass &b for its address", every such
argument reported (the refusal does not latch the panic flag, so `rp(b) + ri(b)` names both). WAS: lane 0's
bits as the address (139 / 139 / a cx fault / PE exit 5), except Win64's `rp(b)` into `*Pt`, which passed `&b`
by accident.
ON WIN64 EVERY VECTOR-RETURNING CALL GETS ITS HIDDEN POINTER (FX-2): a discarded statement, an untyped
`var x = callptr(&f, ..)`, an argument to an untyped parameter, a method, an operator fn — at top level too
(storage: a frame temporary, or a hidden global at top level; the value, where one is read, is lane 0). WAS:
exit 5, or `s.mv(..)` overwriting `s`.
6.7.12's open list (a vector call result or `*pv` into `*Pt` / `*i64`; a vector local into a by-value struct; a
METHOD or explicit-instance result into `*V`; `rd(*&b)` / `rd(**ppb)`; a captured vector into another pointer kind;
`lanes(mkv(..))` into a value-form `x: f64v2`) closed in 6.7.13 — below. Field notes:
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml vector_addresses_typed_copies_and_zero_as_none_v6712 (the step word's vector field;
the Win64 hidden pointer at every call site). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] FX-1, FX-2.

6.7.13 — VECTOR VALUES: RECEIVES, ARGUMENTS AND DEREFERENCES (the user's D29 – D34, D40; CHANGELOG [6.7.13] VC-1 ..
VC-12, INT-1):
  - A VALUE-FORM VECTOR LOCAL takes, besides a local of its type and a call declared to return it (by name, wrapped,
    `fncallN(&f, ..)`, `callptr(&f, ..)`): an EXPLICIT INSTANCE `mkg<i64>(..)` (a call by name, VC-3); a METHOD
    `s.mv(..)` on a single name declared `: V` (D29; Win64: the local's address is the method's hidden result pointer,
    before `self`); and a DEREFERENCE `*p` of a `*V` — `var v: f64v2 = *p;` / `v = *p;` copy the 16 / 32 bytes (D34;
    `p` a local, parameter, capture, field, global, `: *V` call, `p + n`, `(*p)`, `**pp`, `*&w`). A `*q` of another
    vector type is refused "cannot copy 'q' into a variable of a different struct/vector type: 'v'" (same-size ones
    too).
  - An UNANNOTATED `var v = callptr(&f, ..)` / `fncallN(&f, ..)` (the whole initialiser, `f` declared `: V`) is a V
    local (D29) — it was one word; through a callee in a variable it stays a word. `var v = mkg<i64>(..)` is the V
    local too (VC-3). `return callptr(&f, ..)` / `return fncallN(&f, ..)` from a `: V` fn is accepted (D29; Win64
    through a temporary copied into the result pointer).
  - A VECTOR ANNOTATION IS NEVER DROPPED (VC-3): a mismatched or scalar destination of a vector call — an explicit
    instance included — is refused "vector return type does not match the declared type" / "… the assigned
    variable". Through 6.7.12 `var v: f64v2 = mkg<i64>(..)` silently became a one-word local.
  - The value-form local's refusal names its sources: "a value-form vector local is initialised only from a local of
    its type, a call declared to return it (by name, a method, or fncallN / callptr with the callee written `&f`) or
    a dereference of a pointer to its type (`*p`) - got `X`"; the out-pointer tail only for a callee held in a
    variable; a call to a MISSING fn is the undefined-function diagnostic's (`undefined function 'f64v2_splat'` — add
    `include "lib/simd.cyr"`) (VC-12).
  - A VALUE-FORM PARAMETER TAKES A VECTOR CALL RESULT (D30, VC-6): `lanes(mkv(4, 2))`, `f64v2_add(a, mkv(3, 4))`, a
    method, an explicit instance, `callptr(&f, ..)` — SysV / AAPCS64 / cx through a frame temporary loaded after the
    integer arguments, Win64 the temporary's address. A call of a vector the local rule refuses is refused by name;
    at top level there is no frame (D40). Anything else that is no local is refused once: "a value-form vector
    argument to 'lanes' is a local of its type or a call declared to return it (..) - got `X`". On Win64 a WORD is
    refused there too — a scalar, a pointer word holding a vector's address (`lanes(pv)`) — where the by-address ABI
    ran those shapes through 6.7.12 (kept by the user's 6.7.14 D51: one answer on every target). `&w` (accepted on
    Win64 only through 6.7.13) and a captured vector (refused on every target in 6.7.13) are 6.7.14's — below. An
    inline-replayed callee given `callptr(&f, ..)` / `fncallN(&f, ..)`
    (`la(callptr(&mkv, 4, 2))` with a small `fn la(x: f64v2)`) read 2 where 42 is right on x86_64 until the
    integration fixed the frame high-water (INT-1; field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
    inline_replay_indirect_call_spill_below_the_frame_v6713).
  - A VECTOR VALUE INTO ANOTHER POINTER KIND OR A BY-VALUE STRUCT (D31, VC-7): a call result (any source) and `*pv`
    into `*S` / `*i64` / `*u8` / `**T` are refused with 6.7.12's D27 words naming the callee or `*pb`; any vector
    into a by-value struct parameter (any size) is "cannot pass 'b' to a by-value struct parameter in a call to 'bv'
    - a vector is not a struct". A METHOD result into `*V` passes a temporary's address (VC-5). A CAPTURED vector
    meets the same rules (D32, VC-8; into its own `*V` it passes the closure's copy). A STRUCT into a `*V` parameter
    is refused "cannot pass 's' to a parameter that is a pointer to a vector type …" (D33, VC-9); `&s` converts.
  - `rd(*&b)` / `rd(*(&b))` pass `&b` (VC-11; `b` a local, parameter, capture or global) while `&b` itself stays a
    byte pointer (`&b + 8` is lane 1); `rd(**ppb)` passes `*ppb` (D9 (a)).
  - 6.7.13's open list — Win64's 16-byte check; `&w`, a parenthesised argument, a struct local and an
    identifier-led expression on Win64; `lanes(*pb)`; `ri(**ppb)` / `ri(*GPV)` / `ri(*&b)`; the untyped `var v =
    s.mv();` / `var v = *p;`; `return s.mv();` / `return *p;`; a struct field into `*V`; a captured vector into a
    value-form parameter; a struct named like a vector type — is closed in 6.7.14, item by item, below. Field notes:
    field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml vector_values_receives_arguments_and_dereferences_v6713;
    field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml vector_pointers_step_vectors_and_windows_value_form_v6713.

6.7.14 — VECTOR VALUES: VALUE-FORM ARGUMENTS, RETURNS AND UNTYPED RECEIVES (the user's D31 – D41, each A; CHANGELOG
[6.7.14] VC-1 .. VC-17, VC-4r):
  - A VALUE-FORM PARAMETER takes a local of its type or a call declared to return it (6.7.13), and since 6.7.14:
      - the same inside parentheses wrapping the WHOLE argument (VC-3): `lanes((v))`, `lanes((mkv(4, 2)))` — refused
        "got `(...)`" on x86_64 / aarch64 / cx through 6.7.13; `(v) + 1` and a tuple literal keep that refusal;
      - a closure's CAPTURED vector, as the local (D31, VC-4): `|k| lanes(b)` copies the capture where the argument
        stands (Win64: the env words' address, which the callee copies in — copied first when a later argument may
        write the capture); refused on every target through 6.7.13 (6.7.14's D51 had kept it refused on Windows;
        D31's one answer on every target superseded that clause — kept by the user's 6.7.15 D70);
      - a DEREFERENCE `*p` of a pointer to it, copied through the pointer (D32, VC-5): `lanes(*pb)`, `(*pb)`, `**ppb`,
        `*gp(pb)`, `*&b` (Win64 passes the pointer itself) — refused on x86_64 / aarch64 / cx and a page fault on
        Win64 through 6.7.13.
    The class rule is the local's: `x: f64v2` takes ANY 16-byte vector (an f32v4 capture or `*pf` too — 6.4.4's
    16-byte class; kept by the user's 6.7.15 D65 (the capture) and D66 (the dereference)); `f32v4` and the 32-byte types
    take exactly their type. A `*q` of another class is refused with the local's words, of no vector (`*pi`) by name.
    The refusal names the dereference: "a value-form vector argument to 'lanes' is a local of its type, a call
    declared to return it (..) or a dereference of a pointer to its type (`*p`) - got `X`".
  - WIN64 TAKES EXACTLY THOSE SOURCES (VC-2): a vector local of another class into a 16-byte parameter ("value-form
    SIMD arg must be a 16-byte vector", "… type mismatch (callee expects f32v4)"), a struct value (a local, a by-value
    parameter, a global, a call, `*ps`) and an expression led by a name (`lanes(q + 1)`, "… - got `q`") are refused
    with the other targets' lines, word for word; through 6.7.13 each compiled on Windows only and the callee read
    whatever the address held (or the expression's value as the address).
  - `&w` INTO A VALUE-FORM PARAMETER IS REFUSED ON EVERY TARGET (D33, VC-6) — Windows compiled it through 6.7.13: `&`
    converts into a pointer parameter, never into a value. Write `lanes(w)`. `f64v2_add(&a, &b)` still means
    `f64v2_add_ptr`, and since 6.7.14 Windows' own-call receives (a declaration's initialiser, an assignment, a wrapped
    or call-temp receive, a vector `return`) route it there too (overload_dispatch_pointer_vs_value).
  - A VECTOR RETURN takes `return *p;` (D35, VC-13 — also `(*p)`, `**pp`, `*gp(p)`, `*h.pv`, `*&w`) and `return
    s.mv();` of a method declared to return it (D39, VC-14); each was refused "… got `this expression`" / "got `s`".
    On Win64 both go through a temporary copied into the result pointer. Another vector, or no vector, is refused by
    the 6.6.6 words.
  - UNTYPED RECEIVES BIND THE VECTOR: `var v = *p;` with `p: *V` (D36, VC-13) and `var v = s.mv();` with `mv` declared
    `: V` (D37, VC-14; a generic method and a `*Mv` receiver too) are V locals — each bound ONE word through 6.7.13
    (where 161 is right, `var v = *p;` read 114 on x86_64, and `var v = s.mv();` 1 on x86_64 / aarch64, 155 on cx,
    195 on Windows). Only the WHOLE initialiser counts; a `*pi` of no vector binds the word. A wrapped `var x =
    (mk2(..))` / `(s.m2())`, a top-level `var w = mkv(..)` and a chained receiver `var v = w.s.mv();` were still one
    word, silently, through 6.7.14 — 6.7.15 settles each (VC-7, VC-9, VC-4 — below).
  - A METHOD'S VECTOR INTO A SCALAR, STRUCT OR POINTER destination (`var x: i64 = s.mv();`, `x = s.mv();`, a for step)
    is refused "vector return type does not match the declared type" / "… the assigned variable" (D38, VC-14; it
    stored a word: 0 / 41; its reach to struct, `f64` and pointer destinations and a for step kept by the user's 6.7.15
    D67). `callptr(&f, ..)` / `fncallN(&f, ..)` of another vector into a vector local gets the same words (VC-8). At a
    `: f64` destination the method was stricter than the by-name call through 6.7.14 (`var x: f64 = mkv(41, 7);`
    compiled and stored a word); since 6.7.15 the by-name call is refused too (VC-6 — below).
  - A CALL TO A MISSING FN is the undefined-function diagnostic's at EVERY vector position — wrapped, a `return`, a
    value-form argument (VC-7): "undefined function 'f64v2_splat'" (add `include "lib/simd.cyr"`). A "got `..`"
    refusal there names a call that exists. (So a vector fn that returns a call to a missing fn and is never called
    builds with the undefined-function warning, by 6.7.13's rule for the unwrapped local — kept by the user's 6.7.15
    D69.) A vector local's assignment no longer warns "assigning non-pointer to
    typed pointer" beside it (VC-9).
  - EVERY `*V` DEREFERENCE INTO ANOTHER POINTER KIND OR A BY-VALUE STRUCT OF 8 B OR LESS is refused by name, however it
    is spelled — `ri(**ppb)`, `ri(*gp(pb))`, `ri(*h.pv)`, `ri(*GPV)`, `ri(*(pb + 0))`, `ri(*&b)`, wrapped, in a closure,
    a method, an `#inline` fn, at top level (VC-10, 6.7.13's D31): "cannot pass '*ppb' to a parameter that is not a
    pointer to its vector type in a call to 'ri'" — through 6.7.13 only `ri(*pb)` of a local was refused, the rest
    pushed lane 0 as the address (139 / a cx fault / PE exit 5). The quoted name was the operand's LAST name through
    6.7.14 (`ri(*gp(pb))` printed '*pb'); since 6.7.15 it is the argument as written (VC-12 — below).
  - A STRUCT FIELD INTO `*V` IS REFUSED (D34, VC-12): `rd(w.s)` into `p: *f64v2`; pass `&w` (plus an offset) if the
    bytes are meant.
  - A VECTOR TYPE'S NAME IS NOT RESERVED (D41, VC-16): a program's own `struct f32v4 { .. }` (any vector name; a union
    too — kept by the user's 6.7.15 D68) is that struct EVERYWHERE — a local, a return, a by-value parameter — and
    `lib/simd.cyr`'s functions of that name see the struct, as a program's own `struct slice` shadows the built-in
    slice (slice_type_v58x). Through 6.7.13 it was the vector as a parameter type and a struct as a local; `struct
    f32v8` as a value parameter compiles again, as through 6.7.12. Since 6.7.15 (ST-1) a forward call above a fn that
    takes or returns such a struct declared below it reads the struct too (relaxed_fn_ordering).
  - A closure's frame covers the temporaries an `#inline` call's arguments take (VC-4r): `|k| li(lanes(b), k)` read 40
    for 42 on x86_64 (139 on aarch64), and integer-only nested inline calls in a closure, `li(k + 1, li(k, 2))`, read 26
    for 57 on x86_64 / PE — older than the lane. The plain fn's half — a frame temporary taken while an `#inline` fn's
    arguments are evaluated lay past the frame, `id(a) + id(a + 1)` with a u128 read 84 for 83 on x86_64 / aarch64 /
    PE — was fixed in 6.7.15 (VC-1; inline_directive_v6563).

6.7.15 — A VECTOR CALL IS THE VECTOR AT EVERY RECEIVE, AND NOTHING ELSE TAKES IT (the user's D21, D26 – D29, each A;
D67 kept; CHANGELOG [6.7.15] VC-4 .. VC-13, VC-6r). The rule: an annotation or destination that is not the callee's
vector is a type error (v6.5.61), by name, by method, through `fncallN(&f, ..)` / `callptr(&f, ..)` and wrapped alike.
  - FIELD-CHAIN METHODS (VC-4): a method declared `: V` is the vector whatever its receiver's spelling — a single name,
    a field chain `w.s.mv()`, `pw.s.mv()` through a pointer, `h.w.s.mv()`, `n.ps.mv()` through a `*S` field, a generic
    method, an explicit instance — at the untyped and annotated declarations, the assignment, a value-form or `*V`
    argument (a temporary's address) and the return. Into a non-vector destination it is refused. Through 6.7.14 the
    untyped `var v = w.s.mv();` held one word of stack garbage (x86_64 104, aarch64 144, cx 98, Windows 195 where 161 is
    right), `rd(w.s.mv())` pushed lane 0 as the address, and the other forms were refused "got `w`". A head that is a
    parenthesised dereference or a call — `(*ps).mv()`, `gw(&w).s.mv()` — is the vector since 6.7.16 (RV-4 — below).
  - A BY-NAME CALL INTO AN ANNOTATION OF NO INTEGER WIDTH (VC-6, VC-6r; kept by 6.7.16's D57): `var x: f64 = mkv(..);` is refused "vector
    return type does not match the declared type", and so are `: f32`, `: bool`, `: cstring`, an enum, `: Vec`,
    `: Result`, `: Option` and an explicit instance. Through 6.7.14 `x` was silently a two-slot vector local labelled
    f64 (`load64(&x + 8)` read the second lane) — "no annotation" and "an annotation of no integer width" were one test.
  - PARENTHESES AROUND A WHOLE VECTOR CALL ARE TRANSPARENT (D28, VC-7; its reach kept by 6.7.16's D58): `var x = (mkv(..));`, `var x = (s.mv());`, a
    double wrap, `g<T>`, a chain, `(callptr(&f, ..))` are the vector's local; `var x: i64 = (mkv(..));` and
    `x = (s.mv());` are refused. Through 6.7.14 each held one word (0 / 1 on x86_64 and aarch64, 154 / 155 on cx, 195
    on Windows). A WRAPPED call of ANOTHER vector into a vector local reads "vector return type does not match .."
    (VC-5), not the receive's "got `(...)`".
  - A METHOD STARTING AN INITIALISER (D29, VC-7; its reach kept by 6.7.16's D59): `var x: i64 = s.mv() + 1;` is refused, as the by-name call starting
    one has been since 6.7.11; so is `callptr(&f, ..) + 1`. The assignment twin `x = s.mv() + 1;`, a wrapped start `var
    x: i64 = (mkv(..)) + 1;` and `var w = mkv(..) + 1;` into a vector local are refused since 6.7.16 (D24 – D26 —
    below).
  - GLOBAL DESTINATIONS (VC-8; kept by 6.7.16's D60): a vector call assigned to a global that is no vector is refused "vector return type does
    not match the assigned variable", as into a local — `G = mkv(..);`, `G = s.mv();`, `G = fncall2(&mkv, ..);`, in a fn
    or a closure (it stored one word: 0, cx / Windows 41). So is `x = fncallN(&f, ..);` / `callptr(&f, ..)` into a
    scalar local or declaration. A vector global keeps its own rule (read and written through its address). A
    top-level ASSIGNMENT is refused the same way since 6.7.16 (ZN-13 — below).
  - TOP-LEVEL DECLARATIONS (D26 / D27, VC-9; its reach kept by 6.7.16's D61): a call declared `: V` into a top-level declaration is refused in both
    zones. Untyped (`var w = mkv(..);`, `var v = s.mv();`) by name: "'mkv' returns a vector by value, and a vector
    result needs storage in a fn's frame - call it inside a fn"; annotated with anything but the vector (`: i64`,
    `: f64`, a struct, `: bool`, a pointer), "vector return type does not match the declared type"; a vector
    annotation keeps "vector global 'w' takes no initializer but 0". Each held one word (0, cx 154, Windows 195). Make
    the call in a fn.
  - NAMES (VC-12): a refused dereference argument is quoted as written — '*gp(pb)', '*&b', '*h.pv', '**ppb', '*(pb)',
    the address-passed arm's '*gq(pq)', another vector's '*g4(pb)'. Through 6.7.14 it gave `*` and the operand's last
    name ('*pb', '*b', '*ppb' — one `*` short) or the last name alone ('pq').
  - `*(&p)` OF A TYPED POINTER IS p, ITS TYPE KEPT (D21, VC-13): with `p: *f64v2`, `lanes(**(&p))`, `return **(&p);`,
    `var v: f64v2 = **(&p);` and the untyped `var v = **(&p);` are the vector (refused, or one word plus a stack word,
    through 6.7.14) — typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617.
  Field notes: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml, the 6.7.15 vectors-lane entry (one recogniser for every receive).

6.7.16 — A VECTOR VALUE IS NEVER A WORD (the user's D24 – D26, 2026-10-19, and D100, D101, 2026-10-20; 6.7.14's D27 (a) /
D38 and 6.7.13's D29; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] RV-4 – RV-7, RV-5r, RV-6r, RV-10r, RV-14, ZN-13, SS-49, SS-50, SS-50r, SS-50r2,
SS-52). Items marked ⚠ go beyond their decision's text and are filed for confirmation:
  - A METHOD DECLARED `: V` IS THE VECTOR ON EVERY RECEIVER THE METHOD ARM TAKES (RV-4; no new decision): a parenthesised
    head (`(*ps).mv()`, `(s).mv()`, `(*pw).s.mv()`, `(ps + 0).mv()`), a call's result (`gw(&w).s.mv()`, `mks(1).mv()`,
    `callptr(&gw, &w).s.mv()`, `fncall1(&gs, &s).mv()`, `gwg<W>(&w).s.mv()`) and a method's result mid-chain
    (`o.gp(&w).s.mv()`) — at the untyped and annotated declarations, the assignment, a value-form or `*V` argument and the
    return. Through 6.7.15 each held one garbage word. An integer, `: f64` or other-vector destination is refused
    (6.7.14's D38's words).
  - A VECTOR CALL THAT STARTS AN INTEGER SOURCE IS REFUSED HOWEVER IT IS SPELLED (RV-5, RV-5r): a method, `callptr(&f, ..)`
    or `fncallN(&f, ..)` starting an ASSIGNMENT (D25), and a call behind any leading parentheses (D26) — `(mkv(..)) + 1`,
    `(mkv(..) + 1)`, ⚠ `(mkv(..) + 1) * 2` (parentheses that close after an operator: beyond D26's text, covered by D100)
    — "vector return type does not match the declared type / .. the assigned variable".
  - THERE IS NO VECTOR `+` (D24, RV-6): an operator after a vector call is refused at an untyped declaration and at a
    vector local or assignment — `var w = mkv(..) + 1;`, `var w: f64v2 = mkv(..) + 1;`, `w = mkv(..) + 1;`, `var w =
    s.mv() * 2;` — "a value-form vector local is initialised only from .. - got `mkv`" (".. got `s`", ".. got `(...)`"); at
    top level "'mkv' returns a vector by value ..". Through 6.7.15 x86_64 / aarch64 dropped the operator, cx applied it to
    one lane, Windows refused it as a parse error, and the method bound one word. To compute, bind the vector and read its
    lanes. And `return mkv(..) + 1;` from a vector fn is refused by the 6.6.6 rule, "… - got `mkv`" (RV-7; it returned the
    call's vector on x86_64 / aarch64).
  - A VECTOR CALL IS NO OPERAND ANYWHERE AND NO WORD SLOT'S WHOLE VALUE (D100, "wherever it sits in the source"; SS-50,
    SS-50r, SS-50r2): beside any operator (`var x: i64 = 1 + mk2(..)`, `take(mk2(..) + 1)`, a top-level `var X = 1 +
    mk2(..);`), a compound assignment's right side (`x += mk2(..)`), under a unary operator, as a store into a field, an
    element or through a pointer to no vector (a call result's field or element too), a condition (`if (mk2(..))` took
    the branch on cx / PE only), an index, a range bound, a struct literal's field, a tuple literal's element — refused
    at the call, once, in the words of what receives it (D24 – D26's). `rd(mk2(..) + 1)` into a `*f64v2` and `var q =
    *mk2(..);` crashed (139).
  - A WHOLE VECTOR CALL IS NO SCALAR FN'S RETURN VALUE AND NO WORD ARGUMENT (D101, "Refuse it"; SS-49): `fn r(): i64 {
    return mk2(41, 7); }` is "a vector value cannot be returned as i64 - 'mk2' returns a vector" (the return type as the
    definition writes it; "i64" for none); `takei(mk2(..))` into `x: i64` (or an untyped parameter) is "a vector value
    cannot be passed to an integer parameter - 'mk2' returns a vector, passed to parameter 'n' of 'takei'"; ⚠ an `f64` /
    `f32` parameter, a builtin's or an indirect call's argument (`syscall(60, mk2(..))`: "... passed to 'syscall'") and a
    closure's expression body (counted as a return) go beyond D101's text. Through 6.7.15 each handed over one word (rax's
    leftover 0, lane 0 on cx, a Windows fault). 6.7.12's "an argument to an untyped parameter runs and discards its
    vector" no longer holds.
  - A VECTOR CALL IS NO DESTRUCTURE SOURCE (SS-52; 6.7.14's D27 (a) applied to the declaration): `var a, b = mk2(..);` — a
    method, an explicit instance, wrapped, `callptr(&mk2, ..)` / `fncall2(&mk2, ..)` — is "cannot destructure 'mk2' - it
    returns a vector, not multiple values" (it bound the integer return registers a vector return does not write: 0 and
    0); the re-assignment's words are 6.7.11's.
  - A TOP-LEVEL ASSIGNMENT of a vector call to a global that is no vector is refused "vector return type does not match
    the assigned variable", as in a fn (ZN-13; 6.7.14's D38 / D27 (a)): `G = mkv(..)`, `G = s.mv()`, wrapped,
    `fncallN` / `callptr`, `g<T>(..)`, `G = mkv(..) + 1`, as the first statement or in a top-level for step. Through
    6.7.15 it stored one word: 0 on x86_64 / aarch64, 41 on cx / Windows.
  - AN `#inline` FN'S `return`, replayed inside a vector-returning fn, is the callee's (RV-14): `store64(&w, one(40))` in an
    `f64v2` fn was refused at the callee's line. A vector fn's `return E;` whose E has an error of its own reports that
    error alone (RV-10r). `.field` / `.m()` on a vector local is "SIMD vector has no named fields" alone (GB-12; it went
    on with "undefined function 'enum.m'" and "refusing to emit binary").
  - ⚠ Still a word (filed at the 6.7.16 close, unplaced): `*pv = u` through a `*V` copies lane 0 (and `*pv = mk2(..)`
    differs by target), a vector LOCAL read as a word is lane 0, and a multi-value fn's vector element is one word.

[[entries]]
name = "f32v4_packed_single_simd"
description = "f32v4 — 128-bit packed single-precision (4× f32 lanes) value type + addps/subps/mulps, on the structured-descriptor SLTYPE encoding. Complete on all four backends: x86 SSE (Phase 1, v6.4.4), aarch64 NEON (v6.4.28), Win64 PE value-form (v6.4.31), cx per-lane bytecode (v6.4.32). SIMD Phase 5 done."
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var a: f32v4 = f32v4_make(0x40000000, 0x40000000,   # 2.0f × 4
                              0x40000000, 0x40000000);
    var b: f32v4 = f32v4_splat(0x3F800000);             # 1.0f × 4
    var r: f32v4 = f32v4_add(a, b);                     # 3.0f × 4 (addps)
    var l0     = f32v4_lane0(r);                        # extract lane (i32 bits)

    # value-form param + return (reuses the f64v2 XMM ABI):
    fn scale2(v: f32v4): f32v4 { return f32v4_add(v, v); }

f32 values are their i32 BIT PATTERNS (1.0f = 0x3F800000,
2.0f = 0x40000000). Convert with f32_from / f32_to.

THE ENCODING (Phase 0 structured descriptor):
    A SIMD vector's SLTYPE is a STRUCTURED descriptor in a
    reserved negative band <= -2048 (collision-free — struct
    sids cap at 1024, so struct sltypes are >= -1024). One
    `_vec_desc()` (src/common/util.cyr) decodes it:
        desc       = |sltype| - 2048
        is_float   = desc & 1
        lane_log2  = (desc >> 2) & 7   # 2 → 4-byte lanes
        lanes_log2 = (desc >> 5) & 7   # 2 → 4 lanes
    f32v4 = sentinel -2121 (desc 73 = float | 4-byte | 4-lane
    → 16 bytes). Legacy f64v2/f64v4 keep their FLAT -20/-21,
    so the f64 SIMD path is byte-identical; f32v8 / integer
    lanes extend by adding descriptor VALUES, not code arms.

FOOTPRINT + ABI:
    f32v4 = 16 bytes = 2 slots = 1 XMM = the SAME footprint as
    f64v2, so it reuses f64v2's value-form ABI machinery
    (ESTOREPARM_F64V2 / ELOAD_F64V2_TO_XMM). Value-form params
    are gated `#ifdef CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS` (non-PE), same
    as f64v2/f64v4 — PE consumers use the pointer-form wrappers.

PACKED OPS (x86 SSE, Phase 1):
    f32v_add / f32v_sub / f32v_mul → addps / subps / mulps —
    the f64 packed path MINUS the 66 prefix (SIB scale ×4,
    stride 4 bytes/lane). Builtin tokens 138-140 (NOT 135-137:
    135 is `await`). aarch64 NEON EMIT_F32V_LOOP shipped v6.4.28
    (fmul+fadd, NOT fmla, to match x86 rounding) and cx per-lane
    loops shipped v6.4.32 → f32v4 compute runs on x86, aarch64,
    PE, and cx.

MATMUL OPS (x86 SSE, Phase 2, v6.4.5) — flat-array builtins:
    f32v_fmadd(dst, a, b, c, n) → dst[i] = a[i]*b[i] + c[i]
        (mulps + addps; token 141). The dense-matmul inner loop.
    f32v_dot(a, b, n) → f32 bit pattern (sum a[i]*b[i]); token 142.
        mulps+addps accumulate, then TWO haddps fold the 4 lanes,
        movd eax extracts the 32-bit result. CALLER CONTRACT: n a
        multiple of 4, or over-allocate to a multiple of 4 with
        ZEROED padding — the tail iteration sums up to 3 lanes past
        n (same footgun as f64v_dot). aarch64 NEON (v6.4.28,
        EMIT_F32V_FMADD/DOT) + cx per-lane (v6.4.32) implement
        these — SIMD Phase 5 complete.

lib/simd.cyr WRAPPERS (value + pointer form):
    f32v4_make / f32v4_splat / f32v4_lane{0..3} /
    f32v4_add / f32v4_sub / f32v4_mul  — `&IDENT` auto-routes
    to the `_ptr` form (same overload dispatch as f64v2).
    f32v4_fmadd(a,b,c) / f32v4_dot(a,b) (v6.4.5) — the n=4
    single-vector forms of the matmul ops (always lane-safe;
    dot returns f32 bits, use f32_to for the value).

GUARDS (v6.4.4, adversarial-review-found):
    - `v.field` on any SIMD vector HARD-ERRORS ("SIMD vector
      has no named fields") — the -2121 sentinel must never be
      read as a struct id (it exceeds the 1024 struct cap →
      would be an out-of-bounds struct-table read). Extract
      lanes via f32v4_laneK(&v), not `.field`.
    - A value-form SIMD arg is type-checked against the param
      when the ABI engages (SIMD-returning callee): an f64v2
      arg to an f32v4 param is rejected, not silently run as
      addps over f64 bit patterns.

[[entries]]
name = "integer_vector_simd"
description = "Integer SIMD vectors (128-bit): i8v16/i16v8/i32v4/i64v2 (+ unsigned u*) + packed add/sub/mul (iv_add/sub/mul), value-form typed ops (i32v4_add(a,b)), and the u8·i8→i32 widening dot iv_dp8 (the b1.58 GEMM inner loop). SIMD Phase 3a/3b (v6.4.6/v6.4.7); implemented on x86 SSE2, aarch64 NEON (v6.4.30), and cx (v6.4.32) — Phase 5 complete."
category = "cyrius"
---
TYPES (128-bit, structured-descriptor SLTYPE like f32v4):
    i8v16 / u8v16   — 16 lanes ×  8-bit
    i16v8 / u16v8   —  8 lanes × 16-bit
    i32v4 / u32v4   —  4 lanes × 32-bit
    i64v2 / u64v2   —  2 lanes × 64-bit
  Descriptor: signed = bit 1 (i* set, u* clear), lane_log2 = bits
  2-4, lanes_log2 = bits 5-7 (util.cyr _vec_desc / GVEC_SIGNED).
  i32v4 = sentinel -2122 (vs f32v4 -2121: the float/signed bits).
  All share f32v4's 16 B / 1-XMM value-form ABI (descriptor-driven
  via _is_simd128 — return/receive/param, byte-identical for f64).

PACKED OPS — flat-array builtins (x86 SSE2):
    iv_add(dst, a, b, n, w)   dst[i] = a[i] + b[i]   (paddb/w/d/q)
    iv_sub(dst, a, b, n, w)   dst[i] = a[i] - b[i]   (psubb/w/d/q)
    iv_mul(dst, a, b, n, w)   dst[i] = a[i] * b[i]   (pmullw/pmulld)
  n = lane count; w = lane BYTE-WIDTH, a COMPILE-TIME LITERAL
  (1/2/4/8) that selects the instruction. Tokens 143-145. add/sub
  are two's-complement (signed==unsigned); mul is i16/i32 ONLY
  (w=2/4) — w=1/8 hard-errors (no 1-instruction pmulb/pmulq).
  Caller contract (same tail footgun as f32v_dot): n a multiple of
  16/w, or over-allocate the lane count. aarch64 NEON (v6.4.30,
  EMIT_IVEC_BINOP) + cx per-lane (v6.4.32) implement these too.

WIDENING DOT — iv_dp8(a, b, n) → i32 (SIMD Phase 3b, v6.4.7):
    iv_dp8(a, b, n)   sum(a[i] * b[i])   a = u8, b = i8, n%16==0
  The BitNet/b1.58 quantized-matmul inner loop (ternary weights
  {-1,0,1} are just i8). EMIT_IVEC_DP8 = pxor accumulator +
  pcmpeqw/psrlw ones-vector, 16-lane loop of movups / pmaddubsw
  (u8×i8 → i16 pairs) / pmaddwd ×ones (i16 → i32 widen) / paddd,
  reduced with two phaddd + movd eax + movsxd rax,eax (SIGN-extend
  — a negative dot must not come back zero-extended). Token 146.

lib/simd.cyr WRAPPERS:
  POINTER form (v6.4.6): i32v4_make(a,b,c,d) / i32v4_splat(x) /
    i32v4_lane{0..3}_ptr / i32v4_add_ptr / _sub_ptr / _mul_ptr ;
    i16v8/i8v16/i64v2 splat/make + _add_ptr/_sub_ptr(/_mul_ptr).
  VALUE form (v6.4.7): i32v4_add/_sub/_mul(a,b) + i32v4_lane{0..3}(v);
    i16v8_add/_sub/_mul ; i8v16_add/_sub ; i64v2_add/_sub. Integer
    128-bit VALUE params now route through XMM as a full 16 bytes:
    the value-form param-mask (class 1) was generalized from "exactly
    f64v2" to "any 16-byte vector" (_is_simd128), and the caller
    class-1 arg type-check relaxed to match (f32v4 code 3 stays
    strict). VALUE-form SIMD args must be BARE LOCAL IDENTs (not
    call-result temporaries) — the same constraint f32v4 carries.

[[entries]]
name = "f64v256_ymm_packed_double"
description = "f64v256_add/sub/mul/div — the 256-bit packed-DOUBLE kernel (4× f64 in one YMM), v6.5.24. The f64 sibling of f32v8, ten minors later: SIMD fix-list item 3, which the roadmap had gated behind an IR/regalloc substrate it never needed. Measured 15.9 -> ~7.9 ns."
category = "cyrius"
---
BUILTINS: f64v256_add / f64v256_sub / f64v256_mul / f64v256_div (dst, a, b, n)
  Tokens 154-157, CONSECUTIVE so `ptyp - 154` is the op index. Lexed in
  LEXKW_EXT (klen == 11 arm, shared 8-byte prefix "f64v256_" + a 3-byte op
  tail; all three tail bytes checked). EMIT_F64V4_LOOP, x86/float.cyr.

⚠ WHY NOT NAMED `f64v4_add`. That IS the f32v8 family's convention — the
  256-bit TYPE names the builtin — but for f64 the name was already taken:
  lib/simd.cyr defines the VALUE-FORM `fn f64v4_add(a: f64v4, b: f64v4)`.
  f64v4 shipped years before its widening; f32v8 shipped after, so only
  f32v8 ever got the plain name. Reserving `f64v4_add` as a keyword would
  make that lib definition a "reserved word as identifier" parse error and
  break every consumer of the value form. So the KERNEL is named for its
  register WIDTH — which also leaves `f64v512_*` free for AVX-512.

ENCODING: the `pd` sibling of EMIT_F32V8_LOOP. 2-byte VEX C5, second byte
  FD instead of FC (pp=01, the 66-prefix slot) so every op is packed
  DOUBLE; SIB 0xF2 = [rdx+rsi*8]; rsi += 4; vzeroupper on loop exit.
    C5 FD 10 04 F2   vmovupd ymm0, [rdx+rsi*8]
    C5 FD 58/5C/59/5E C1   vaddpd/vsubpd/vmulpd/vdivpd ymm0, ymm0, ymm1
    C5 FD 11 04 F2   vmovupd [rdx+rsi*8], ymm0
    C5 F8 77         vzeroupper
⚠ FD vs FC is ONE BIT. Emit FC and every f64 op silently becomes a
  packed-SINGLE operation on double bit patterns — wrong numbers, no
  diagnostic, no crash. tests/gates/codegen/f64v4_ymm_disasm.sh pins the
  bytes AND the mnemonics for exactly this reason.

⚠ rsi ADVANCES BY 4, so this kernel can NEVER serve an f64v2 caller — a
  4-lane stride over a 16-byte vector runs off the end. f64v2 keeps tokens
  89-91 / EMIT_F64V_LOOP. Mutation-proven that a value test cannot see a
  wrong stride: with the stride set to 2, crossos/f64v4_ymm.tcyr still
  passed 23/23 while writing 16 bytes PAST the vector, because the second
  iteration recomputes lanes 2-3 correctly.

GATING: unconditional VEX, so lib/simd.cyr picks it only when
  simd_has_avx2() == 1 and otherwise keeps the 128-bit f64v_* path. All
  EIGHT f64v4 wrappers carry the gate (4 ops × value/_ptr form).
  simd_has_avx2() returns 0 on every non-x86 target (its CPUID probe is
  #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86), so aarch64 / Mach-O-arm64 / cx take the
  128-bit path for free. The aarch64 + cx EMIT_F64V4_LOOP stubs DELEGATE
  to their 128-bit loop rather than `return 0;` — a return-0 stub makes
  the verb a silent no-op, the defect class the cx multi-return stubs
  turned out to be at v6.5.21.

cycc itself never calls these, so its own self-host emits no VEX byte and
  stays byte-identical everywhere — same as every prior SIMD phase.

[[entries]]
name = "f32v8_avx2_256bit_simd"
description = "f32v8 — 256-bit SIMD (8× f32 in one YMM). SIMD Phase 4 R1 (v6.4.8), x86 AVX2 with a runtime SSE fallback. The FIRST VEX/AVX instructions the toolchain ever emits (before this: zero VEX; decode.cyr says 'no AVX/VEX')."
category = "cyrius"
---
TYPE: f32v8 = 8 lanes × f32 = 32 bytes = 256-bit = one YMM.
  Structured-descriptor sentinel -2153 (util.cyr _vec_desc → nslots 4,
  laneb 4). Shares f64v4's 32-byte / 2×128-XMM value-form return ABI —
  the f64v4 return/receive paths (parse_fn.cyr + parse_decl.cyr) were
  generalized from exact -21 to _is_simd256 (byte-identical for f64v4).
  Construct/return works cross-arch (aarch64 too); arithmetic now runs on
  aarch64 (v6.4.29, via a 2×128-bit NEON fallback = EMIT_F32V_LOOP) and cx
  (v6.4.32) as well as x86 AVX2. NATIVE 256-bit stays x86-AVX2-only — the
  aarch64 256-bit emitters are return-0 stubs never reached at runtime;
  lib/simd.cyr routes f32v8 through native f32v4 NEON, so the verb works.

OPS (x86 AVX2, SSE2 is NOT baseline — vaddps ymm #UDs on pre-AVX2):
    f32v8_add / f32v8_sub / f32v8_mul (dst, a, b, n)  tokens 147-149
  EMIT_F32V8_LOOP = the f32v4 loop widened to 256-bit VEX: 128-bit SSE
  bytes + a 2-byte VEX prefix (C5 FC), 8 f32/iter (rsi += 8), vzeroupper
  (C5 F8 77) on exit. vaddps ymm = C5 FC 58 C1 (llvm-mc verified; the
  vvvv field is the 1's-complement-of-src1 byte-order trap). aarch64
  (v6.4.29, 2×128-bit NEON fallback) + cx (v6.4.32, aliases f32v_loop)
  implement these; the simd_f32v8 XFAIL was removed at v6.4.29. Disasm-gated
  (tests/simd_f32v8_disasm.sh) host-CPU-independently.

RUNTIME FALLBACK (the reason f32v8 is usable off AVX2 CPUs):
  simd_has_avx2() (lib/simd.cyr) — CPUID leaf 7 EBX bit 5, a byte-for-byte
  clone of sigil's _sha_ni_cpuid_probe; cached, idempotent, no CAS latch.
  The f32v8_*_ptr wrappers branch INSIDE the single call site: AVX2 →
  vaddps ymm; else f32v_*(…, 8) = 2×SSE. Each path emitted once
  program-wide (zero per-call bloat). cycc has no f32v8 → stays SSE2,
  self-hosts byte-identical everywhere.

lib/simd.cyr WRAPPERS (pointer-form first; value-form deferred):
    f32v8_make(8 args) / f32v8_splat(x) / f32v8_lane{0..7}_ptr /
    f32v8_add_ptr / _sub_ptr / _mul_ptr.
  R2 (v6.4.9): f32v8_fma (vfmadd231ps ymm = C4 E2 7D B8 D1 — FMA3, the
    FIRST 3-byte VEX; token 150) + f32v8_dot (token 151: vmulps/vaddps ymm
    accumulate, then vextractf128 xmm1,ymm2,1 + vaddps xmm **L=0** fold +
    vzeroupper + the f32v4 haddps×2/movd tail — two-haddps alone can't
    cross the 128-bit split). f32v8_fma_ptr / f32v8_dot_ptr wrappers.
    FMA needs a DIFFERENT CPUID bit than AVX2: simd_has_fma() = leaf 1 ECX
    bit 12 (clone of aes_ni's leaf-1 probe), NOT leaf 7 EBX bit 5. The
    fma fallback (mulps+addps, 2 roundings) can differ 1 ULP from fused —
    use exact-product inputs for bit-equality. bench_f32v8_gemm ~1.48×
    (256 vs 128-bit; sub-2× — fixed per-cell reduce overhead). Phase 4
    CLOSES: f32 SIMD complete on x86 (f32v4 128-bit + f32v8 256-bit).

PARAMETERS (6.7.12): `p: *f32v8` is a `*V` — `r8(e)` with a vector local
  passes `&e` (it passed lane 0 and faulted through 6.7.11; see
  typed_simd_value_type_abi). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] FX-1.
  6.7.13 (VC-2): a value-form `v: f32v8` PARAMETER is its 32 bytes — two
  XMM (SysV), two V (aarch64), four cx slots, a 32-byte copy-in on Win64.
  Through 6.7.12 it was ONE integer word (lane 0's bits; every read past
  it 0, no diagnostic, on every target — `f32v8` had no parameter class).
  Its argument is an f32v8 local or a call declared to return one; an
  f64v4 local is refused ("value-form SIMD arg type mismatch (callee
  expects f32v8)") and vice versa. ⚠ An ABI change for a C / `fncallN`
  caller: it now meets f64v4's convention (two vector registers, by
  pointer on Win64). `async fn f(v: f32v8)` is refused, as every
  value-form vector parameter is. CHANGELOG [6.7.13] VC-2.

[[entries]]
name = "overload_dispatch_pointer_vs_value"
description = "Pointer-form overload routing: `f64v2_add(&x, &y)` auto-routes to `f64v2_add_ptr`; `f64v2_add(x, y)` calls value-form base"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    # Pointer form — auto-routes to f64v2_add_ptr:
    var x: f64v2; var y: f64v2;
    var r: f64v2 = f64v2_add(&x, &y);

    # Value form — calls f64v2_add (value-form base) directly:
    var a: f64v2; var b: f64v2;
    var r: f64v2 = f64v2_add(a, b);

    # The pointer form routes the same way on PE (overload-routed):
    var r: f64v2 = f64v2_add(&x, &y);  # → f64v2_add_ptr

HOW IT WORKS:
    At PARSE_FNCALL in src/frontend/parse_fn.cyr, when the
    first argument is an `&local` (PEEKT == 27), the parser
    constructs the name `<base>_ptr` in a 128-byte stack
    scratch and runs `_FINDFN_CSTR` against it. If found,
    `fi` is rerouted to the _ptr variant; the call site
    emits a call to the pointer-form fn.

    No new heap region is used; this is name-mangling +
    registry lookup. Defensive guard: `_po_base_len < 120`
    prevents stack-scratch overflow (lesson from v5.10.39
    in-slot bug where original 16-byte scratch overflowed
    for ≥12-char base names).

WHY:
    Cyrius stdlib SIMD modules ship BOTH forms — value-form
    (faster, register-passed) and pointer-form (universal,
    PE-compatible). Without dispatch, consumers writing
    `f64v2_add(&x, &y)` would get "fn signature mismatch"
    because the f64v2_add VALUE-form expects f64v2 args, not
    pointers. Dispatch hides the dual-form impl detail.

CONSUMER POV:
    Write `f64v2_add(...)` with whatever shape your args
    happen to be in — `&local`, value, mix. The parser
    picks the right variant. ⟳ This said PE "only has
    pointer-form": Win64 has value-form vector params since
    v6.4.31 (passed by address, copied in), and
    CYRIUS_HAS_VAL_SIMD_PARAMS is predefined on every target
    since v6.4.81 (typed_simd_value_type_abi). Dispatch still
    falls back to the pointer form when a value form does
    not exist.

6.7.14 — WINDOWS' OWN-CALL RECEIVES ROUTE TOO (the user's D33;
CHANGELOG [6.7.14] VC-6): the old "on PE, only pointer form"
example was not true of Win64's own-call vector receives — a
declaration's initialiser, an assignment, a wrapped or
call-temp receive and a vector `return` emit the call
themselves (the hidden result pointer), past PARSE_FNCALL, and
through 6.7.13 they called the VALUE form with the two
addresses (right only because Win64 passes the parameter by
address). Since 6.7.14 they route `f64v2_add(&a, &b)` to
`f64v2_add_ptr` as PARSE_FNCALL does (`_pe_venter` /
`_pe_vroute`), so the value is the same on every target. A
receive of a program's own value-form fn written with
addresses and no `_ptr` sibling (`var c: f64v2 = vadd(&a,
&b);`) is refused on Windows as on x86_64, and `lanes(&w)`
into a value-form parameter with no `_ptr` sibling is
refused on every target ("… - got `&w`"): `&` converts into
a pointer parameter, never into a value. Write `lanes(w)`, or
give your value-form fn a `_ptr` sibling.

GOTCHA — duplicate parse paths:
    Argument stash for these intrinsics happens in MULTIPLE
    dispatch blocks (PARSE_SIMD_EXT for ptyp 93-130, plus
    a separate dispatch at ptyp 89-91 for f64v_add/sub/mul).
    Stash slots MUST clear locname[idx] (= -1) at each stash
    site or stale name entries from prior fn parses cause
    FINDLOCAL misroute. See gotchas.cyml
    locname_staleness_duplicate_dispatch_paths for the full
    bug class write-up.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_byval_int_pair_return_abi"
description = "9-16B int-class user struct return ABI (v5.10.45-.47 arc). x86 SysV rax+rdx pair; aarch64 AAPCS64 X0+X1 pair; PE uses same rax+rdx (cyrius-internal-ABI deviation from MS x64 strict retptr). Str's 16B handle-shape stays scalar-rax via _STR_SID carve-out."
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    struct Point { x: i64; y: i64; }
    fn make(): Point {
        var p: Point;
        p.x = 7;
        p.y = 35;
        return p;          # 9-16B → pair-return mode
    }
    fn use(): i64 {
        var got: Point = make();
        return got.x + got.y;   # 42
    }

WHAT v5.10.45-.47 ADDED:
    - 9-16B int-class struct returns transit via register-pair
      ABI (rax+rdx on x86 SysV; X0+X1 on aarch64 AAPCS64;
      cyrius-internal rax+rdx on Win64 PE — deviation from
      strict MS spec).
    - Caller-side `var got: StructName = fn();` allocates 2
      stack slots + receives the pair via the per-backend
      `EFLSTORE_STRUCT_INT_PAIR` helper.
    - Callee-side `return IDENT;` emits the pair via
      `EFLLOAD_STRUCT_INT_PAIR`.
    - Distinguished from `var s: Str = str_from(...)` (Str's
      16B handle-shape pointer-mode) via `_STR_SID(S)` lookup
      at slot entry; Str-returning fns continue to use scalar
      rax for the heap-pointer.
    - For ≤8B struct returns: scalar rax (unchanged).
    - For >16B struct returns: hidden retptr via RDI/X8/RCX
      (v5.5.36 / v5.9.26 / Win64 — unchanged).

cx (6.7.8): the pair is r0:r4 — r4 is cx's rdx peer, the
multi-value second slot, so a `return p;` read by `var a, b = f();` /
`rethi()` agrees with no sync. (This note also said a struct fn's
`return (a, b);` is received as the struct: that form is REFUSED since
6.7.11 — a tuple is not the struct a fn returns.) Before 6.7.8 cx's pair emitters were
the v5.10.45 stubs: no program returning or receiving a 9-16 B
struct built for cx ("cx: int-class 16B struct pair-return ABI not
supported"). aarch64 returns the pair in x0:x1 with the multi-value
second slot in x2. 6.7.7 synced x1 from x2 (ESTRUCT_PAIR_HI_SYNC) for a
multi-value landing read as the struct pair; 6.7.13 REMOVED that sync
from all three backends (MV-12) — nothing that compiles lands a
multi-value return in a 9-16 B struct fn any more (the tuple literal
is refused since 6.7.11, `ret2` since 6.7.13). The one crossing left
runs the other way: a `return p;` read as two values, where
EFLLOAD_STRUCT_INT_PAIR writes x2 as well as x1.

PRE-v5.10.45 (the bug):
    Pre-fix, ANY 9-16B struct return returned only the LOW
    8 bytes via scalar rax. `got.y` (the high half) was
    read from whatever slot happened to be adjacent in the
    caller's frame — typically 0. Live for the entire v5.x
    cycle; surfaced via empirical premise-check at v5.10.44
    ship.

WIN64 ABI DEVIATION NOTE:
    Strict Microsoft x64 ABI requires hidden-RCX retptr for
    any ≥9B composite return (no rax+rdx pair). Cyrius uses
    rax+rdx pair on PE too — internal-ABI deviation that
    works because all callers + callees agree on the
    convention. Future foreign-C interop with value-typed
    struct returns would need a separate PE-retptr-mode emit
    path (out of scope for v5.10.x). Documented per the
    closed-system / no-consumer-impact rationale in
    CHANGELOG [5.10.47].

CONSUMER POV:
    Same syntax that was always documented for value-typed
    struct returns; the implementation now matches the
    type signature. No source migration needed for any
    Str-using consumer — Str's pointer-mode semantics
    preserved.

6.7.11 — THE CLASSES, JUDGED (RET-4, ST-6, ST-7, ST-15, ST-16):
    - The words are the return registers: `var a, b = f();` from a 9-16 B struct binds its two words;
      three names, a struct over 16 B ("which is not in registers - bind it to one name"), and two names
      from an 8 B struct or a `Str` ("returns 1 value") are refused — judged by the DECLARED return.
    - A struct return is checked by its STRUCT in every class: at 8 B or less another struct's value, a
      `*U` and a handle of another struct are refused by name ("a fn returning the struct `S8` takes a
      value of that struct, or a pointer to one — got `u` (a `U8`)") — through 6.7.10 the small class
      returned u's bytes.
    - `return E;` with E a `*S` (local, parameter, global, `: *S` call / method, field, arithmetic,
      `*E`) returns a COPY of the pointee in all three classes (from an 8 B fn it returned the address).
      `return &s;` was untyped through 6.7.12 (refused in other words by the pair and retptr classes);
      since 6.7.13 it is `return s;` in every class (D4 (a), ST-10) — `&u` of another struct refused by
      name. `return &h;` of a handle, a `*T` or an untyped `self` (its SLOT's address, returned as the
      struct's word at 8 B through 6.7.13) is refused by name in every class since 6.7.14 — "… `&q` is the
      address of q's slot, not a struct — return q" (D19 (a), ST-11). `return *pp;` / `return **pp;`
      through a `**S` return the struct in every class (ST-7).
    - `return (a, b[, c]);` in a fn declared `: S` (a named struct, any size) is refused — "a fn returning
      the struct `P` returns that struct — `(a, b)` is a tuple, another type"; since 6.7.13 `ret2(a, b)`
      there is refused in every class too, "a fn returning the struct `P` returns that struct — `ret2`
      writes two values" (D22, MV-11). (It returned 0s over 16 B, dropped the second value at 8 B, and was
      right at 9-16 B only by coincidence.)
    - Since 6.7.13 `return mk(..);` of a MULTI-VALUE call from a struct fn of 8 B or less is refused, "is
      not returned as struct 'P1' - bind it first" (D25, MV-14); the 9-16 B and over-16 B classes already
      refused it.
    - In a 9-16 B struct fn a returned call must END the value: `return mk2(1) == p;` is refused by name;
      `return mk2(1) + p;` (a dispatch) and `return mk2(1);` are unchanged.
    See struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711. CHANGELOG [6.7.11].

6.7.16 (CHANGELOG [6.7.16] RV-10, RV-11, RV-15; the user's D105, 2026-10-20: "Keep both errors"): a `return E;` whose E
has an error of its own reports E's error ALONE, at 16 and at 24 B as at 8 B — `return zz;`, `return &(q);`, `return 5 +
;`, `return zz + 1;`, a tuple literal `return (zz, 1);`, and a method / operator `return` — the class refusal ("returned
in two registers … got `zz`") is printed only when the statement reported nothing (through 6.7.15 both printed). By
D105 `return nofn(1);` with nofn defined nowhere keeps BOTH errors (the undefined fn and the class refusal) — no change.
A struct over 8 B returned from a METHOD ENDING A CHAIN (`return w.p.q16();`, `return (*pw).p.q16();`, `return
mk().p.q16();`) is returned as `return p.q16();` is since 6.7.16 (SB-3) — struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711.

[[entries]]
name = "bare_return_in_fn"
description = "v5.10.48: bare `return;` in fn body synthesizes `return 0;` per cyrius's int-only return convention; since 6.7.11 a `return` outside a fn body is an error"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    fn early_zero(x): i64 {
        if (x > 0) { return; }    # synthesizes return 0
        return 99;
    }

WHAT v5.10.48 ADDED:
    PARSE_RETURN peeks for `;` before calling PCMPE. If
    present, emits `mov rax, 0` + jump-to-epilogue — byte-
    for-byte equivalent to `return 0;` at that position.

PRE-v5.10.48:
    `if (x > 0) { return; }` errored with
    `error:<source>:N: unexpected ';'` because PCMPE was
    called unconditionally and rejected an empty expression.
    Required workaround: `return 0;` explicitly.

CONSUMER POV:
    Idiomatic early-exit shape now works. No-op for fns that
    already used `return 0;` explicitly; new ergonomic for
    early-exit branches in conditional bodies. Cyrius
    treats every fn as returning int (no `void`), so
    bare `return;` is unambiguously sugar for `return 0;`.

6.7.11 (GF-9, D11): a `return` OUTSIDE a fn body is an error — `return outside a fn body`, reported once — at top
level and in a top-level block (an `if`, a loop, a `switch` case), as `break` / `continue` / `defer` / `?` have been
since 6.6.7. A program exits with `syscall(60, v)`, by running off its end, or through `fn main`'s auto-call. Through
6.7.10 it compiled and did not return: its jump was recorded into a fn's patch list from outside every fn and never
patched — x86_64 / PE fell through (`e9 00000000`), aarch64 / cx branched to itself and hung. Since 6.7.13 `ret2(..)`
outside a fn body (top level, a top-level block, a global's initializer) is refused by name too, "ret2 outside a fn
body", once (D20, MV-9) — it compiled and never returned through 6.7.12, with the same fall-through / hang.

[[entries]]
name = "enum_ident_array_size"
description = "v5.10.48: var name[IDENT] accepts an enum-const ident as a compile-time-known array size, in both fn-local PARSE_ARRAY and top-level PARSE_GVAR_ARR paths"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    enum BufSz { SMALL = 8; MEDIUM = 64; }
    var small_buf[SMALL];          # top-level
    fn use(): i64 {
        var local[SMALL];          # in-fn
        store64(&local, 42);
        return load64(&local);
    }

WHAT v5.10.48 ADDED:
    Both PARSE_ARRAY (in-fn) and PARSE_GVAR_ARR (top-level)
    accept an IDENT in `[]` if it resolves to a compile-time-
    known enum constant (high-bit-set marker at
    `enum_const_val[idx]` per the v5.5.2 fold registration).

PRE-v5.10.48:
    `var buf[SIZE]` (where SIZE is an ident) errored with
    `error: expected number, got identifier 'SIZE'`.
    Workaround: inline the literal `var buf[16];`. The
    ident error pointed at the offending token without
    explaining the rule.

PLAIN var-NAME = LITERAL NOT SUPPORTED:
    `var SIZE = 16; var buf[SIZE];` still errors — cyrius
    has no named-constant infrastructure outside enums.
    The idiomatic pattern is `enum Sz { N = 16; }
    var buf[N];`. A specific error message points at the
    enum-const idiom rather than the generic
    "expected number, got identifier".

CONSUMER POV:
    Named constants for array sizes via enums. Bad-shape
    ident triggers a specific diagnostic explaining the
    enum-only rule, not the generic message.

[[entries]]
name = "socket_syscall_wrappers_v51118"
description = "7 socket-family syscall wrappers in lib/syscalls_x86_64_linux.cyr + lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr peers (sys_socket, sys_bind, sys_listen, sys_connect, sys_recvfrom, sys_recvmsg, sys_accept4)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX (Linux only — Windows / macOS use platform-native APIs):
    var fd = sys_socket(domain, type, protocol);
    var rc = sys_bind(fd, sockaddr_ptr, addrlen);
    var rc = sys_listen(fd, backlog);
    var rc = sys_connect(fd, sockaddr_ptr, addrlen);
    var n = sys_recvfrom(fd, buf, len, flags, srcaddr_ptr, srcaddrlen_ptr);
    var n = sys_recvmsg(fd, msghdr_ptr, flags);
    var cfd = sys_accept4(fd, srcaddr_ptr, srcaddrlen_ptr, flags);

USE CASES:
    sys_socket + sys_bind + sys_recvfrom: sd_notify dgram for
        systemd-style service-readiness notification (kybernet 1.1.5+).
    sys_socket + sys_connect + sys_recvmsg: SCM_CREDENTIALS peer-cred
        capture for trusted-peer auth (argonaut 1.6.2+ already used
        sys_recvmsg via per-arch #ifdef enum — now centralized).
    sys_socket + sys_bind + sys_listen + sys_accept4: supervisor
        control-socket accept loop (kybernet 1.2.x edge-boot).

CROSS-ARCH SAFETY (load-bearing motivation):
    Pre-v5.11.18 consumers re-rolled per-arch syscall dispatch:
        #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86_64
            enum SockSysNr { SOCKET = 41; BIND = 49; RECVFROM = 45; }
        #else
            enum SockSysNr { SOCKET = 198; BIND = 200; RECVFROM = 207; }
        #endif
    Forgetting the dispatch (or copy-pasting from an x86 example)
    silently misrouted on aarch64: x86 41 → socket; aarch64 41 →
    pipe2. CI on x86 passes; aarch64 deployment misbehaves.
    The stdlib wrappers centralize this — no consumer can route
    wrong.

ARCH-SPECIFIC SYSCALL NUMBERS (encoded in lib/syscalls_*.cyr's
SysNr enums):

    Wrapper            x86_64  aarch64
    sys_socket            41     198
    sys_connect           42     203
    sys_recvfrom          45     207
    sys_recvmsg           47     212
    sys_bind              49     200
    sys_listen            50     201
    sys_accept4          288     242

TEST: tests/tcyr/socket_syscalls.tcyr runtime-exercises
sys_socket on AF_UNIX/SOCK_DGRAM (load-bearing aarch64
verify — a misrouted dispatch returns pipe2 fd, not socket).
Other 6 wrappers verified via &fn compile-time callability.

[[entries]]
name = "bote_async_primitives_v51115"
description = "lib/thread.cyr chan_try_recv + lib/async.cyr cancel_token_* (cancel_token_new / signal / check) — bote-streaming-dispatch building blocks"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    # Non-blocking channel receive (lib/thread.cyr)
    var val = chan_try_recv(ch);     # 0 if empty (avoid sending 0
                                     #   if 0-as-sentinel matters)

    # Cancellation tokens (lib/async.cyr)
    var tok = cancel_token_new();    # alloc 8-byte atomic-backed slot
    cancel_token_signal(tok);        # idempotent set; atomic_store(tok, 1)
    var cancelled = cancel_token_check(tok);  # 0 live, 1 cancelled

INTENDED PATTERN (bote streaming dispatch):
    # Transport thread:
    var tok = cancel_token_new();
    dispatch_streaming(handler, tok, progress_chan);
    # ... on $/cancelRequest:
    cancel_token_signal(tok);

    # Worker thread (handler):
    while (more_work()) {
        if (cancel_token_check(tok) == 1) {
            cleanup();
            return;
        }
        chan_send(progress_chan, partial_result);
        do_work_chunk();
    }

UNDERLYING:
    chan_* shipped at v5.5.31 (MPSC, futex + mutex backed,
        chan_new/send/recv/close). chan_try_recv added v5.11.15
        completes the non-blocking surface. MPMC-safe, and blocking
        wherever CHAN_BLOCKING == 1, since 6.6.16.
    cancel_token_* backed by lib/atomic.cyr's atomic_load /
        atomic_store (aligned i64 ops on x86_64 + aarch64; explicit
        API documents the atomicity intent). No busy-wait — worker
        polls between work chunks at its own cadence.

PREMISE-CHECK NOTE: bote's original filing speculated 3 missing
primitives (MPSC channel, cancellation, per-thread arena). All 3
already existed; the slot shipped 4 thin shims (`chan_try_recv` +
3 cancel-token verbs) instead of inventing parallel infrastructure.
See CHANGELOG [5.11.15] for the discovery doc block at top of
lib/async.cyr.

[[entries]]
name = "arena_lifecycle_v51114"
description = "Arena lifecycle in lib/alloc.cyr — arena_new/alloc/reset/free (v5.11.14) + the v6.5.9 growable arena and ARENA_FULL_* exhaustion policy"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var a = arena_new(SIZE);      # fixed-capacity arena
    var p = arena_alloc(a, n);    # bump-pointer alloc
    arena_reset(a);               # rewind to start (per-frame reuse)
    arena_free(a);                # FINAL release (v5.11.14)

THE LIFECYCLE:
    arena_new → many arena_alloc → arena_reset (reuse) → many
    arena_alloc → ... → arena_free (terminal)

LOAD-BEARING USE CASE (bote per-frame reuse):
    # Per WS / SSE frame: alloc scratch buffers, dispatch handler,
    # reset arena for next frame, terminate arena at session end.
    var a = arena_new(64 * 1024);
    while (session_alive == 1) {
        var msg_buf = arena_alloc(a, MAX_FRAME);
        var resp_buf = arena_alloc(a, MAX_RESP);
        handle_frame(msg_buf, resp_buf);
        arena_reset(a);
    }
    arena_free(a);

== EXHAUSTION IS A POLICY, NOT A RETURN VALUE (v6.5.9) ==

What an exhausted arena DOES was the gap, not what it returns.
`arena_alloc` returning 0 was always correct; the problem is that
a `Str` of 0 is indistinguishable from a valid `Str`, there is no
option type and no error channel through the `_a` families, so
the 0 flows on and the first thing that touches it dereferences
it. "Arena too small" was observable as a SIGSEGV several layers
away, in a JSON walker, with no indication which allocator ran out.

⭐ THE REGRESSION DIRECTION IS THE POINT. Before threading a route
onto an arena, an oversized response was a LEAK on the no-free
global bump. After threading — a change whose whole purpose is to
stop leaking — the same response is a CRASH. Adopting the feature
correctly made the failure worse and quieter.

Four behaviours, one field on the arena header:

    ARENA_FULL_NULL  = 0   return 0 — TODAY'S BEHAVIOUR, still the
                           DEFAULT, so no existing arena moves
    ARENA_FULL_GROW  = 1   chain another chunk
    ARENA_FULL_SPILL = 2   serve overflow from the global allocator
                           (weaker — spilled bytes are never
                           reclaimed by reset — but the crash
                           becomes the pre-arena cost)
    ARENA_FULL_ABORT = 3   die loudly AT the allocation instead of
                           faulting three layers away

    var a = arena_new_growable(64 * 1024);   # = new + policy GROW
    arena_set_on_full(a, ARENA_FULL_ABORT);  # or set it explicitly
    var pol = arena_on_full(a);              # read it back (-1 if a == 0)
    var cap = arena_capacity_total(a);       # whole chain, which
                                             # arena_used cannot show
                                             # once it has grown

Allocator-vtable form: `arena_allocator_growable(initial)` and
`arena_allocator_set_on_full(al, policy)` (the vtable's state
pointer IS the arena handle, so the setter reaches through).

⚠ GROW RETAINS ITS CHUNKS ACROSS `arena_reset`. The bump allocator
underneath has no free(), so releasing them is not expressible —
and it is not what an arena wants anyway. Reset rewinds to the
FIRST chunk and re-uses the chain, so a request loop converges on
its high-water mark and then allocates nothing.

⚠ `arena_free` previously cleared only base/ptr/end, leaving the
policy set — so a "freed" growable arena took the grow path and
SUCCEEDED. It now resets the policy to `ARENA_FULL_NULL`, which is
what makes "further allocs fail" true again.

Header layout is `{ base, ptr, end, policy, first_chunk, cur_chunk,
chunk_size }` = 56 bytes; base/end mirror the CURRENT chunk so the
hot path stays two loads.

PREMISE-CHECK NOTE: bote's original filing was titled "fl_free" —
premise check showed `arena_reset` (the load-bearing per-frame
reuse primitive) already shipped at v5.5.x; the actual missing
surface was the terminal `arena_free`. v5.11.14 added the
terminator. The v6.5.9 filing (from agnosai) likewise asked for
THREE things — a growable arena, a spilling arena, and an
exhaustion policy — which collapse into the one policy field
above, which is why all three shipped together.

[[entries]]
name = "sock_set_recv_timeout_v51113"
description = "lib/net.cyr sock_set_recv_timeout(fd, secs, usecs) — Slowloris defense for HTTP / WS servers"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var rc = sock_set_recv_timeout(fd, 5, 0);    # 5-second timeout
    var rc = sock_set_recv_timeout(fd, 0, 250000);  # 250ms

SEMANTICS:
    Sets SO_RCVTIMEO via setsockopt. After setting, sock_recv(fd, ...)
    that blocks longer than (secs, usecs) returns -EAGAIN (or -EWOULDBLOCK).
    Per-fd setting; persists for the fd's lifetime.

LOAD-BEARING USE CASE (Slowloris defense):
    # Per accept(), set a recv timeout so a misbehaving client
    # can't tie up a worker thread by trickling bytes:
    var cfd = sys_accept4(lfd, ..., SOCK_CLOEXEC);
    sock_set_recv_timeout(cfd, 5, 0);    # 5s wall-clock cap on slow reads
    serve_http(cfd);

VERIFICATION (from bote 1.9.5 audit response):
    var fd = sys_socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, 0);
    # ... connect to a slow-server fixture ...
    sock_set_recv_timeout(fd, 1, 0);    # 1-second timeout
    var t0 = gettime_ns();
    var n = sock_recv(fd, buf, len);    # blocks ~1.056s, returns -EAGAIN
    var elapsed_ms = (gettime_ns() - t0) / 1000000;
    assert(elapsed_ms >= 1000 && elapsed_ms < 1200);

GAP (part B of original bote filing): getaddrinfo equivalent —
DNS resolver surface — was deferred (multi-slot scope) and not
pinned in v5.11.x. Track via bote consumer asks.

[[entries]]
name = "ws_handshake_rfc6455_v51116"
description = "lib/ws_server.cyr ws_server_handshake enforces RFC 6455 §4.1: Sec-WebSocket-Key must be 24-char base64 (16-byte payload encoded with `=` padding)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SEMANTICS:
    ws_server_handshake(cfd, req_buf, req_len) parses the HTTP
    upgrade request. Pre-v5.11.16, it accepted any non-empty
    Sec-WebSocket-Key value — including malformed lengths.

POST-v5.11.16 (RFC §4.1 conformance):
    Sec-WebSocket-Key is the base64 encoding of a 16-byte value.
    Base64 of 16 bytes = 22 chars + 2 chars of `=` padding = exactly
    24 chars. Pre-fix accepted "short" / 27-char / arbitrary lengths;
    post-fix rejects all but 24-char and returns 0 (per docstring:
    caller responds 400 Bad Request).

FIX SHAPE (5 lines in lib/ws_server.cyr::ws_server_handshake):
    var klen = strlen(key);
    if (klen != 24) { return 0; }      # NEW: reject malformed length
    var magic = _wss_magic();
    # ... SHA-1 + base64 Accept derivation continues unchanged

CONSUMER IMPACT:
    Spec-compliant clients send 24-char keys; pre-fix they were
    accepted regardless of compliance. Post-fix they're STILL
    accepted (no behavior change for compliant clients), but
    malformed clients now fail-closed at the handshake instead of
    silently progressing to bidirectional frame I/O.

WHY THIS MATTERS:
    The handshake produces Sec-WebSocket-Accept = base64(sha1(key +
    magic)). A SHA-1 over arbitrary-length input still produces a
    syntactically-valid Accept header — the client would receive the
    upgrade and the WS connection would proceed normally over a
    spec-violating handshake. Closes a real conformance hole bote
    filed in audit.

[[entries]]
name = "cap_raises_v51118_v51119"
description = "Compiler capacity caps and their history. CURRENT (6.6.20): fn_table init 2048 growable to a 131072 hard ceiling, identifier pool 8 MiB at 0xEC00000, var_table to 1,048,576, fixup table to 64M entries, preprocessed source 24 MB. The v6.4.76 block and the v5.11.18/.19 raises this entry was written for (131072 → 262144 / 4096 → 8192) are HISTORY — see the SUPERSEDED blocks."
category = "cyrius"
---
CURRENT CAPS (6.6.20 — verify against src/common/util.cyr
`_capacity_warnings`, which tests/gates/memory/capacity_meter_denominators.sh
derives; READ THIS BLOCK, the rest is history):

    fn_table:           init 2048 (v6.5.51), doubles, hard ceiling 131072
                        (v6.5.40; `error: too many functions (max 131072)`)
    identifier pool:    8,388,608 B (8 MiB) at 0xEC00000 (v6.5.40)
    var_table:          init 8192, doubles to a hard 1,048,576 (v6.3.0)
    fixup_table:        init 1,048,576, doubles to a 67,108,864-entry
                        runaway ceiling (the warning still divides by
                        1,048,576)
    string_data:        2,097,152
    code buffer:        8 MiB base, +8 MiB steps, 64 MiB ceiling (v6.4.49)
    preprocessed source: 24 MB (`_SRC_CAP` = 25,165,824, v6.5.40)

WARNING THRESHOLDS (85% — integer `count * 100 / cap >= 85`):
    fn_table warn at:           111,412 fns   (85% of the 131072 CEILING,
                                               not of the live _fnt_cap)
    identifier pool warn at:    7,130,317 B   (85% of 8,388,608)
    LEX hard error at:          np + need >= 8,388,336 (lex.cyr; 272 B of
                                slack for the inner write loop)

SUPERSEDED v6.4.76 (this was the CURRENT block until 6.6.20, 4-16x
stale by then):

    fn_table:           8192 INITIAL, doubles to a 32768 hard ceiling
    identifier pool:    524288 bytes (512 KB) at heap 0x60000-0xE0000
    var_table:          8192
    fixup_table:        1048576
    fn_table warn at 27853 fns; identifier pool warn at 445645 bytes;
    LEX hard error at np + need >= 524016.

SUPERSEDED v6.4.75 / v6.4.76 — what actually changed, and why the old
numbers here were dangerous:
    * v6.4.75 (P0): six fn-INDEXED side tables were still pinned to
      FIXED 8192-slot heap bands even though the fn table itself became
      growable at v6.2.0. Past 8192 fns, index `fi` of each table wrote
      into slot `fi-8192` of its NEIGHBOUR — silent metadata corruption
      with NO diagnostic. Fixed by lazy-alloc'ing all six at the 32768
      max. The capacity WARNING was complicit: it divided by the LIVE
      `_fnt_cap`, so it screamed "99 %" at 8191 fns and went SILENT the
      instant the table doubled (9000/16384 = 54 %) — exactly when the
      corruption started. It now divides by the fixed 32768 ceiling.
    * v6.4.76: the identifier pool doubled 256 KB → 512 KB IN PLACE
      (0x60000-0xE0000, into the free 0xA0000-0x100000 band). The pool
      END had to stay <= 0x100000 — above that physical ceiling the
      compiler HUNG rather than erroring. LEX thresholds moved
      261872 → 524016. (HISTORY since v6.5.40 moved the pool out of
      that band entirely, to 8 MiB at 0xEC00000.)
    * NOTE ON "8192" (v6.4.76): most 8192 references in the tree were
      then CORRECT — it was the fn_table INITIAL cap (and the var_table
      cap outright). A blanket 8192 → 32768 sweep would have INTRODUCED
      errors. Today 8192 is the var_table INITIAL cap only; the
      fn_table starts at 2048.

SUPERSEDED v6.2.0 (Phase 0):
    The fn-tables (and both fn hashes) became GROWABLE — when the count
    would exceed `_fnt_cap` (init 8192), REGFN doubles the family and
    rehashes the name-hash, up to the 32768 ceiling (the 2 B hash slot
    stores fi+1, so fi+1 <= 65535). So the "load factor degrades to 1.0
    at 8192" cliff below no longer applies: the hash grows with the cap
    (mask = cap-1), keeping the factor bounded.

--- HISTORY (v5.11.18 / v5.11.19, the raises this entry was filed for) ---

CAPS AS OF v5.11.19:

    fn_table:           4096  → 8192    (v5.11.19)
    identifier buffer:  131072 → 262144 bytes  (v5.11.18)

WARNING THRESHOLDS THEN (85% of the then-cap):

    fn_table warn at:           >= 6963 fns        (was >= 3686)
    identifier buffer warn at:  >= 222822 bytes    (was >= 111430)

CONSUMER MOTIVATION:
    kybernet 1.1.0 (AGNOS PID-1 init system) assembled the full
    AGNOS surface (stdlib + agnosys-full + agnostik + libro + patra
    + argonaut + sigil + sakshi) and hit:

        fn_table at 92% (3779 / 4096)
        identifier buffer at 85% (112094 / 131072 bytes)

    Warned thresholds; non-recoverable at 100%. kybernet 1.1.1
    worked around via profile-bundle trim (agnosys-full →
    agnosys-core); v1.2.0 edge-boot work brings agnosys-storage +
    agnosys-trust back into scope — would tip past hard cap.

UPSTREAM FIX SHAPE:
    v5.11.18 Part A.i — identifier buffer doubled in-place; uses
        existing 0xA0000-0x18C100 heap-map gap; no relocation.
    v5.11.18 Part B — 7 socket-syscall wrappers (separate consumer
        ask from kybernet 1.1.5 P(-1) audit).
    v5.11.19 Part A.ii — fn_table heap-map refactor: 7 scattered
        fn_* tables relocated, contiguous extended + primary blocks
        stride doubled, IR + fixup regions shifted +0x80000. cc5
        self-host byte-identical at 804,456 B (was 804,464).
        Hash tables (fn_name_hash + fn_start_hash) kept at 8192
        slots × 2B (mask 8191 preserved) — gap couldn't fit doubled
        hash tables AND doubled per-fn tables; trade-off documented.

CONSUMER REGRESSION TESTS:
    `tests/tcyr/capacity_*` (existing gates) verify warn-threshold
    semantics with the new caps. programs/check.cyr::_capacity_gate
    bumped the synthetic stress fixture from 3500 fns → 7000 fns
    at v5.11.19 to re-trigger the >= 85% threshold (3500/8192 =
    42.7% no longer fires).

NO ALGORITHMIC / API / ON-DISK CHANGE:
    Pure cap raise. Existing consumer source compiles unchanged.
    Hash table load factor at 8192 fns degrades to 1.0 (was 0.5
    at 4096); for the kybernet 3779-fn worst case load factor is
    0.46 — well below probe cliff. (Both superseded — see the two
    SUPERSEDED blocks at the top of this entry.)

[[entries]]
name = "named_op_refactor_drift_gate_v51135_v51139"
description = "v5.11.35-.39 parser-to-emit named-op refactor arc: per-backend EMIT_<OP> helpers replace direct EB(S, 0xXX) byte emits in parse_*.cyr. Classes B (PIC-vs-direct address loads), C (f64 unary ops), D (v5.7.12 audit doc). v5.11.39 drift gate locks the pattern: programs/check.cyr scans parse_*.cyr for EB(S,...) and fails if any reappear"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.11.35-.38 migrated parser-to-emit direct-byte calls
to named-op helpers per backend:

  CLASS B (v5.11.36) — PIC-vs-direct address loads:
      EMIT_LEA_ABS / EMIT_MOVI_ABS per backend
      (x86: 48 8D 05 disp32 vs 48 B8 imm64;
       aarch64: ADRP+ADD vs LDR=literal)

  CLASS C (v5.11.37) — f64 unary ops:
      EF64_NEG, EF64_FROM, EF64_TO, EF64_ABS, EF64_SIN,
      EF64_COS, EF64_EXP, EF64_LN (8 new named ops
      per backend covering ptyps 71/83/84/85/86/87/88/99)

  CLASS D (v5.11.35) — aligned with v5.7.12 cx audit
      doc; covers ptyp 89-91 missed by v5.10.35's PARSE_SIMD_EXT
      locname-staleness fix

  v5.11.38 — Class B missed-site closeout
  v5.11.39 — ESWITCH_DISPATCH_* family; drift gate

DRIFT GATE — `_parse_emit_drift_gate()` in
`programs/check.cyr` scans all `parse_*.cyr` files
for any line containing `EB(S,` (the direct byte-emit
shape) and fails check.sh if found. The 6 parse files
covered (`parse_decl.cyr`, `parse_expr.cyr`,
`parse_fn.cyr`, `parse_ctrl.cyr`, `parse_types.cyr`,
`parse_top.cyr`) are now structurally locked against
re-introducing parser-side architecture-specific
direct emits.

WHY THIS MATTERS — cross-arch leak prevention:

Pre-refactor pattern: a parser fix lands a `EB(S, 0x48); EB(S, 0xB8); ...`
sequence for x86. The same parser code runs on the
aarch64 build, but those bytes are x86 — silent
miscompile when the aarch64 build emits them. Named
ops force each backend to provide its own
EMIT_<OP>(S) impl; missing impl = link error, not
silent garbage.

The drift gate locks the refactor. Without it, the
next parser fix that "just adds a few bytes" would
quietly re-introduce the class.

[[entries]]
name = "f64_abs_peephole_v51140"
description = "v5.11.40 — f64_abs(x) peephole optimization. Single fold replaces 6-instruction sequence (mov-xmm + and-mask + ret) with a 2-instruction sequence (ANDPS xmm0, [rip+mask] / RET). Long-pinned audit optimization landed at .40"
category = "cyrius"
---
The `f64_abs(x)` peephole consolidates a 6-instruction
sequence into 2 instructions. Long-pinned in the audit
doc (Class C refactor scope) and landed at v5.11.40
after the .37 Class C f64-unary named-op refactor
made the consolidation site singular instead of
scattered.

EF64_ABS(S) is the named op per backend (x86 emits
ANDPS xmm0, [rip+0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF mask] which clears
the sign bit; aarch64 emits FABS d0, d0).

[[entries]]
name = "cve_08_hardening_cld_v51141"
description = "v5.11.41 — the missing-cld bug security hardening: emit cld (0xFC, 1 byte) before any rep movsb / rep stosb sequence. Closes the direction-flag-set-by-prior-callee class"
category = "cyrius"
---
Per the v5.0.x security audit, calling convention does
NOT guarantee DF (direction flag, EFLAGS bit 10) is
cleared at function entry. A misbehaving callee that
sets DF (via `std`) would cause `rep movsb` in the
caller to walk backwards through memory — silent data
corruption.

Fix at v5.11.41: cyrius's PE / Linux / Mach-O backends
now emit `cld` (0xFC, 1 byte) immediately before any
`rep movsb` / `rep stosb` sequence. 1-byte hardening
per call site; cc5 size delta +<10 B.

Reference: docs/audit/2026-04-13-security-audit.md
the missing-cld bug.

[[entries]]
name = "lsp_semantic_tokens_locals_params_v51142"
description = "v5.11.42 — cyrius-lsp textDocument/semanticTokens/full legend extended to color locals + parameters (was: only keywords + types + identifiers)"
category = "cyrius"
---
cyrius-lsp's `semanticTokens/full` provider now emits
distinct token-type values for:

  - Local variables (`var x = ...` inside fn bodies)
  - Function parameters (`fn f(p1, p2)`)

These were previously emitted as plain identifiers,
indistinguishable from globals / fn names / type
names in syntax highlighting. v5.11.42 added the
legend entries + the per-token-type emission logic
in `programs/cyrius-lsp.cyr`. Editors with LSP
semanticTokens support (Helix, Zed, VS Code via
extension, JetBrains Fleet) now render locals
differently from globals.

[[entries]]
name = "elf64_kernel_multiboot2_emit_v51143"
description = "v5.11.43 — EMITELF64_KERNEL: ELF64 + multiboot2 + EFI64-entry tag kernel emit. Gated by env var CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1 flipping _TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL flag. Routes kmode==1 dispatch to the new emitter instead of EMITELF_KERNEL (legacy ELF32+mb1)"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.11.43 added ELF64-kernel emit for AGNOS UEFI x86_64
boot via GRUB-EFI long-mode handoff. Same `kernel;`
source-language directive; new env var
`CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1` flips `_TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL`
at `src/main.cyr:1003` and routes the kmode==1
dispatch in `src/backend/x86/fixup.cyr:1884`:

    if (L64(S + 0x18FCA0) == 1) {
        if (_TARGET_ELF64_KERNEL == 1) { EMITELF64_KERNEL(S); }
        else { EMITELF_KERNEL(S); }
        return 0;
    }

EMITELF64_KERNEL writes:

  ELF64 header        64 B  (e_ident class=2 ELFCLASS64;
                              e_type=ET_EXEC; e_machine=EM_X86_64)
  Program header       56 B  (PT_LOAD, p_vaddr=0x100000,
                              p_flags=RWX, p_align=0x1000)
  Multiboot2 header    48 B  (magic 0xE85250D6, arch i386,
                              tags: ENTRY_ADDRESS_EFI64 type=9,
                                    MODULE_ALIGN type=6, END type=0)
  Code                 cp B   (.text)
  BSS-as-zeros        totvar B (in p_filesz; SHT_NOBITS in shdr)
  RODATA              spos B   (.rodata strings)
  Section header tab  + .shstrtab (GRUB compat — v5.11.29)

→ entry = 0x100000 + 120 + 48 = 0x1000A8

KEY GOTCHA pinned at v5.11.46: FIXUP's kmode==1
hard-coded `entry = 0x100060` (legacy ELF32+mb1
layout) which produces a 0x48-byte fixup shift on
ELF64-kernel binaries. See
field_notes/compiler/gotchas/elf64_kernel_fixup_entry_mismatch_v51146.

6.7.11: a `kernel;` image (both emitters — ELF32 multiboot1 and `CYRIUS_ELF64_KERNEL=1`) SHRINKS under `CYRIUS_DCE=1`:
the dead fns are removed and `.bss` / `.rodata` move down with the code (by a multiple of 16), every data address
moving with them; 6.6.20 – 6.7.10 only NOP-filled it. A `--pie` kernel still declines. language/tooling.cyml § DCE.

[[entries]]
name = "efi_application_pe_emit_v51147"
description = "v5.11.47-.49 — UEFI Application PE32+ emit mode. CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1 env var flips both _TARGET_PE=1 and _TARGET_EFI_APPLICATION=1. Subsystem byte 3→10 (WINDOWS_CUI→EFI_APPLICATION); EEXIT emits single ret; no kernel32 imports; Data Dirs [1]/[12] zeroed; RELOCS_STRIPPED cleared (.49)"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.11.47-.49 added UEFI Application emit unblocking
the AGNOS sovereign UEFI bootloader (gnoboot).

ACTIVATION:

    CYRIUS_TARGET_EFI=1 cyrius build src/main.cyr build/BOOTX64.EFI

Sets both `_TARGET_PE = 1` (EFI implies PE32+
container + MS x64 ABI + 4KB SectionAlignment +
0x200 FileAlignment + .reloc + DllCharacteristics
NX_COMPAT) and `_TARGET_EFI_APPLICATION = 1` (the
deltas below).

DELTAS FROM WINDOWS-CUI PE:

  pe/emit.cyr:746   Subsystem byte 3 → 10 (EFI_APPLICATION)
  x86/emit.cyr:545  EEXIT emits single 0xC3 (ret) — firmware
                    reads rax as EFI_STATUS (vs the 13-byte
                    kernel32!ExitProcess IAT call in CUI mode)
  pe/emit.cyr:439   skip _pe_imp_add("ExitProcess") in _pe_layout
  pe/emit.cyr:_pe_register_kernel32
                    fail-fast on any kernel32 reroute under EFI
                    mode — UEFI has no kernel32; silent miscompile
                    avoided
  pe/emit.cyr:Data Dirs [1] + [12] zeroed under EFI
  pe/emit.cyr:776   DllCharacteristics force NX_COMPAT under EFI
                    even with zero .reloc (v5.11.48)
  pe/emit.cyr:714   COFF Characteristics clears RELOCS_STRIPPED
                    under EFI (v5.11.49 — UEFI firmware needs
                    this latitude to place the image at any
                    free address)

CONSUMER ENTRY-POINT SHAPE:

UEFI firmware calls AddressOfEntryPoint with
RCX=ImageHandle, RDX=SystemTable (MS x64 ABI). cyrius
PE entry prologue is `e9 00 00 00 00` (5-byte jmp +0,
register-preserving), so firmware-supplied RCX/RDX
survive into the first user instruction. CAPTURE
PATTERN: first user statement must be inline asm
(fn calls clobber RCX/RDX with arg-setup mov's).
See programs/efi_probe.cyr for the canonical shape.

REGRESSION GATES:

  _efi_emit_gate()         (v5.11.48) — structural
                                        Subsystem + NX_COMPAT + Data Dirs zeroed
  _efi_ovmf_smoke_gate()   (v5.11.49) — runtime
                                        boot qemu+OVMF; grep "hello, uefi"

UNBLOCKS: gnoboot (sovereign UEFI bootloader, ~2000
LoC of cyrius) for AGNOS MVP boot path. Consumer pin
`cyrius = "5.11.49"` in cyrius.cyml.

[[entries]]
name = "efi_probe_canonical_uefi_program_v51148"
description = "programs/efi_probe.cyr — minimal 64-LoC cyrius UEFI Application that writes 'hello, uefi' via SystemTable->ConOut->OutputString. Single asm block; no globals, no fixups, all runtime register-indirect addressing. Canonical inline-asm-first capture pattern for UEFI entry args"
category = "cyrius"
---
programs/efi_probe.cyr is the lower-bound shape for a
cyrius UEFI Application:

```cyrius
kernel;
asm {
    # mov rcx, [rdx+0x40]    ; ConOut = SystemTable->ConOut
    0x48; 0x8B; 0x4A; 0x40;
    # mov rax, [rcx+0x08]    ; OutputString fn ptr
    0x48; 0x8B; 0x41; 0x08;
    # lea rdx, [rip+0x0E]    ; rdx = &msg (14 bytes past end-of-LEA)
    0x48; 0x8D; 0x15; 0x0E; 0x00; 0x00; 0x00;
    # sub rsp, 0x28          ; shadow space + 8B align (MS x64)
    0x48; 0x83; 0xEC; 0x28;
    # call rax               ; OutputString(rcx=ConOut, rdx=msg)
    0xFF; 0xD0;
    # add rsp, 0x28
    0x48; 0x83; 0xC4; 0x28;
    # xor eax, eax           ; EFI_SUCCESS = 0
    0x31; 0xC0;
    # ret                    ; firmware reads rax as EFI_STATUS
    0xC3;
    # nop (align msg to 2-byte CHAR16)
    0x90;
    # UTF-16LE "hello, uefi\r\n\0" (28 bytes, 14 CHAR16)
    0x68; 0x00;  # h
    0x65; 0x00;  # e
    # ... (msg bytes)
}
```

NO GLOBALS by design — zero fixups means zero DIR64
entries means no .reloc section (test the cyrius PE
backend's "EFI mode with empty .reloc" path).

RIP-RELATIVE LEA with hand-computed displacement —
no need for inline-asm fixup-record machinery (which
cyrius doesn't have). The msg sits 14 bytes past the
end of the LEA instruction; disp32 = 0x0E.

UEFI ABI USED:

  MS x64 calling convention — RCX/RDX = first 2 args
  0x20 byte shadow space + 16-byte stack alignment
  EFI_STATUS return in rax

SYSTEM TABLE LAYOUT (UEFI spec § 4.3):

  offset 0x40 — ConOut* (EFI_SIMPLE_TEXT_OUTPUT_PROTOCOL)

SIMPLE_TEXT_OUTPUT_PROTOCOL (UEFI spec § 12.4):

  offset 0x00 — Reset
  offset 0x08 — OutputString  ← this is what we call
  offset 0x10 — TestString
  ...

CHAR16 STRINGS:

  UTF-16LE, NUL-terminated, 2-byte aligned

USE AS REFERENCE: when writing a heavier cyrius UEFI
app (gnoboot, future EFI tools), capture the firmware
args via inline asm first thing, then transition to
regular cyrius code. The captured handles can be
stored in globals once captured (subsequent global
writes won't clobber the firmware-supplied registers
because they're already saved).

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_dce_verbose_env_v51145"
description = "v5.11.45 — CYRIUS_DCE_VERBOSE=1 env var gates the per-fn name list in cycc's 'note: N unreachable fns ...' stderr output. Default-off: 38 lines collapse to 1 line per compile. Verbose mode opt-in for actual cleanup work"
category = "cyrius"
---
Pre-v5.11.45 cycc always emitted the full list of
unreachable fn names to stderr during compile:

    note: 37 unreachable fns (22871 bytes — set CYRIUS_DCE=1 to eliminate)
      dead: TS_lex_token_advance
      dead: TS_parse_type
      dead: _macho_emit_load_command
      ... 37 entries ...

Most of these are mode/arch-dispatched false positives
(TS_*, _macho_*, ir_*, aarch64-only fns reachable
only via --mode flags or other main_*.cyr) per the
v5.8.48 dead-code-audit-scope rule. 38 lines of
noise per compile.

v5.11.45 gated the per-fn list behind
`CYRIUS_DCE_VERBOSE=1`:

  Default mode:       1 line summary "note: N unreachable fns (B bytes)"
  CYRIUS_DCE_VERBOSE=1: full per-fn list (38+ lines)

Pre-existing byte-count off-by-2 bug fixed in the
same slot (was dropping the `)\n` from each line's
byte estimate).

6.7.14 (IN-18): the x86 dead set is INSTRUCTION-ALIGNED —
what reaches a fn is a `call` / `jmp rel32` that starts an
instruction, no longer any E8 / E9 byte whose next four
bytes happen to point at a fn (an immediate or a
displacement kept a dead fn alive). So a `dead:` list can be
LONGER than 6.7.13's for the same source (717 inputs: 69
notes higher, none lower; binaries unchanged), and it no
longer moves when an unrelated global is added (data
addresses moved with every global). Code the length decoder
cannot walk exactly keeps the old byte scan (a fn holding a
raw `asm { 0x.. }` byte, the top level once it holds one, a
switch's jump table), so nothing called is counted dead.
aarch64 scans 4-byte words already (unchanged).

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_vet_restored_v51145"
description = "v5.11.45 — cyrius vet restored as a real audit tool. build/cybs had been silently replaced with the 12 KB bootstrap binary (no vet/deny dispatch); install.sh _rebuild_stale skipped because mtime was newer than source. Fixed via explicit syscall(60) exit in cybs.cyr + scan-buffer cap raise 16 KB → 256 KB. cybs 12,344 B → 44,672 B"
category = "cyrius"
---
`cyrius vet` walks an entry .cyr source's include
graph and lists transitive dependencies. `cyrius deny`
lists policy violations.

At v5.11.45 entry, `cyrius vet src/main.cyr` was
silently returning "no dependencies" — incorrect:
main.cyr has 14 includes. Root cause: `build/cybs`
had been replaced with the 12 KB bootstrap binary
(which has no vet/deny dispatch). `install.sh
--refresh-only` carried the bad binary into
`~/.cyrius/bin/cybs`; the `_rebuild_stale` check
skipped rebuild because the bad binary's mtime was
newer than the source — same shape of silent slip
as build/cc5's mabda contamination.

FIXES IN v5.11.45 P(-1) BUNDLE:

  programs/cybs.cyr — added explicit `syscall(60, r);`
    exit (vet was running twice; the second run was
    being preempted by the runtime fall-through path)
  programs/cybs.cyr — scan-buffer cap 16 KB → 256 KB
    (was truncating reads on src/main.cyr's 86 KB
    source and falsely reporting "no dependencies")

Post-fix: `cyrius vet src/main.cyr` correctly lists
14 includes, exit 0; `cyrius deny` lists 0 violations.
cybs 12,344 B (bootstrap) → 44,672 B (real audit tool).

[[entries]]
name = "cyrius_lsp_argv0_self_resolution_v51144"
description = "v5.11.44 — cyrius-lsp uses /proc/self/exe to resolve its install dir and find cycc via <install-dir>/cycc BEFORE the 3 existing fallback paths. Editors with minimal-env LSP launchers (Claude Code et al.) no longer silently disable diagnostics"
category = "cyrius"
---
cyrius-lsp's `find_cc5()` previously tried 3 fallback
paths to locate the cycc binary:

  1. $CYRIUS_HOME/bin/cycc
  2. ~/.cyrius/bin/cycc
  3. /usr/local/bin/cycc

In editors that launch LSP servers with a minimal env
(no $HOME, no $CYRIUS_HOME) — Claude Code is the
canonical example — all 3 fallbacks miss and the LSP
silently returns "diagnostics disabled, can't find
cycc". User sees no syntax errors / type errors /
unresolved-name warnings on edit; manual `cyrius
build` is the only way to surface them.

v5.11.44 added a 4th, FIRST-tried path:
`<install-dir>/cycc` where install-dir is resolved via
`/proc/self/exe → realpath → dirname`. The cyrius-lsp
binary is shipped at `~/.cyrius/bin/cyrius-lsp`
alongside `~/.cyrius/bin/cycc`, so the self-resolution
finds cycc immediately regardless of caller env.

IMPLEMENTATION SHAPE (mirrors v5.11.39
programs/cyrius-init.cyr::_resolve_templates_dir):

```cyrius
fn _resolve_install_dir(): Str {
    var buf[1024];
    var n = sys_readlink("/proc/self/exe", &buf, 1024);
    if (n <= 0) { return str_from(""); }
    # walk back to dirname
    while (n > 0 && load8(&buf + n) != '/') { n = n - 1; }
    return str_from_buf(&buf, n);
}
```

Startup log line `[cyrius-lsp] found cycc:
<resolved-path>` makes the resolution path debuggable.

Reference: docs/development/proposals/archived/cyrius-lsp-argv0-self-resolution.md
(filed 2026-05-02, closed 2026-05-13).

[[entries]]
name = "ts_tsx_to_js_emitter"
description = "cycc --emit-js <file.tsx> — emit runnable browser JavaScript from TypeScript/TSX (v6.1.11)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Cyrius has a TypeScript/TSX frontend (`src/frontend/ts/*`) and a JS
backend (`src/backend/js/emit.cyr`). `cycc --emit-js file.tsx`
type-strips and lowers TS/TSX to real, runnable JavaScript:

- Strips interfaces / type aliases / annotations / `as` / `!` /
  generics (`<T>`).
- Lowers JSX to `h(tag, props, ...kids)` calls — the pragma is
  configurable via `CYRIUS_JSX_PRAGMA` (default `h`), with a
  standalone `h` runtime prelude emitted.
- ESM passthrough with type-only export pruning; verbatim
  string / template literals.
- AST-driven (built on the v6.1.10 children-list allocator fix);
  emits indented output (v6.1.12). `for` / `for-of` / `for-in`
  headers fixed at v6.1.12.

The emitted JS passes `node --check` + a `--parse-ts` round-trip.
Through 6.7.14 this was x86-Linux-only (the TS frontend was not in
the cross binaries: every other compiler skipped `--lex-ts` /
`--parse-ts` / `--emit-js` like an unknown flag and compiled the
TypeScript as cyrius). Since 6.7.15 (the user's D38; CHANGELOG
[6.7.15] PL-13) every compiler but `cycc_cx` reads the three modes —
the aarch64 Linux cross and native, both macOS and both Windows
compilers — with x86_64's exit codes, diagnostics and JS bytes, from
stdin or `<flag> <file>` (a Windows path split as CommandLineToArgvW
splits it); each fork grows ~262 – 336 KB for it. Since 6.7.16 (TL-9)
the /proc-reading drivers read the WHOLE command line, so a path past
byte 255 is opened whole (it was cut and the cut name opened, and a flag
past it dropped); a command line of 2 MiB or more is refused by name —
language/tooling.cyml `compiler_flags`. See also `build_target_js`.

[[entries]]
name = "build_target_js"
description = "cyrius build --target=js — CLI wrapper over cycc --emit-js (v6.1.12)"
category = "cyrius"
---
`cyrius build --target=js <file.tsx>` is the wrapper-level entry
point for the TS/TSX → JS emitter (`ts_tsx_to_js_emitter`). It
resolves the source, invokes `cycc --emit-js`, and writes the
JavaScript output — the consumer-facing way to compile a `.tsx`
to browser JS without calling `cycc` directly. v6.1.12.

Since 6.6.6 it refused by name off x86_64 Linux; since 6.7.15 (D38's
reach, PL-13 — kept by 6.7.16's D50) it runs on every POSIX host, the
compiler there having the TS front end — on arm64 macOS only since
6.7.16 (TT-15: with no selector the CLI refused it there, comparing the
compiler it would run with the `cycc` slot while the host's default is
`cycc_aarch64`). `--aarch64 --target=js` is refused on every host
(6.7.12's D20). On Windows it refuses by name — "build --target=js
is not available on this target": the CLI has no Windows spawn that
writes the output to a file, so run `cycc.exe --emit-js <file.ts> >
<out.js>` directly.

[[entries]]
name = "preprocessor_if_numeric"
description = "#if NAME >= VALUE — numeric preprocessor comparison (==, !=, <, >, <=, >=) (v5.6.x)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Beyond `#ifdef` / `#ifndef` (defined-ness), the preprocessor
evaluates NUMERIC comparison directives:

```
#define LEVEL 3
#if LEVEL >= 2
  ... compiled in ...
#endif
#if LEVEL >= 4
  ... compiled out ...
#endif
```

Operators: `==` `!=` `<` `>` `<=` `>=`. The LHS is a `#define
NAME VALUE` integer (a bare `#define NAME` carries presence only,
no value — and `cyrius build -D NAME` likewise sets presence, NOT
a value, so a threshold knob must be set in source). Implemented in
`src/frontend/lex_pp.cyr` (`PP_EVAL_IF`) since the v5.6.x
preprocessor work; works at top level AND inside fn bodies.

Production use: sakshi's `#define SAKSHI_LEVEL <0..5>` compile-time
log-level threshold (`#if SAKSHI_LEVEL >= n` per level) — every
level above the threshold costs zero bytes and zero cycles.

[[entries]]
name = "pie_codegen"
description = "--pie / CYRIUS_PIE=1 — position-independent (ET_DYN) executables (x86_64 v6.1.6, aarch64 v6.1.8)"
category = "cyrius"
---
`--pie` (or env `CYRIUS_PIE=1`) emits a position-independent
executable: `ET_DYN` ELF with `p_vaddr=0` and RIP-relative (x86_64)
/ `adrp`+`add` (aarch64) addressing, so the loader can ASLR the
whole image. Userland PIE shipped x86_64 at v6.1.6 and aarch64 at
v6.1.8 (the full `.tcyr` corpus runs as ASLR'd PIE binaries). It
reuses the proven `shared` / object-emit (`_IS_OBJ`) machinery;
non-PIE output stays byte-identical. A kernel-PIE ELF wrapper
(`EMITELF64_KERNEL`, AGNOS KASLR) exists under `--pie` for the
`kernel;` form but awaits an AGNOS boot harness.

[[entries]]
name = "gnu_hash_dynamic_link"
description = ".gnu.hash dynamic-linking — O(1) Bloom-filter symbol lookup, drops SysV .hash (v6.1.9)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Cyrius shared objects (`EMITELF_SHARED`) emit a single-bucket
`.gnu.hash` section + `DT_GNU_HASH` instead of the legacy SysV
`.hash` / `DT_HASH`. The native loader (`lib/dynlib.cyr`) was
already gnu-hash-only, so this flips `.so` symbol resolution onto
the O(1) Bloom-filter path instead of a linear `.dynsym` fallback.
x86-only (aarch64 has no `.so` path). v6.1.9.

[[entries]]
name = "windows_directory_listing"
description = "dir_list / is_dir / dir_walk on Windows via FindFirstFileW (v6.1.18)"
category = "cyrius"
---
On Windows (PE), `lib/fs.cyr`'s directory primitives now work —
they were no-ops (returning `-38`) before v6.1.18 because
`getdents64` has no Windows route. `lib/fs_win.cyr` (included under
`#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_WIN`) routes `dir_list` to
FindFirstFileW / FindNextFileW / FindClose and `is_dir` to
GetFileAttributesW, via four new `0xF016`-`0xF019` kernel32
reroutes. `dir_walk` / `dir_list_full` / `find_files` work
transitively. This completes the Windows file surface (process /
thread / TLS-via-args / env / file-I/O / directory enumeration all
work as of v6.1.16–v6.1.18). v6.1.18.

Since 6.7.12 `fs_win` also carries `win_handle_under(h, root_h)`: 1
when the object behind `h` lies strictly inside the directory behind
`root_h`, by their FINAL paths; 0 when not; -1 when a final path
cannot be had (never 1 — it fails closed). Through a new kernel32
reroute, 0xF04E `GetFinalPathNameByHandleW`; the `[embed]` open uses
it to refuse a junction planted after the check (language/tooling.cyml
the `[embed]` entry). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] WN-3.

[[entries]]
name = "bayan_ganita_stdlib_carve"
description = "Data-format + advanced-math carved OUT of the core stdlib into opt-in siblings — lib/bayan.cyr (json/toml/cyml/csv/base64/bigint/u128, v6.1.25) + lib/ganita.cyr (matrix/linalg/advanced-math, v6.1.26)"
category = "cyrius"
---
The data-format and advanced-math families no longer ship inside the
always-present stdlib. They were carved into two opt-in sibling
modules:

  - `lib/bayan.cyr` (v6.1.25) — json / toml / cyml / csv / base64 /
    bigint / u128.
  - `lib/ganita.cyr` (v6.1.26) — matrix / linalg / advanced-math.

OPT-IN — they are NOT auto-prepended. A consumer that wants them
includes explicitly:

    include "lib/bayan.cyr"
    include "lib/ganita.cyr"

NAMING:
    Canonical symbols are `bayan_*` / `ganita_*`. The legacy names
    (`base64_encode`, `json_parse`, `csv_*`, `bigint_*`, `matrix_*`,
    etc.) are kept as THIN SHIMS that forward to the canonical fns —
    existing source keeps compiling, new code should prefer the
    `bayan_*` / `ganita_*` spelling.

DELETED FILES:
    The old standalone `lib/base64.cyr`, `lib/csv.cyr`,
    `lib/json.cyr`, `lib/toml.cyr`, and `lib/bigint.cyr` were
    REMOVED — their content lives in `lib/bayan.cyr` now. Replace any
    `include "lib/json.cyr"` (etc.) with `include "lib/bayan.cyr"`.

RATIONALE:
    Bare-metal / kernel consumers (AGNOS objects) were dragging
    data-format + math code into the link whenever they pulled in a
    transitive stdlib include. Carving these out keeps kernel objects
    free of JSON / bignum / linear-algebra code they never call —
    you pay for the data-format/math surface only when you opt in.

[[entries]]
name = "native_tls_default"
description = "Native TLS 1.2/1.3 (client+server, over sigil) is the no-flag DEFAULT since v6.1.21 — libssl is now opt-OUT"
category = "cyrius"
---
Native TLS — the pure-cyrius TLS 1.2 / 1.3 stack (client AND server)
built over sigil — is the DEFAULT transport as of v6.1.21. No flag is
needed to get it; `tls_*` calls resolve to the native implementation
out of the box.

OPT-OUT (libssl):
    The legacy libssl-backed path is now opt-OUT, selected with:

        cyrius build -D CYRIUS_TLS_LIBSSL ...

    Without that define, no OpenSSL / libssl dependency is linked or
    dlopen'd — the TLS surface is fully sovereign.

This is the inverse of the pre-v6.1.21 default (libssl was the
no-flag path; native TLS was opt-in). The flip makes the
no-dependency, no-crates posture the one you get for free.

[[entries]]
name = "f64_named_type_and_operators"
description = "`var x: f64` / `var x: f32` tags a variable IEEE-754 double / single, so bare `+ - * /` route to EMIT_F{64,32}_BINOP and `< > <= >= == !=` to EF{64,32}_CMP (a REAL float compare, not integer-bit ordering) on x86/PE/Mach-O (SSE2/SSE) + aarch64 (NEON) + cx. Scalar `f64` is also a legal fn RETURN type (xmm0, sentinel -9). Shipped v6.2.18 (f32 conv) + v6.2.19 (named f64) + v6.4.55 (f64 return) + v6.4.56 (f32 ARITHMETIC + comparison + WARN-only typecheck) + v6.4.57 (float params + compound assign) + 6.6.11 (compound assign on a float GLOBAL and in a `for` step, the f32 right-operand warning, typed copies `var t = p.y`)."
category = "cyrius"
---
NAMED FLOAT TYPE (v6.2.19 for f64, v6.4.56 for f32):
  var pi: f64 = ...;        # local
  var e: f64;               # global
  var s: f32 = ...;         # single precision (v6.4.56)
A `: f64` / `: f32` annotation tags the var in the type table, so bare
operators on it lower to float ops instead of integer ops:

  ARITHMETIC  + - * /          -> EMIT_F64_BINOP   (x86 addsd/subsd/mulsd/
                                  divsd; aarch64 fadd/fsub/fmul/fdiv .d)
                               -> EMIT_F32_BINOP   (x86 addss/subss/mulss/
                                  divss; aarch64 fadd/fsub/fmul/fdiv s)
  COMPARISON  < > <= >= == !=   -> EF64_CMP / EF32_CMP  (real float compares —
                                  verified on negatives, NOT integer-bit order;
                                  ucomisd / ucomiss + the NaN parity fold)

Normal arithmetic precedence; the typed-operator dispatch reuses the
existing GESTYPE / SESTYPE infrastructure. Literals and the conversion
builtins propagate their result type, so `f64_from(n)` feeds straight
into operator expressions. A 3-byte-prefix collision once let `f64`
hijack the f64v2 / f64v4 SIMD types — the scalar f64/f32 checks run
AFTER the SIMD ones to disambiguate.

f32 SCALAR ARITHMETIC IS LIVE (v6.4.56 — this entry said "type-only /
no f32 arithmetic" until the v6.4.82 closeout; that was WRONG from
v6.4.56 onward):
    var a: f32 = f32_from(f64_from(3));
    var b: f32 = f32_from(f64_from(4));
    var s: f32 = a + b;            # addss / fadd s0,s0,s1
    if (a < b) { ... }             # ucomiss / fcmp — a REAL f32 compare
    var out = f64_to(f32_to(s));   # extract back to an i64 you can print
  A f32 is BOXED as its IEEE-754 bit pattern in the low 32 bits of the
  GP accumulator. There is no f32 literal syntax — CONSTRUCT with
  `f32_from(f64_from(int))`, EXTRACT with `f64_to(f32_to(x))`. Since
  6.7.6 a WRITE of an f64 value rounds: `var x: f32 = 1.5;` holds 1.5f
  (f32_writes_round_and_u128_arithmetic_v676; it stored the f64 bits).
  Complete on x86 / Win64 PE / Mach-O / aarch64 NEON (S-regs) / cx
  (widen-op-narrow). `f32_from` / `f32_to` (v6.2.18, cvtsd2ss /
  cvtss2sd / fcvt) remain the conversion pair.

SCALAR f64 AS A RETURN TYPE (v6.4.55) — returned in xmm0:
    fn half(x: f64): f64 { var two: f64 = f64_from(2); return x / two; }
  Return-type sentinel -9 (the SIMD band is <= -2048). The callee emits
  `movq xmm0, rax` before the epilogue, the caller `movq rax, xmm0` after
  the call and tags the result F64_TYID, so a bare operator on the call
  result dispatches to the f64 emitters. Real `movq` on x86/PE/Mach-O,
  no-op on aarch64/cx (both already return the bits in the GP reg).
  **`: f32` is NOT a legal return type** — the allow-list is
  i8/i16/i32/i64/Result/Option/Tagged/cstring/f64/f64v2/f64v4 + structs
  + the SIMD sentinels. Return an f32 as `i64` (it is already just bits).

FLOAT PARAMS + COMPOUND ASSIGN (v6.4.57): a `: f64` / `: f32` PARAM's
local slot is tagged too, so `fn inc(x: f64): f64 { ... x + one ... }`
computes instead of integer-adding the bit pattern; and `x += y`,
`-=`, `*=`, `/=` on a float local route through EMIT_F{64,32}_BINOP.

⚠ COMPOUND ASSIGNMENT ON A FLOAT GLOBAL AND IN A `for` STEP (6.6.11).
Until 6.6.11 only a float LOCAL got float arithmetic from `+= -= *= /=`:
    var G: f64 = 1.5;
    G += 1.0;          # 6.6.10: 9216616637413720064 (the two bit patterns
                       # added as integers); `G -= 0.5` and `G *= 2.0` gave 0
    for (var x: f64 = 0.0; x < 1.0; x += 0.25) { ... }   # 6.6.10: 2 passes, not 4
— silently, at top level and inside a fn, on x86 and aarch64 alike; an
f32 global was garbage the same way. The classic-`for` step was its own
integer-only copy of the compound arm and knew five of the ten compound
operators (`i <<= 1` as a step was "expected '='"). Since 6.6.11 the
statement and the step share one implementation (`_asg_compound_tok` /
`_asg_compound_load` / `_asg_compound_op`, parse.cyr): `+= -= *= /=`
on an f64 / f32 local OR global, in a statement, a top-level statement
or a `for` step, are float operations, and the RIGHT operand is checked
as `x = x op y` would be (`t += 1` on an f64 warns — 1's bits are a
subnormal; write `t += 1.0`). `%= &= |= ^= <<= >>=` are integer
operations on any variable, and a `for` step accepts all ten.

TYPECHECK — A WARNING, NOT AN ERROR (v6.4.56/.57): a plain `+ - * /` on
an f64/f32 value with a NON-float right operand warns on stderr:

    var a: f64 = f64_from(3);
    var b = a + 1;
    warning:<source>:2:14: f64 arithmetic with a non-f64 right operand

  This is the exact trap where `x + 1` silently means "add the
  denormal whose bits are 1", not "add 1.0". It stays a WARNING because
  an error would break the legal i64-boxed-float idiom (ADR-002). Gated
  on the default-on `CYRIUS_TYPE_CHECK`. The compare-mix warning
  (v6.4.57) suppresses literal 0, so `if (x > 0)` does not fire but
  `if (x > 5)` does.

  f32 GOT ITS OWN WARNING AT 6.6.11 (kind 5). An f32 `+ - * /` takes
  the LOW 32 BITS of its right operand, so an f64 literal or an integer
  there is reinterpreted, not converted — with `x: f32`, `x * 2.0` is 0,
  `x + 1.0` is `x`, and `f32_from(u) + 1.0` drops the 1.0 — and the f32
  arms never checked the right operand while the f64 arm beside them did:

    var x: f32 = f32_from(3.0);
    var y: f32 = x * 2.0;
    warning:<source>:3:25: f32 arithmetic with a non-f32 right operand

  Write `x * f32_from(2.0)`. An f64 literal is NOT narrowed for you.
  Warn-only, like kind 1; `CYRIUS_TYPE_CHECK=0` silences it. The one
  ecosystem site it found at landing (ranga, `one - ganita_f32_min(..)`)
  is a callee declared `: i64` that returns f32 bits — bind the call to
  a `var m: f32` first.

TYPED COPIES (6.6.11). A `var` with no annotation that is a pure COPY of
a declared float takes its type: `var t = p.y;` (an `f64` field, or a
chain `o.i.x`), `var u = y;` (an f64 / f32 local or parameter) and
`var g = G;` (a typed global) make `t`, `u`, `g` floats, so `t + t` is a
float add. Before 6.6.11 the copy stayed i64 and `t + t` added the bit
patterns (only `-t` noticed, as a warning). The rule is deliberately
NARROW — the initialiser must be exactly a name or a field chain, the
v6.5.21 rule `var x = f();` already applied to a declared `: f64`
return. A literal (`var z = 0.0;`), a float builtin (`var s =
f64_sqrt(u);`) and any arithmetic (`var t = p.y * 2.0;`) stay UNTYPED,
so existing `==` / `<` code does not silently become float compares.
Globals in the leading declaration block are judged by the same shape
(`= G;` or `= G.f;`). Across 1,652 ecosystem compiles no output binary
changed.

PARSER NOTE — an intrinsic call on EITHER side of a TERM-tier operator
(`a * f64_from(2)`, `f64_from(2) * a`, `x * f32_from(2.0)`) parses and
computes correctly; it was a parse ERROR until v6.4.83 (re-verified on
cycc 6.6.11). This entry said "cannot flank" until the 6.6.11 refresh —
see field_notes/language/parser_syntax.cyml
`float_bit_intrinsics_cannot_flank_term_operators` (a misnomer kept for
link stability).

Scalar f64 was usable before the named type (literals, f64_add/sub/mul/div,
f64_lt/gt/eq, f64_sqrt/abs/floor, int<->f64 f64_from / f64_to) — v6.2.19 is
the SUGAR letting you write `a + b` instead of `f64_add(a, b)` once `a`/`b`
are `: f64`. (CHANGELOG 6.2.18 / 6.2.19 / 6.4.55 / 6.4.56 / 6.4.57.)

6.7.11 (ARG-7): an ARGUMENT for an `f64` / `f32` parameter is a float slot for the kind-4 warning — `f(3)` warns
"an integer stored into an f64/f32 slot keeps its integer bits" (a call, a method, a tail call, `#inline`, a
named argument, an operator's right operand); the value keeps its bits (ADR-002: `f(3)` passes a subnormal,
about 1.5e-323). Write `3.0` or `f64_from(n)`. The class is recorded in pass 1 too, so a call ABOVE the
definition warns.
6.7.11 (ST-11, D41): ONE integer + f64 mix warns ONCE, however far its result is carried (`a + d + d` reports
the first link only).
6.7.16 (the user's D98, "ZA is f64, as in a fn"; SS-45): an UNTYPED GLOBAL FROM AN f64 CALL is an f64 at top level as
in a fn — `var Y = mkf(1.5);` with `mkf: f64`, in the declaration zone (typed at the end of pass 1, so every fn body,
later global and `kernel;` build sees it) and after the first statement — and a zone DESTRUCTURE binds its f64 elements
(`var ZA, ZB = mm3(1.5);` with `mm3: (f64, i64)`: ZA is an f64). The shapes a fn types count: a free, generic, wrapped,
known `&f` or method call, f64 arithmetic (`+ - * /`) on a leading call, a written or bound `(.., f64, ..)` element. A
comparison or `% & | ^ << >>` on it stays an integer. Through 6.7.15 both were untyped words holding the double's bits
(`Y * 2.0` read 0 and warned; the filed `f64_to(ZA * 2.0) + ZB` was 7 where 10 is right). ⚠ The single global goes
beyond D98's text (it answered the destructure). For the bits, `load64(&Y)` or declare `var Y: i64 = mkf(1.5);`.
And the saturating / checked `+| +? -| -? *| *?` give an integer whatever their right operand since 6.7.16 (D104) —
struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711.

[[entries]]
name = "closures_v637_v638"
description = "Closures: `|params| body` (and `|| body` thunk) as a first-class value; a closure referencing an enclosing local CAPTURES it BY VALUE at construction (heap env object). Invoke via callptr(f, args). Non-capturing closures stay bare fn pointers. Shipped v6.3.7 (non-capturing base) + v6.3.8 (lexical capture). DEFAULT-ON (not gated). PE: non-capturing only."
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:
    var add = |a, b| a + b;          # two-param closure
    var r   = callptr(add, 20, 22);  # -> 42

    var thunk = || 7;                # zero-param closure (|| is NOT
                                     # logical-or here — at a factor
                                     # position it's an empty-param
                                     # closure; `a || b` still parses
                                     # as short-circuit OR)

    var block = |x| { var t = x * 2; t + 1; };  # { block } body

CAPTURE (v6.3.8) — BY VALUE:
    var base = 100;
    var addbase = |x| x + base;      # captures `base` = 100
    base = 999;                      # does NOT change the closure's copy
    callptr(addbase, 1);             # -> 101, not 1000

    A closure that references a name failing FINDLOCAL in its own scope
    (and that isn't a global/fn) records it as a CAPTURE. A capturing
    closure emits a heap env object `[fn_ptr, cap0, cap1, …]`
    (`alloc((1+ncap)*8)`) — the closure VALUE is that object. So a
    capturing closure REQUIRES `include "lib/alloc.cyr"` + `alloc_init()`
    (ERR_MSG fails loud if alloc is unresolved). A NON-capturing closure
    is detected as such and stays a BARE fn pointer (no heap).

INVOCATION — callptr auto-dispatches:
    `callptr(f, args…)` is the canonical call. When the callee local
    carries the CLOSURE_TYID (0x40000003) var-type tag, callptr loads the
    fn pointer from `[obj]` and passes the object itself as the hidden
    first (env) argument — so the call site is IDENTICAL whether or not
    the closure captured. (A capturing closure fn gains an env parameter
    at arg-reg 0 / local slot 0; user params shift up by one — handled
    transparently.) fncallN also works for the bare-fn-pointer case.

SEMANTICS / IMPLEMENTATION:
    A closure is an anonymous fn emitted INLINE in the enclosing fn.
    v6.3.7 fixed the four bugs that made the scaffolded feature
    non-functional (param passing via stale regalloc, unpatched frame
    size, shared local-table clobbering, regalloc post-pass rewriting the
    inline code → SIGILL) — a fn that emits any closure skips the
    regalloc byte-scan post-pass (`_cur_fn_has_closure`).

PLATFORM: capturing closures run on Windows PE too since v6.4.26 (the
v6.3.8 fail-loud guard `capturing closures not yet supported on Windows
PE` is removed; PE routes them through `ECALLPTR_PE`, env as arg 0 →
rcx). Re-verified 6.6.20 under wine: a closure capturing a local returns
42. Capturing and non-capturing closures run on every target.

CROSS-REF: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`closure_inline_emit_shares_enclosing_fn_state` (v6.3.7 codegen bugs) +
`closure_lexical_capture_by_value_env_object` (v6.3.8 env object).

6.7.11: a captured name is not assignable in a closure — `x = v` / `x OP= v` on one is "'x' is captured by value; a
captured name is not assignable in a closure", one error at `x` (it was "undefined variable"); copy it into a local
(since 6.7.13 the fix-it reads `var x_l = x;`, RC-15). A captured struct's FIELD (`h.n += d`) writes the closure's own copy (ST-17, RET-10). Names the body
declares are its own (GF-14): a `for … in` variable (after its start bound or collection, for the loop only — a
range's END bound reads it), a local `const`, a destructure's second and third names (after the right-hand side).
A captured SLICE is a copy (GF-15): `s.len` / `s.ptr` read the captured copy, any other field is refused as on a
local, a store into the copy (`s.len = n`) is refused by name, `s[i]` stays refused.

6.7.13 (the user's D35 A; CHANGELOG [6.7.13] RC-12, RC-13, RC-14, VC-8):
  - A DECLARATION'S OWN NAME BINDS AFTER ITS TAIL: `var a = a + n;`, `var a: i64 = a + n;`, a classic
    `for (var a = a; ..)` header and a destructure's FIRST name read — and capture — the ENCLOSING `a` (a capture needs
    lib/alloc.cyr, as any capture does); after the declaration `a` is the closure's own. Through 6.7.12 `a` was
    shadowed too early: it read the new, uninitialised local — silently when a global had the name.
  - A NESTED closure literal's parameters are its own: `|n| { var g = |m| m + 1; .. }` captures no enclosing `m`.
  - A NARROW scalar local (`i8` .. `u32`) is captured as its VALUE, sign-extended for a signed type: a captured `i8`
    -3 reads -3 (it read 253 — the env copy took the slot's whole 8 bytes, stale upper bytes included).
  - A CAPTURED VECTOR follows the vector local's argument rules (D32): refused into another pointer kind, another
    vector's `*V` or a by-value struct; into its own `*V` it passes the CAPTURED COPY's address (a write reaches the
    copy, not the enclosing local). Into a VALUE-form parameter it was refused on every target in 6.7.13 (on Windows,
    where it ran through 6.7.12, too); since 6.7.14 it passes — below.
  - With a same-named GLOBAL, a write to a captured name (`y += d;`) compiled and wrote the GLOBAL through 6.7.13 while
    the closure's read saw the capture; refused since 6.7.14 — below.

6.7.14 (the user's D31 A; CHANGELOG [6.7.14] RC-1, RC-4, VC-4, VC-4r):
  - A WRITE TO A CAPTURED NAME IS REFUSED WHETHER OR NOT A GLOBAL HAS THE NAME (RC-1) — `y += d`, `y = ..`, a for init /
    step, `a, y = f()`, a nested closure: "'y' is captured by value; a captured name is not assignable in a closure —
    copy it into a local (`var y_l = y;`)". Through 6.7.13 a same-named global silently took the write (the write arms
    resolved local → global → capture). Inside a closure an enclosing local's name is the capture for reads AND
    writes; a closure that sees no enclosing `y` writes the global.
  - A CLOSURE'S PARAMETERS ARE UNTYPED WORDS (RC-4): `|m: i64| ..` is refused at the `:` by name, "a closure's
    parameters are untyped — drop `: T`", as a default is ("a closure's parameters take no defaults", 6.7.7). It was
    refused before too, but as "expected unknown, got ':'" and then the type parsed as the body ("undefined variable
    'i64'", five errors for a block body); beside an enclosing `m` the capture pre-scan read `m` as a capture.
  - A CAPTURED VECTOR PASSES A VALUE-FORM PARAMETER AS THE LOCAL DOES (D31, VC-4): `|k| lanes(b)` copies the capture
    where the argument stands; on Win64 it passes the env words' address, copied first when a later argument may
    write the capture. A capture of another class is refused with the local's words (an f32v4 capture into `x: f64v2`
    passes, as the local does — 16-byte class). The copy workaround (`var w: f64v2 = b; lanes(w)`) still works.
  - A closure's frame covers the temporaries an `#inline` call's arguments take (VC-4r): the closure sized its frame
    from GFLC alone, so `|k| li(lanes(b), k)` read 40 for 42 (x86_64; 139 on aarch64) and integer-only nested inline
    calls `li(k + 1, li(k, 2))` in a closure read 26 for 57 (x86_64 / PE) — older than 6.7.14.

6.7.16 (the user's D96, D3, D72; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] FB-1, GB-9 – GB-11, GB-13, SS-16):
  - A CAPTURED NAME SHADOWS A GLOBAL OF THE SAME NAME for `.field`, a field store and `.m()`, as the local shadows it
    outside the closure (D96, "Refuse it, as outside the closure"; FB-1): `var x = 5; var f = |k| { return x.a + k; };`
    beside a struct global `var x = Q { 7, 8 };` is refused "no struct type in scope for 'x'" — an untyped capture, an
    `i64` capture, an untyped `var x = &q;` capture and a capture of a capture alike. Through 6.7.15 the closure read or
    wrote the GLOBAL's field while `x` alone read the capture (exit 7). A struct-typed capture reads its own copy; a
    closure capturing no `x` reads the global.
  - A CAPTURED NAME IS THE VARIABLE IT CAPTURES for `.field`, a field store and `.m()` (GB-13): an untyped capture is "no
    struct type in scope for 'x'" as the untyped local is (it was "undefined variable 'x'"), a captured vector "SIMD
    vector has no named fields", and after a refused annotation the use stands down through any nesting. A bounded T's
    member on a CAPTURED receiver at a scalar T (`|| v.show()` at T = i64), and `self` captured in a trait default
    instantiated for a scalar, compile and call the bound's impl (they were "undefined variable 'v'").
  - A CLOSURE'S PARAMETER IS UNTYPED WHATEVER AN EARLIER FN LEFT IN ITS FRAME SLOT (GB-9, GB-10): through 6.7.15 `|x|
    x.b` after `fn h(w: *Q)` read through `*Q` (exit 139), and a capturing closure's first parameter took the enclosing
    fn's struct type (`|k| w.s.gs(k)` bound T = W). Now `x.b` is "no struct type in scope for 'x'" and `k` binds i64.
  - A CAPTURED f64 BINDS `T = f64` (D3, "Yes: binds f64, like the local it captures"; GB-11): `|| f64_to(gid(h) * 2.0)`
    with a captured f64 `h` runs the f64 instance (it ran the i64 base on the double's bits: 30 where 33 is right). A
    captured f32 still binds i64 (f32 is no type argument).
  - A NARROW PARAMETER'S CAPTURE is its narrowed value since 6.7.16 (D72, SS-16: the parameter is narrowed in the fn, so
    its capture copies the narrowed value — through 6.7.15 it copied the caller's whole word; the 6.7.13 bullet above
    covered narrow LOCALS only) — multi_width_types.

[[entries]]
name = "generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310"
description = "Generics via MONOMORPHIZATION: `fn f<T>(a: T): T`, `var x: Box<i32>`, generic structs + fns, scalar + struct type-args, explicit `f<i64>(x)` + inferred receivers. Shipped v6.3.9 (i64 fns) + v6.3.10 (non-i64 + structs + explicit syntax), engine finalized through v6.3.39. DEFAULT-ON as of v6.4.0 (the CYRIUS_MONOMORPH flip); `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0` opts out. Since 6.7.14: bracketed nested instance names, a checked type-argument vocabulary, signatures read at the definition, `p.m<T>(x)`, the pointer-parameter binding rule and the `**T` carrier. Since 6.7.15: a `>>` before a `,` leaves the `,` to the enclosing list, `Pt<i64>` on a non-generic type refused (D6), an explicit `Box<i64>` is the base struct, a scalar pointee binds through `p: *T` / `pp: **T` (D11), parentheses / a dereference / a capture / pointer ± n bind as the plain spelling (D12, D22, D23), `***T` carries T (D24), `x.m<..>(..)` is a method call everywhere, a `: T` word instance into a struct local is a handle (D13), diagnostics once per site. Since 6.7.16: three stub lines (`.field`, `return &n`, a struct result stored in or through a T value — D1, D99), `return &n` of a non-T name refused at the definition (D2), an untyped local of a T value takes T (D102), a captured f64 binds f64 (D3), `pp: **Box<T>` carries T, the base reads its signature at T = i64, type arguments only on a generic struct in every position (D7, D88, D93), at most as many arguments as parameters (D5) and at most two parameters (D8, D92), a list on a non-generic callee refused at its name, a generic operator fn binds as its call (D85 (a)), linear compile time and a ~300-link chain bound."
category = "cyrius"
---
STATUS — DEFAULT-ON as of v6.4.0:
    Generics compile WITHOUT any env flag. `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0` is the
    OPT-OUT (previously `=1` was the opt-in). The flip is byte-identical:
    cycc has no generic fns, so flag-on cycc == flag-off cycc (size
    UNCHANGED, 1,024,552 B). The precise per-fn signal that made the flip
    safe is GFTP(S, fi) (generic-fn-type-params; non-zero => generic); the
    flip decoupled monomorph from general inlining (_INLINE_OK) and
    narrowed the v6.3.29 frame-trim to generic fns only. Verified correct
    behind the flag at v6.3.39; v6.4.0 de-risked the default flip.

SYNTAX:
    fn id<T>(x: T): T { return x; }          # generic fn
    fn add<T>(a: T, b: T): T { return a + b; }
    var r = id<i64>(5);                      # explicit type argument
    var s = add<i32>(3, 4);                  # non-i64 instance
    var t = add(3, 4);                       # inferred (i64) receiver

    struct Box<T> { val: T; }                # generic struct
    var b: Box<i32> = Box<i32> { 7 };        # literal names its instance (6.6.12)
    struct Holder<T> { v: T; tag: i64; }
    var h: Holder<Point> = ...;              # struct type-argument
    id<i32>(4);                              # explicit call as a bare statement (6.6.12)

GENERIC STRUCT LITERALS (6.6.12): `Box<Point> { p, 5 }`, `Box<Point> { 1, 2, 5 }`,
`Pair<i32> { 40, 2 }` build the INSTANCE — in a fn, in the leading declaration block
and after the first top-level statement. Before 6.6.12 every one of them was
`undefined variable 'Box'` (the three literal heads committed only on `Name {`). It
is the instance an annotation names, so `var b: Box<Point> = Box<i64> { 1, 2 };` is
refused ("struct literal type does not match the declared type of this variable"),
and `Box { .. }` with no arguments is the base (all-i64) struct. See
struct_literals_generic_heads_and_nested_values_v6612.

EXPLICIT GENERIC CALL AS A STATEMENT (6.6.12): `id<i32>(4);` on its own was
`expected '=', got '<'` (in a fn and at top level) while the inferred `id(4);` and the
expression `var r = id<i32>(4);` worked — PARSE_STMT sent only `IDENT (` to the call
path. It now behaves exactly like the `IDENT (` statement: the result is dropped for
every return class, a `.field` / `.method()` on the result works, `#must_use` warns,
and a postfix `?` on a `Result`-returning generic propagates both halves
(`g<i32>(n, d)?;`). `a < b;` never reaches it (the lookahead needs a generic callee
and `name<..>(`). ⚠ STILL OPEN at 6.6.12 (backlog): the EXPRESSION form
`var v = g<i32>(n, d)?;` on a `Result`-returning generic exits 139 — the expression
side recognises a pair-returning callee only as `name (`, not `name<T>(` (and
`var t, p = g<i32>(n, d);` is refused: "multi-value destructure needs a call on the
right-hand side"). Use the statement form, or the INFERRED call, which reaches the
i64 instance and handles both (`var v = g(n, d)?;`, `var t, p = g(n, d);` — measured
right on 6.6.12).

HOW IT WORKS — instantiate-once:
    In an i64-everywhere language the BASE fn/struct IS its own i64
    instantiation (body emitted once with `T -> i64`). A non-i64 use mints
    `foo$ty` / `Box$ty` into the name pool (`$` separator), with FINDFN /
    FINDSTRUCT as the dedup index; on a miss the specialized instance is
    emitted by RE-INVOKING PARSE_FN_DEF / PARSE_STRUCT_DEF on the base's
    verbatim tokens with a concrete `T -> conc` binding override (full
    fidelity — params, ABI masks, struct args, return, regalloc — for
    free). Explicit `foo<…>(` is disambiguated from `a < b` via a
    non-consuming lookahead gated on the flag + a generic-fn callee.

INFERENCE (6.6.10): a call's T is inferred from the argument in the FIRST
parameter T types, wherever it sits (`fn g<T>(n, p: T)` -> `g(2, p)` binds
from `p`). A struct argument (local, by-value parameter, global, struct-
returning call) binds T to the struct on EVERY call path — expression,
`var r = g(p)`, `r = g(p);`, `b.v = g(p);`, `return g(p);`, an argument, a
struct receive — exactly as `g<Pt>(p)` does. Only a WHOLE argument binds T:
`g(p.y)`, `g(p.x + p.y)` and a `pp: *Pt` pointer parameter infer i64.
Before 6.6.10 only the inline replay inferred a struct (positionally, and it
then re-typed the param i64), so every other path ran the i64 base.

INFERENCE THROUGH A GENERIC STRUCT PARAMETER (6.7.8): `fn gx<T>(b: Box<T>)`
with `b: Box<Pt>` — `gx(b)` calls `gx$Pt`, as `gx<Pt>(b)` does (refused
before: the `Box` base ran). Slot-aware (`Pair<i64, T>` binds from slot 1;
`Pr<T, U>` binds both from one parameter); a scalar argument instantiates
(`Box<i32>` -> `gx$i32`); a `: T` return is received as the instance's
struct. The whole argument must BE the instance (a local, a by-value
parameter, a struct global, a call returning it). Through `*Box<T>` (the
user's fork F1 = (a)) T comes from what the argument points at — `&b`, a
`*Box<Pt>` name or call, a `Box<Pt>` value, a field chain whose leaf is
declared `*Box<Pt>` (`gx(h.bp)`): ⚠ those compiled before and ran the i64
base with `Box<i64>`'s layout (4 where 8 is right). An `x: T` parameter
still binds first; `Box<Pair<T>>` is not inferred (a generic call's own
result IS since 6.7.11, below); a POINTER passed to a by-value `b: Box<T>`
binds nothing (the base runs, as before); a plain `pp: *Pt` parameter keeps
the rule above. (A generic that reads its carrier's `T` field as a struct,
`b.v.y`, compiles since 6.7.11 — below.)

NO SCALAR INSTANCE (6.6.10): a generic that uses a T-typed value (param or
`var q: T` local) as a struct (`p.y`) has only a dead-stub base. A scalar call
(`g(5)`, `g<i64>(5)`, `g<i32>(5)`, tail included), `&g`, and a generic that
forwards its T into one (`outer(5)` with `outer<T>(p: T) { return g(p); }`, in
either definition order) are compile errors naming the fn. They all compiled
and returned 0 before. See field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
`generic_using_t_as_a_struct_has_no_i64_instance`. Since 6.7.16 two more kinds of line make a stub —
`return &n` of a T value, a struct result stored in or through one — and every stub refusal names its line in a
note (D1, D99 — the 6.7.16 block below).

SIGNATURE TYPES (6.6.10): `fn mk<T>(x: T): Box<T>` returns `Box<Pt>` from its
`Pt` instance; `fn f(r: Box<Pt>): Box<Pt>` takes and returns the instance,
`return mk(p);` / `return mk<Pt>(p);` included in the register-pair and retptr
classes (was sized as the base `Box`: `r.v.x` failed to parse). All-i64 args =
the base. An instance's `: T` return with T a struct returns that struct by
value, like a `: Pt` fn (`var q: Pt = id(p)`; it returned the address).

COVERED (x86 + aarch64 + PE + cx): generic fns over i64 + narrow scalars +
struct type-args; generic structs; explicit + inferred receivers; struct
type-arg on a generic fn (`wrap<Point>(pt)`, v6.3.39); multi-field generic
struct return/receive. INTENTIONALLY rejected (6.6.10): a struct type-arg beside a
second type-arg (`Two<Point,i64>`, or two inferred structs) — on every
call path, struct receives and assignments included. ⟳ No longer true of an
explicit two-parameter instance: `g2<i32, P>` (`g2$i32$P`), `g2<P<R>, i32>` and
`Two<P, R<i32>>` compile and run by 6.7.14 (GN-1, below).

CROSS-REF: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`generic_fn_i64_base_and_ret_stash` (v6.3.9) +
`generic_instantiate_once_reinvoke` (v6.3.10 engine).

6.7.11 — STRUCT SOURCES (ST-4): a generic call declared `: *T` with T bound to a struct (explicit, or inferred when
the arguments infer T = S) is a struct copy source like a `: *S` call (`l = gp<S>()` stored the word). An `x: *T`
parameter bound T = S from a `*S` LOCAL only through 6.7.13 (`&G` inferred i64); since 6.7.14 every struct pointer
binds its pointee (the user's D8 (a), GN-17 — below).

6.7.11 — INSTANCES, BOUNDS, METHOD INFERENCE, CARRIER FIELDS, NESTED CALLS (GF-1 .. GF-5, ARG-5r):
  - INSTANCE NAMING (GF-1): an instance is `Base$<frag>`; the scalar fragments are `i8` / `i16` / `i32` / `i64` / `f64`
    (f64 was a bare `i` through 6.7.10, colliding with a `struct i` — FINDFN found the first instance and the second
    call ran its body); a struct named exactly like a scalar is `Base$.<name>` (`gen$.f64`, `gen$.i32`); an i64
    instance is the bare base. A scalar type argument never shares an instance with a struct one. A two-parameter
    generic whose type argument is itself an instance (`g2<P<R>, i32>` vs `g2<P, R<i32>>`) minted one name for both,
    `g2$P$R$i32`, through 6.7.13 — the second call ran the first's body; bracketed since 6.7.14 (GN-1, below).
  - BOUNDS (GF-2): a bound is met at `f64` with only `impl Tr for f64`; a by-value bounded call (`x.show()`) is a
    trait use, never a struct use; the i64 base is judged on its own (an i64 call without the i64 impl is refused
    naming i64). A refused explicit instance gets ONE note naming the type the call binds.
  - METHOD INFERENCE (GF-3, GF-3r; D6): `p.m(x)` binds type parameters exactly as `P_m(p, x)` does — a struct
    argument of any size, a carrier, an f64; a type parameter on `self` binds the receiver (`p.me()` -> `P_me$P`).
    A scalar binding of a stub (a body using T as a struct) or of a bound-invalid base is refused naming the
    method's fn (`p.pick(9)` returned 0 silently through 6.7.10); a generic that forwards T into such a method is
    itself a stub — through a single name, a field chain (`h.p.pick(t)`, a `*T` field) or a call's result
    (`mk().pick(t)`, `h.gp().pick(t)`, a generic's plain `: S` result).
  - CARRIER FIELD (GF-4): a generic that reads its carrier's T field as a struct (`b.v.y`, `b: Box<T>` or
    `*Box<T>`, a `var x: Box<T>` local, any slot) has only the dead-stub base — it compiles, `gy(b)` calls `gy$Pt`,
    a scalar binding is refused. One field deep: `o.inner.v.y` through a `Box<T>` field needs
    `var t: T = o.inner.v;`.
  - A GENERIC CALL'S OWN RESULT (GF-5, D7): `gx(mk1(p))`, `g(mk1(p))`, nested, explicit inner — T is inferred from
    the instance the inner call reaches; a `: T` generic's struct instance binds (`g(id(p))` -> `g$Pt`).
  - THROUGH A WRAP OR A DEREFERENCE (ARG-5r): a generic struct carrier binds T through `(w)` and `*wp` as through
    `w` / `wp` — `gw((w))` / `gw(*wp)` call `gw$i32`, `gx((b))` / `gx(*bp)` call `gx$Pt` (through 6.7.10 the 8 B
    spellings ran the i64 base with the base's layout: a negative `i32` field tested non-negative). A tuple literal
    `((b, 3))` binds nothing.
  The coverage map names the instances (`gen$f64`, `gen$.f64`). Field notes:
  field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml generic_instances_inference_and_the_frontend_remainder_v6711. CHANGELOG [6.7.11].
6.7.13 (VC-3, VC-6): an EXPLICIT INSTANCE returning a vector (`mkg<i64>(..)`) is a call by name for every vector
destination — a declaration, an assignment, an unannotated `var v = mkg<i64>(..)`, wrapped, `return`, a `*V` parameter,
a value-form parameter. Through 6.7.12 the declaration silently dropped its vector annotation (a one-word local reading
0 / 154 / 195). 6.7.13 filed "a generic instance's `pp: **T` PARAMETER reads its pin without the binding" (`*pp + 1`
stepped 8, `take(**pp)` faulted); the cause was another — the instance pinned right, and the IMPLICIT call bound
T = i64 because `**T` carried nothing. Resolved in 6.7.14 (D40, VC-15, below).

6.7.14 (the user's D1 – D8, D40, each (a) / A; CHANGELOG [6.7.14] GN-1 .. GN-18, GN-5r, GN-14r, GN-18r, VC-15):
  - INSTANCE NAMES (GN-1, D1): a two-parameter instance whose type argument is itself a generic struct's instance
    brackets that fragment — `g2<P<R>, i32>` is `g2$<P$R>$i32`, `g2<P, R<i32>>` is `g2$P$<R$i32>` (through 6.7.13 both
    were `g2$P$R$i32` and the second call silently ran the first's body; a generic STRUCT minted the same way, so
    `Two<P, R<i32>>` took the other's layout). Every other name is unchanged (`g2$i32$P`, `Two$i64$i32`,
    `Box$Pair$i32`, `gen$P$R`); these are the names a diagnostic, the coverage dump and `cyrius syms` show — a script
    that greps instance names should allow `<` `>`.
  - THE TYPE-ARGUMENT VOCABULARY (GN-7, GN-8, GN-9; D2 (a), D3 (a)): a type argument is a struct, i8 / i16 / i32 / i64,
    f64, a type parameter in scope, or a generic struct instance `G<..>`. Refused, once, at the token, by what it is: a
    token that is no name ("a type argument is a type name, got number 1"; `u128` "cannot be a type argument"), an
    unknown `Name<..>` ("unknown type 'Nonexist' as a type argument"), a type parameter given arguments ("a type
    parameter takes no type arguments: 'T'"), the built-in `slice<..>` ("a slice cannot be a type argument"). Through
    6.7.13 such a spelling fell through to the i64 base silently, and a number could compile as whatever type name its
    value happened to point at (`Box<7>`). A refused explicit argument mints no instance and its call's arguments are
    not judged (GN-8). A NON-generic struct or a scalar given arguments, `Pt<i64>`, was still accepted with the
    arguments ignored through 6.7.14; refused since 6.7.15 (D6 — below).
  - SIGNATURES (GN-11, D6 (a)): a parameter's or return's `G<..>` reads its type arguments at the definition, called
    or not, as a local's annotation does — `fn gm(m: Mp<K, V>, b)` with K / V undeclared, `m: Box<Zork>`, `m: Box<u8>`
    are refused (the shapes a local already refused, `m: Box<u8>` among them, go beyond D6's text — kept by the user's
    6.7.15 D42); a generic's own `fn gm<K, V>(m: Mp<K, V>)` and a struct declared below the fn are fine. A multi-value
    element, a trait member's signature and a `*fn(..)` parameter type were still unread through 6.7.14; read since
    6.7.15 (D7 — below).
  - EXPLICIT TYPE ARGUMENTS ON A METHOD CALL (GN-16, D7 (a)): `p.id<f64>(h)` is `P_id<f64>(&p, h)` — it calls `P_id$f64`,
    and `var a = p.id<f64>(h);` is an f64; also on a chain, a `*P` receiver, a statement, a struct receive / return, a
    bounded member, and `p.pick<T>(t)` forwarding T in a generic. It is a call only when `id` is a generic method and NO
    field of the receiver's struct: a field of that name wins, so `p.n < k` is a comparison. The multi-value destructure
    lookahead (and 16 others) did not see it through 6.7.14 (`var a, b = p.mm<i64>(3);` was refused); since 6.7.15 it
    is a method call to every lookahead (GN-21 — below).
  - HOW T IS INFERRED FROM A POINTER (GN-17, D8 (a)) — the carrying parameter's shape decides. Through `p: *T` every
    struct pointer binds its pointee: `&G`, `&l`, a `*S` local, parameter (an untyped impl `self`), capture or global, a
    `*S` field (`w.p`, `wp.p`, `G.p`), a `: *S` call (a generic one at its instance) — `gsz(&GS)` runs `gsz$S` as
    `gsz<S>(&GS)` does (through 6.7.13 only a `*S` LOCAL bound, the rest ran the i64 base: `sizeof(T)` 8, a struct-using
    body refused as its stub, `var r: Pt = gd(gpp)` SIGSEGV). `&p` of a pointer binds nothing. Through `x: T` no pointer
    binds: a `*S` local infers i64 (it bound S since 6.6.17, against the guide) — a struct-using `g(q)` is refused
    (it exited 5; kept by the user's 6.7.15 D43, and the zone's `var G = gd(&GS);` refused "'gd$S' returns a struct by
    value" by D44); write `g<S>(q)`. A SCALAR pointee (`*i32`) through `p: *T` still inferred i64 through 6.7.14
    (pointer arithmetic inside the generic stepped 8 bytes); since 6.7.15 it binds the scalar (D11 — below).
  - THE `**T` CARRIER (D40, VC-15): a T that no `x: T` / `p: *T` parameter and no generic struct carries is carried by
    the FIRST parameter declared exactly `pp: **T`. It binds the struct a `**S` argument points through to — a `**S`
    local, parameter, capture or global, a field chain, a `: **S` call (a generic one at its instance), `&p` / `&G` of a
    `*S` — for free calls and generic methods alike, exactly as `st<S>(..)` does: `st(pps)` with `pps: **P3` runs
    `st$P3` (through 6.7.13 the i64 base: `*pp + 1` stepped 8, `take(**pp)` faulted). A direct or struct carrier wins.
    These bound nothing (i64) in 6.7.14: a `*S`, a `**i32`, a `***S`, and a parenthesised or computed argument —
    `st((pps))` / `st(pps + 0)` ran the i64 base (`tg((pps))`'s `take(**pp)` faulted). Since 6.7.15 the wrapped and
    computed forms bind (D22 / D23), a `**i32` binds i32 (D11) and a `***T` parameter carries T from a `***S` (D24) —
    below; a `*S` into `**T` still binds nothing.
  - STATUS & LIMITS (GN-4): an inline-replayed generic's (or `#inline` fn's) `return` is its own, never judged by the
    caller's struct-return class (`return GPA;` in a `: *T` generic replayed in a `fn r(): P2` was judged as P2's pair
    return).
  - `: *T` / `: *Box<T>` RETURNS (GN-5, GN-5r, GN-6): `var h: S = gpg<S>();` from a `: *T` generic is a HANDLE (the
    6.7.10 handle line), as is a generic returning an untyped value. A generic returning its own `: T` was not such a
    source through 6.7.14 (`var a: P3 = gid(5);` was refused at `a.z`); since 6.7.15 it is decided by what the reached
    instance returns (D13 — below). `fn mkp<T>(x: T): *Box<T>` compiles; `mkp<Pt>(p).n`, `var q: *Box<Pt> = mkp(p);`
    and `gp(mkp(p))` into a `*Box<T>` carrier work.
  - STUBS (GN-12): a T forwarded inside a struct carrier (`gx(mk1(x))`, `gx(b)` with `b: Box<T>`, `q.pick(b)`) into a
    stub generic makes the forwarder a stub; a bare T-typed value in a carrier position did not through 6.7.14 (it does
    since 6.7.15, GN-17 — below).
  - BOUNDS: a forward of T to a bounded generic whose i64 base the bound refuses (GN-14) and a bounded call on a
    carrier's T field (GN-15) — trait_bounds_v671.
  - DIAGNOSTICS (GN-18, GN-18r): an error inside a generic body, and its notes, print ONCE, not once per instance (the
    error emitters latch per token and message). The direct error writers outside util.cyr (an arity error) and every
    warning still printed once per instance through 6.7.14; once per site since 6.7.15 (GN-22, GN-23 — below).
  - TABLES (GN-3): no per-generic cap on struct carriers, T-typed names or bounded receivers (they were 64, dropping
    the 65th silently — the symptom was a parse error lines later).

6.7.15 (the user's D3 – D13, each (a), and D22 – D24, each A — 2026-10-18; CHANGELOG [6.7.15] GN-1 .. GN-25, GN-12r,
GN-15r, GN-20r, GN-25r; VC-14, VC-15; ST-4):
  - A `>>` / `>>>` BEFORE A `,` (GN-1, the release's High; a silent miscompile 6.3.33 – 6.7.14, every target): a nested
    type argument whose closing `>`s lex as one `>>` / `>>>` token and that is NOT the last argument of its list
    swallowed the next one — `g2<P<P<R>>, i16>` ran with B = i64 (the filed repro 8 for 2), `sizeof(Two<P<P<R>>, i16>)`
    was 24 for 18, and `fn f(a: Box<Box<Pt>>, b: i64)` was refused "unknown type 'b' as a type argument". The `,` now
    belongs to the enclosing list: B binds i16, `Two<P<P<R>>, i16>` is 18 B. A `> >` spelling is no longer needed. A
    program whose numbers came from the dropped argument changes.
  - TYPE ARGUMENTS ON A NAME THAT TAKES NONE (D6, GN-3): a NON-generic struct or a scalar given arguments is refused
    once, at the name — "'Pt' is not a generic struct: it takes no type arguments", "'i64' takes no type arguments" — at
    a local, a global, a `*Pt<..>` local, `sizeof`, a literal head, a parameter, a struct return, `Vec<Pt<..>>` and a
    type argument (`g<Pt<i32>>`, `g<i64<u8>>`). Through 6.7.14 the list was skipped unread (`Box<Pt<nosuch>>` never
    checked `nosuch`). ⟳ A pointer field `*Pt<i64>`, a trait member's parameter, a multi-value element, `*fn(Pt<i64>)`,
    `**Pt<i64>` and `*i32<u8>` still took the list unread through 6.7.15; refused since 6.7.16 (D7, D93 — below).
  - AN EXPLICIT ALL-i64 ARGUMENT IS THE BASE (GN-4): `gx<Box<i64>>(bx)` reaches `gx$Box`, as the annotation `Box<i64>`
    is the base `Box` (6.6.10) — it minted a second struct `Box$i64` and was refused "cannot pass 'bx' to a parameter of
    a different struct type". Instance names over `Box<i64>` lose the `$i64` (`gx$Box`, `Pair$Box$..`). A refused `dyn`
    type argument (`Box<dyn Show>`) is its one refusal: no `Box$dyn Show` instance is minted to cascade (GN-2).
  - THE REMAINING SIGNATURE POSITIONS READ THEIR TYPE ARGUMENTS (D7, GN-9): a multi-value element `fn mv(): (Box<Zork>,
    i64)`, a trait member's parameter or return, a `*fn(Box<Zork>): i64` type — "unknown type 'Zork' as a type argument
    (...)", once, used or not (every type-argument refusal applies, beyond D7's text — kept by 6.7.16's D37). A pointer field's list is read at
    the field (D8, pointer_fields_returns_and_linked_lists_v6617); trait members, traits_checked_v670.
  - A `var t: *T` LOCAL in an instance where T binds a struct is that struct's receiver (GN-5) — `t.y`, `t.y = 4`,
    `t.m()` — as the `p: *T` parameter is (it was "no struct type in scope for 't'"); a scalar T steps sizeof(T).
  - A SCALAR POINTEE BINDS (D11, GN-14): through `p: *T` a `*i32` / `*i16` / `*i8` binds that width and a `*f64` (or
    `&h` of an f64 local, parameter or global, `&i` of a signed narrow local or global) binds f64 — from a local,
    parameter, capture, global, `: *T` call or field chain; through `pp: **T` a `**i32` / `**i16` / `**f64` likewise;
    each call instantiates exactly as the explicit `second<i32>(pa)` does. `*u8` .. `*u64`, `*f32`, `*bool`, `*i64`, an
    enum pointer and a `***i32` through `**T` stay i64 (no type arguments); a narrow PARAMETER's `&i` binds i64
    (measured). Through 6.7.14 `second(pa)` with `pa: *i32` ran the i64 base — `p + 1` stepped 8 and `*p` read 8 (a[2]'s
    word for a[1]). ⚠ An untyped `var q = p + 1` inside the generic is an i64 word whose `*q` reads 8 bytes even in the
    i32 instance: write `var q: *T = p + 1`. Spell `g<i64>(..)` for the old base.
  - PARENTHESES, A DEREFERENCE, A CAPTURE BIND AS THE PLAIN SPELLING (D12, GN-15, GN-15r): through `x: T` / `p: *T`,
    parentheses around the WHOLE argument carry what they wrap — a struct (`gs((l))`, `gsz((&GS))`) AND a scalar
    (`g((h))` with `h: f64` is the f64 instance — kept by 6.7.16's D41); a whole dereference `gs(*p)` of a `*S` binds S through `x: T`; a
    captured struct binds S (through `p: *T` the capture's copy's address is passed — it was refused "not a pointer to
    its struct type"). Still i64: a pointer through `x: T` (`gs((p))`, `gs(*pp)` of a `**S`), an operator's operand
    (`gs(*p + 1)`), a tuple literal. A captured f64 through `x: T` bound i64 through 6.7.15; f64 since 6.7.16 (D3 —
    below).
  - WRAPPED AND COMPUTED POINTERS, DEEPER CARRIERS (D22 / D23 / D24, VC-14, VC-15): a pointer argument wrapped in
    parentheses, or a typed pointer plus or minus n, binds T as the pointer does — through `pp: **T` `tg((pps))`,
    `tg((&ps))`, `tg(pps + 0)`, `tg(0 + pps)`; through `p: *T` `g(ps + 1)`, `g(1 + ps)`, `g((ps) - 1)` (`p + 1` steps 24
    for a 24-byte S, it stepped 8), a `*i32`'s `pa + 1` binds i32. These bind nothing: `&s + n` (a byte pointer), a
    distance `ps - qs`, a bitwise result `ps + 1 & 3`; through `x: T` no pointer binds (6.7.14's D8). And `ppp: ***T` —
    any deeper `*..*T` — carries T one pointee down per star: `s3(ppps)` steps 24, `t3(ppps)` hands `take(***ppp)` the
    struct, `&pps` of a `**S` binds through `***T`, a `****T` binds from a `****S`, a `***i32` binds i32; a `**S` or
    `&ps` into `***T` binds nothing. Through 6.7.14 each ran the i64 base (`take(**pp)` faulted: 139, cx 254, Windows
    5). The ladder for a T with no direct carrier: (1) a struct carrier; else (2) the first `*..*T` parameter of two or
    more stars, read d − 2 pins down the argument's pointer-to-pointer pin chain. ⚠ A generic forwarding such a call's T
    into a method: `var r = gd(pp + 0); return r.pick(t);` through `p: *T` compiles; through `pp: **T` / `***T`,
    `gd2(ppp)` was refused naming the stub method through 6.7.15; it compiles since 6.7.16 (GB-16 — below).
  - `pp: **Box<T>` (ST-4; kept by 6.7.16's D54): a generic's `pp: **Box<T>` parameter compiles (it was "unknown type 'T' as a type argument"):
    its pin is read under the base's T → i64 or the instance's binding. The INFERRED `nb(&q)` with `q: *Box<Pt>` bound no
    T through 6.7.15 (the i64 base ran, at `Box<i64>`'s offsets); since 6.7.16 it runs `nb$Pt` (GB-15 — below).
  - `x.m<..>(..)` IS A METHOD CALL WHEREVER `x.m(..)` IS (GN-21; its refusals beyond the decisions' text kept by 6.7.16's
    D42): the 17 lookaheads that knew only `x . m (` —
    destructure, `?` on a `: stack` pair, vector receives, a dyn source, the multi-value refusals — take it.
    `var v = p.rs<i64>(3)?;` took the boxed `?` lowering and crashed (SIGSEGV) through 6.7.14. A field of that name
    still wins (`p.n < k >> (j)` compares).
  - A `: T` GENERIC INTO A STRUCT-ANNOTATED LOCAL (D13 (a), asked on its own; GN-25; its reach beyond D13's text kept by
    6.7.16's D43): `var a: S = gid(..)` with `fn
    gid<T>(x: T): T` is decided by what the reached instance RETURNS — a struct by value is received (a copy, `gid(p)`
    with `p: S`); a word (the i64 base, a scalar or f64 instance: `gid(5)`, `gid<i32>(5)`, `gid(w.v)`) makes `a` a
    HANDLE, as `var b: S = wid(5)` has been since 6.7.10 — `var a: P3 = gid(alloc(24)); a.z = 4;` writes the record,
    `gid(5)`'s `a.z` reads address 5 + 16 (the fault `wid(5)` gives), and `a` passed where another struct is expected
    is refused. Through 6.7.14 the annotation was dropped (`a.z` refused "no struct type in scope for 'a'", or `return
    a;` silently). Declare the local untyped to keep a plain word.
  - STUBS (GN-16, GN-17): a T-typed name (`x: T`, `p: *T`, `var y: T`) passed WHOLE into a stub generic's struct-carrier
    position makes the forwarder a stub — `fn outer<T>(x: T) { return gx(x); }` with `fn gx<T>(b: Box<T>)` reading
    `b.v.y`: `outer(b)` calls the `Box<Pt>` instances, `outer(5)` is refused naming `outer` at the call (it was refused
    at outer's definition, called or not). The stub scan's token binding follows the parser's pointer rule, so `var r =
    gd(&GP0); return r.pick(t);` compiles (it was refused naming the stub method).
  - DIAGNOSTICS ONCE PER SITE (GN-22, GN-23, GN-24): a warning in a generic or `#inline` body (typed-pointer, `match`,
    `#must_use`, nested `@unsafe`, an f64 / integer mix, the syscall-number warnings) and an error a direct writer
    prints (an arity error, a subscript of a subscript, a wrong-shape argument or return) print once per token and text,
    not once per instance; a different text at one token still prints each. A generic METHOD's refusals name it as the
    call spells it — "generic 'P.pick' has no i64 (or other scalar) instance", "generic 'P.bm': its type argument does
    not implement trait 'Show'" (it was 'P_pick'); the free spelling `P_pick(p, 9)` keeps `P_pick`.
  - Bounds and traits: trait_bounds_v671 (a carrier field's contract — D5; any forward depth), traits_checked_v670 (D3,
    D4, D7, D9), nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617 (a Vec element's bound — D10). Field notes:
    field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml and field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml, the 6.7.15 generics entries.

6.7.16 (the user's D1 – D10, 2026-10-19; D85 (a), D88 – D93, D99, D102, 2026-10-20; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] GB-1 .. GB-24,
GS-7 .. GS-18, FF-31, FF-32, FF-42, FF-45, FF-48, FF-49, FB-2, SS-41, SS-42, SS-46, SS-47, SB-1, RV-13):
  - NO SCALAR INSTANCE — THE STUB LINES (D1, GB-19; D99, SS-46, SS-46r; SS-42, SS-47): a generic whose body only works
    when T is a struct has only a dead-stub base. Three kinds of line make it one: a `.field` / `.m()` on a T-typed value
    (6.6.10); `return &n;` of a name typed by T in a fn returning a struct (`var n: T = x; return &n;` in a `: S8` fn;
    `n` a `p: *T` or an untyped `var n = x;` from one); a struct result over 8 bytes stored IN a name typed by T
    (`var y: T = mkp();`, `y = mkp();`, `p = mkp();` into a `*T` / `**T`) or THROUGH one (`*p = mkp();`, `**pp`,
    `*(p + 0)`, `p[0]`, a carrier's `*T` field). The result is read in every spelling the parse types — a call, a
    method, an operator or an operator chain, a known `&f`, a generic callee's `: T` / `: G<..>` / operator result at the
    binding its call makes, a wrapped receiver, a local from a generic call. A scalar call (`h(3)`, `g(5)`, `g<i32>(5)`),
    `&h` and a forwarder's scalar call are refused "generic 'h' has no i64 (or other scalar) instance", and every stub
    refusal, the `.field` one included, carries a `note:` naming the line that needs a struct. Through 6.7.15 the base
    was not judged: `h(3)` returned a dead slot's address, `g(5)` kept the struct's first word. Not such a line: a `: T`
    return, a closure's own `return &n`, an 8-byte struct or a `Str` result. Bind a field (`var y: T = mkp().a;`) to keep
    the body scalar-safe. (The genbind lane first left `*p = mkp();` unjudged, GB-19r; D99 made it a stub line.)
  - `return &n;` OF A NAME TYPED WITH NO TYPE PARAMETER (D2, GB-20) is refused at the generic's definition, called or
    not, with 6.7.15's D18 words — `var n = 5;`, `var n: i64`, `*i64`, `**S8`, a `k: i32` or untyped parameter,
    `var n = sizeof(T);` — in a parsed base and in a stub's skipped base, as in a plain fn.
  - AN UNTYPED LOCAL OF A T VALUE TAKES T (D102, FB-2): `fn g<T>(x: T): i64 { var y = x; return y.b; }` returns `q.b` from
    `g(q)`; at a scalar T, `y.b`, a field store, `y.m()`, `y = mk();`, a forward of `y` into a stub and a closure's `y.b`
    make the generic a stub (refused at the scalar call; through 6.7.15 "no struct type in scope for 'y'" at the
    definition). The value is the WHOLE initializer: `x`, `(x)`, another such local, or a generic call whose `: U` /
    `: G<..U..>` return binds U to one (`id(x)`, `idp(&x)`, `first(b)`, nested). ⚠ Beyond D102's text (on the filed
    keep-or-restore list): a by-value carrier `var y = b;` with `b: Box<T>` (or `mkbox(x)`) makes `y` a carrier. Still
    untyped: `var y = p;` of a `*T`, `*p`, `x.f`, `x + 1`, a non-generic call. ⚠ A T-typed parameter's name REBOUND by a
    closure's `|x|`, a `for x in` or a destructure is still read as T by `x.show()` / `x.b` there (a bounded call runs
    the impl on the word and crashes, every target — filed unplaced at the 6.7.16 close): rename the inner name.
  - A CAPTURED f64 BINDS T = f64 (D3, GB-11): `|| gid(h)` runs the f64 instance and is an f64 — a capture binds as the
    local it captures (a struct by D12, a pointer by D11). A captured f32 binds i64 (f32 is no type argument).
  - `pp: **G<..T..>` CARRIES T (GB-15, GB-16): read through its pointers as `pp: **T` is (6.7.14's D40, one pointee per
    extra star as D24) — `nb(&q)` with `q: *Box<Pt>` is `nb$Pt`; a `*Box<Pt>` into `**Box<T>` binds nothing. The stub
    scan reads a `**T` binding and a generic `: *U` call as the parser binds them: `var r = gd2(&GPP); return
    r.pick(t);` and `var r = gd(gpi(&GP0)); return r.pick(t);` compile (they were refused naming the stub method).
  - THE BASE READS ITS SIGNATURE AT T = i64 (GB-18): as its body reads the same annotation — `: G1<Box<T>>` is `G1$Box`,
    `b: Box<Pt>` is `Box$Pt` (a bare `: G1<T>` is still the base). `fn mkn<T>(v: T): G1<Box<T>> { var g: G1<Box<T>>;
    g.v.v = v; return g; }` compiles and `mkn<i32>(33)` returns 33 (refused at every call through 6.7.15: the stub scan
    read the middle `v` of `g.v.v` as the parameter `v` — a name after `.` is a field, never a parameter's use). ⚠ No
    decision covers it (on the filed list): the base `Box` / `Box<i64>` passed BY VALUE to the base's `b: Box<Pt>` is
    refused as another struct type; through a pointer it is D97's refusal (struct_argument_type_checked_v673).
  - TYPE ARGUMENTS ONLY ON A GENERIC STRUCT, IN EVERY POSITION (D7, GS-11, GS-12, GS-14; D93, FF-45): a pointer field (its
    pointee above or below), a by-value struct or union field, a trait member's type (parameter, return, `*T`, tuple
    element), a multi-value element, a `*fn( .. )` type (its list and its own return), a `**T` parameter, a slice
    element, a scalar return `: i64<u8>`, `*i32<u8>` as a local / global / return / field / element, and a tuple element
    `(i64<u8>, i64)` in a local, global, parameter or field — "'Pt' is not a generic struct: it takes no type arguments"
    / "'i32' takes no type arguments", once, silent under `--syntax-only`. An enum: tagged_unions_v58x (D88).
  - A LIST ON A CALLEE THAT IS NOT GENERIC (GB-6, FF-32, FF-31, RV-13) is one error at its name — "'nf' is not a generic
    fn: it takes no type arguments", "'Pt.m' is not a generic method: it takes no type arguments" — an intrinsic or a
    builtin too (`mulh64<i64>(2, 3)`, `sizeof<i64>(H)`, `load64<i64>(p)`, `syscall<i64>(..)`), the call parsed on; a
    name defined nowhere is "undefined function 'nofn'", once (`nofn<i64>(1)`, also as a statement and with `.f` / `[i]`
    after it; lint's `--syntax-only` pre-pass accepts it). `x.m<i64>(1)` on a receiver with no struct is `x.m(1)`'s one
    error. Through 6.7.15 two or three "undefined variable" / "expected ';', got '>'" errors. A list is read as type
    arguments only when every name in it is a type: `p.n < a, b > (c)` and `nofn<Nope>(1)` stay comparisons.
  - TYPE-ARGUMENT COUNT (D5, GS-7): a list may not be longer than its generic's parameter list — `Box<i64, i64>` for
    `struct Box<T>`, `g<i32, Pt>(5)` on `fn g<T>`: "'Box' takes 1 type argument, 2 given", once, at the first extra
    argument, in every position (through 6.7.15 the extra argument was read and dropped).
  - AT MOST TWO TYPE PARAMETERS (D8, GS-8; D92, FF-42): a third — `fn g3<A, B, C>`, `struct Three<A, B, C>`, a generic
    method, a bodiless trait member's own `fn g<A, B, C>(..);` — is "a third type parameter 'C': a generic takes at most
    two type parameters" at the declaration, under `--syntax-only` too; its uses say nothing more. Through 6.7.15 it
    bound i64 (`sizeof(C)` 8 for a 24-byte struct).
  - A SLICE ELEMENT MAY BE AN INSTANCE (D89, FF-48): `var e: [Box<i64>] = 0;`, `slice<Box<Pt>>`, `[*Box<Pt>]` declare, the
    element the instance at its size (it was "expected ']', got '<'"); slice_type_v58x for the widths a subscript reads.
  - MULTI-VALUE ELEMENTS BY INSTANCE (GB-22, GB-23): `fn mm2<V>(x: V): (V, i64)` at f64 returns `(f64, i64)` —
    `var a, b = mm2<f64>(h);` binds `a` as f64 (a narrower integer stays an i64 slot), the inferred method `p.mm(h)`
    included. ⚠ No decision covers it (on the filed list): `var t: (i64, i64) = mm2<f64>(h);` and `mm2<Pt>(p)` into
    `(i64, i64)` are refused (they compiled). Refusals spell the fn as written (`mt<f64>`, `Q.pair`, `(Box<Pt>, i64)`).
  - A `*Str` INTO `x: T` AT T = Str (GB-21): `gg<Str>(*p)` passes the header p points at, in every spelling (it passed
    the data word) — struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616.
  - A GENERIC OPERATOR FN BINDS AS ITS CALL (D85 (a), SS-41): with `fn P1_add<T>(l: P1, r: T): G1<T>`, `b + p` calls
    `P1_add$Pt` as `P1_add(b, p)` does — the left operand is argument 0, the right argument 1, only a whole operand binds
    — in a fn, the zone and after the first statement; a stub's scalar operator call and a failing bound are refused
    naming the operator fn. Through 6.7.15 the operator called the i64 base (`y.v` held p's address).
  - CHAINS (SB-1, SB-1r – SB-4r2): explicit and inferred generic methods are method calls to every lookahead on a chain
    too, and a generic STEP inside a chain is typed without instantiating it — `w.p.idv(q).m2()`, `w.p.gq<f64>(h).m2()`,
    a `: *T` step `w.gpx(&w).p.m2()`, a generic call head `gw(2).p.rs(3)` (ret2_rethi,
    stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555). A step whose argument is itself a generic call is still refused.
  - COMPILE TIME AND DEPTH (GB-1, GB-2): a signature's type parameter resolves without scanning the file, so compile time
    is linear in generic definitions (it was quadratic). A CALLED forwarding chain is bounded by the compiler's stack —
    about 300 links on Linux, macOS and aarch64, 290 on Windows (each instance is parsed inside the call that reaches
    it); past it one refusal, "nesting too deep (N generic instances, each parsed inside the call that reaches it, used
    over 6 MB of the compiler's stack)". Lexical nesting keeps its 256-level bound.
  - DIAGNOSTIC NAMES (GS-15, GS-17, GS-18): the bounded-forward refusal and its notes name a generic method `P.bk`; a
    generic's wrong-count `return` is ONE report naming `'gr'` (a method's `'P.mv'`) however many instances re-parse it.
  - ⚠ Filed unplaced at the 6.7.16 close (the user places them): a generic call's struct-typed FIELD or struct-returning
    METHOD argument binds T = i64 (`sz(w.p)` / `sz(b.mp2())` give `sizeof(T)` 8 for a 16-byte struct, `sz(q)` 16 — bind
    a local first); parameter and type-argument lists judged by position (`struct P<5>` compiles; `g<i32>(3)` is
    "undefined function" under `CYRIUS_MONOMORPH=0`); an instance's mangled name (`B$i32`) in 33 diagnostics; a refused
    explicit type-argument list's cascade; the rebound T name above. Bounds and traits: trait_bounds_v671 (D4, D102),
    traits_checked_v670 (D9, D91, D92), nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617 (D10). Field notes:
    field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml and field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml, the 6.7.16 entries.

[[entries]]
name = "async_await_futures_v6311"
description = "async/await: an `async fn` call builds a Future (does NOT run the body); `await` forces it. A plain Future runs its body ONCE (force-once memo, 6.6.10); an async fn that awaits in its body is a stackless COROUTINE (x86 family only). OPT-IN behind CYRIUS_ASYNC=1. Needs lib/alloc.cyr + lib/fnptr.cyr + lib/async.cyr. Shipped v6.3.11; coroutines v6.5.69; memo, arity, fall-off and await-value fixes 6.6.10."
category = "cyrius"
---
STATUS — GATED, default-OFF: behind `CYRIUS_ASYNC=1` in the COMPILER'S
environment (`CYRIUS_ASYNC=1 cyrius build …` passes it on; there is no
command-line flag). Default off → cycc self-host byte-identical; the tokens
are lexed unconditionally but the FEATURE is gated in pass 2.

SYNTAX:
    async fn work(n): i64 { return n * 2; }   # `async` before `fn`
    var fut = work(21);                       # builds a Future — does
                                              # NOT run the body
    var v   = await fut;                      # forces it -> 42
    var v2  = await work(21);                 # await a call directly

PLAIN FUTURES (an async fn with no `await` in its body), every backend:
    The call compiles to a CONSTRUCTOR that allocates
    `[ &f$impl, argc|256, args…, done, value ]` and returns its pointer;
    the body is a hidden `f$impl`. `await fut` = `future_force(fut)`: the
    FIRST force runs the impl (fncallN) and keeps the value, every later
    force answers from it (6.6.10 — before, each await re-ran the body).
    A hand-built `[ fp, argc, args… ]` (no marker) re-runs per force.
    0–8 parameters; 9+ is refused at the declaration, naming the fn
    (6.6.10 — it used to compile and exit 70 at the first force).
    Spawnable with `async_spawn_future`, driven by `async_run`.

COROUTINES (an async fn that awaits in its own body, v6.5.69) — x86_64
Linux, x86_64 macOS, PE only; aarch64 and cx refuse them by name:
    Locals live in a heap frame; the argc word is `1|512` (SELF is the one
    argument, whatever the declared arity — a 0-param coroutine SIGSEGV'd and
    a 9-param one exited 70 before 6.6.10). Each force resumes at the last
    `await` and runs to the next (answering 0) or to the end; a completed
    coroutine answers with its value and runs nothing (6.6.8). Falling off
    the end completes it with 0 and runs its defers (6.6.10 — it hung).
    `await e` INSIDE a coroutine (6.6.10 — it used to yield the suspend
    INDEX): e is evaluated and the coroutine suspends; at the next force
      - e a call to an `async fn`, or a bare variable → forced; `await`
        yields its value, re-suspending while a coroutine Future is still
        pending (pass 1 marks async fns, so a callee defined later counts);
      - anything else (a plain call, e.g. `await async_wait_fd(rt, fd)`,
        the park idiom) → e's own value.
    The test is on the operand's SHAPE; parentheses do not change it
    (`await (inner(a))` forces). A Future reached any other way — a field,
    `vec_get`, an index — yields its POINTER: bind it first
    (`var F = vec_get(futs, i); await F`).
    `await` may sit anywhere in an expression (`t = t + await f`,
    `add(1000, await g())`, `s += await g()`, `store64(p + 8, await g())`):
    the words the expression had pushed before the await are kept in the
    coroutine frame across the suspend (6.6.10 — they were lost, so
    `b + await five()` gave 5 and the store64 form SIGSEGV'd).
    Under the reactor (`async_spawn_future(rt, co(..))` + `async_run(rt)`, or
    `task_join(rt, h)`) a coroutine is driven to its value. One that PARKS
    (`await async_wait_fd(rt, fd)` / `async_wait_writable`) sleeps until its fd
    is ready; one whose `await` did not park (an `await` of a Future, or of a
    call that does not park) stays runnable and resumes on the next step, and
    while one does the Linux / macOS / Windows reactors still poll — without
    blocking — for the fds, timers and deadline sentinels other tasks are
    parked on, so neither side starves (6.6.11). ⚠ Until 6.6.11 every backend's
    `_async_step` marked a task that returned still-READY as DONE, so such a
    coroutine was finished with the 0 its suspend returned (measured: 0 where
    `future_force` gave 41), and this entry told you to force it yourself
    with future_force. That workaround is no longer needed.

REQUIRES: `include "lib/alloc.cyr"` + `lib/fnptr.cyr` + `lib/async.cyr`
(future_force / future_pending / async_spawn_future / async_run). The
constructor and lib/async.cyr ship in LOCKSTEP (the argc markers).
(6.6.11: the constructor lost a redundant leading skip-`jmp` that sat in
no fn's range — DCE's seed pass took it as a root for the NEXT fn, so the
fn defined right after an `async fn` was never reported dead. 5 B less per
async fn on x86.)

DEFERRED: async generic fns, value-form vector params, by-value struct
returns over 8 bytes (all refused by name).

CROSS-REF: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`async_await_futures_dce_nameread` (the DCE name-read landmine when a
modifier keyword precedes `fn`). Gate: cyrius
tests/gates/frontend/coroutine_fnptr_and_completion.sh.

6.7.11 (RET-9, D25): an `async fn` returning SEVERAL values is refused — at a `: (T1, T2[, T3])`
declaration and at `return (a, b);` / `ret2(a, b)` in an undeclared async body, plain or coroutine:
"`async fn g` returns 2 values, and a Future carries one i64 - return a pointer to a struct holding them"
(or split the fn). `ret2(a, b);` as an expression statement is still a multi-value return (it returns from
the fn it is written in). An async fn FORWARDING a multi-value call (`return h(..);`, h declared, counted, a method or a
generic; wrapped too) was the open case through 6.7.12; since 6.7.13 it is refused at the call with the same text, in
any async fn not declared multi-value — a `: i64`-declared one included (6.7.13's D24, MV-13; the declared case kept by
the user's 6.7.14 D49: a Future carries one i64 whatever is declared). And the value-form vector parameter refusal covers
`v: f32v8` too since 6.7.13 (VC-2: `f32v8` had no parameter class before).
6.7.14 (FE-22, FE-25; the user's D14 (a)): `#inline` on an `async fn` — a plain Future or a coroutine — warns
"#inline ignored: async fn 'f' runs through its Future" (its body is `f$impl`, reached only through the Future, so no
call can replay it); through 6.7.13 the directive did nothing and said nothing, or named another rule ("fn takes more
than 2 parameters"). A warning only: the binary is byte-identical. And an `async fn` may follow top-level statements,
as a `fn` may (`syscall(39); async fn af(): i64 { return 1; }` builds; it was "expected identifier, got reserved
keyword 'fn'") — relaxed_fn_ordering.
6.7.16 (the user's D72, SS-16): a NON-suspending `async fn`'s narrow parameter (`u8` .. `i32`) is narrowed in the fn as
a plain fn's is (multi_width_types). ⚠ A SUSPENDING `async fn`'s (a coroutine's) narrow parameter still holds the
caller's whole word — 98304 where D72 gives -32768 (filed unplaced at the 6.7.16 close).

[[entries]]
name = "reserved_word_class_toknames"
description = "Reserved words are a CLASS, not a short list: 79 builtin/intrinsic names (TOKNAME_BUILTIN in src/common/util.cyr) plus 28 tokens IS_KEYWORD_TOK enumerates itself (26 statement keywords + `f64_sqrt` / `callptr`) = 107 reserved token types at 6.6.20. Since v6.4.77 the parser NAMES the one you hit instead of saying `got unknown`; since 6.6.2 no token is shared, so it names exactly the word you wrote."
category = "cyrius"
---
THE RULE: you cannot use a builtin/intrinsic name as an identifier —
not as a `var`, not as a struct field, not as an fn name. That set is
**79 builtin/intrinsic names** (TOKNAME_BUILTIN: 67 at v6.4.77, 76 at
v6.6.1, 79 since 6.6.13's f64_le/f64_ge/f64_trunc) plus **28 tokens
`IS_KEYWORD_TOK` enumerates separately**: the 26 statement keywords
(`if`, `while`, `fn`, `enum`, `match`, `pub`/`public`, `private`,
`shared`, `in`, `default`, `object`, `stack`, …) and the two intrinsics
renumbered at 6.6.2, `f64_sqrt` (136) and `callptr` (137), which TOKNAME
names, not TOKNAME_BUILTIN = **107 reserved token types** (`pub` and
`public` share one). Derive, never quote:
`sed -n '/^fn TOKNAME_BUILTIN/,/^}/p' src/common/util.cyr | grep -cE 'if \(typ == [0-9]+\)'`
(79) and the same over `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` (28).

THE 79 IN TOKNAME_BUILTIN (families, not a hand-list — read the table):
    syscall
    load8 / load16 / load32 / load64
    store8 / store16 / store32 / store64
    the whole  f64_*  family   (f64_add/sub/mul/div/from/to/eq/lt/gt/
                                le/ge/neg/abs/floor/ceil/round/trunc/
                                sin/cos/exp/ln/atan …; f64_sqrt is
                                reserved too, via IS_KEYWORD_TOK)
    the whole  f64v_* / f32_* / f32v_* / f32v8_* / iv_*  families
    union · defer · secret · async · await · u128
    bitget / bitset / bitclr · ret2 / rethi

THE DIAGNOSTIC (v6.4.77) — it now names the collision:

    $ echo 'var iv_add = 1;' | cycc > /dev/null
    error:<source>:1:5: expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'iv_add'
        (cannot be used as an identifier; rename the variable/field/fn)
        var iv_add = 1;
            ^

Before v6.4.77, `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` and `TOKNAME` both stopped at token
111, so all 67 of these fell through to `expected identifier, got
unknown` — a syntactically clean identifier called "unknown". It cost
the **hisab** consumer its whole 312-assertion suite, found only by
bisection. `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` now derives its BUILTIN half from
`TOKNAME_BUILTIN`, so for the 79 builtin/intrinsic names the "is it
reserved?" set and the "what is it called?" set cannot drift. The other
28 tokens (the statement keywords plus `f64_sqrt` and `callptr`) are
still enumerated in `IS_KEYWORD_TOK` itself, separately from `TOKNAME`,
and those two lists CAN drift — util.cyr's own comment on
`IS_KEYWORD_TOK` says so ("Adding to one and not the other is exactly
the drift that note claims is impossible").

NO TOKEN IS SHARED SINCE 6.6.2: `f64_sqrt` is token 136 and `callptr`
137. They were 79 and 111, shared with `object` / `stack`, so a
collision used to print both spellings (and before v6.4.77 named the
WRONG one). Every collision now names the exact word: `var f64_sqrt =
1;` -> `reserved keyword 'f64_sqrt'` (re-verified 6.6.20, as are
`object`, `callptr`, `stack`). The one double SPELLING left is `pub` /
`public`, one token, printed `'pub'/'public'`.

6.7.x — THE STATEMENT-KEYWORD HALF GREW (the 107 above is the 6.6.x
count; derive it): `trait` (6.7.0), `const` (6.7.2), `true` / `false`
(6.7.3), `do` (6.7.5, token 172) and `dyn` (6.7.8, token 173 — the
checked trait-object type, dyn_v678); `loop`, like `kernel`, is
CONTEXTUAL (a keyword only as `loop {` at a statement's start). A new
reserved word is mirrored in the same change by `[embed]`'s reserved
list (cbt/manifest.cyr — its gate derives the set), the LSP's keyword
arms and the VS Code grammar.

6.7.14 (FE-1) — TOKNAME NAMES EVERY TOKEN THE LEXER EMITS: a new
`TOKNAME_OP` (called by TOKNAME before TOKNAME_BUILTIN) names the 24
operator and literal tokens that printed `got unknown` — `<=` `>=` `&`
`%` `|` `^` `~` `<<` `>>` `&&` `||` `...` `?` `@unsafe`, the nine
wrapping / saturating / checked operators (`+% +| +? -% -| -? *% *|
*?`) and a float literal: `var x <= 1;` is "expected '=', got '<='"
(it was "got unknown"), `var x 1.5;` "expected '=', got float literal".
Pinned by tests/gates/diagnostics/tokname_every_emitted_token.sh (it
derives the emitted set from the lexer and fails on any unnamed number).
The RESERVED set (TOKNAME_BUILTIN + IS_KEYWORD_TOK) is unchanged —
TOKNAME_OP is deliberately not in TOKNAME_BUILTIN, from which
IS_KEYWORD_TOK derives it.

6.7.16 — A RESERVED INTRINSIC IS NO STATEMENT AND TAKES NO TYPE
ARGUMENTS (the reserved set itself is unchanged: neither table moved).
`mulh64(2, 3);` / `sizeof(H);` alone is one error at the name, "the
value of 'mulh64(..)' is discarded - a statement is an assignment or a
call" (the user's D11, FE-3; it was "undefined function 'mulh64'"
twice, and under `--allow-undef` a binary that died with SIGILL);
`mulh64(2, 3).n = 5;` / `sizeof(H).n;` is "expected ';', got '.'"
alone (FF-3); a list on one — `mulh64<i64>(2, 3)`, `sizeof<i64>(H)`,
`load64<i64>(p)`, `syscall<i64>(..)` — is "'mulh64' is not a generic
fn: it takes no type arguments" (FF-32). `fncall0` .. `fncall8(..);`
stay calls. ⚠ Filed unplaced at the 6.7.16 close: a value builtin as
a bare statement — `load8(p);` compiles, `load16(p);` / `load32(p);` /
`load64(p);` / `f32_from(x);` / `f32_to(x);` are "unexpected".
CHANGELOG [6.7.16] FE-3, FF-3, FF-32.

DON'T EXTEND A LIST HERE. Earlier docs named three or four of these
("`secret`, `pub`, `shared` are reserved") — a partial observation
written down as a language rule. If you need to know whether a name is
reserved, either read `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` or just compile
`var <name> = 1;` and read the error. RENAME (`iv_add` → `ivadd`,
`f64_sqrt` → `fsqrt`) is the only fix; there is no escape syntax.

[[entries]]
name = "diagnostics_column_caret_multierror"
description = "Compiler diagnostics carry line:COLUMN + a source excerpt with a caret (v6.4.60), report MANY errors per compile with panic-mode recovery instead of stopping at the first (v6.4.62), emit NO output file when any error fired, and never hang on hostile input."
category = "cyrius"
---
WHAT AN ERROR LOOKS LIKE NOW (v6.4.60 + v6.4.62):

    error:<source>:1:5: expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'iv_add'
        (cannot be used as an identifier; rename the variable/field/fn)
        var iv_add = 1;
            ^

  `file:LINE:COLUMN`, then the source line, then a caret under the
  offending token. Warnings carry the same header:

    warning:<source>:2:14: f64 arithmetic with a non-f64 right operand

COLUMN SEMANTICS (v6.4.60): the column is the CURRENT parse token. For a
backward-looking check ("expected `;`") that is the token AFTER the one
you probably want — read the caret, not the number, in those cases. Tabs
count as ONE column. Implemented with ZERO new heap: the token byte
offset is packed into the HIGH 32 bits of the existing `tok_lines` entry.

MANY ERRORS PER COMPILE (v6.4.62): a syntax error no longer stops the
compile. The parser enters PANIC MODE, skips to the next statement
boundary, and keeps going, so one run reports the whole batch. Scope is
the `ERR_EXPECT` syntax class (346 sites); ~25 inline fatal errors still
fail fast on the first hit.

THREE GUARANTEES THAT COME WITH IT:
  1. **No output on error.** If ANY error fired, the compiler writes no
     binary (all forks, including cx). You can never accidentally run a
     stale/partial artifact — a 0-byte file is the failure signature.
  2. **It never hangs.** A `_had_error`-gated watchdog inside `PEEKT`
     aborts cleanly if the token index stalls past a bound far above any
     legal recursive-descent depth. On VALID input none of this
     machinery ever runs.
  3. **Truncated input terminates.** `PEEKT` clamps to EOF past the
     token count (v6.4.78) — a truncated file used to cascade 166,670
     error lines; it now prints 5.

PRACTICAL: pipe stderr through `head` when bringing up a new file — you
will get the real first error plus its neighbours, not one error per
edit-compile round trip.

6.7.14 — ONE MISTAKE, ONE ERROR (FE-7; diagnostics only, nothing that
compiled changes):
  - An UNCLOSED CALL no longer cascades into the next statement's header:
    `g(1, 2;` then a `for` gave "expected ')', got ';'", "expected '=',
    got '<'" and "undefined variable 'x'"; a missed `)` / `;` now never
    steps a `;`, `}`, end of file or statement keyword, and an unclosed
    list gets no arity verdict ("'g' expects 2 arguments, got 1") and no
    `syscall` arity warning.
  - A MISSING `}` with several blocks open locates every error at the end
    of file's real `<file>:line:col`, with its excerpt — no more
    `error:0:1:` (one clamp covers every emitter).
  - A CALL AS A CLASSIC-`for` STEP (`c.inc()`, `f()`) is "expected '=',
    got '.'" with a note naming the rule — "note: a for step is an
    assignment (`i = i + 1`, `i += 1`, `p.n += 1`); a call is not one -
    make the call in the loop body" (it added "undefined function 'inc'"
    twice, or a false "undefined variable 'f'").
  - A `const fn` body's unknown name is reported once (by its definition
    check), not again by the run-time compile of the same body.
  - In a generic body an error and its notes print once, not once per
    instance (GN-18; generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310).
CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-7.

6.7.16 — ONE MISTAKE, ONE ERROR, CONTINUED (diagnostics only unless named; CHANGELOG [6.7.16]):
  - A refusal made in sync (a subscript, `*p`) clears only its latch: the next statement is parsed and reports its own
    error (FE-1). An error in a block head's header no longer swallows the block's second statement (FF-6); a refused
    enum, struct, union or trait header and a refused signature over an empty body end at their own `}` (FF-12 – FF-14,
    FF-12r, FF-20); a `)` / `]` that closes nothing at the top level is its own error (FF-19); "too many struct
    definitions (max 1024)" is said once (FF-18). A body written without braces (`if (c) y = 1;`) and a fn signature
    with no body are one error each, the enclosing body keeping its `}` (FF-7, FF-44 — refused before and after;
    core.cyml `syntax`).
  - A field type's refusal and a type argument's name refusal are reported AT THE NAME (FF-35, FF-53), not at the `>`
    after it — a script matching columns from 6.7.15 moves.
  - Every bound-contract refusal of a compile prints (GB-3: pass 1's panic latch swallowed all but the first); a
    generic's wrong-count `return` is one report naming the generic (GS-17).
  - ⚠ Filed unplaced at the 6.7.16 close (the user places them): a statement missing its `;` before a `}` or a
    statement keyword loses what follows (the next fn is "defined inside" the last, later errors are never reported; a
    first fix was tried and reverted); diagnostics that print the compiler's internal position or name (columns past a
    `#derive` / macro expansion, the undefined-name caret one token late, a generic instance's mangled `B$i32` in 33
    diagnostics); one mistake, more errors or the wrong one (a refused explicit type-argument list cascades, a refused
    initializer leaves its name untyped, an untypable chain's destructure says "needs a call"); a declaration-zone
    initializer's error printed after a LATER line's pass-1 refusal (`var p = g(1 2);` then `const X;` prints 3:8 before
    2:13 — buffering diagnostics to source order is a day-plus redesign).

[[entries]]
name = "visibility_public_private_v650"
description = "private / public — file-scoped visibility for fns and global vars (v6.5.0; scoped at symbol insertion since v6.5.38; enforced on every resolution path incl. &fn, methods, struct receives since v6.6.4; forward references enforced and the whole file covered regardless of where the marker sits since v6.6.5)"
category = "cyrius"
---
SYNTAX:

    private                        # THIS FILE is private-by-default
    fn helper(): i64 { return 7; } # file-private
    var _state = 0;                # file-private (vars too)
    public fn api(): i64 {         # re-exposed
        return helper();           # in-file use is unrestricted
    }
    public var CONFIG = 7;

`pub` is a synonym for `public` — the same lexer token (73).

THE OPT-IN RULE — this is the part to internalise. A file with NO `private`
declaration behaves EXACTLY as it did before v6.5.0. Visibility is not
retrofitted onto the tree; you turn it on one file at a time. That is why the
entire ecosystem kept compiling on adoption day with a blast radius of zero.

SCOPE OF `private`: the FILE the keyword appears in. NOT the files it includes,
and NOT the file that includes it. Cyrius includes are textual, so this is the
distinction to be careful about — the boundary is the ORIGIN file each fn/var
was parsed from, tracked per-item, not the expanded translation unit.

VIOLATIONS ARE HARD ERRORS (not warnings):

    error:main.cyr:12:9: 'helper' is private to lib/thing.cyr
            var x = helper();
                ^

Reported through the multi-error path (v6.4.62), so ONE compile lists every
violation. Enforcement covers ordinary calls, TAIL calls, operator-overload
dispatch, and global-var reads — the var path enforces inside `FINDVAR`, which
is the single resolver every global read reaches.

PRIVATE SYMBOLS ARE SCOPED AT INSERTION (v6.5.38) — THE HALF THAT WAS MISSING.
Two files may each define a private `_helper`; each file's calls bind to its
OWN, and neither can capture the other's. A same-named PUBLIC fn stays
reachable from everywhere else.

    # a.cyr                            # b.cyr
    private                            fn _helper(x) { return 7; }
    fn _helper(x) { return 1; }        fn b_entry() { return _helper(0); }  # 7
    public fn a_entry() {
        return _helper(0);             # 1 — always a.cyr's own
    }

⛔ v6.5.0 THROUGH v6.5.37 DID NOT DO THIS, and the failure was silent. The fn
table held ONE entry per NAME, so b.cyr's definition overwrote a.cyr's —
INCLUDING for a.cyr's own internal calls — under a `duplicate fn ... last
definition wins` warning that reads as benign shadowing. `private` on BOTH
sides did not help: visibility was enforced only where a name was USED, layered
on an unchanged global symbol table. Filed from owl 1.4.7, where vyakarana and
sankoch each define a private `_stream_grow` that disagree about arity, buffer
offsets AND RETURN POLARITY — a wrong binding there does not fault, it reports
every successful buffer grow as a FAILURE, inside a tokenizer, on file content.
v6.5.37 closed only the arity-differing case (as a hard error); v6.5.38 closed
the same-arity half, which is the dangerous one precisely because no check can
see its shape.

MECHANISM: definitions SPLIT into separate symbols when a private one is
involved and the origin files differ. Resolution then prefers (1) a definition
private to the caller's own file, (2) the first non-private one, (3) the first
match — arm 3 exists so `_vis_check` still produces the precise "is private to
its file" error instead of a misleading "undefined function". The split is
flagged per-fn, so a name with one definition costs nothing extra to resolve.

UNCHANGED ON PURPOSE: duplicates between two NON-private files still only warn,
and two definitions in ONE file are still a duplicate (a redefinition of one
symbol, not a collision between two files).

VISIBILITY ALSO CONTROLS LINKAGE: a private fn is omitted from the exported
symbol table, so it disappears from `.dynstr` / `nm` and from
`cyrius api-surface`. Measured on adoption of `lib/regex.cyr`: exported
symbols 24 -> 14. The API surface a consumer sees is now the API surface you
declared, rather than every non-underscore fn in the file.

RELATION TO THE `_` PREFIX: the leading-underscore convention still suppresses
export and is unchanged. `private` is the enforced mechanism; `_` remains a
naming convention. They compose — a `_`-prefixed fn in a `private` file is
private for both reasons.

RESERVED WORDS: `public`, `pub` and `private` cannot be used as identifiers.
They are part of the `TOKNAME_BUILTIN` reserved CLASS (see the reserved-word
entry) — do not treat this as a three-name list.

EVERY RESOLUTION PATH, NOT JUST THE DIRECT CALL (v6.6.4): the boundary is
enforced per RESOLUTION SITE (fn side) — `_vis_check` is one helper, but each
path that resolves a user identifier through FINDFN and emits its own
call/fixup must call it. v6.5.0 wired three (ordinary call, tail call,
operator overloading). hisab found `&_helper` callable from any file through
callptr/fncallN; the sweep found EIGHT unchecked paths: `&fn`, `s.method()`,
the retptr (asv) and pair (asp) struct receives (`var s: T = f()` and the
inferred `var s = f()`), and four Win64-only SIMD receive/return paths. All
eleven now check; `grep -n '_vis_check(' src/frontend/` is the list. Two
same-class stamp gaps closed with it: a monomorphized instance inherits its
BASE's visibility (a `public fn pgen<T>` in a private file used to refuse
`pgen<i32>(42)` from another file while accepting `pgen(42)`), and top-level
ARRAYS are stamped (PARSE_GVAR_ARR returned before the "one point every global
var reaches", so `var _buf[N]` in a private file was never private). A
field access on a private global reports ONCE per line (it resolved its base
twice). Enum constants and type names carry no visibility — always public.
THE MARKER IS SCOPED TO ITS ITEM (v6.6.4): `_TL_VIS` arms `_pub_pending` only
when the next token can carry visibility — `var`/`secret`, `fn`/`async`, or a
fn-attribute directive (`_PUB_CAN_ARM`, a POSITIVE list). Before, it armed
unconditionally and only fn/var definitions cleared it, so `public enum E {..}`
(and bare `public struct` / `union` / `impl` / `use`, and `public var a[N]`)
in a private file silently re-exposed the NEXT fn or var — hisab's `_ad_pow`,
one leak among 457 probes (456 refused). Pass 2 had the same shape one level deeper: its
inline `var` skip re-armed at `public var` and the next PASS-2-ONLY definition
(an impl's first method, the first relaxed-ordering fn after top-level code)
inherited it — PARSE_IMPL and that path now clear a stale marker on entry.
`public impl` therefore marks NO method; per-method `public fn` is the form.
`public var a, b = f();` covers every name (it stamped b private). Derived
CODECS inherit the type's `public` as the v6.6.3 accessors did — closing the
leak would otherwise have made a public type's `T_to_json` unreachable — and
the once-per-unit `_cy_enum_name_eq` helper is emitted public (a second
private file's enum codec was refused when the first file was private).
Globals on the PARSE_PROG path (after the first top-level statement) are now
stamped; they were never private. Gate:
tests/gates/frontend/public_marker_scoped_to_its_item.sh (56 rows; the 6.6.3
compiler reds 30). PARSE_PROG itself clears a stale marker on entry too — the
first declaration after top-level code used to consume one left by a
`public var` above it.

FORWARD REFERENCES ARE CHECKED, AND THE MARKER'S POSITION NO LONGER MATTERS
(v6.6.5) — the last residual, and it was four holes rather than one. The
enforcement above is only as good as what PASS 1 STAMPED, and pass 1 did not
stamp: impl methods (all seven forks brace-skipped impl bodies), `mod`-scoped
fns (a `GMOD == 0` guard), every fn after the FIRST TOP-LEVEL STATEMENT (pass 1
stops there; PARSE_PROG keeps defining fns), and anything above the `private`
line (the flip was recorded as pass 1 walked past the marker, so an earlier
definition was prescanned while the file still read public — and a global above
the line was never private in either order). An unstamped callee has no flag
and no owner, so `_vis_check` returned at its FIRST test and a call that merely
came earlier in the concatenated stream reached it from any file.

⭐ THE SAME ROOT RAN THE OTHER WAY, IN ORDINARY INCLUDE ORDER, which is why
this is not a corner case: pass 1 kept one entry per NAME, so a second file's
same-named helper overwrote the first file's owner, arity and private bit
before either body was parsed — FALSE `is private to its file` and FALSE
`expects N arguments` on legal programs, and the two-file `_helper` example
above REFUSED when b.cyr was included first (the private bit was only ever set,
never cleared, so the PUBLIC fn inherited it). And with no pass-1 signature a
forward call passing a >8-byte struct took the mask-0 ABI: silent SIGSEGV on
x86, aarch64 and PE.

Pass 1 now stamps every definition kind and is the AUTHORITY: the file-level
mark happens once before either pass, the v6.5.38 identity rule runs in BOTH
passes from one place (so a same-named definition splits in pass 1, before any
forward call can bind), PARSE_FN_DEF finds the entry pass 1 gave THIS definition
by its own `fn` token and takes its bit, and the bit is CLEARED for a public
definition. Under it, `_vis_check` defers a reference whose callee has no owner
and no body and re-judges it at the end of PARSE_PROG — fail-closed-later, so a
future unstamped definition kind is reported rather than let through.
Gates: tests/gates/frontend/visibility_private.sh (per-path axes, now in BOTH
include orders), tests/gates/frontend/private_forward_reference.sh (7-fork
static parity + a forward-vs-backward refusal differential) and
tests/tcyr/crossos/forward_ref_abi_binding.tcyr (the ABI half, real hardware).

NO PER-ITEM `private`: `private fn h()` is a hard error naming the file-level
form (v6.5.56). ⚠ Until v6.6.5 it printed TWICE and privatised the file anyway,
so a legitimate sibling fn was then reported private at its caller.

6.7.11: the boundary covers CONST contexts too — `const X = pk();` calling another file's `private` const fn is
refused, as the run-time call always was (const_and_const_fn_v672).
6.7.14 (FE-2, FE-4): a const context's SKIPPED arm is a call path too — the right operand of a short-circuit (`const X =
1 || pk();`, `0 && pk() == 3`) and an if-expression's untaken arm naming another file's `private` const fn are refused
"'pk' is private to its file", once, at the call (they compiled — no decision was asked; kept by the user's 6.7.15
D45); a `#deprecated` const fn there warns once. And a
private call inside an `#inline` or generic body is reported ONCE PER CALL SITE, not once per replay (the body is
re-parsed per expansion / instance: `return w() + w();` with `#inline fn w(): i64 { return pk(); }` printed the line
three times). The verdict is unchanged.
6.7.16 (FF-26, FE-10, FE-11; diagnostics only, refused before and after): a per-item `private` in a fn body, a block
or after the first top-level statement is the ONE error "per-item `private` is not supported; put `private` alone on a
line to make the file private" (it added "unexpected private", and `private fn g` in a body "fn 'g' is defined inside
fn 'f'"); the marker is stepped, so a `var` / `const` after it still declares and a `fn` where a fn may be defined is
defined. A `pub` / `public` in a fn body or a block is "a `pub` declaration belongs to the declaration section at top
level, not inside a fn body", once (it was "unexpected pub'/'public"); a late `pub var x = 5;` at module scope still
declares `x`. ⚠ Filed unplaced at the 6.7.16 close: the FILE-level `private` after the first top-level statement is
still "unexpected private" and swallows the next error — put `private` at the top of the file. CHANGELOG [6.7.16].

[[entries]]
name = "threads_detached_and_is_done_v658"
description = "lib/thread.cyr — thread_create_detached / thread_is_done (v6.5.8), and why sys_exit_group (v6.5.6) is the epilogue once any thread exists"
category = "cyrius"
---
== THE EPILOGUE COMES FIRST (v6.5.6) ==

`sys_exit` is exit(2) — it ends the CALLING THREAD, not the process. A
program that has spawned any worker and exits through the idiomatic
`syscall(SYS_EXIT, r)` does not terminate: the main thread dies, the
workers keep running, and the process hangs with no diagnostic. Measured,
one token apart:

    syscall(SYS_EXIT, r);      # exit 124 (hung)
    sys_exit_group(r);         # exit 42

`sys_exit_group` is defined on all three syscall peer families (Linux
common routes SYS_EXIT_GROUP; Windows routes SYS_EXIT because
ExitProcess already ends every thread; agnos routes SYS_EXIT because `#0`
tears down the whole proc). `cyrius init` shipped the old epilogue in
seven templates — it only bites once a program grows its FIRST thread,
long after the epilogue was written, which is why nothing flagged it.

== FIRE-AND-FORGET WORKERS (v6.5.8) ==

    var ok = thread_create_detached(&worker, arg);   # 1 = spawned, 0 = failed

No handle is allocated at all: CLONE_PARENT_SETTID /
CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID exist so a joiner can wait on the tid word, and
nobody waits here, so both flags come off. The child unmaps its own
stack in the trampoline tail (musl's `__unmapself` shape), and the TLS
block is carved from the TOP of that same mapping so ONE munmap frees
stack and TLS together.

Measured: 100 unjoined threads leaked **210,124,800 B** of VA — exactly
100 × 2,101,248 — now **0**. Freeing only the 2 MiB and leaving the 4 KB
TLS behind would have been the same half-fix shape that cost v6.5.7 seven
found-by-ports defects.

== NON-BLOCKING COMPLETION PROBE (v6.5.8) ==

    var t = thread_create(&worker, arg);
    while (thread_is_done(t) == 0) { ... keep serving ... }
    thread_join(t);

`thread_is_done(t)` returns 1 = done, 0 = still running, -1 on a null
handle. It reads the same CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID slot `thread_join`'s loop
already tests, so it adds no state. It exists because `thread_join`
blocks unconditionally, which made a reaper — "collect whichever workers
have finished, keep serving the rest" — impossible to write without a
detached thread plus a side channel.

⛔ VALID ONLY BEFORE `thread_join`. Join CONSUMES the handle — on Windows
it CloseHandle()s it — so a post-join query inspects a closed handle and
reports "not done". Caught on real cass hardware by a test that asserted
the wrong contract. A detached thread has no handle by design, so it
cannot be probed this way.

== THE JOIN DEADLOCK THAT PROMPTED ALL OF THIS ==

`thread_join` used to read the tid word TWICE per iteration — once for
the loop condition, once as the `FUTEX_WAIT` expected value — under a
comment asserting the two were "in lockstep". They are the opposite: a
worker exiting between the loads let the kernel's one-shot
`CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID` wake fire with nobody parked, after which the
second load returned 0 and `FUTEX_WAIT(t, 0)` parked on an address that
would never be woken again. Permanent, silent, log-less; ~1 join in 150k;
SIGKILL the only recovery. Fixed v6.5.8 — one load now feeds both uses,
so the joiner can only park on a tid it positively observed live.

Gates: `tests/gates/concurrency/thread_join_single_load.sh`, plus the
`tests/tcyr/crossos/` corpus on real hardware (that corpus was selected by a
`vr01_` filename prefix until v6.5.11; it is the DIRECTORY now).

[[entries]]
name = "darwin_concurrency_syscall_wrappers_v6543"
description = "sys_bsdthread_* and sys_ulock_* — Darwin's threading and blocking primitives, routed on both Mach-O backends (v6.5.43)"
category = "cyrius"
---
macOS has neither `clone` nor `futex`. Real threading on Darwin is
`bsdthread_create` / `bsdthread_terminate` / `bsdthread_register`, and blocking
is `__ulock_wait` / `__ulock_wake`. v6.5.43 routes all five on BOTH Mach-O
backends and adds the peer wrappers:

    fn sys_bsdthread_register(threadstart, wqthread, flags,
                              stack_addr_hint, targetconc, dq_offset): i64
    fn sys_bsdthread_create(func, func_arg, stack_addr, pthread, flags): i64
    fn sys_bsdthread_terminate(stackaddr, freesize, port, sem): i64
    fn sys_ulock_wait(operation, addr, value, timeout_us): i64
    fn sys_ulock_wake(operation, addr, wake_value): i64

Source numbers are the v6.5.7 private-alias band (>= 1000, renumbered by
ESYSXLAT / EMACHO_SYSXLAT, spelled `1000 + the native number` so the alias
documents itself): 1360 -> 360, 1361 -> 361, 1366 -> 366, 1515 -> 515,
1516 -> 516 (and since 6.7.12 1329 -> 329, `__pthread_sigmask`, behind
`sys_sigprocmask` — sys_sigprocmask_on_macos_v6712). `UL_COMPARE_AND_WAIT` is operation 1; `ULF_WAKE_ALL` is 0x100;
`sys_ulock_wait`'s timeout is MICROSECONDS, 0 meaning forever.

⛔ THESE DO NOT MAKE A THREAD ON THEIR OWN. `bsdthread_register` must run once
before any `bsdthread_create`, and the created thread enters the REGISTERED
TRAMPOLINE with a Darwin-specific register layout — not the user fn. Phase 1
(v6.5.43) is routing and reachability only; the trampoline, stack mmap, real
create/join and the `__ulock` mutex are phase 2.

⚠ WHERE THEY LIVE, which is not where you would guess. `lib/syscalls.cyr`
selects `lib/syscalls_aarch64_linux.cyr` for aarch64-Linux AND for arm64-macOS;
`lib/syscalls_macos.cyr` is x86-macOS ONLY. So the arm64-macOS wrappers live in
the file named for Linux, guarded by `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS`, and real
aarch64-Linux — which has futex and clone — is untouched. That surprise is why
the first `kqueue` pass built on Intel-Mac and failed on ecb with
`undefined function sys_kqueue`.

⚠ `stack` IS A RESERVED KEYWORD, so `sys_bsdthread_create`'s third parameter is
`stack_addr`. See TOKNAME_BUILTIN — reserved words are a CLASS, not a list.

⚠ ARITIES COME FROM DARWIN'S `syscalls.master` and phase 2 is what confirms them
by execution. `bsdthread_register` takes 6 args in older SDKs and 7 in newer
ones (a trailing `tsd_offset`); the 6-arg form is used because cyrius `syscall()`
carries at most 6.

[[entries]]
name = "threads_concurrent_capability_v6544"
description = "THREADS_CONCURRENT — ask whether thread_create really produces a second thread, instead of assuming per target (v6.5.44)"
category = "cyrius"
---
Every cyrius target exposes the same threading surface (`thread_create` / `thread_join` /
`thread_create_detached` / `thread_is_done` / `gettid` / the `chan_*` family), but not every
target runs bodies on a SECOND thread. Two peers (x86-macOS, agnos) run them INLINE at `thread_create` —
correct results, serially. `THREADS_CONCURRENT` says which you have:

    1  Linux (real clone(2) threads)
    1  arm64-macOS  (real Darwin threads via __got[5] pthread_create, v6.5.44)
    1  Windows      (real CreateThread threads since v6.0.61; the flag read 0 until 6.6.16)
    0  x86-macOS    (static LC_UNIXTHREAD binary: no libSystem, no __got)
    0  agnos

For the channel, ask `CHAN_BLOCKING` (6.6.16, `chan_blocking_capability_v6616`): real threads
do not imply a blocking channel, and arm64 macOS and Windows had the first without the second
through 6.6.15.

Use it to guard anything whose CORRECTNESS depends on two things running at once — a
rendezvous, a producer/consumer handoff, a benchmark that claims parallel speedup:

    if (THREADS_CONCURRENT == 1) {
        # ... assertions that an inline backend cannot satisfy ...
    }

⚠ DO NOT GUARD ON THE TARGET NAME INSTEAD. A capability guard starts asserting the day a peer
becomes real; a `#ifdef CYRIUS_TARGET_MACOS` guard waits for a human to remember to remove it.
That is not hypothetical — macOS bodies ran inline from v6.5.11 to v6.5.43 and the whole
cross-host concurrency corpus was green throughout, because every assertion in it ("the worker
ran", "join returned 0", "the count is exact", "the mutex excluded") is honestly satisfiable by
an inline call. See the field note `a_corpus_that_cannot_tell_real_threads_from_inline_calls`.

⚠ A BACKEND CAN ALSO UNDER-REPORT (6.6.16 — the mirror image). Windows had real threads from
v6.0.61 but declared 0 from v6.5.44 to 6.6.15, and the "honesty" gate
(`macos_arm64_real_threads.sh` axis 6) hard-coded it in its serial list, so the gate ENFORCED
the wrong value. An honesty gate asserts each backend's value from its mechanism (clone /
pthread_create / CreateThread -> 1, inline `fncall1` -> 0), never from a frozen list.

⚠ A `0` PEER IS NOT BROKEN. Bodies run and results are correct; only the parallelism is
absent. Code that uses threads for THROUGHPUT still works there, just serially — so guard on
this only where concurrency is a correctness precondition, not everywhere threads appear.

[[entries]]
name = "chan_blocking_capability_v6616"
description = "CHAN_BLOCKING — ask whether chan_recv / chan_send block, instead of assuming it from the target or from THREADS_CONCURRENT (v6.6.16)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["threads", "channels", "chan", "capability", "macos", "windows", "v6.6.16"]
---
`THREADS_CONCURRENT` says whether `thread_create` starts a second thread. It does NOT say whether
the channel waits. Through 6.6.15 the two disagreed on arm64 macOS and Windows: real threads, but
a `chan_recv` that answered 0 on an empty channel and a `chan_send` that failed on a full one.
`CHAN_BLOCKING` answers the channel question, and every thread peer exports it:

    1  Linux        futex-parked ring
    1  arm64-macOS  __ulock-parked ring (6.6.16)
    1  Windows      ring whose waiters block on an I/O completion port (6.6.16)
    0  x86-macOS    serial ring: chan_recv answers 0 when empty, chan_send -1 when full
    0  agnos        serial ring, the same
    0  cx           one guest thread: a wait that only another thread could end spins

On 1 the ring is locked and shared safely by any number of producer and consumer threads
(MPMC — the Linux ring deadlocked with several producers AND consumers until 6.6.16, see
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml `one_futex_word_two_kinds_of_waiter_wake_one_strands_the_other_kind`).
chan_recv blocks until a value arrives and returns 0 once the channel is closed AND empty.
chan_send blocks while the ring is full and returns -1 once the channel is closed.
chan_try_send / chan_try_recv / chan_close keep one contract on every target.

A pool of workers that loops on `chan_recv` and treats 0 as "closed" needs CHAN_BLOCKING == 1.
Off it, serve inline:

    if (CHAN_BLOCKING == 1) {
        # spawn workers; each loops `v = chan_recv(ch); if (v == 0) break; ...`
    }
    if (CHAN_BLOCKING == 0) {
        # serve on this thread — a worker's first chan_recv would answer 0 at once
    }

⚠ DO NOT KEY THIS ON THE TARGET NAME, and do not key it on THREADS_CONCURRENT. sandhi 1.10.7
keyed its pooled server on CYRIUS_TARGET_LINUX because no capability existed. A target guard
stays wrong after the peer gains the behaviour. THREADS_CONCURRENT was 1 on arm64 macOS while its
channel did not block, so it was the wrong question.

[[entries]]
name = "stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555"
description = "enum Name: stack — payload variants that return (tag, payload) in registers instead of a 16-byte heap box (v6.5.55)"
category = "cyrius"
---
A payload-carrying enum variant normally **allocates**. `Ok(42)` is a 16-byte box from the
global bump allocator — tag at `+0`, payload at `+8` — and the only reclaim is `alloc_reset()`,
which invalidates every pointer the allocator has ever returned, so a long-running server can
never call it. 100 `sock_send` calls grow the heap 1600 bytes, permanently.

`: stack` on the declaration opts that variant family out:

```
enum Res: stack { Ok(v); Err(e); }

fn parse(x): i64 {
    if (x > 0) { return Ok(x * 2); }
    return Err(0 - x);
}

fn use(): i64 {
    var tag, val = parse(21);    # zero allocation
    if (tag == 0) { return val; }
    return 0 - val;
}
```

The constructor hands back `(tag, payload)` in the multi-return register pair — rax/rdx on
SysV and Win64, x0/x2 on aarch64 — and you read it with the destructuring bind the language
already had.

RULES, all enforced or diagnosed:
- **Zero or one field per payload variant.** The pair holds a tag and one value; `Pair(a, b)`
  in a `: stack` enum is a compile error (`stack enum variant takes 0 or 1 fields`), not a
  silently dropped field. A **nullary** variant — `None()` — carries no payload, so it needs no
  second register: it returns its tag alone and binds to a single variable. (Until v6.5.67 the
  rule read "exactly 1", which refused `enum Option: stack { None(); Some(v); }` outright.)
- **Bare variants are unchanged** — still integer constants, still sharing the discriminant
  numbering with the payload variants.
- **The destructuring bind works anywhere** (top level since v6.6.0).
- **A pair must be bound as a pair.** `var r = f();` (v6.5.67), `r = f();` and `store64(&slot, f());`
  (v6.6.0) are refused: *"a `: stack` enum returns two values — bind both: `var tag, val = f();`"*. The
  check follows the value through `return`, so a forwarding wrapper is caught too. A top-level
  `var v = f();` is refused too since 6.7.6. **Since 6.7.7 every one-slot store is** (user, 2026-10-09):
  `h.n = f();` (any field type — a struct-typed field took the tag as its first word; a slice's `.len`),
  `a[i] = f();`, `*p = f();`, every `OP=` (`x += f();` on a variable, field, element, `*p`, a u128), and a
  tuple-literal element `(f(7), 3)` — each compiled clean and kept the TAG — in a for step, at top level,
  and under `--syntax-only` for a field the file cannot see. `?` consumes the pair at each (`h.n = f()?;`
  stores the payload and propagates the Err); re-assign both with `t, v = f();` (6.7.7). Since 6.7.11 a
  STRUCT-LITERAL ELEMENT is refused too ("bind both"): `P { f(7), 3 }`, `P { a: f(7) }`, a global's
  initialiser in either zone, a nested field's leaves; `P { f(7)?, 3 }` stores the payload. By the user's
  choice (D22, 2026-10-13) a PLAIN ARGUMENT receives the TAG — `is_ok(f())`, `g(f())` — and an operand
  reads the tag (`f(7) == 0`, `f(7) + 1`): documented behaviour, not refused. Since 6.7.13 (D19, MV-8)
  PARENTHESES AROUND A WHOLE PAIR CALL ARE TRANSPARENT to every pair check: a wrapped one-slot store
  (`var v = (f(7));`, `P { (f(7)), 3 }`) is refused "bind both", `(f(7))?` propagates and `return (f(x));`
  forwards the pair — through 6.7.12 the wrap escaped every check and `(f(7))?` crashed. `g((f(7)))` still
  receives the tag (6.7.11's D22). Since 6.7.14 (D46, RC-21) PARENTHESES AROUND THE WHOLE VALUE CHANGE NOTHING
  either: a store whose whole value is parenthesised and STARTS with a pair call — `var v = (f(7) + 1);`, `h.a =
  (f(7) + 1);`, `x = (f(7) * 2);`, `((f(7) + 1))`, `((f(7)) + 1)` — at every store, both top-level zones, a for
  step, `x +=`, an element, `*p`, `store64`, a struct- or tuple-literal element and an if-expression branch is
  refused "bind both" as its unwrapped spelling is (through 6.7.13 it compiled and kept the tag). `x = (3 + f(7));`
  keeps the tag as `x = 3 + f(7);` does (the operand rule), `x = (f(7)? + 1);` propagates, and `(f(7) + 1) * 2` is
  no whole wrap. And `var a, b = (f(7));` destructures since 6.7.14 (D45, RC-20; ret2_rethi); a wrapped pair
  captured into a tuple (`var t: (i64, i64) = (sf(7));`) captures the tag and the payload, as unwrapped.
- **`?` propagates the pair** (v6.6.0), in expression position and as a statement; the Err path re-emits
  both halves. (v6.5.67 to 6.6.0 it was a diagnostic; before that it read the tag as a pointer.)
- **`f` is any call spelling** (v6.6.16): `f(..)`, an explicit generic `g<T>(..)` and a method `p.m(..)`
  behave the same for all three rules above. Before 6.6.16 the generic and method spellings were not
  recognised: `?` crashed (rc 139), the single binds dropped the payload silently, and their destructure was
  "multi-value destructure needs a call" (field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
  `pair_callee_any_spelling_6_6_16`).
- **A pair METHOD's `impl` may sit above or below its caller** (6.7.11, RET-6) — and since 6.7.13 (MV-2) a
  method the impl inherits from its trait's DEFAULT body may sit below too (through 6.7.12 `?` crashed and a
  single bind kept the tag there).
- **A fn that calls a one-statement pair generic** (replayed inline) is not a pair fn itself (6.7.13, MV-3 /
  MV-4: a `return` or `?` inside an inline replay belongs to the replayed callee, never to its caller).
- **A `return f(..)` inside a closure body is the closure's** (v6.6.16): it no longer marks the enclosing
  fn as a pair fn (`closure_returns_belong_to_the_closure_since_6_6_16`).
- **A fn that propagates a pair with `f(..)?` returns one WHEREVER IT IS DEFINED** (6.7.14, RC-15, RC-22): above or
  below its callers — impl methods, inherited trait defaults and dyn slots too. Through 6.7.13 a caller ABOVE
  `fn qb(x) { var v = parse(x)?; return v; }` took `qb(x)?` as the boxed `?` and dereferenced the returned tag (139 on
  x86_64 / aarch64, 10 on cx, 5 on PE), and `var v = qb(x);` kept the Err tag silently; now the forward `?` chain
  propagates the pair, the single bind is refused "bind both", and a dyn formed above an impl whose slot's only route
  is `?` is refused by 6.7.8's dyn pair check. A closure's own `?` in it — `|a| parse(a)?`, a block body — is the
  closure's, never the enclosing fn's. A `?` on a BOXED call stays unflagged.
- **A fn that returns a pair on one path and a single value on another WARNS** (v6.6.0): "`f` returns a `: stack`
  pair on another path but a SINGLE value here — the tag is dropped, so the caller reads this value as its TAG".
  Through 6.7.14 the walk judged depth-0 fns and closures only; since 6.7.15 (RC-8) an `impl` method and a trait
  default it inherits are judged as a fn is, named as written (`Pt.qm`): `impl Pt { fn qm(self) { if (self.x > 0) {
  return Ok(self.x); } return 5; } }` warns at `return 5;` (it built silent and `var t, v = p.qm();` read 5 as the
  tag). A method whose only pair route is `?` warns too, an override is judged as its own method, a generic method and
  an untyped `self` are judged; a default inherited by several impls warns once, at its own `return` in the trait,
  named as its trait's member, `Dv.qd` (6.7.16, GS-16; through 6.7.15 after the first impl that inherited it, `Pt.qd`);
  an override keeps its impl's name. A warning only. Return a variant on that path (`Err(x)`, `None()`).
- **A method ending a CHAIN is that call too** (6.7.16, SB-1, SB-1r – SB-4r2; no decision: a compiler failing valid
  cyrius): `var t, v = w.p.rs(3);` destructures, `w.p.rs(3)?` propagates (a statement `w.p.rs(0)?;` and `(w.p.rs(3))?`
  too) and a one-wide bind or store is refused "bind both" — on a field chain at any depth (an inline or `*P` field, a
  `*W` parameter, `self`, a global, a capture), a method's result `w.gp().rs(3)`, a call's `mk().p.rs(3)` /
  `callptr(&gw, &w).p.rs(3)`, a parenthesised head `(*pw).p.rs(3)`, after a generic step (`w.p.idv(q).rs(3)`) and on a
  generic call head (`gw(2).p.rs(3)`). Through 6.7.15 every asker typed a single-name receiver only: `?` took the boxed
  lowering (SIGSEGV) and `var v = w.p.rs(3);` kept the tag in silence. A forwarder of a chain's pair (`return
  w.p.rs(3);`, or a body whose only pair route is `w.p.rs(3)?`) is a pair wherever it is defined; a single name's
  explicit generic `p.rg<i64>(5)?` as a fn's only pair route makes it a pair to a caller above it too (SB-1r4; 6.7.16's
  D42 — "both spellings compile, refuse and run alike"); and the dropped-tag warning reads the chain's method
  (`return w.p.rs(4);` beside `return Err(2);` draws no false warning). ⚠ Filed unplaced at the 6.7.16 close: a `: stack`
  pair fn called through `callptr(&f, ..)` / `fncallN(&f, ..)` is no pair — `callptr(&f, 4)?` crashes on every target
  and `var v = callptr(&f, 4);` keeps the tag: call it by name.

⚠ **OPT-IN, AND THE DEFAULT DELIBERATELY DID NOT CHANGE.** The boxed layout is not an internal
detail; it is the documented representation. `?` reads `+8`, `lib/result.cyr`'s `is_ok` /
`result_unwrap` read `+0` and `+8`, and roughly 300 sites across the ecosystem do the same by
hand. A register pair read as a pointer is a plausible-looking address, so flipping the default
would have broken all of them SILENTLY.

📎 `stack` is not a new keyword — it is token 111, reserved since v5.5.36 for
`stack var buf[N]` ("lives on the call stack rather than being hoisted"), which is the same
meaning one level up. Introducing a `value` keyword instead would have broken any program using
`value` as an identifier.

[[entries]]
name = "inline_directive_v6563"
description = "`#inline` marks the next fn for the inline-replay path — opt-in, per function, and it WARNS when it cannot be honoured"
category = "cyrius"
---
`#inline` (v6.5.63) requests that the next `fn` definition be inlined at its call sites:

    #inline
    fn Pt_x(p) { return load64(p + 0); }

    #inline
    fn addk(a, b) { return a + b + 7; }

⚠ BEFORE v6.5.63 THIS DIRECTIVE DID NOT EXIST. `#` opens a comment in cyrius, so `#inline`
lexed as a comment and did nothing — silently. Consumers were already writing it: svara
carried four markers in `src/formant.cyr`, and its own `src/lod.cyr` recorded measuring one
at "+0.7% -- noise" — a measurement of an optimisation that was never there. If you are on
6.5.62 or earlier, your `#inline` markers are inert.

WHEN IT PAYS. Small, flat, hot functions — above all generated accessors. Measured on an
8-getter loop: 20.84 ms -> 6.04 ms, a 3.45x speedup, for +192 bytes of `.text`.

WHAT QUALIFIES. The request is honoured only for a function that is:
  - at most 2 parameters,
  - at most 32 body tokens,
  - free of control flow (`if` / `while` / `for`) and of `var` declarations,
  - free of struct/aggregate parameters (SIMD vector params are fine).

WHEN IT CANNOT BE HONOURED, YOU GET A WARNING naming the constraint:

    warning: #inline ignored: fn takes more than 2 parameters
    warning: #inline ignored: body exceeds 32 tokens
    warning: #inline ignored: struct/aggregate parameter
    warning: #inline ignored: body has control flow or a var declaration
    warning: #inline ignored: async fn 'f' runs through its Future        (6.7.14, FE-22)

That diagnostic is half the feature. A directive that silently does nothing is the exact
defect `#inline` was added to end, so a request that cannot be met says so rather than
quietly reverting to a call. A warning never changes behaviour: the function is still called
and still returns the right answer.

⚠ THE ARITY LIMIT IS STRUCTURAL, not a tunable. The inline record packs two 32-bit parameter
name offsets into one 64-bit word, so a third parameter has nowhere to go. Restructure to two
parameters (or a pointer) if you need the inline.

⚠ GENERAL INLINING IS NOT AVAILABLE. `_INLINE_OK` is 0 on every backend. Two source comments
justified that with "+25.6 % on cycc at v1.11.3" for about fifteen majors; re-measured at
v6.5.63 that figure is unquotable, because a compiler built with `_INLINE_OK = 1` does not
compile at all — it dies with "function exceeds 1023 jump targets (x86 LASE cap)". `#inline`
is the supported route, and being per-function it cannot drive any one function toward that
cap the way whole-program inlining does.

The async reason (6.7.14): an `async fn`'s body is compiled as `f$impl` and reached only through its Future, so no
call can replay it; the async reason wins over the other rules (a 3-parameter `#inline async fn` names it). Through
6.7.13 the directive on an async fn did nothing and said nothing (async_await_futures_v6311). CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-22.

THE REPLAY'S FRAME AND LATCH (6.7.15 — CHANGELOG [6.7.15] VC-1, VC-2; no decision needed): a frame temporary taken
while an `#inline` fn's arguments are evaluated (a u128 operation's 16-byte temp, the hidden temp that parks a `*S`
dereference before a struct operator) stays inside the caller's frame. Through 6.7.14 the replay released the local
count to its parameter slots and recorded only those in the frame's high-water, so the temp could lie past the frame,
over the pushed left operand: `id(a) + id(a + 1) + 2` with `a: u128 = 40` read 84 for 83 on x86_64, aarch64 and
Windows (cx was right), and its closure twin 43 or 84 — a silent wrong value. Frames of fns with such calls can grow
by a slot or two (no ABI change). And a refused argument in a call to an `#inline` fn (or a small fn the compiler
replays) leaves the panic latch its caller set: one refusal, as a non-inline call prints — the replay re-parsed the
callee's body as statements, whose end cleared the latch, so x86_64, aarch64 and cx printed the next statement's
refusal too (Windows, a real call, printed one).

RELATED: `#naked`, `#regalloc`, `#alloc` follow the same "attribute on the next fn" shape.


[[entries]]
name = "compile_time_assert_v6610"
description = "`#assert A OP B, \"msg\";` — a compile-time check over literals, sizeof(T) and enum constants (bare or qualified since v6.6.10)"
category = "cyrius"
---
`#assert` checks a compile-time fact and stops the build when it does not hold:

    enum Wire { HDR = 16; }
    struct Hdr { magic; len; }
    #assert sizeof(Hdr) == Wire.HDR, "Hdr must match the wire header";

Add a third field to `Hdr` and the build stops (exit 1, no output):

    error:<source>:3:34: #assert failed: Hdr must match the wire header

GRAMMAR — `#assert ATOM [OP ATOM] [, "message"];`
  - ATOM is ONE of: an integer literal, `sizeof(T)` (a scalar name or a struct), or an
    enum constant spelled `NAME` or `Enum.NAME`.
  - OP is `== != < > <= >=`; a lone atom asserts non-zero.
  - There is NO arithmetic inside an `#assert`; precompute the constant as its own enum
    variant. Only `,`, the message string, `;` or the end of the line may follow the
    operands, and anything else
    is refused: `#assert: expected `,` or `;` after the operands (no arithmetic inside
    #assert)`. ⚠ Before that check (v6.6.10) the rest of the line was skipped UNCHECKED, so
    `#assert E.EB * 2 == 9;` evaluated `E.EB` alone and a FALSE assertion compiled clean.
  - It works at top level (a declaration-phase directive — structs, enums and fns may follow
    it, wrapped or on one line) and inside a fn body. It emits no code.

⚠ BEFORE v6.6.10 ENUM CONSTANTS WERE REFUSED. `#assert EB == 4` and `#assert E.EB == 4`
failed with "expected number or sizeof()" on every backend, because #assert's evaluator
predates the enum-constant fold. The same release taught BOTH array-size parsers the qualified
form: `var buf[E.EB]`, `var a: i64[E.EB]` and the fn-local `var b[E.EB]` were refused while
`var buf[EB]` and `syscall(60, E.EB)` compiled. All three now go through one recogniser
(`_enum_atom_idx`, parse.cyr), so they cannot drift again.

⚠ `sizeof` MUST BE THE WHOLE WORD. The recogniser compared a 6-byte prefix, so
`#assert sizeofzz(P) == 16` compiled as if it said `sizeof` (v6.6.10 compares 7 bytes,
including the terminator). So must its TYPE (6.6.11): `#assert sizeof(i16v8) == 2` used to
PASS because the scalar ladder matched the `i16` prefix; `i16v8` and `i8zz` are now
`sizeof: unknown type` (see `sizeof_operator`).

⚠ `Enum.NAME` MUST NAME NAME'S OWN ENUM (6.6.11). `#assert Nope.EB == 2` is refused with
`'Nope' is not an enum`, and `E2.EB` with `'EB' is not a variant of 'E2'` — in #assert, in
array sizes and in expressions alike (one resolver; see `enum_namespacing`). Before 6.6.11 the
qualifier was ignored and any base compiled.

⚠ AN `#assert` WITH NO `;` ENDS AT THE END OF ITS LINE (6.6.11). It took the line of the
token at the cursor — already the NEXT line's — so

    fn f() {
        #assert 1 == 1
        return 42;
    }

dropped `return 42;` and f returned garbage, rc 0, on every backend. The message may still
wrap onto the next line (`#assert X,` then `"msg";` below it): a string at the cursor
continues the assert.

⚠ A FAILING `#assert` NO LONGER STOPS THE COMPILE ON THE SPOT (6.6.11). It exited during the
declaration phase, so an earlier syntax error in the same file (`var x = ;` above a failing
top-level assert) was never reported. Now each failing assert records its error and the parse
continues: every failing assert and every other error is printed, and no binary is written.
An assert inside an `#inline` body replayed per call reports once.

⚠ ONE MISTAKE, ONE ERROR. A bad operand is reported by name — "expected a number, sizeof(T)
or an enum constant" — and no longer followed by a message-less "#assert failed" for the same
mistake.

⚠ A VARIABLE IS NOT A CONSTANT. `var n = 4; #assert n == 4;` is refused, and a base that is a
variable (`#assert p.x == 0`) is a field access, not an enum variant — the same rule the
expression parser applies to `Enum.VARIANT`.

⚠ A REFUSED `#assert` ENDS AT ITS LINE (6.7.16, FF-5; diagnostics only, refused before and
after). With no `;` it ends in sync at its line, as any `#assert` does, so the next line's
statement is parsed and its own error reported (through 6.7.15 `#assert zz9 == 1` then
`syscall(60, zz);` reported zz9 alone). In a fn / block / impl body a `}` on the assert's line
closes the block (`{ #assert 1 == 1 }` added an invented "expected '}', got end of file"), and
`{ #assert 1 == 1, "m" }` is "#assert: expected `;` or a line break before `}`". A
`Color.NOPE` operand is "'NOPE' is not a variant of 'Color'" (FF-15). ⚠ Filed unplaced at the
6.7.16 close: an `#assert`'s TAIL after its message is still skipped unread — it can eat an
impl's method and drop the rest of the program; write nothing after an `#assert`'s message on its line.

RELATED: `enum` constants as array sizes (`var buf[Sz.BUF]`), `sizeof`, and
`tests/gates/frontend/assert_enum_constants.sh`.


[[entries]]
name = "string_literal_escapes_and_line_accounting_v6611"
description = "String-literal escapes are a CLOSED list: since cycc 6.6.11 any other byte after `\\` is `unknown string escape`; `\\` + newline KEEPS the newline (it is not a C line-splice); and a newline inside a string counts as a source line, so diagnostics after a multi-line string name the right line and file. Since 6.6.15 identical literals share storage and a literal differing in any byte (an embedded NUL included) never does"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE WHOLE ESCAPE LIST for string literals (char literals have their own, shorter ladder):

    \n  \r  \t  \0          LF, CR, TAB, NUL
    \\  \"  \'              backslash, double quote, single quote
    \a  \b  \f  \v          BEL, BS, FF, VT (v5.7.13)
    \x##                    one byte, exactly 2 hex digits (`\x1b`)
    \u####                  one BMP codepoint as 1-3 UTF-8 bytes, exactly 4 hex digits
    \u{1..6 hex}            1-4 UTF-8 bytes, up to U+10FFFF (D800-DFFF refused)
    \ + LF                  KEEPS the LF (0x0A)
    \ + CR LF               keeps BOTH bytes (0x0D 0x0A)

⚠ SINCE 6.6.11 ANY OTHER BYTE AFTER `\` IS A LEX ERROR, reported at the backslash:

    var s = "ab\q";
    error:<source>:1:12: unknown string escape

cycc 6.6.10 and earlier dropped the backslash and stored the byte, so `"ab\q"` compiled to
`abq` with no diagnostic (char literals already refused an unknown escape). A comment-,
char- and string-aware scan of every cyrius source under ~/Repos and ~/.cyrius found no
other escape in use when this landed, so nothing downstream broke. Write a literal
backslash as `\\`.

⚠ `\` AT THE END OF A LINE IS NOT A C LINE-SPLICE. It is an escape whose value is the
newline, so the string still contains it:

    var s = "a\
b";
    # s is 3 bytes: 'a', 0x0A, 'b'

That is the v6.5.18 contract `cyrius fmt` relies on when it wraps a long literal
(tests/gates/toolchain/cyrfmt_string_continuation.sh). If you want the two halves JOINED,
end the first half with `"` and start a second literal; do not reach for `\`.

⚠ A NEWLINE INSIDE A STRING IS A SOURCE LINE (6.6.11). LEX's string loop stored a raw LF
(and a `\` + LF) without bumping its line counter, so after any multi-line string every
later token was reported ONE LINE HIGH PER NEWLINE:

    var s = "a
    b
    c";
    var t = 1;
    var x = ;      # line 5 — 6.6.10 reported `error:<source>:3:9: unexpected ';'`

A wrong line number was the mild symptom. Line 1 of the NEXT `include` landed on that
include's own `#@file` marker line and printed a bare `error:4:12:` with no file name; and
`private`, which decides a token's FILE from its line, attributed code to the wrong file,
so a call to another file's private fn COMPILED and the privacy error (if any) landed on an
innocent name (the multi-line string attribution bug). The "diagnostics in backend/x86/fixup.cyr are one line high" report
was this defect, triggered by the single raw-newline literal in parse_expr.cyr. Since 6.6.11
both paths count the line; the string token itself keeps its OPENING line, matching the
column its diagnostic head prints.

WHAT YOU CAN DO WITH A NEWLINE IN SOURCE: raw newlines inside a literal are legal and stay
in the string (as does `\` + newline). Neither changes line numbering any more, so there is
no reason to avoid multi-line literals for diagnostic accuracy.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/lexer_errors_name_file_line.sh (axis 6: the line after a
multi-line string, the include's file name, and the `private` refusal of a cross-file call
that compiled on 6.6.10; axis 4: the escape rules). CROSS-REF:
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`file_attribution_is_a_line_lookup_so_the_lexer_line_counter_is_a_security_boundary`.

SHARED STORAGE (6.6.15). Identical literals share one copy (pointer-equal); a literal that
differs in ANY byte — an embedded NUL included — never shares. Until 6.6.15 a literal holding a
NUL could be aliased onto the tail of an earlier one and lose bytes to the next literal
(`"a\0a"` after `"a"` read `'z'` at offset 2 once `"zzzz"` followed), silently — so a project
rule of the form "no NUL bytes in string literals" (sankoch's ADR 004) is no longer needed.
Mechanism: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`an_intern_search_that_writes_the_candidate_before_searching_must_stop_at_the_committed_end`.


[[entries]]
name = "struct_value_copies_and_result_type_checks_v6611"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.11 a struct copied OUT of a struct-typed field (`q = b.v`, `var p: P3 = b.v`) is a whole byte-exact copy; a struct-valued call / method / operator result is type-checked against a struct destination at EVERY size (field, assignment, declaration; in a fn, at top level and in the leading declaration block); a typed `self: T` of 8 bytes or less is passed BY VALUE; and `var y: Q = x` from an address-passed struct parameter copies instead of aliasing the caller"
category = "cyrius"
---
Five shapes that each stored ONE WORD (or aliased) silently before 6.6.11, on x86, aarch64,
PE and cx alike. The 6.6.10 half (a struct copied INTO a field) is in
field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
`struct_field_types_checked_and_f64_fields_typed_since_6_6_10`.

1. A STRUCT VARIABLE TAKEN FROM A STRUCT-TYPED FIELD COPIES THE WHOLE STRUCT.

       struct Pt { x; y; }
       struct Box { v: Pt; n; }
       q = b.v;              # into a local, a global or a by-value param
       var p: Pt = b.v;      # p is its OWN copy — writing p.x leaves b.v alone

   Before 6.6.11 the assignment copied one word (`q.y` stayed stale) and the declaration
   stored that word into a slot typed `Pt` that was not inline, so the next `p.x`
   dereferenced it — rc 139 on x86 and aarch64, a page fault on PE, garbage on cx. A field of
   a DIFFERENT struct type is refused by name.

2. A TYPED `self: T` OF 8 BYTES OR LESS IS PASSED BY VALUE — `x.m()` passes `self` exactly as
   `T_m(x)` would.

       struct Odd { a: i8; b: i16; }          # 3 bytes
       fn Odd_sum(self: Odd) { return self.a + self.b; }
       x.sum();                               # 3, same as Odd_sum(x)

   Writing `self.a` inside such a method changes the method's copy, not `x`. Before 6.6.11 the
   dot form pushed `&x` for EVERY self, so a small typed self read its fields out of an
   address (-23905 / 11130 / 4047 on x86 / aarch64 / cx, want 3). Unchanged: an UNTYPED self
   (the `impl` form) is the receiver's address. A typed self OVER 8 bytes is address-passed —
   and since 6.6.16 copied on entry like every struct parameter, so it is a value too
   (struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616).

3. A STRUCT RESULT IS TYPE-CHECKED AGAINST A STRUCT DESTINATION AT EVERY SIZE. A call,
   method or operator returning a different struct is refused, whatever its size and whichever
   way it is stored:

       p = mkq();                 # cannot copy 'mkq' into a variable of a different
                                  #   struct/vector type: 'p'
       var z: Odd = y.same();     # fn return struct-id differs from declared var type
       h.o = y + y;               # cannot copy 'Odd_add' into a struct field of a
                                  #   different struct type: 'o'

   Before 6.6.11: a result of 8 bytes or less from a METHOD or OPERATOR was never checked (it
   arrives in rax untyped), and an ASSIGNMENT `p = mkq()` from a call returning a different
   struct compared the type only on some exits and otherwise fell to the scalar store — 24-,
   16- and 3-byte mismatches all compiled and stored one word. A generic whose inferred
   instance differs is refused too: `s = mk(r.v)` into a `Box<Pt>` infers `T = i64` (a FIELD
   argument is not a whole argument), so the call reaches `Box<i64>` — copy the field into a
   `Pt` first (`var p: Pt = r.v; s = mk(p);`).

4. `var y: Q = x;` FROM AN ADDRESS-PASSED BY-VALUE STRUCT PARAMETER COPIES. A by-value
   struct parameter over 8 bytes holds the caller's address; `y = x;` has copied through it
   since 6.6.6, but the DECLARATION bound `y` as a second pointer to the CALLER's struct, so
   `y.a = 9` changed the caller and `return y;` returned garbage. It is a copy now.

5. GLOBALS: THE LEADING DECLARATION BLOCK IS CHECKED LIKE EVERYTHING ELSE. Globals declared
   before the first top-level statement are replayed by a separate path (EMIT_GVAR_INITS), and
   that path had no struct checks at all:
     - `var G: W1<Pt> = ...;` there was typed as the generic BASE `W1` (`v: T` an i64), so
       `G.v.x` failed to parse and `w1s(G)` read the wrong storage. It is the `W1<Pt>`
       instance now, as after a statement.
     - a struct-valued call there — `var G: Pt = mkp(2);`, generic or not — built and
       SIGSEGV'd on `G.x`. It is refused as at any other top-level position: "'mkp' returns a
       struct by value, and a struct result needs storage in a fn's frame — call it inside a
       fn". A mismatched method / operator result into a struct global is refused too.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_field_value_copy.tcyr (18 new rows, cross-OS),
tests/gates/frontend/struct_result_type_refused.sh (30 refusals at 3 / 8 / 16 / 24 B plus 11
same-type acceptances, each against a field-by-field control),
tests/tcyr/crossos/generic_struct_inference.tcyr `run_globals`, and
tests/gates/frontend/generic_type_arg_unknown_refused.sh axis Q.

6. (6.7.11, RET-5) A MULTI-VALUE CALL IS NOT A STRUCT VALUE. Given where a named struct is expected —
   a by-value argument (`take(mk(3))`, `take(b.mk(3))`, a named argument), a struct-typed declaration in a
   fn or at top level, a struct field, an assignment / classic-`for` step — it is refused at EVERY struct
   size: "a multi-value call 'mk' is not a struct 'P' argument - bind it first: `var a, b = mk(..);`" (and
   "does not initialize struct 'P'", "is not stored into a struct 'P' field", "is not assigned to struct
   'P'"). A declared multi-value return is MULTIRET_SID and an undeclared one 0, so every destination fell
   to the value path and took the FIRST value as the struct's address. Exemptions: a `*P`, a `Str` handle
   and a scalar take the first value. A `: P` callee is a P (and its own `return (x, y);` is refused since
   6.7.11). Bind first: `var a, b = mk(..); var p = P { a, b };`.


[[entries]]
name = "typed_array_subscripts_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 an integer element-typed array `var a: T[N]` (T = i8..i64, u8..u64) takes a subscript: `a[i]`, `a[i] = v` and all ten `a[i] OP= v`, in a fn, at top level, in a `for` step and inside a closure. `a[i]` is exactly the element at `&a + i * sizeof(T)`, read sign- or zero-extended by T and stored truncated to T. There is NO bounds check and a fn-local array is NOT zero-initialised. A bare `var a[N]`, `stack var a[N]`, a scalar, a pointer, a u128 element and an f64 / f32 / bool / struct element are refused by name. (6.7.8: f64 / f32 / bool elements and `*T` subscripts ship — pointer_subscripts_v678 — and an opt-in mode checks bounds — bounds_checked_mode_v678.)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["arrays", "subscript", "typed-array", "element-width", "sign-extension", "v6.6.12"]
---
Before 6.6.12 the language had NO subscript, read or write: `return a[1];` was
`expected ';', got '['` and `a[i] = 5;` was `expected '=', got '['` — local or global, in a
fn or at top level — so `store64(&a + i * 8, v)` was the only spelling. (The roadmap item
"`var c: i64[4];` inside a fn is refused" was this same diagnostic misread as a declaration
error; the declaration always compiled.)

    var g: u32[3];                 # a global array (zeroed, like every global)
    fn f(): i64 {
        var t: i8[8];              # a fn-local array: NOT zeroed, see below
        var i = 0;
        while (i < 8) { t[i] = 0 - 120 - i; i = i + 1; }
        t[2] += 1;                 # all ten: += -= *= /= %= &= |= ^= <<= >>=
        var s = 0;
        for (var k = 0; k < 8; k = k + 1) { s = s + t[k]; }   # -987: i8 sign-extends
        var u: u8[4];
        u[0] = 300;                # stores 44: a store truncates to the element
        if (t[0]) { s = s + 1; }   # tests the ELEMENT
        return s;
    }
    g[2] = 7;                      # top level works too
    g[2] <<= 2;                    # 28

WHAT `a[i]` IS. The bytes `load16(&t + i * 2)` / `store16(&t + i * 2, v)` touch for an
`i16[N]`: the element at `&a + i * sizeof(T)`, read at T's width (i8/i16/i32 sign-extended,
u8/u16/u32 zero-extended) and written at T's width. The `load*` / `store*` idiom stays valid.
The index is any expression — a call, another subscript (`a[b[1]] + a[two()]`). A subscript
read carries T's width as the expression width, so storing an `i64` element into a narrower
slot draws the usual "narrowing assignment" warning.

⚠ NO BOUNDS CHECK. `a[N]` and `a[0 - 1]` compile and touch the neighbouring bytes, silently.
A `slice<T>` local keeps its own bounds-checked `s[i]` (slice_type_v58x), unchanged.
⟳ 6.7.8: a CONSTANT index out of range (`a[5]` on a `u8[5]`, `a[K]`, `a[0 - 1]`) warns in every build,
and the opt-in bounds-checked mode checks `a[i]` against the exact declared N at run time
(bounds_checked_mode_v678). Outside the mode a run-time index is still unchecked.

⚠ A FN-LOCAL ARRAY IS NOT ZERO-INITIALISED. Measured on 6.6.12: a fn fills its
`var b: i64[8]` with 77 and returns; a second fn reads its own `var a: i64[8]` before any
store, and `a[3]` is 77 — the first frame's leftovers. Globals are zeroed; a stack array is
whatever the stack held. Initialise before you read. (An array local over the per-fn frame
budget, about 120 KB, keeps STATIC storage and the compiler warns so.)

⚠ INSIDE A CAPTURING CLOSURE the subscript reads and writes the closure's captured COPY,
exactly as `&a` there does — captures are by value. Measured: `a[1] = 10`, then a closure
doing `a[1] = a[1] + 5; return a[1];` returns 15 while the enclosing fn's `a[1]` is still 10.

REFUSED BY NAME — one message, `cannot subscript 'a': ...`, that lists the reasons:

    var a[8];              # bare: no element width (bytes in a fn, slots at top level)
    stack var a[8];        # same
    var a: f64[4];         # f64 / f32 / bool / struct (Pt[4]) element: integer only
    var a = 5;             # a scalar
    var a: *i64 = p;       # a pointer: pointer subscripts are not in the language
    var a: u128[4];        # own message: a u128 element does not fit one register -
                           #   use load64/store64 on its two halves

For a bare array keep `load*` / `store*` at an explicit byte offset, or declare the width:
`var a: u8[N]` for bytes, `var a: i64[N]` for slots.
⟳ 6.7.8: the f64 / f32 / bool rows and the `*i64` row above COMPILE now (pointer_subscripts_v678), and
the one catch-all message is gone — each refusal names its own reason ("cannot subscript 'b': a bare
`var a[N]` states no element width - …", "… it is not a typed array, a *T or a slice local - …").

⚠ STILL OPEN at 6.6.12 (backlog): INSIDE A FN, `var a: f64[N]` AND `var a: Pt[N]` RESERVE
ONLY N BYTES. The fn-local path sizes a float or struct element array like a bare `var a[N]`
(the element type is not passed to it). Measured: two adjacent `var a: f64[4]; var b: f64[4];`
(or `Pt[4]`) sit 8 bytes apart, where two `i64[4]` sit 32 apart, so `store64(&a + 16, ..)`
writes into whatever follows. Top level sizes them N*8. Until it is fixed, spell a fn-local
float buffer `var a: i64[N]` (the same 8-byte slots) and move f64 bit patterns through
`load64` / `store64`, or alloc() it. ⟳ Measured fixed on 6.7.7 and 6.7.8: two adjacent fn-local
`f64[4]` sit 32 bytes apart, two `Pt[2]` 32 apart.

HOW IT IS BUILT. The declaration records a one-byte element descriptor (width 1/2/4/8/16,
0x40 = signed): a stack array keeps it in its depth word (`SLAEW` / `GLAEW`), a global — and a
fn-local array that fell back to static storage — in the per-var sign table as `0x100 | desc`,
a range disjoint from that table's scalar 0/1 (no heap-map change). `_arr_sub_addr`
(src/frontend/parse_expr.cyr) resolves the base exactly as `&a` does (a local, a closure
capture, a global) and computes the address; `_arr_sub_read`, `_arr_sub_stmt` and
`_arr_sub_assign` (shared with the classic-for step) load and store at the width, the compound
operators through the shared `_asg_compound_op`. Two compiler traps were closed on the way:
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml `flags_reflect_rax_is_emitter_state_so_a_load_after_an_eaddr_must_clear_it`
and the sixth instance of `any_pass_that_walks_or_moves_emitted_bytes_must_know_every_site_in_the_stream`.

BEHAVIOUR CHANGE: a global array redeclared with a different element type (`var a: u8[8];`
then `var a: i8[8];`) is now the "redeclared with a different type or size" error —
signedness is part of a global's shape, as it already was for scalars. 6.6.11 accepted it.

NOT PART OF THIS: `*T` pointer subscripts, slice writes, an opt-in bounds-checked mode — all three
shipped in 6.7.8 (pointer_subscripts_v678, slice_type_v58x, bounds_checked_mode_v678).

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/typed_array_subscript.tcyr (69 rows: every width, local and
global, canaries around every array, the ten compound operators, index expressions, `if` /
`while` on an element, the for step, a closure capture, top level; green on x86,
`CYRIUS_IR=3`, aarch64, PE, cx and the four real hosts) and
tests/gates/frontend/array_subscript_forms.sh (15 refusals, 4 typed controls). The guide's
"Subscripts" section is the reference.


[[entries]]
name = "struct_literals_generic_heads_and_nested_values_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 a positional struct literal fills the struct's LEAVES in declaration order at every nesting depth, each at its own offset and width (a nested field used to be flattened at 8 bytes per inner field and never descended, writing past the object); a nested struct field also takes a whole struct VALUE of its type; and a generic struct literal names its instance, `Box<Pt> { p, 5 }` — it was `undefined variable 'Box'`; since 6.7.14 a literal is an initializer only, and since 6.7.15 its elements are separated by commas (D1)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "struct-literal", "generics", "nested-structs", "silent-corruption", "v6.6.12"]
---
THREE THINGS ABOUT `Name { ... }`, all on every backend (the literal is frontend-only).

1. NESTED LEAVES ARE WRITTEN AT THEIR OWN OFFSET AND WIDTH.

       struct Odd { a: i8; b: i16; }            # 8 bytes with padding
       struct HO  { o: Odd; t: i8; u: i32; }
       var H = HO { 0x11, 0x2222, 0x33, 0x44444444 };

   Before 6.6.12 the literal flattened a nested struct field as ONE 8-BYTE STORE PER INNER
   FIELD, whatever the inner widths, and never recursed: that literal wrote at +0, +8, +16,
   +17 — 13 bytes past the object, over the next global or (fn-local) the caller's frame —
   and every field but the first read back wrong. Measured with a canary global declared right
   after `H`: 6.6.11 got `H.o.a` right and nothing else, canary included; 6.6.12 gets all five.
   A struct nested TWO levels deep was never descended at all, so it took the wrong NUMBER of
   values. An all-i64 nesting is byte-identical before and after, which is why cycc's own
   fixpoint never noticed.

2. A NESTED STRUCT FIELD TAKES A WHOLE STRUCT VALUE.

       struct Point { x; y; }
       struct Box   { v: Point; n; }
       struct Outer { t; b: Box; }
       var a = Point { 2, 3 };
       var o  = Outer { 1, a, 4 };          # a fills Box.v (Point is Box's FIRST field's type)
       var o2 = Outer { 5, mkp(7), 9 };     # a call returning Point, likewise
       var b  = Box { a, 7 };               # the direct case

   The value may be a local, a parameter, a global, a struct-typed field, a call, a method or
   an operator result; it is copied byte for byte. A value whose type is on the field's
   FIRST-field chain fills that inner struct; a struct of any other type is refused by name
   ("cannot copy 'q' into a struct field of a different struct type: 'p'"). At TOP LEVEL a
   call returning a struct has no frame to land in and is refused by name ("'mkp' returns a
   struct by value, and a struct result needs storage in a fn's frame — call it inside a
   fn"), as the ONLY error. Before 6.6.12 `B2 { p, 5 }` with `p: Pt` was `unexpected '}'`;
   only the flattened `B2 { 1, 2, 5 }` worked.

3. A GENERIC STRUCT LITERAL NAMES ITS INSTANCE.

       struct Box<T> { v: T; n; }
       var G = Box<Pt> { 1, 2, 5 };                   # leading declaration block
       fn f() { var b = Box<Pt> { p, 7 }; var c: Box<i64> = Box<i64> { 9, 6 }; }

   All three literal heads (PARSE_VAR, the pass-1 global lookahead and the leading-block
   replay) committed only on `Name {`, so every form above was `undefined variable 'Box'` on
   6.6.11. One recogniser, `_lit_head`, now resolves the instance with the annotation's own
   resolver, so `var b: Box<Pt> = Box<i64> { .. }` is refused ("struct literal type does not
   match the declared type of this variable"). The leading declaration block now compares a
   literal with its annotation at all — `var G: Pt = Q { 1, 2 };` compiled on 6.6.11.

4. A STRUCT LITERAL IS AN INITIALIZER ONLY (6.7.14 — the user's D12 (a); CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-24, FE-14).

       var t: P = P { 5, 6 };          # the one place a literal goes (a fn, either top-level zone)
       var q: P = (P { 5, 6 });        # parentheses around the WHOLE initializer are transparent
       G = t;  f(t);                   # assign or pass the variable

   Anywhere else — an assignment's right side (`G = P { 5 };`, at top level too), an argument
   (`f(P { 5, 6 })`), an operand (`1 + P { .. }`), a selector after it (`P { 5 }.a`), a nested
   literal element (`W { P { 2 } }`), `return P { .. };` in every size class, a statement
   `P { 5 };`, a generic head (`G = Box<i64> { 5, 6 };`) — it is ONE error at the struct's name:
   "a struct literal is an initializer: declare `var t: P = P { .. };` and assign or pass t".
   Through 6.7.13 each was "undefined variable 'P' (missing include or enum?)" plus two to five
   invented errors. A generic or `#inline` body reports it once; `--syntax-only` reports it too.
   The wrapped initializer (any depth; positional, named, generic) is byte-identical to the
   unwrapped form — it was "undefined variable 'P'" + "expected ')'". Nothing that compiled
   changes. A NAMED-field literal (`var p = P { a: 1, b: 2 };`) initialises a DECLARATION-ZONE
   global too since 6.7.14 (FE-14; it was refused there) — see
   declaration_zone_constant_initializers_v6714. Since 6.7.15 (FE-7) an UNCLOSED wrap around
   a declaration's whole literal (`var q: P = (P { 5, 6 };`, a double wrap one `)` short, a
   generic or named literal, either zone) is the one error "expected ')', got ';'" — the
   initializer rule, which the source did not break, is no longer reported first. An operand
   (`var t = (P { 5, 6 } + 1;`) or an assignment (`t = (P { 5, 6 };`) keeps both errors.

5. THE ELEMENTS ARE SEPARATED BY COMMAS (6.7.15 — the user's D1 (a), 2026-10-18; CHANGELOG
   [6.7.15] FE-8; a language change).

       var p = P { 3, 4 };            # positional
       var q = P { b: 1, a: 2, };     # named; a trailing comma is allowed

   A missing comma is ONE error at the second element, "expected ',' between struct literal
   elements" — positional or named, in a fn, after the first statement, in the declaration
   zone (a `kernel;` build too), a nested struct's leaves, a generic literal `Box<i64> { 1 2 }`,
   a generic fn's body (once), `--syntax-only` (`cyrius lint`). Through 6.7.14 `P { 3 4 }` and
   `P { b: 1 a: 2 }` compiled, the values landing where the commas would have put them (a
   `kernel;` build's late replay segfaulted); `R { a 3 }` was "unexpected '}'". Add the commas.
   A census of the tree found no commaless literal. Closed in 6.7.16: a commaless TUPLE literal
   `(1, 2 3)` says "expected ',' between tuple literal elements" (FE-15; `(P { 1 2 }, 3)` is
   refused first by 6.7.14's D12 (a)); ENUM members need a `;` or `,` too (D12, FE-16 —
   tagged_unions_v58x); and the declaration zone reports one error per declaration — two
   commaless zone literals give two errors (ZN-8; the replay's panic latch swallowed every later
   declaration's error).

6. 6.7.16 (CHANGELOG [6.7.16] SS-26, SS-27, FF-16): a POSITIONAL literal short of a field is
   "missing struct field in initializer", as the named form short of one (SS-27); a literal whose
   name is nothing (`Q { 1 }`, Q declared nowhere — a missing include) is "undefined variable 'Q'
   (missing include or enum?)" alone, at the name (SS-26); a tuple literal as a struct literal's
   element (`W { (1, 2), 3 }`) is R9's one error, "a tuple `( .. )` is a value only as a `var`
   initializer, ..." (FF-16). Each was "unexpected '}'" or a cascade; nothing that compiled changes.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_field_value_copy.tcyr (every field AND a canary after the
object, leading-block global and fn-local, two-level rows, 16 whole-value rows),
tests/tcyr/crossos/generic_struct_inference.tcyr (the LIT group),
tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_literal_wrapped_initializer.tcyr (6.7.14) and
tests/gates/frontend/struct_literal_type_refused.sh (the CM / CT / WU rows, 6.7.15). See also
generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310.


[[entries]]
name = "field_chains_any_depth_and_call_result_fields_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 a field chain reaches any depth for reading and writing (`n.v.v.x = 3;` stopped after two levels), and a `.field` applies to a call that returns a struct — in any expression position and as a bare statement, with chains, widths, f64 typing, a method on the result and a struct-typed field taken whole. At top level it is refused by name (no frame), as is an assignment to a result's field and a field of a call that returns no struct"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "field-access", "call-result", "temporaries", "v6.6.12"]
---
Both were LOUD before 6.6.12 — syntax errors, nothing compiled wrong.

FIELD CHAINS OF ANY DEPTH. `_resolve_leaf_field`, which serves the field load and the field
store, descended exactly ONE nested level (an `if`), so `n.v.v.x = 3;` was
`expected '=', got '.'` and `var r = n.v.v.x;` was `expected ';', got '.'` — through a local,
a global (inline or pointer-mode) and a by-value or `*T` parameter alike. It is a loop now
(a `..` range and a `.method(` after a nested field keep their own paths).

A `.field` ON A CALL RESULT:

    struct Pt  { x; y; }
    struct Box { v: Pt; n; }
    fn mk(a): Box { var b: Box; b.v.x = a; b.v.y = a + 1; b.n = 5; return b; }
    fn Box_total(self: Box): i64 { return self.n + self.v.x; }
    fn take(p: Pt): i64 { return p.x + p.y; }
    fn demo(): i64 {
        var y = mk(3).v.y;          # 4
        var s = mk(3).n + 1;        # 6 — an INTEGER (6.6.11 typed s as Box, wanted Box_add)
        var q: Pt = mk(7).v;        # a struct-typed field, copied whole
        var t = mk(2).total();      # a method on the result itself
        mk(3).total();              # ... and as a bare statement
        return y + s + take(mk(1).v) + q.x + t;
    }

Inside a fn the result lands in a frame temporary and the field is read through the same code
a named receiver uses: widths, sign-extension, f64 typing, deeper chains. Every return class
works (a <= 8 B struct in rax, 9-16 B in rax:rdx, larger through a retptr; a `Str` handle into
a pointer-mode slot).

REFUSED BY NAME:
- at TOP LEVEL, for every return class — `var G = mk(3).n;` and the bare `mk(3).n;` get
  "'mk' returns a struct by value, and a struct result needs storage in a fn's frame — call
  it inside a fn";
- an assignment to a BY-VALUE result's field, `mk(3).n = 5;` — "cannot assign to a field of a
  call result: the result is a temporary" (a `: *T` result's field is an lvalue since 6.7.13 —
  below). At TOP LEVEL it is the no-frame refusal ALONE since 6.7.14 (ST-2, ST-3): `mk().n = 5;`,
  `mk().n += 1;`, `mkp().p[0] = 5;` and `o.mk().n = 5;` were two errors (the second "the result is
  a temporary", or a false "cannot subscript field"). Since 6.7.15 (ST-19, ST-20) a by-value
  METHOD's `*T` field subscripted — `o.mk().p[0] = 5;`, `+= 5`, the read `o.mk().p[0];`, `var x =
  o.mk().p[0];` — and the free call's value twin `var x = mk().p[0];` (an argument too) are the
  no-frame refusal alone (each added a false "cannot subscript field 'p': it is not a *T field");
- a field of a call that returns no struct — "cannot take a field of the result of 'sc': it
  does not return a struct".

A METHOD ON A NESTED FIELD (`mk(3).v.sum()`, like `b.v.sum()`) is a call — `self` is the inline
field's address (6.7.0's A5; a `Str` field's method since 6.7.15, method_chains_v6617). The
"`expected ';', got '.'` — bind the field first" this entry said here went stale at 6.7.0
(re-measured on 6.7.16: both compile and run).

6.7.13 — A `: *T` CALL'S FIELD IS AN LVALUE, AND A KNOWN `&f` CALL TAKES THE CHAIN (D8 (a) ST-15; D16 RC-9):
- `gp().n = 5;`, `gp().m += 5;` (the call runs once), `gpg<H>().n = 5;`, `o.gp().n = 5;` and
  `h.get().b = 5;` store through the pointer a `: *T` call returns. A by-value result's field is
  still "the result is a temporary". (A `for` step `gp().n += 1` and the top-level two-error
  `mk().n = 5;` were open here; both 6.7.14 — below.)
- A call through `fncallN` / `callptr` whose callee is written `&f` (wrapped too) takes the chain a
  free call's result takes: `fncall1(&mk3, 5).b`, `callptr(&mk3, 1).dbl().c`, a `*T` callee's
  field, `fncall1(&gp, 5).b = 40;` (a store through, D8 (a)) and the statement
  `fncall1(&mk3, 5).tot();`. At top level it is refused once, by name. A callee held in a
  VARIABLE followed by `.name` is refused by name — "its callee is a value, not `&f`, so the result
  is a word". `callptr(&gp, 5).b = 40;` (the statement-form store through `callptr`) was "expected
  ';', got '='" through 6.7.13; since 6.7.14 it stores — below.

6.7.14 — THE STEP, THE `callptr` STORE, AND ONE TOP-LEVEL ERROR (CHANGELOG [6.7.14] ST-2, ST-3, ST-4, RC-8):
- A CLASSIC-`for` STEP stores through a `: *T` call's result as the statement does (ST-4; 6.7.13's
  D8 (a) with 6.7.5's every-assignment-is-a-step rule): `gp().n += 1`, `gp().n = i`, `o.gp().n = v`,
  `gpg<H>().n += 1`, a chain `gp().h.b += 3`, `gq().q[1] += 2`, `fncall0(&gp).n += 1`, a whole
  struct-typed field `gp().h = v`. It was "expected '=', got '('". As a step a by-value result is
  the statement's one refusal ("the result is a temporary", or at top level the no-frame refusal);
  a step with no `=` (`f()`, `gp().n`, `o.inc()`) keeps "expected '='".
- `callptr(&gp, 5).b = 40;` and `+= 40` store through a `: *T` result as statements, as the
  `fncall1` spelling does (RC-8).
- At top level a by-value result's field store is ONE error, the no-frame refusal (ST-2, ST-3;
  REFUSED BY NAME above).

6.7.15 — AN UNDEFINED CALLEE, A SIBLING MODULE'S CALL (CHANGELOG [6.7.15] ST-17 – ST-20; diagnostics, no decision):
- A `.field` of an UNDEFINED fn's result names the fn, once (ST-17): `undefd().n += 1;`, the step `undefd().n += 1`,
  `var x = undefd().n;`, a method `undefd(1, 2).m(3);` or a subscript `undefd().p[0] = 5;` on the result — "undefined
  function 'undefd'" at the name, the statement's one error, in a closure, at top level, once for a generic's
  instances. Through 6.7.14 it was "expected ';', got '.'" (the step "expected '=', got '('"), the fn named only by the
  end-of-compile refusal. It is an ERROR: `--allow-undef` does not downgrade it.
- `--syntax-only` (`cyrius lint`) accepts a field store on a SIBLING module's call (ST-18): `gp().n = 5;` and the step
  `gp().n += 1` with `gp` in another file were refused "the result is a temporary" — true only of a callee KNOWN to
  return a struct by value; they now get the one undefined-function warning and the value is still parsed. A by-value
  callee the file defines keeps the refusal; the full compile is untouched. The cx compiler's `--syntax-only` refused it
  through 6.7.15; since 6.7.16 `cycc_cx --syntax-only` accepts a call to a fn the file does not define and names each
  such fn once (TL-10, TL-10r).
- At top level the subscripted `*T` field of a by-value method or free call result is the no-frame refusal alone
  (ST-19, ST-20 — REFUSED BY NAME above).

6.7.16 (CHANGELOG [6.7.16] ST-8, ST-9, SS-36; diagnostics, no decision):
- A `.field` of an undefined fn's result AFTER an earlier plain call to it (`undefd(); undefd().n += 1;`, a step, a
  value, a method) is "undefined function 'undefd'" too (ST-8); it was "cannot take a field of the result of 'undefd':
  it does not return a struct". An enum variant's payload constructor called above its enum keeps "does not return a
  struct" (true: it returns a pointer).
- `cyrius lint` also accepts `gp().n = 5;` when gp is defined IN the file as `fn gp(): *Foo` and `Foo` is a sibling
  module's struct (ST-9); `: Foo` by value, `: **Foo` and `: *i64` keep "the result is a temporary".
- A `.name` past a field that is no struct — `w.n.b` with n an i64, f64, narrow or bool field, a store `w.n.b = 1;` /
  `+= 1` — is "field 'n' is not a struct: it has no field 'b'" at the name, the rest of the chain stepped (SS-36; it was
  "expected ';', got '.'" plus "undefined variable 'b'"). Past a field whose type its declaration refused, that
  declaration's error is the one.

COMPILER NOTE: the new factor arm parses its own call and must clear the literal-fold flag
AFTER the call, not before — see field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`cfo_const_fold_rewind_class_four_occurrences_grep_the_shape_not_the_operator_and_every_tier_that_reenters_the_fold`
(occurrence 5).

GATES: tests/tcyr/lang/struct_field_chain_depth.tcyr (29 rows: 3- and 4-level read and
write through every base, packed i8/i16/i32 leaves checked on the storage, an f64 leaf),
tests/tcyr/crossos/generic_struct_inference.tcyr (the CF group, 30 rows; cx has no
register-pair struct return, so those rows are off there) and
tests/gates/frontend/call_result_field.sh (18 rows).


[[entries]]
name = "struct_field_arguments_and_toplevel_copy_init_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 a struct-typed FIELD passed to a by-value struct parameter (`take(r.v)`) is a COPY (inside a fn from 6.6.12; everywhere since 6.6.16, when the callee makes it), and a top-level struct copy-init (`var B: Pt = A;`, `var G: Pt = BX.v;`) gives the global its own bytes. Both used the source's FIRST WORD as the struct's address and SIGSEGV'd. The source of a top-level copy must be declared ABOVE the copy; a source of another struct type is refused by name"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "by-value", "copy-init", "globals", "declaration-order", "sigsegv", "v6.6.12"]
---
Two more places where a struct moved as one word, both SIGSEGV (rc 139 on x86 and aarch64,
a page fault on PE, 0 on cx) before 6.6.12. The 6.6.11 half (a struct copied OUT of a
field into a variable) is struct_value_copies_and_result_type_checks_v6611.

1. A STRUCT-TYPED FIELD AS A BY-VALUE ARGUMENT.

       struct P3 { a; b; c; }
       struct R  { k; v: P3; }
       fn take(p: P3): i64 { p.a = 100; return p.a + p.b + p.c; }
       fn f(): i64 { var r = R { 1, 10, 20, 30 }; return take(r.v); }   # 150; r.v.a stays 10

   A by-value struct parameter over 8 bytes is address-passed. For `take(r.v)` the call
   pushed the field's first word as that address, so the callee dereferenced 10 — from a
   local, a global, a by-value parameter and a pointer-mode base, and through `mk<P3>(r.v)`.
   Now the callee gets a COPY, so its writes do not reach `r.v`. From 6.6.12 to 6.6.15 the copy
   was the CALLER's (a frame temporary), so it existed only inside a fn: at top level the callee
   got the field's own address and wrote it (`take(BX.v)` left `BX.v.a == 100`), and a NAMED
   struct argument was never copied at all. Since 6.6.16 the callee copies every struct
   parameter on entry, so both hold everywhere (struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616). A field of a
   different struct type is refused ("cannot pass 'v' to a
   parameter of a different struct type in a call to 'take'"). Since 6.7.3 every struct
   argument is checked, not only a field: see struct_argument_type_checked_v673.

2. TOP-LEVEL COPY-INIT.

       var A  = P3 { 3, 4, 5 };
       var B: P3 = A;              # B has its OWN 24 bytes; A.a = 99 later leaves B.a == 3
       var BX = R { 1, 7, 8, 9 };
       var G: P3 = BX.v;           # from a global's field, any chain depth

   Pass 1 knew only the LITERAL form as an inline struct global, so `var B: P3 = A;` got ONE
   8-byte pointer-mode slot holding A's first word and `B.a` dereferenced 3 — in the leading
   declaration block and after the first statement alike. One predicate (`_gci_src`) is now
   asked by pass 1, by the leading-block replay and by PARSE_VAR's top-level arm: a whole
   source of the declared struct registers the global inline and byte-copies it. A source of
   another struct type is refused ("cannot copy 'A' into a variable of a different
   struct/vector type: 'B'"). A POINTER-MODE source (`var p: P3 = mk();` in a fn, or a
   pointer-mode global) keeps the pointer copy, as in a fn.

⚠ DECLARE THE SOURCE ABOVE THE COPY.

       var B: P3 = A;              # error: cannot copy-init 'B' from a global declared
       var A = P3 { 3, 4, 5 };     #   below it (declare the source first): 'A'

   Pass 1 cannot see a global declared later (so it registered the pointer-mode slot) while
   the replay sees a whole `P3`, and the store that disagreement fell back to was the crash
   again. And a global initialised at run time is initialised in DECLARATION ORDER, so even an
   inline copy would read the source before its init. The refusal covers a struct over 8
   bytes (measured at 12, 16 and 24 B); an 8-byte struct copy-init from a global declared
   below it compiles and reads 0 (measured: `G.a` is 0 for a `{ a: i32; b: i32; }`), as any
   forward reference to a run-time-initialised global does (`var b = a; var a = f();` —
   6.6.11 the same). The value is 0, not a crash, and the rule is the same: declare the
   source first. ⟳ Since 6.7.16 (SS-38) the 8-byte-or-less case is refused too: a struct global
   of 8 B or less from a global declared below whose value is stored at run time — `var A: P8 =
   *GP8;` / `= GP8;` above `var GP8: *P8 = &GS8;` (it faulted at startup), `var A: P8 = GS8;`
   above `var GS8 = P8 { f() };` (it read 0) — is "cannot copy-init 'A' from a global declared
   below it (declare the source first): 'GP8'", at every size; a source the image holds from
   program start (a constant, a literal of constants) is read. ⚠ Beyond the filed shapes, with no
   decision of its own (on the filed keep-or-restore list): a whole struct global, a field chain's
   global and a call's value declared below, which read 0, are refused too.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_field_value_copy.tcyr (15 field-argument rows, 11
copy-init rows, 94/94 on x86, aarch64, PE and the four real hosts) and
tests/gates/frontend/struct_copy_source_type_refused.sh (12 refusals, each the only error;
every one compiled clean on 6.6.11).

6.7.11 (ST-5): a top-level copy-init from a field reached THROUGH A POINTER (`var G: S = P.s;`, `gp().s`, `VV.p.s`,
over 8 B) copies; a source declared BELOW G in the leading block is refused by name (pass 1 and the replay ask one
predicate, so the global is never laid out as a slot and initialised as a struct).


[[entries]]
name = "unterminated_string_literal_is_an_error_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 a string literal still open at the end of the source is `unterminated string literal`, reported at the OPENING quote's file:line:col — including at the end of an INCLUDED file, where the literal used to run on into the preprocessor's `#@file` resume marker and the error landed in the main file, on the wrong token"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["lexer", "strings", "diagnostics", "include", "v6.6.12"]
---
LEX's string loop treated end-of-input as a closing quote, so the error surfaced later and
somewhere else. It never compiled (the exit status was never 0), but the message pointed at
the wrong place:

    var a = 1;
    var s = "abc                 # 6.6.11: error:5:9: expected ';', got end of file
                                 # 6.6.12: error:<source>:2:9: unterminated string literal

    "abc                         # a bare one — 6.6.11: unexpected string

A `\` as the very last byte read one byte past the source as its escape; it now escapes
nothing and reaches the same report.

⚠ THE INCLUDED-FILE CASE was the misleading one, and it is the usual shape in a
`cyrius build` project. The preprocessor splices an included file into one stream and follows
it with a resume marker line, `#@file "<parent>" N`. A literal left open at the end of the
included file did not reach end-of-input at all: it ran into that marker, closed on the
marker's own quote, and every later quote paired wrongly. With `part.cyr` ending in
`var s = "oops;` and the includer declaring `var source = "fine";`:

    6.6.11: error:7:16: undefined variable 'source'      # the main file, a VALID literal
    6.6.12: error:part.cyr:2:13: unterminated string literal

Mechanism: a LF inside a literal followed by a WHOLE line in the marker's exact shape
(`#@file "<name>" <digits>`) is taken as the end of the included file. The whole line is
matched, so a valid string that merely contains `#@file ` still compiles.

GATE: tests/gates/frontend/lexer_errors_name_file_line.sh axis 4 (eight unterminated rows —
single-line, multi-line naming the opening line, trailing backslash, bare statement, four in
an included file — plus two anti-vacuous rows; 6.6.11 fails all eight). The escape rules and
line accounting are string_literal_escapes_and_line_accounting_v6611.


[[entries]]
name = "aarch64_xattr_statx_rlimit_fchown_wrappers_and_native_region_exemption_v6612"
description = "cycc 6.6.12 names the xattr family, fchown, statx and getrlimit in the stdlib (`sys_{,l,f}{set,get,list,remove}xattr`, `sys_fchown`, `sys_statx`, `sys_getrlimit`); on ELF-aarch64 most of them could not be reached by number at all, so the aarch64 peer spells them through the 1000+N private alias band. The raw-syscall literal warning is no longer raised inside a region compiled only for aarch64 (where the number IS native), and a wrong 3-arg `kill` warns again on x86-Linux"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["syscalls", "aarch64", "esysxlat", "alias-band", "xattr", "statx", "diagnostics", "darwin", "v6.6.12"]
---
THE WRAPPERS. Fifteen, in lib/syscalls_linux_common.cyr:

    sys_setxattr  sys_lsetxattr  sys_fsetxattr     sys_getxattr  sys_lgetxattr  sys_fgetxattr
    sys_listxattr sys_llistxattr sys_flistxattr    sys_removexattr sys_lremovexattr sys_fremovexattr
    sys_fchown(fd, uid, gid)   sys_statx(dirfd, path, flags, mask, buf)   sys_getrlimit(resource, rlim)

with `SYS_*` names in the x86_64, aarch64, x86-macOS and Windows peers. Linux: real
everywhere. macOS: `sys_fchown` and `sys_getrlimit` are real (Darwin 123 / 194 —
`RLIMIT_NOFILE` is 8 there, not 7); the xattr calls and `sys_statx` return -78, because
Darwin's xattr calls take two extra arguments and Darwin has no statx. PE and agnos: -38 for
all fifteen. Measured on 6.6.12: statx of `/`, getrlimit(7), getxattr on a missing path
(-2) and fchown(fd, -1, -1) all right on x86 and on aarch64 under qemu.

WHY aarch64 NEEDED THE ALIAS BAND. `ESYSXLAT`'s aarch64-Linux arm renumbers x86-compat
numbers so raw x86 syscalls keep working on ARM — and those row SOURCES are native aarch64
numbers too: fstat 5, lstat 6, poll 7, mmap 9, mprotect 10, munmap 11, brk 12, ioctl 16,
getsockopt 55. So native setxattr 5 ran fstat, lgetxattr 9 ran mmap, fremovexattr 16 ran
ioctl, fchown 55 ran getsockopt (qemu-aarch64 -strace). And the x86 numbers were no use
either: x86 lremovexattr 198 / fremovexattr 199 are aarch64's own socket / socketpair. So the
aarch64 peer declares them in the v6.5.7 **private alias band** — a source number >= 1000 no
OS will ever mint, spelled `1000 + native`:

    SYS_SETXATTR = 1005 ... SYS_FREMOVEXATTR = 1016   SYS_FCHOWN = 1055   SYS_STATX = 1291
    SYS_GETRLIMIT = 163      # native: neither a row source nor a row product

renumbered by fourteen pure rows at the TAIL of the ELF chain (below every compat row whose
source their product would re-match). getxattr 8, flistxattr 13, removexattr 14,
lremovexattr 15 and statx 291 are not row sources but take the band anyway: declaring
`SYS_GETXATTR = 8` natively would have silenced the raw-literal warning for x86 lseek (8),
because the generator drops a warning row for any number the aarch64 peer declares below 1000.
Cost: ESYSXLAT rows are emitted inline at every syscall site, so each ELF-aarch64 syscall site
grew 14 x 12 B = 168 B (the native aarch64 compiler +66 KB); x86 `cycc` is unaffected.
Mechanics and the ordering rule: field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml
`raw_syscall_numbers_on_aarch64_and_the_darwin_at_flag_divergence`.

THE NATIVE-REGION EXEMPTION. The raw-literal warning (v6.5.51) runs in the parser, after the
preprocessor has already dropped the `#ifdef`, so a correct native call was told the x86 name:

    fn a(): i64 {
    #ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64
        return syscall(8, path, name, buf, n);   # 6.6.11: "raw syscall 8 is x86_64 `lseek`;
    #endif                                       #  on ELF-aarch64 that number is `getxattr`.
        return 0;                                #  Use SYS_LSEEK ..." — 6.6.12: silent
    }

and spelling it through an `enum` folded to the same literal, so there was no way to mark a
number native. Since 6.6.12 the preprocessor writes `#@a+` / `#@a-` marker lines where a
TAKEN branch whose condition implies aarch64 opens and closes — `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64`,
`#ifplat aarch64`, `#ifndef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86`, and the `#else` of their opposites — and the
warning is skipped for a call inside one (a literal or an enum constant alike). Source text
that spells a marker is neutralised like a forged `#@file`. Outside such a region the warning
is unchanged.

⚠ SILENT IS NOT SAFE. The exemption removes a WRONG suggestion; it does not route anything.
A native number that is also a compat-row source is still rewritten before the `svc`, and now
without a word: measured on 6.6.12, `syscall(9, ...)` (native lgetxattr) inside
`#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` compiles silently and runs `mmap` under qemu. The guide's rule 3
stands: never write the aarch64-native number — use the peer's `SYS_*` name or the wrapper.

6.7.12 — THE RENUMBER IS WARNED (DW-1, D1). On ELF-aarch64 a raw syscall LITERAL that an x86-compat ESYSXLAT
row renumbers is warned when it sits inside a native-aarch64 region opened in the call's OWN file (the region
is `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64`, `#ifplat aarch64`, `#ifndef CYRIUS_ARCH_X86`, or the `#else` of their
opposites), naming the spelling that would reach the call you meant:

    raw syscall 9 inside a native-aarch64 region is renumbered → 222 (mmap); write 1009 for native lgetxattr
    … no 1000+N alias exists for native <name> (the chain has no 1NNN row)

The number is STILL translated (the user's decision D1, 2026-10-15: warn only), so no binary changes; the
alias is derived by running the chain with 1000+N. Judged in 6.7.12: one integer-literal token (hex too, `0x9`)
in a region of the call's own file — a file `include`d from inside a region, and any constant, were not judged.
6.7.14 widens both (below). Gate: tests/gates/platform/native_region_literal_warns.sh. Field notes:
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml native_region_rules_and_the_macho_fold_report_v6712 (the inherited vs
file-local region rules). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] DW-1.

6.7.14 — THE REGION CARRIES THROUGH INCLUDE, AND A DECLARED CONSTANT IS JUDGED (PL-6, D61). The warning judges a
number spelled as ONE token at the `syscall()`:
  - AN INTEGER LITERAL inside a native-aarch64 region at the call is judged, and THE REGION CARRIES THROUGH `include`
    (the user's D61, 2026-10-16): a per-arch file included from inside `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` is judged, and the
    warning names the included file — the rule `PP_A64_NATIVE_AT` / the 6.6.12 x86-name report already use:
        warning:src/arch_aarch64.cyr:2:45: raw syscall 9 inside a native-aarch64 region is renumbered → 222 (mmap);
        write 1009 for native lgetxattr
  - AN `enum` CONSTANT OR A TOP-LEVEL `const` (bare or `Enum.NAME`) is judged when it is DECLARED inside a region
    opened in its OWN file — a constant is judged where it is declared, not where it is used:
        syscall constant NR_UMOUNT2 = 39, declared inside a native-aarch64 region, is renumbered → 172 (getpid);
        write 1039 for native umount2
  - NOT judged: `(9)` and `9 + 0`; a constant declared at a file's top level outside a region of that file — which
    covers the stdlib peers' x86 `SYS_*`, which renumber by design (aarch64 `SYS_FSYNC = 74` → 82).
  - Still silent through 6.7.14: a fn-local `const` declared in a region; a constant declared at the top level of a
    per-arch file included under the guard (the peers' shape). Both are judged since 6.7.15 — below.
  - Still WARN ONLY (6.7.12's D1): no binary changes.
  - The stdlib spells its deliberate x86 numbers BY NAME: `fd_wait_ready` and the macOS `sys_nanosleep` /
    `sys_sched_yield` arms issue `SYS_POLL` (7, new in the x86_64-Linux, x86-macOS and aarch64 peers), and aarch64's
    `sys_epoll_wait` issues `SYS_EPOLL_WAIT` (232, new in the aarch64 peer) — so a stdlib program prints no warning:
    every `lib/*.cyr` builds for aarch64 with 0. ⚠ A program that declares its own `SYS_POLL` (not 7), or on aarch64
    its own `SYS_EPOLL_WAIT` (not 232), now gets "duplicate symbol … redefined with conflicting value (last definition
    wins)" AND a different binary (kept by the user's 6.7.15 D53).
  Gate: native_region_literal_warns.sh axes 7, 10 and 11. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] PL-6.

6.7.15 — YOUR OWN PER-ARCH FILE, AND A FN-LOCAL CONST (the user's D39, 2026-10-18: "Yes, for files outside lib/";
CHANGELOG [6.7.15] PL-9, PL-10). Still warn only (6.7.12's D1): no binary changes.
  - A constant (an `enum` member or a top-level `const`) declared in a file OUTSIDE `lib/` is judged by the region
    carried into that file through `include` — the raw literal's rule (6.7.14's D61). The top of your own
    `src/arch_aarch64.cyr` included under `#ifdef CYRIUS_ARCH_AARCH64` now warns:
        syscall constant NR_UMOUNT2 = 39, declared inside a native-aarch64 region, is renumbered → 172 (getpid);
        write 1039 for native umount2
    (through 6.7.14 it ran getpid with no warning, while the literal 39 in the same file warned).
  - A file UNDER `lib/` keeps the 6.7.14 file-local rule — only a region opened in the file itself counts. It is
    decided by the include name AS WRITTEN: it starts `lib/` or has a `/lib/` component, so `vendor/lib/x.cyr` counts,
    and the store-slot fallback, overlay and `#@incdir` retry keep the name. Not an exemption (a lib/ file's own region
    still warns): it keeps the stdlib peers' x86-numbered `SYS_*` silent, so stdlib programs stay at 0 warnings.
  - A FN-LOCAL `const` declared inside a region is judged too (PL-10), where it is DECLARED: declared outside and used
    inside stays silent; an `#inline` fn's local const is judged by its declaration in the inline body, never the call
    site. Its warning printed once for the definition and once per call through 6.7.15; once per site since 6.7.16
    (TL-7 — the backend's renumber warning no longer repeats per `#inline` emission; nothing emitted changes).
  - Not judged: `(9)`, `9 + 0` — "silent is not safe" now holds only for those spellings.
  Gate: native_region_literal_warns.sh axes 12 and 13.

THE 3-ARG `kill` ARITY WARNING IS BACK ON x86-LINUX. 6.6.10 skipped the arity check for
Darwin's `kill(pid, sig, posix)` with no target test, and the Linux arity table runs on every
non-aarch64 backend, so `syscall(62, pid, sig, x)` compiled SILENTLY on x86-Linux from 6.6.10
on (6.6.9 warned). The skip is x86-macOS only now: x86-Linux warns `syscall arity mismatch`,
x86-macOS is silent for the 3-arg form and still warns for a 1-arg one (measured).

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/xattr_statx_rlimit_fchown_wrappers.tcyr (62 rows; Linux, macOS,
PE, agnos), tests/gates/platform/esysxlat_row_order.sh (every alias the peer declares has its
row, in order), tests/gates/diagnostics/raw_syscall_native_exempt_and_kill_arity.sh (19 rows:
eight native shapes silent, six anti-vacuous rows still warn, the kill rows).


[[entries]]
name = "cx_tail_calls_and_cxvm_guest_traps_v6612"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.12 the cx backend compiles `return f(args);` as a real tail call (it was a call plus epilogue, so deep tail recursion overran cxvm's stacks and returned garbage), and cxvm TRAPS a guest fault instead of corrupting itself: every load/store, the syscall buffers, both VM stacks, the guest stack, a negative pc and an unknown opcode are checked (the cxvm host-memory bug). A trap prints `cxvm: <what> <value> at pc <N>` and exits 1. cxvm is still NOT a sandbox for the calls it passes — since 6.7.14 only the translated, served and scalar-only calls reach the host; every other number answers -ENOSYS (-38); since 6.7.15 cxvm serves each listed call through the host's own stdlib wrapper (the call the guest names on every host), and the path calls (mkdir, unlink, rename, stat, …) are served"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cx", "cxvm", "bytecode", "tail-call", "sandbox", "the cxvm host-memory bug", "v6.6.12"]
---
TAIL CALLS. cx `ETAILJMP` emitted a normal call + epilogue, under a comment claiming a jump
broke when the arguments contained nested calls. They do not — the shared tail path has
popped every argument into r3.. before `ETAILJMP` — so it is now `mov sp, fp; popc fp;
jmp f`, the x86 `mov rsp, rbp; pop rbp; jmp` shape. Measured:

    fn tr(n, acc): i64 { if (n == 0) { return acc; } return tr(n - 1, acc + 1); }
    syscall(60, tr(2000000, 0) % 256);
    # 6.6.12 cycc_cx + cxvm: exit 128 (correct, 2.3 s interpreted)
    # 6.6.11 cycc_cx + cxvm: exit 1 — garbage from the 513th frame on

GUEST FAULTS TRAP (the cxvm host-memory bug). Before 6.6.12 `programs/cxvm.cyr` checked nothing a guest
controls: a guest address past the 1 MB data segment (8 MiB since 6.7.12) (or negative) read and wrote cxvm's own
heap — the register file, the stacks, the loaded code; guest address 0 (the in-memory copy of
the bytecode) stored silently; the 1024-entry call stack had no bound, so the 513th nested
frame overwrote the loaded code; `read` / `write` / `getrandom` / `clock_gettime` buffers ending
past 1 MB had the HOST kernel write cxvm's heap; an unknown opcode was a silent no-op. Now:

    fn f(): i64 { store64(2000000, 5); return 3; }
    # cxvm: guest address out of range: 2000000 at pc 44        (exit 1; 6.6.11 exited 3)
    store64(0, 5)          # cxvm: guest address out of range: 0 at pc 40
    load64(0 - 8)          # cxvm: guest address out of range: -8 at pc 48
    fn d(n) { return 1 + d(n + 1); }    # cxvm: guest stack overflow, sp 160 at pc 16

- every load / store is checked for its FULL width against `[8, _CX_MEM_SIZE)` — `[0, 8)` is
  the null page;
- the translated syscall buffers are checked for their full length (a path up to its NUL) and
  answer `-EFAULT` without reaching the host;
- both VM stacks hold 524288 entries since 6.7.12 (65536, 512 KiB each, at 6.6.12) and trap on
  overflow and underflow, so the guest's own segment (8 MiB since 6.7.12; 1 MB before) — not the
  host arrays — bounds recursion (non-tail depth 5000 returns the right value; it gave garbage
  past 512 before; at 65536 entries an 8 MiB guest's runaway recursion would have met the
  call-stack trap first);
- `sub sp` traps at the guest BREAK — the image plus the brk heap above it (6.7.14; through
  6.7.13 it trapped before the loaded image, its globals and the static `lib/alloc_cx.cyr` heap);
- a negative pc traps, an unknown opcode traps (`cxvm: unknown opcode 0xNN at pc <N>`).

No `.cyx` format bump: a newer `.cyx` on an older cxvm is refused only by the new cxvm's trap.

⚠ STILL NOT A SANDBOX for the calls it passes. Since 6.7.14 (the user's D55, LIB-6) the syscall
opcode (0x70) lets only three kinds of call reach the host:
  - the TRANSLATED calls: read / write / open (their buffers checked), with lseek / close / exit at
    their own arity;
  - the SERVED calls: getrandom / clock_gettime through the host's stdlib, and brk in GUEST space
    (cx_alloc_heap_is_the_guest_break_v6714);
  - the SCALAR-ONLY list (`_cx_sys_scalar` in programs/cxvm.cyr, keyed on the guest's x86 number):
    exit_group, fork, dup / dup2, getpid / getppid, kill, sched_yield, fsync / fdatasync, flock,
    ftruncate, fchmod / fchown, getuid / getgid / geteuid / getegid, socket, listen, unshare,
    timerfd_create, epoll_create1, and fcntl for its scalar commands only.
Every other number answers -ENOSYS (-38) WITHOUT reaching the host — a raw `syscall()` too: stat,
statfs, munmap, mprotect, mmap, nanosleep, mkdir (no directory is made), uname, wait4, futex, the
address-taking socket calls, ioctl, `fcntl(F_GETLK)` (stat and mkdir are served since 6.7.15 — below). So a guest
address no longer reaches the host
through a refused call: through 6.7.13 the forwarded pointer calls could hit cxvm's own image on
Linux (0x400000 / 0x600000) — `cyr_munmap(0x400000, ..)` killed cxvm (139), and an fstat into
0x600100 wrote over cxvm's globals. On the Macs a refused number no longer SIGSYS-kills cxvm.
cxvm is still not a sandbox for the calls it passes: a passed call runs with the guest's raw
register values. Through 6.7.14 on aarch64 Linux and macOS HOSTS the passed numbers went through
the host's own renumbering, built for the host stdlib's numbers, so a passed call could run a
DIFFERENT host call (on pi a guest lseek ran getxattr, kill ran lseek, fork ran close; on arm64
macOS several killed cxvm with SIGSYS) — fixed in 6.7.15 (below). Treat a `.cyx` you did not build
like a native binary: verify its origin, and run cxvm under the OS's own isolation when the input
is not yours. The contract is docs/platform-status.md "cyrius-x guest contract (cxvm)" in the
cyrius repo. Gate: tests/gates/codegen/cx_guest_syscall_refusal.sh. CHANGELOG [6.7.14] LIB-6, LIB-7.

6.7.15 — THE GUEST'S CALL ON EVERY HOST, AND THE PATH CALLS (CHANGELOG [6.7.15] PL-11, PL-11r, PL-12; no decision
needed — within 6.7.14's D55, "translation can be added one call at a time"):
  - cxvm has no runtime-numbered syscall left: it serves each listed call through the HOST'S OWN stdlib wrapper, so it
    runs the call the guest names on every host — read / write / open / close / lseek / exit / exit_group / getpid /
    fsync / fdatasync / ftruncate / fcntl everywhere; getppid, kill, dup, dup2, flock, fchmod, fchown, the uid / gid
    getters, sched_yield and fork off Windows (-38 there, where the PE peer has only stubs); socket, listen, unshare,
    timerfd_create and epoll_create1 on Linux hosts only (-38 on macOS and Windows). Every other number still answers
    -38. `open`'s and `F_SETFL`'s flags are mapped: the access mode + O_CREAT / O_EXCL / O_TRUNC / O_APPEND /
    O_NONBLOCK / O_DIRECTORY / O_NOFOLLOW / O_CLOEXEC to the host's names (the identity on x86_64 Linux and Windows);
    any other bit is dropped off those two.
  - Do NOT read this as "x86_64 Linux's answers on every host" (PL-11r): ⚠ on macOS a failed call's errno is Darwin's
    (ELOOP reaches the guest as -62) and `kill`'s signal number reaches Darwin unmapped (SIGUSR1 10 is Darwin's SIGBUS);
    off x86_64 Linux and Windows `F_GETFL` reported only the nine mapped bits (O_LARGEFILE dropped) — each closed in
    6.7.16 (below).
  - THE PATH CALLS ARE SERVED (PL-12): mkdir, rmdir, unlink, rename, symlink, readlink and stat answered -38 (so
    `xmkdir`, `xsymlink`, `is_symlink`, `xstat` failed and `file_write_atomic` left its temp file behind); they now run,
    their paths and buffers translated (-14 when one leaves the guest segment). A guest `struct stat` is x86_64 Linux's
    on every host; off x86_64 Linux only mode / nlink / uid / gid / size / mtime / mtime nsec are filled. Windows has
    no readlink or stat route (-38). Still -38 at 6.7.15: lstat, chmod, getdents64, mknodat.

6.7.16 — THE GUEST'S ANSWERS ARE x86_64 LINUX'S ON EVERY HOST (CHANGELOG [6.7.16] PL-4 – PL-8, PL-7r; no decision but
D29 — the `.cyx` ABI is x86_64 Linux's):
  - On a macOS host a failed call answers x86_64 Linux's errno (ELOOP -40, ENOTEMPTY -39, ENOSYS -38; a Darwin-only
    errno the nearest Linux code) (PL-4); `kill` sends the Linux signal the guest names (SIGUSR1 is SIGUSR1), and
    SIGSTKFLT / SIGPWR / the realtime band answer -22 there (PL-5).
  - `fcntl(fd, F_GETFL)` is x86_64 Linux's word on every host, O_LARGEFILE 0x8000 included for an opened file or device
    (6.7.16's D29, PL-6); an `open()`ed FIFO reads without it on a Mac.
  - getdents64, mknodat and wait4 are served on every POSIX host (buffers and paths translated, -14 past the segment),
    and `poll` only as `poll(NULL, 0, ms)` — a sleep, so `sleep_ms` sleeps on cx (it was a no-op) (PL-7). A Windows host
    answers -38 for the three, and a poll with pollfds is -38 everywhere. Still -38: lstat, chmod.
  - On a Darwin host a getdents64 count under 64 is -22, and a buffer under 1048 bytes may end a devfs listing early
    with -22 — never a repeated or silently skipped entry (PL-7r).
  - On a Windows host an `O_CREAT | O_EXCL` open over an existing directory answers -17 (it was -13), and the crossos
    file rows name the rows the host has no route for (PL-8).

ALSO cx at 6.6.12: a store to a narrow slot wrote 8 bytes (`EVSTORE_W` / `EFLSTORE_W`
ignored the width), clobbering packed narrow globals; both directions now use the sized
cxvm opcodes and a signed load sign-extends (see multi_width_types). And `thread_join` no
longer issues a FUTEX_WAIT with a guest offset (it spun on a Linux host): it returns -1 on
cx, where no thread can exist.

GATE: tests/gates/codegen/cx_tailcall_and_vm_traps.sh (axis A tail calls — a 2M-deep self
recursion, mutual recursion, nested-call arguments, a >6-arg call; axes B-D the traps; axis
E the narrow stores; every trap reddened by its own revert).

6.7.11: cx calls `fn main` from the entry epilogue like every image target (auto_call_main_v59x); a cx test that left
its asserts in `main` used to pass vacuously (cxvm printed nothing, exit 0).

6.7.12 — THE SIZES AND THE GUEST CONTRACT (LIB-7, LIB-8; D12, D13). cycc_cx holds a program's bytecode in a FIXED
4 MiB off-heap code region (`_CX_CODE_CAP`; it was 512 KiB) and refuses by name past it: "cx bytecode exceeds the cx
code region (4194304 B): … build this program for a native target". cxvm's guest memory is 8 MiB (`_CX_MEM_SIZE`
8388608), its two VM stacks 524288 entries, and it reads a `.cyx` up to the guest memory + 8 — past it "error: .cyx
exceeds cxvm's guest memory (8388608 B of code and data)". A 4.1 MB `.cyx` runs. The guest contract (D13): on cx
`cyr_mmap` answers -1 (so `mmap_anon` 0) and `sys_pipe` / `sys_fstat` / `sys_unlink` -38 — they REFUSE instead of
handing the host a guest offset (they answered -EFAULT, and unlink left the file). (`sys_unlink` acts on cx since
6.7.15 — PL-12, above.) The seven corpus files that hit
the contract assert the refusal and print a named SKIP.
⚠ AND THE SANDBOX CAVEAT GREW: since the 8 MiB segment a guest address could equal cxvm's own Linux image (text
0x400000, data 0x600000) — `cyr_munmap(0x400000, …)` killed cxvm, and a raw fstat into guest 0x600100 wrote over
cxvm's data. Closed in 6.7.14 by D55's -38 at the dispatch (above). The two raw-mmap paths that returned a HOST
address as success on cx — `mmap_stack` (lib/thread.cyr) and `fl_alloc` (lib/freelist.cyr's `_fl_mmap`) — answer
-1 / 0 since 6.7.14 (LIB-5), as `cyr_mmap` does. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] LIB-7, LIB-8.

[[entries]]
name = "cx_alloc_heap_is_the_guest_break_v6714"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.14 cx `alloc()` bumps over a break that cxvm serves in GUEST space — the free guest memory between the image and 1 MiB below the stack (~7 MiB less the image), zeroed when granted — instead of a 128 KB static heap baked into every allocating `.cyx`; cycc_cx and cxvm are a pair: a 6.7.14 `.cyx` that allocates needs the 6.7.14 cxvm"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["cx", "cxvm", "alloc", "brk", "heap", "guest", "v6.7.14"]
---
THE RULE (CHANGELOG [6.7.14] LIB-7, LIB-7r):
  - cx `alloc()` (lib/alloc_cx.cyr) bumps over a BREAK that cxvm serves in guest space (brk, syscall 12, is dispatched
    before the scalar list and never forwarded). The break starts at the image end rounded up to 16.
  - The break moves down to its start, or up while it stays 1 MiB (`_CX_STACK_RESERVE`) below BOTH the live guest sp
    AND the 8 MiB segment's top (the segment bound is LIB-7r: sp is a guest register, so a guest that raised it past
    8 MiB was granted a break past the segment and cxvm zeroed its own heap). `alloc` grows it in 64 KB steps
    (`_CX_HEAP_GRAIN`); a refused step is retried at the exact size.
  - Memory it grants READS ZERO (the guest stack may have dirtied it; Linux hands out zeroed pages).
  - So the heap is "the free guest memory between the image and 1 MiB below the stack": ~7 MiB less the image (it was
    a 128 KB static buffer, `_cx_heap_buf`, sized at v6.6.6 for the 1 MB guest — a program needing more stopped at
    `alloc: cx static heap exhausted (128 KB)` with ~7 MiB free).
  - Exhaustion prints `alloc: cx heap exhausted at <N> B (the guest break cannot grow by <M> B more)` and exits 1 —
    anything reading the old `alloc: cx static heap exhausted (128 KB)` text must read the new one.
  - A raw `brk(addr)` on cx answers a GUEST address, and Linux's contract holds: below the start it only asks; a
    refused move answers the old break. Through 6.7.13 brk went to the host and answered the host's break.
  - The guest-stack trap (`sub sp`) compares sp with the current break, not the image end
    (cx_tail_calls_and_cxvm_guest_traps_v6612).
  - The 128 KB static `_cx_heap_buf` (and `enum CxHeap` / `_CX_HEAP_SLOTS`) is gone from every allocating `.cyx`: an
    `alloc(16)` program is 22,494 B, where it was 151,546 B. `alloc_reset` / `alloc_used` are unchanged; the public
    surface is unchanged.

⚠ cycc_cx AND cxvm ARE A PAIR. A 6.7.14 `.cyx` that allocates needs the 6.7.14 cxvm: a 6.7.13 (or older) cxvm hands
brk to the host, so the program stops at its first allocation with `cxvm: guest address out of range: <host break>`,
exit 1. A cxvm that answers brk -38 makes `alloc_init` name the mismatch: `alloc: this cxvm does not serve brk (12): a
.cyx from cycc_cx 6.7.14 or later needs cxvm 6.7.14 or later` (no released cxvm does). An OLDER `.cyx` (the static
heap) runs on the new cxvm.

Measured on x86_64 Linux, qemu-aarch64, pi, ecb, ach, wine and cass: 3 MiB in 48 × 64 KB allocations runs (it exited
1); allocate-until-refused names the exhaustion at 7,274,496 B; a deep recursion after a 6 MiB heap traps `guest stack
overflow` just under the break, never into the heap. GATE: tests/gates/codegen/cx_heap_guest_brk.sh (13 rows).

[[entries]]
name = "v6613_language_changes_builtins_array_sizes_global_layout"
description = "cyrius 6.6.13 language-visible changes: `f64_le` / `f64_ge` / `f64_trunc` are compiler BUILTINS (reserved names; no `lib/math.cyr` needed); `var a: T[N]` reserves N * sizeof(T) for EVERY element type; every global starts at its natural alignment; narrow globals initialise at their own width; a captured struct copies its bytes inside a closure; the Str -> `: cstring` warning sees call results, globals, fields, tail and method calls"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.6.13", "builtins", "f64_le", "f64_ge", "f64_trunc", "reserved", "typed arrays", "alignment", "globals", "closures", "Str", "cstring", "diagnostics"]
---
**Builtins (I5).** `f64_le(a, b)`, `f64_ge(a, b)` and `f64_trunc(x)` compile inline like `f64_lt` /
`f64_floor` (x86 `roundsd $3`, aarch64 `frintz`, cx opcode 0x6E). NaN semantics are unchanged: `f64_le`
and `f64_ge` are false when either side is NaN. The three names are now RESERVED (TOKNAME_BUILTIN holds
79 names), so a repo that still vendors a pre-6.6.13 `lib/math.cyr` (which defines them) fails with
`reserved keyword` at a pin bump until `cyrius deps` re-vendors it.

**Array sizes (M2).** `var a: T[N]` reserves `N * sizeof(T)` for every element type — f64 8, f32 4,
struct / union / generic instance STRUCTSZ, `*T` 8, vectors 16 / 32, bool / enum 8 — and the subscript
stride agrees. It used to reserve 8 bytes inside a fn for `f64[4]` or `Pt[2]` (N x 8 at top level for
structs). A generic `var a: T[N]` sizes its instance by STRUCTSZ. An unknown element type name is an
error. `var x[N]` (no type) is still N BYTES locally.

**Global layout (I9).** Every global starts at its natural alignment (1/2/4 for narrow scalars, 8 for
anything else). `var a: u8[N]` still gives N usable bytes; up to 7 bytes of padding may follow it. Code
that reached the NEXT global by adding to a global's address was always wrong; it is now visibly wrong.

**Narrow globals (M1).** `var a: u8 = 0;` (or a non-constant / call initialiser) stores 1 byte, not 8 —
it used to clobber the globals after it.

**Closures (M3).** Inside a closure, `b.v = p`, `var q: Pt = p` and struct literals copy a CAPTURED struct's
bytes, as outside a closure.

**Str -> `: cstring` (I11).** The warning fires for a `Str` named local or param, a call returning `Str`,
a `: Str` global (declared or inferred from its initializer), a `: Str` field, tail calls and method
calls, `x.data` and `str_data(x)` (a Str's data pointer is not NUL-terminated at its length). The fix is
`str_cstr(x)` (a NUL-terminated copy), `str_eq_cstr(x, "lit")` for comparisons, or a `: Str` parameter —
never `str_data(x)`. Warnings only: no binary changes.

[[entries]]
name = "indirect_calls_at_top_level_since_6_6_16"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.16 `callptr(f, ..)` and `fncall0..8(f, ..)` work at top level exactly as inside a fn, capturing closures included: a top-level `callptr` compiles (it was refused), and a top-level `fncallN` on a capturing closure that a fn built and returned no longer crashes (SIGSEGV; 0xC0000005 on Windows; 0 on cx)"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["closures", "fnptr", "callptr", "fncall", "top-level", "cx", "v6.6.16"]
---
    include "lib/alloc.cyr"
    include "lib/fnptr.cyr"
    fn mk(b) { return |x| b + x; }      # captures b
    var g = mk(41);
    var a = fncall1(g, 1);              # 42 — top level, no wrapper fn needed (6.6.16+)
    var c = callptr(g, 1);              # 42 — a compile error before 6.6.16
    fncall1(g, 1);                      # statement position works too

Before 6.6.16 a top-level `fncallN` skipped the closure-aware lowering and called lib/fnptr.cyr,
which jumps to the closure's bit-63-tagged value; on cx it returned 0 for EVERY callee, plain fn
pointers too. The workaround was to call the closure from inside any fn
(`fn call1(f, a) { return fncall1(f, a); }`); it still works and is no longer needed.
A top-level indirect call opens its own small stack frame (on Windows with the usual stack
probe); inside a fn nothing changed. Still NOT closure-aware: an address-taken `&fncallN`
called through another indirect call runs lib/fnptr.cyr directly (and returns 0 on cx).
Compiler side: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`top_level_has_no_frame_so_a_lowering_that_needs_one_must_refuse_or_bring_its_own`.

6.7.11 (ARG-1): a top-level `fncallN(&f, ..)` / `callptr(&f, ..)` whose `f` returns a struct over 8 B is
refused by name (no frame to hold the result), as a top-level `f(..)` is; an 8 B result is a struct value
there (an 8 B global in the declaration zone included). See struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_assignment_handle_and_value_v6616"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.16 a struct ASSIGNMENT between a handle (a variable whose slot holds a plain struct's address: a pointer-mode local or global, a `*T` parameter) and a struct VALUE copies the struct — out of the pointee, or into it (`*a = q`). handle <- handle stays a pointer rebind at any pair of types; the DECLARATION `var q: T = a;` stays an alias from a pointer-mode local or global (from a parameter, a `*T` one included, it copies, as since 6.6.11). Since 6.7.10 the rule holds at EVERY size (through 6.7.9 only over 8 bytes), and a `*T` global, a `: *T` call / method, a `*T` field and pointer arithmetic on a `*T` are copy sources too. Since 6.7.11 every destination takes every source the same way: a struct-typed field, a typed `*S` GLOBAL (`GP = l` copies into `*GP`) and a store `*p = v` (exactly sizeof(S) bytes) included, and a generic `: *T` call and an assignment from a dereference copy."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "assignment", "copy", "pointer-mode", "handle", "v6.6.16", "v6.7.10", "v6.7.11"]
---
TERMS (plain structs of ANY size since 6.7.10 — over 8 bytes only through 6.7.9; Str / Result / Option /
Tagged are unchanged):
    HANDLE  one slot holding the struct's ADDRESS:
              var a: P3 = alloc(24);        # a pointer-mode local
              var G: P3 = 0; G = alloc(24); # a pointer-mode global
              fn f(p: *P3)                  # a `*T` parameter
    VALUE   the struct itself: an inline local or global (`var q: P3;`, `var G = P3{..}`), a
            by-value parameter (`q: P3`), a closure capture, a field `b.v`, a call / method /
            operator result.

THE RULE, for the assignment `dst = src;`:
    value  <- handle   copy the struct src points at            q = a;
    handle <- value    copy INTO the struct dst points at       a = q;  a = mk3(..);  a = b.v;
                       (`*a = q` in C) — a keeps its address    a = q.f();  a = l + r;  a = param;
    handle <- handle   REBIND (one word), at ANY struct types   b = a;  G = a;  p = q (two *T params)
    value  <- value    copy (unchanged)
    *p     <- value    (6.7.11, D4) copy exactly sizeof(S) bytes INTO the pointee of a `*S` — `*p = v`;
                       `*p OP= v` is refused; an untyped value is stored at sizeof(S) bytes, at most 8
    A source that is not a struct value — an untyped pointer, `alloc(..)`, a Str-returning call —
    still binds the handle. A struct value of a DIFFERENT type into a handle is refused by name
    (`cannot copy 'r' into a variable of a different struct/vector type: 'a'`).

WHAT IT WAS (every release before 6.6.16, x86 and aarch64 alike): each assignment arm stored ONE
word:
    q = a;      q.x = a's ADDRESS, q.y stale (printed 1397460178042889 where 12 is right)
    a = q;      a's slot = q.x's VALUE, the next a.x SIGSEGV'd; same for a = G, a = mk(..),
                a = b.v, a = q.dbl(), a = l + r, G = q
    a = q;      from a by-value PARAMETER: a pointed at the CALLER's struct (a.x = 9 wrote it)

THE ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE:
    var q: P3 = a;   # declaration: q takes its storage class FROM a. From a pointer-mode local
                     # or global -> a second pointer (alias). From a PARAMETER -> a COPY, and a
                     # `p: *P3` parameter counts (the 6.6.11 by-value parameter copy-init).
    var q: P3; q = a;# assignment: q is already inline -> a COPY
    The declaration chooses q's layout; an assignment cannot change it. For a private copy of a
    handle's struct, declare first and assign.

ONE SHAPE CHANGED MEANING: smuggling a handle through an inline struct variable —
    var w: P3; w = a; f(w);     # f(v) untyped, reads load64(v + 8)
now passes w's first FIELD (w is a real copy). Pass `a`, or `&w`. Nothing in the ecosystem did it.
Also: two `*T` parameters, `a = b;` — a rebind now (it copied `*b` into `*a`).

6.7.10 — EVERY SIZE, AND MORE `*T` SOURCES (the user's decision 6: struct copies are size-independent):
    `l = p`, `G = p`, `l = q` copy `*p` / `*q`; `q = r`, `p = l`, `p = G`, `self = o` and `p = <a call /
    method / operator / if-expression / field struct result>` copy INTO the pointee — for a struct of 8 B
    or less too. Through 6.7.9 no struct variable of that size was a handle (see the handle line,
    struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710) and a `*T` of one was no struct operand:
    `l = p` / `G = p` stored the POINTER into the first field and `q = r` through a `q: *S8` parameter
    REBOUND q (the caller's struct never changed) — each silently.
    Also copy sources since 6.7.10, at every size (they stored the pointer word into the first field, over
    8 B too): a `*T` GLOBAL (`l = GP`, `G = GP`), a call or method declared `: *T` (`l = gp()`), a `*T`
    field (`l = w.p`), and pointer arithmetic on a `*T` (`l = p + 1`, `l = (p + 1)`, `l = 1 + GP`,
    `G = q - 1`: the struct the result points at). Into a `*T` or a handle they REBIND (`q = GP` repoints
    q; `p = p + 1` still steps). A `*U` of another struct is refused by name (`l = pu`, `l = GU`,
    `l = pu + 1` compiled and stored a word); a `*i64` / an untyped address into a struct stores its word.
    6.7.11 FIXED the 6.7.10 known defects (ST-2, ST-3, ST-4, ST-8): a GENERIC call declared `: *T` is a struct
    source (`l = gp<S>()`, or inferred when the arguments infer T = S); a struct-typed FIELD destination copies
    from these sources (`o.s = GP`, `o.s = gp()`, `o.s = w.p` stored the pointer word); an ASSIGNMENT from a
    dereference copies (`l = *p`, `G = *p`, `o.s = *p` stored one word; and `q = *p` into a `*S` writes q's
    POINTEE — it rebound q to the struct's first word). And `GP = l` (a typed `*S` GLOBAL destination) is
    settled (D2): it COPIES into `*GP`, as a `*S` local or parameter does; only `*S` / handle / untyped sources
    rebind it (`GP = q`, `GP = &x`). struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711. Gate: tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_pointer_source_copy.tcyr,
    small_struct_pointer_copy.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.7.10] (B7-1c, OPS-3, OPS-6).

[[entries]]
name = "struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.16 a parameter annotated with a plain struct is a VALUE at every width: over 8 bytes the struct still travels by address, but the callee copies it into its own frame on entry, so a write to the parameter never reaches the caller — whatever the argument (local, global, pointer-mode local, forwarded parameter, typed self, generic instance, fn pointer, field, top level). `p: *T` (called `f(&x)`) and an untyped self are the spellings for a callee that mutates. An `async fn` refuses such a parameter by name."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "by-value", "parameters", "copy", "self", "async", "abi", "v6.6.16"]
---
THE RULE (decided by the user, 2026-10-02):

    fn bump(p: P3): i64 { p.x = p.x + 1; return p.x; }
    var q: P3 = mk3(3, 0, 0);
    bump(q); bump(q);          # 4, 4 — and q.x is still 3

    fn bumpp(p: *P3): i64 { p.x = p.x + 1; return p.x; }
    bumpp(&q); bumpp(&q);      # 4, 5 — q.x is 5: a pointer parameter IS the caller's struct

  The same for a 16 B struct, an inline global (in a fn or at top level), a pointer-mode local
  (`var h: P3 = alloc(24);` — its heap object stays put), a parameter passed on, a typed
  `self: P3` (`q.bump()` == `P3_bump(q)`), a generic `bumpg<T>(p: T)` with T = P3, a call through
  `fncall1(&bump, &q)`, a field (`bump(b.v)`) and a top-level `take(BX.v)`. Untyped parameters,
  untyped `self`, `p: *T`, `Str` / `Result` / `Option` / `Tagged` (heap handles) and structs of
  8 bytes or less (values in a register) are unchanged. ⚠ Since 6.7.14 (ST-5) `Result` / `Option` /
  `Tagged` are handles BY NAME only while the program declares no struct of that name: a program's
  own `struct Result { .. }` is a plain struct, COPIED into a by-value parameter like any other (a heap
  handle of it passed to `x: Result` no longer aliases the caller's object — use `x: *Result`; `h = t`
  copies and `return h;` from a `: Result` fn returns the struct — kept by the user's 6.7.15 D56).
  `Str` stays a handle by name, whatever declares it.

  6.7.13 — AN INLINE `Str` IS ITS HEADER'S ADDRESS (the user's D7 (1); CHANGELOG [6.7.13] ST-14, ST-14r):
  an INLINE `Str` — `var t: Str = Str { .. }`, or an inline `Str` global — is its header's ADDRESS
  wherever a `Str` value is taken: `f(t)` into `s: Str` (the stdlib's included), `fp(t)` into `p:
  *Str`, `h = t`, and `var h: Str = t;` / `var h = t;`, which BIND h to t's header (no copy, no
  allocation — a write through h changes t; to copy, `Str { t.data, t.len }` or `str_new(t.data,
  t.len)`). `return t;` from a `: Str` fn, and a frame's `t` stored into a `Str` global / field /
  literal element / through a `*Str`, are refused by name ("… would dangle (return a handle:
  str_new(data, len))"); a global's and a capture's never are. Through 6.7.12 an inline `Str` passed
  as a `Str` was its first word (the data pointer), so the callee read the string's bytes as its
  header. 6.7.13 left three shapes open — a bare `var t: Str;`, untyped positions, an escape through a
  bound handle — each decided in 6.7.14, below.

  6.7.14 — THE INLINE `Str`, DECIDED (the user's D18 (c), D20 (a), D21 (a), D22 (b), D23 (b); CHANGELOG
  [6.7.14] ST-12 .. ST-16):
  - A BARE `var t: Str;` IS AN INLINE `Str` TOO (D20 (a), ST-12): its name is its header's address,
    `f(t)` passes its header, it binds handles, and its escapes (`return t;`, `G = t`, `w.s = t`, a
    literal element, `*ps = t`) are refused "… would dangle". Through 6.7.13 its bare name was its
    first word (6.6.16's pun) — to keep a stand-in handle, declare it `var t = a;`.
  - `t = a` FROM A `Str` HANDLE COPIES THE 16-BYTE HEADER (D20 (a), ST-12): from a handle local,
    parameter or global, a `: Str` call or method (`t = str_from(..)`, `t = h.clone()`) or a `Str` field,
    wrapped, in a for step, at top level. Through 6.7.13 it stored a's address into t.data and left
    t.len stale. An untyped source keeps the one-word store.
  - UNTYPED POSITIONS KEEP THE DATA WORD, BY DECISION (D18 (c), "only where a type says Str"):
    `fncallN`, `callptr`, `vec_push`, a tuple element, an untyped if-expression branch — pass `&t`
    there. So `lib/str.cyr`'s `Str_eq` / `Str_cat` / `Str_contains` / `Str_starts_with` /
    `Str_ends_with` take a `Str` operand (ST-13): `h.eq(t)` with an inline t, and `h.eq("lit")` /
    `h.contains("lit")` (a literal is wrapped as a `Str`), compute what `str_eq` does — they read the
    operand's bytes as a header through 6.7.13. A cstring VARIABLE there is still a word (wrap it with
    `str_from`).
  - `var p: *Str = t;` takes t's header, an alias (ST-13; it took the data word).
  - AT TOP LEVEL: `var H: Str = gt;` (gt an inline `Str` global; the zone or after the first statement)
    binds an 8-byte handle to gt's static header — `H.len = 3` writes gt.len (D21 (a), ST-14; through
    6.7.13 H was a 16-byte copy); the UNTYPED `var H = gt;` keeps gt's first word (D22 (b), ST-15), so
    `f(H)` reads garbage — annotate it. A gt declared below H was still refused "cannot copy-init" through
    6.7.14; since 6.7.15 it binds (D17 — below).
  - A HANDLE BOUND TO A FRAME'S t IS `&t` AND DANGLES WITH THE FRAME — documented, not refused (D23 (b),
    ST-16): `var h: Str = t; return h;` compiles, as `var p = &t; return p;` does. Return
    `str_new(t.data, t.len)`.

  6.7.15 — `*Str` SOURCES AND DESTINATIONS (the user's D14 – D17; CHANGELOG [6.7.15] ST-5 .. ST-10, ST-7r):
  - A ZONE `var H: Str = gt;` WITH gt DECLARED BELOW binds H to gt's header (D17, ST-5), as with gt above (`H.len = 3`
    writes gt.len); gt's address is static, so the bind needs no layout pass 1 could not see. It was "cannot copy-init
    'H' from a global declared below it". After the first statement a global declared below is still undefined there.
  - AN INLINE `Str` ASSIGNED INTO A `*Str` TAKES ITS HEADER'S ADDRESS (D16, ST-6), as `var p: *Str = t;` does: `p = t`,
    `p = gt`, `GP = gt` — wrapped, in a for step, at top level, with no warning. Through 6.7.14 they stored the data
    word (`str_len(p)` read the string's bytes as a header). A fn's own `GP = t` into a `*Str` GLOBAL is refused
    "cannot store the inline Str 't' into a global - its header lives in this frame and would dangle (store a handle:
    str_new(data, len))"; a closure's captured copy is not. A `*Str` FIELD destination (`w.p = gt`) and `*pp = gt`
    through a `**Str` still stored the data word through 6.7.15; since 6.7.16 they take the header's address (ST-4 —
    below).
  - A `*Str` DEREFERENCE INTO A `Str` PARAMETER passes the header the pointer points at (ST-7): `str_len(*p)`,
    `str_len(**pp)` (passes `*pp`), `f(*w.p)`, `h.eq(*p)` — any `*Str`-typed operand, wrapped, a tail call, a closure,
    top level. Through 6.7.14 it passed the data pointer as the header, silently (the filed repro 5500 for 5555). ⚠
    `var p: *Str = &h;` with h a `Str` HANDLE types the handle's SLOT (`&h` is the slot; kept by 6.7.16's D55):
    `str_len(*p)` now reads the
    slot as the header (0; it read h's length) — write `var p: *Str = h;`. A generic's `x: T` at T = Str
    (`gg<Str>(*p)`) still passed the data word through 6.7.15; the header since 6.7.16 (GB-21 — below).
  - `t = p` AND `t = *p` WITH p A `*Str` COPY THE HEADER INTO AN INLINE `Str` (D14, ST-8) — also `**pp`, `*w.p`,
    `*gsp()`, `*(p + 0)`, into a local or an inline global, wrapped, in a for step, a closure, at top level — as `t = a`
    from a handle (6.7.14's D20 (a)) and `var t: Str = *p;` do. Through 6.7.14 they stored one word and left t.len
    stale (22 for 55). A `Str` HANDLE destination is not this rule: `h = a` stays a rebind. `t = *pp` with pp a `**Str`
    (one word) and `h = *p` into a handle (the data word) stayed open through 6.7.15 — closed in 6.7.16 (ST-2, D20 —
    below).
  - AN IF-EXPRESSION OF HANDLES INTO AN INLINE `Str` KEEPS THE ONE-WORD STORE, documented (D15, ST-9): `t = if (c) { a }
    else { b };` stores the chosen handle's address into t.data, with the typed-pointer warning — an if-expression's
    value is untyped, as for every inline struct. Assign in each branch.
  - A METHOD ON A `Str` FIELD parses (ST-10): `w.s.len()`, `w.s.clone()`, `w.s.eq(x)` — method_chains_v6617.

  6.7.16 — EVERY `Str` SHAPE BY ITS DESTINATION (the user's D19, D20, D71, D73, D74; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] ST-2 – ST-4,
  SS-8 – SS-14, GB-21). The rule: a `Str` HANDLE or a `*Str` destination (a name, a field, a literal element, through a
  `**Str`, a `*Str` parameter, a `: Str` / `: *Str` return) takes a header's ADDRESS — a rebind; an INLINE `Str`
  destination COPIES the 16-byte header (6.7.15's D14). So:
  - `t = *pp` with pp a `**Str` COPIES into an inline `Str` (ST-2): a local, `var t: Str;`, an inline global, a for step,
    wrapped, `*pp` of a parameter / global / field / `: **Str` call, `**ppp`, a capture, top level (t.data took the
    header's address, t.len stayed stale). `var u: Str = *pp;` is u's own copy too (D74, SS-10), and at top level
    `var U: Str = *GPP;` / `var V: Str = GP;` copy (6.7.15's D17 (a) / D18 (a): "S may be Str").
  - `h = *p` INTO A `Str` HANDLE REBINDS h to the header p points at (D20, ST-3): `h == p`, a write through h is seen
    through p, no warning (it took the data word, with the typed-pointer warning).
  - `*p` of a `*Str` IS p wherever a handle or a `*Str` slot takes it (SS-8, SS-11; D20 carried, D73): a `Str` field
    (`w.s = *p`), a literal's `Str` field (`W { *p, 1 }`), `return *p;` from a `: Str` or a `: *Str` fn, `*q = *p`,
    `**qq = *p`, `*pp = *p`, a `*Str` name / declaration / field / literal element, a `s: *Str` argument. Only an inline
    destination copies (`t = *p`). To copy a header through a `*Str`: `q.data = p.data; q.len = p.len;`.
  - AN INLINE `Str` INTO A `*Str` FIELD, A LITERAL'S `*Str` ELEMENT OR THROUGH A `**Str` takes its header's address
    (ST-4, SS-9): `w.p = gt`, `GW.p = gt`, `pw.p = gt`, a `: *W` call's field, `*pp = gt`, `W { gt, 1 }` — as `p = gt`
    does since 6.7.15's D16. A FRAME's inline `Str` is refused there, a local struct's field too (D19): "... its header
    lives in this frame and would dangle (store a handle: str_new(data, len))"; a capture's copy stores its environment
    address. An inline `Str` returned from a `: *Str` fn is its header's address, a frame's `return t;` there refused as
    would-dangle (SS-14).
  - `*(&t)` of an inline `Str` IS t (D71, SS-13 — typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617); a `Str` handle's name (a handle
    global, a captured handle) into a `*Str` draws no typed-pointer warning (SS-12).
  - A `*Str` DEREFERENCE INTO A GENERIC'S `x: T` AT T = Str passes the header p points at (GB-21): explicit, inferred,
    wrapped, `*w.p`, `**pp`, `*gsp()`, a capture, a forwarder, a generic method, top level (it passed the data word).
  - ⚠ Filed unplaced at the 6.7.16 close (`Str` shapes beyond D19 / D20 / D71 / D73): `h = mkq();` into a `Str` handle
    (SIGSEGV), a nested literal's `*p` leaf, a captured inline `Str`'s `*(&t) = v` (one word into the capture's copy), a
    `Str` field's NAMED init in a nested literal (refused where the positional form compiles), and a top-level untyped
    `var bb = str_from("x");` (untyped where the fn line types it `Str`).

WHAT IT WAS (every release before 6.6.16): the parameter was the CALLER's struct. `bump(q)` twice
gave 4 then 5 and left q.x at 5, for every argument form above; only a field argument inside a fn
was copied (6.6.12, caller-side — top level had no frame for it). The guide documented the alias.

HOW: the copy is made in the CALLEE's prologue (`_sptr_params_localize`, parse_fn.cyr), after
`_stkp_flush` has homed every parameter slot — a stack-passed pointer (SysV 7th+, aarch64 9th+,
Win64 5th+) is not in its slot before that. One site covers every caller, fn pointers included,
and the calling convention is unchanged. From there the parameter is an ordinary inline local.

CONSEQUENCES worth knowing:
  - A bare `q` where an untyped value is expected (`var k = q;`, `raw(q)` into an untyped
    parameter, `load64(q)`) is the struct's FIRST FIELD, as for any inline struct local. Before
    6.6.16 it was the caller's address. Write `&q` for the address (of the copy).
  - The copy happens on entry, so passing something that is not a struct's address (`take(0)`
    for a `q: P3`, e.g. to bind a generic's T) faults there even if the body never reads `q`.
  - An `async fn` with such a parameter is refused: "async fn parameter 'p' is a struct passed by
    value, which an `async fn` does not capture yet — pass a pointer to it (`p: *Pt`)". The body
    runs at force time, so an entry copy would read the caller's struct late.
  - Cost: one byte-exact copy per call (8/4/2/1-byte chunks) plus the struct's size in frame.
    cycc's own source declares no such parameter (self-compile unchanged).

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_arg_is_copy.tcyr, struct_param_byvalue_copy.tcyr,
struct_ptrmode_assign_copy.tcyr; tests/gates/frontend/coroutine_midbody_suspend.sh (the async
refusal). Compiler side: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
`a_cap_is_declared_in_one_place_and_believed_in_five_sweep_every_consumer` (the `*T` record moved
out of a 64-bit slot mask).

[[entries]]
name = "pointer_fields_returns_and_linked_lists_v6617"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.17 `*T` is a type wherever a type is written: a struct / union field `next: *Node`, a return type `fn f(): *Node` (and a multi-value return element), a local or global `var p: *Node`. A `*Struct` local, global, field or call result takes dot syntax like a `p: *Struct` parameter always did, and a field chain goes THROUGH a pointer field, so a linked list is written the obvious way"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["pointers", "typed-pointer", "structs", "field-chains", "linked-list", "v6.6.17"]
---
Before 6.6.17 `next: *Node` and `fn f(): *Node` were `expected identifier, got '*'`, a `var p: *Node`
local was `no struct type in scope` at `p.val`, and `a.next.val` was `expected ';', got '.'`.

    struct Node { val; next: *Node; }       # a field may point at its OWN struct
    fn link(n: *Node, v, head: *Node): *Node {
        n.val = v;
        n.next = head;
        return n;
    }
    fn push(head: *Node, v): *Node { return link(alloc(sizeof(Node)), v, head); }
    fn sum(head: *Node): i64 {
        var s = 0;
        var p: *Node = head;
        while (p != 0) { s = s + p.val; p = p.next; }   # the canonical walk
        return s;
    }
    var h: *Node = push(push(push(0, 1), 2), 3);
    h.next.next.val = 5;                    # chains through pointer fields, reads and writes
    var v = h.next.val + push(0, 7).val;    # ... and on a `: *Node` call result
    # (verified on the merged 6.6.17 tree: sum(h) == 10, v == 9, no warning)

`alloc(..)` returns an untyped word, so `var n: *Node = alloc(..);` warns `assigning non-pointer to
typed pointer`; hand it to a `*Node` parameter (as `push` does) or a `: *Node` fn instead.

RULES:
- `p = q` on two `*Node` REBINDS the pointer; it never copies the struct.
- T is resolved by name; an unknown T is refused (`unknown type 'X' as the target of pointer`).
  T may be the struct being declared or one declared BELOW (two structs can point at each other):
  such a field records T and is sized when it is used.
- `*Box<i32>` points at the INSTANCE (`sizeof(Box<i32>)`), not the base generic.
- The value a `*T` field load, a call, a method call or an explicit generic call yields is a
  `*T` — `p + n` steps `sizeof(T)` (see `typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617`).

6.7.15 (CHANGELOG [6.7.15] GN-6, GN-7, GN-8, ST-1; the user's D8 (a)):
- A GENERIC STRUCT'S `p: *T` FIELD HOPS in its instance where T binds a struct (GN-6): with `struct Pb<T> { p: *T;
  n; }` and `b: Pb<Pt>`, `b.p.y`, `b.p.y = 3`, `b.p.m()` — and inside `fn g<T>(b: Pb<T>)` — read through the field as
  a `p: *Pt` field does. They were "expected ';', got '.'" (the instance's field kept T's step but no pointee struct).
  Steps unchanged.
- A POINTER FIELD `p: *G<..>` (G generic) HAS ITS TYPE ARGUMENTS CHECKED AT THE FIELD, once, used or not (D8 (a), GN-7):
  `struct H { p: *Box<Zork>; n; }` is refused "unknown type 'Zork' as a type argument (...)" at the field, where it
  compiled while unused (the late sizing read the list only at a use); `p: *Box<1>` "a type argument is a type name,
  got number 1". Every type-argument refusal applies there — a tuple, a slice, `u128`, `Result` / `Option` / `Tagged` /
  `cstring`, an enum (beyond D8's text, which named an unknown name and a number; each already refused on a by-value
  `b: Box<..>` field — kept by 6.7.16's D36). A use adds nothing. A struct declared FURTHER DOWN is a name there, so two structs pointing at
  each other through `*Box<B>` / `*Box<A>` still work. A NON-generic head's list (`p: *Pt<i64>`) was accepted and
  ignored through 6.7.15; since 6.7.16 it is "'Pt' is not a generic struct: it takes no type arguments" at the field,
  its pointee above or below (D7, GS-11).
- `fn mk(): **Box<Later>` WITH `Later` DECLARED BELOW compiles (GN-8: pass 1 leaves the `**` pin to pass 2 while the
  type arguments do not resolve), as `: *Box<Later>` has since 6.7.14 — it was refused "unknown type 'Later' as a type
  argument". And a CALL ABOVE such a fn reads the signature the definition reads (ST-1): `var t: Later = *mk();`,
  `take(*mk())`, `**mk()`, a `*Box<Later>` / `**Box<Later>` return and `mk().x` — they faulted (139) or were refused
  "it does not return a struct" through 6.7.14 (relaxed_fn_ordering).
- 6.7.16 — A `**G<..>` FIELD MINTS ITS INSTANCE AFTER THE STRUCT'S `}`, under the struct's own type arguments (GB-14): a
  generic struct's `qq: **Box<T>` / `next: **LL<T>` compiles and `*h.qq + 1` steps the instance; a `**Box<X>` field
  naming `Box<X>` first no longer breaks `Box<X>` (the instance was minted in the middle of the struct's field run and
  the next field overwrote it — its `sizeof` and fields wrong for the whole program).
- 6.7.16 — A FIELD OF TWO STARS OR MORE MAY POINT AT A STRUCT DECLARED BELOW (D103, FB-3), as the one-star `*B` field
  does (6.6.17): `struct A { k; p: **B; } struct B { x; }`, `p: **Box<B>`, `***B`, a union's field, a generic struct's
  `**B` field, and two structs pointing at each other through `**B` / `**A` compile; the pointee is sized where it is
  dereferenced — `*a.p + 1` steps `sizeof(B)`, `var q: *B = *a.p;`, `**a.p` into a `*B` parameter. Through 6.7.15 "type
  'B' is declared after its use" / "unknown type 'B' as a type argument". A by-value `B` field below is still refused;
  `**B<i64>` with B not generic gets D7's line.

⚠ NOT the same as a struct-typed local: `var p: Node = h;` with `h: *Node` COPIES the struct (a
walk written that way overwrites nodes) — at every size since 6.6.20 (it stored the POINTER at 8 B or
less), and 6.7.10 settled every `Struct = *Struct` ASSIGNMENT as a copy too, at every size (the user's
decision 6; `l = p` stored the pointer word at 8 B or less). To walk, write `var p: *Node`.

GATES: tests/tcyr/lang/pointer_field_return_types.tcyr (linked / chains / ptr_locals / call_ptr
groups, a linked-list walk), tests/gates/frontend/typed_pointer_warn_sign.sh axis 9. CHANGELOG
[6.6.17] (b3).

[[entries]]
name = "typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.17 `p + n`, `p - n`, `p += n` and `p -= n` on a `*T` step `sizeof(T)` bytes per element at EVERY site — local, parameter, closure capture, global in either zone, `*T` field, `: *T` result. `q - p` of two `*T` is the element count, a call result is a plain value unless the callee declares `: *T`, and `n + p` steps like `p + n`. Before, the step depended on where `p` was declared"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["pointers", "typed-pointer", "pointer-arithmetic", "sizeof", "behaviour-change", "v6.6.17"]
---
THE RULE (6.6.17, user decision):

    var buf: i64[8];
    var p: *i32 = &buf;
    p + 1          # &buf + 4      (sizeof(i32))
    var q: *Pt = arr;
    q + 2          # arr + 2 * sizeof(Pt)
    var e = (p + 3) - p;    # 3 — the ELEMENT count, a plain integer
    2 + p          # same as p + 2

- the same in a fn parameter `p: *T`, a closure capture of any depth, a global declared before
  or after the first statement, a `name: *T` field and a `fn f(): *T` result;
- `q - p` with different element sizes (`*i64 - *u8`) is refused by name — a pointer value
  carries only its step, so two types of one size cannot be told apart; `&a - &b` (untyped
  addresses) stays a byte difference;
- ⛔ a `*T` minus an UNTYPED var is `p - n`, NOT a byte difference: the var is read as
  an element COUNT. With `var base = &buf; var g: *i64 = &buf; g += 3;`, `g - base` is
  `g - base * 8`, a garbage address, with no diagnostic (measured 6.6.20: it equals
  `(base + 24) - base * 8` exactly, so its low byte moves run to run with ASLR). And
  `g - &buf` written directly is refused as `pointer difference of pointers with
  different element sizes`, even for an `i64[N]` array under a `*i64`. So the same
  subtraction gives an element count, a garbage address or a refusal depending on how
  the right side is spelled. To count ELEMENTS, subtract two `*T` (`var b: *i64 = &buf;
  g - b` == 3). For BYTES, bind BOTH sides to plain vars (`var x = g; x - base` == 24);
- a call's result is a plain value unless the callee declares `: *T`: with `p: *i64`,
  `f(p) + 1`, `load64(p) + 1`, `fncall1(&f, p) + 1` and `q.m(p) + 1` each used to add 8 (the
  expression scale was ambient state left by the last argument);
- a `*T` slot is always an 8-byte word. (`*p` loaded 8 bytes whatever T was until 6.7.8; since
  6.7.8 `*p` through a narrow `*T` is the element — pointer_subscripts_v678.)

WHAT IT REPLACED (measured on the 6.6.17 slot-open compiler): a local `*i8` / `*i16` / `*i32`
stepped 1 and every other local `*T` 8 (`*u8` and `*Pt` included); a parameter and a captured
`*T` 1 whatever T; a declaration-block global 8; a later global 1 / 2 / 4 / 8 by T; `p += n` 1
everywhere; `p + 1` on a `p: *f32` parameter was an f32 ADD of the pointer's bits and on a
`p: *Pt` it dispatched `Pt_add`. A `*T` parameter or capture also loaded as a non-pointer, so
`var q: *T = p;` warned `assigning non-pointer to typed pointer` — it no longer does.

MIGRATION: none measured — no `*T` declaration exists in the `src/` or `lib/` of 143 repos, so
every shape switched with no transition error (the user accepted that on 2026-10-05). The
teaching examples here use `*i64` locals, whose step is 8 under both rules.

`p[i]` on a `*T` was refused here (no element descriptor); it ships in 6.7.8, the step word now
carrying T's element kind as `ps | ed << 40` (pointer_subscripts_v678). `n + p` with a ONE-byte
`*u8` / `*i8` is that pointer since 6.7.9 (decision 1(b): `var q: *u8 = 1 + p;` is silent and
`(1 + p8) - q32` is refused — through 6.7.8 it warned and was a byte difference).
6.7.11 (D37, D15, D14): a ONE-BYTE STRUCT pointer (`pb: *B1`) is such a pointer too — `m + pb` and `0 + pb`
are `pb`, `var r: *B1 = 1 + pb;` is silent and `(1 + pb) - q32` is refused; and `&x` on either side of `+` is a
byte pointer (`n + &x` is `&x + n`: `var r: *u32 = 4 + &B;` is silent, `(4 + &B) - q32` refused). A NARROW
SCALAR (`u8` .. `i32`, local, global or parameter) is an INTEGER, never a pointer: `w + q` steps `w * sizeof(T)`,
`var r: *u32 = w;` warns, `w - q` compiles (through 6.7.10 a narrow LOCAL was a one-byte pointer: `w + q` added
`w` unscaled). `await f` and the saturating / checked `+| +? -| -?` give plain integers (with `q: *i32`,
`(q +| 1) + 1` is `q` + 2 bytes, was + 5; `(await f) + 1` adds 1). A multi-word closure capture stays an integer. GATES:
tests/tcyr/crossos/typed_pointer_step.tcyr, tests/gates/frontend/typed_pointer_warn_sign.sh axes
10-11. Supersedes the step table in field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
`typed_pointer_local_slot_v6616`. CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (b7).

6.7.13 — `*pp` OF A `**T` IS A `*T` (the user's D9 (a), ST-19; VC-11): through a `**T` local, parameter, global,
closure capture, field, `: **T` call, generic instance or `pp + n`, `*pp` is a known-`*T` producer like a name, a
field or a `: *T` call — `*pp + n` and `*pp += n` step `n` elements of `T`, `*pp - *qq` is an element count, `var q:
*T = *pp;` and `q = *pp;` are silent, and `**pp` through a narrow `T` (`**u8`, `**i32`, `**f32`) reads and writes the
element at its width. A `***T`'s `*ppp` is a `**T` and its `**ppp` a `*T`. `*pp - q` with `q` of another element size
(a `*u8`, or `&a`, a byte pointer) is refused "pointer difference of pointers with different element sizes; for a
byte count subtract plain addresses (`var a = p;`)" — copy untyped (`var a = *pp;`) for byte arithmetic. Through
6.7.12 `*pp` was an untyped word: `*(*pp + 1)` read 8 bytes one BYTE past the pointee, `**pp` read and wrote 8 bytes
whatever T, `*pp += 1` added 1, and `var q: *i64 = *pp;` warned. AS AN ARGUMENT (VC-11), `**pps` of a `**S` is the
struct: `take(**pps)` into a `*S` parameter (or a by-value S over 8 B) passes `*pps`; a `**Q` of another struct is
refused by name; a vector's `rd(**ppv)` into a `*V` passes the pointer. The pins are interned per STRUCT, so each
struct counts: 6.7.13 capped a program at 126 distinct pointee types of a pointer to a pointer (127 two-word structs
through `**S` were refused — they compiled on 6.7.12). SINCE 6.7.14 THE CAP IS 16383 (VC-1): the 16384th is refused
"too many pointer-to-pointer pointee types (max 16383)", an error, never a silent word; the pin is bit 62 (a "pinned"
flag) plus 14 bits (48 – 61) of the step word — free because a `**T`'s word names no vector. (Compile time grows with
the distinct pins — a linear intern through 6.7.14; hashed since 6.7.15 (VC-3), the pin numbers and outputs
unchanged.) A GENERIC's `pp: **T` parameter: 6.7.13 filed it as "the pin is read
without the binding" — the cause was another: the instance pinned right, and the IMPLICIT call bound T = i64 because
`**T` carried nothing. Since 6.7.14 (D40, VC-15) `st(pps)` with `pps: **P3` runs `st$P3`, so `*pp + 1` steps 24 and
`take(**pp)` passes the struct (generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310 — the `**T` carrier; through `(pps)` and
`pps + 0` too since 6.7.15, D22 / D23, and a `***T` carries T, D24).
6.7.14 — `**pp` THROUGH A `**S` IS THE STRUCT VALUE `*p` IS, AT EVERY USE (CHANGELOG [6.7.14] ST-7, ST-7r; 6.7.10's
decision 6 with 6.7.13's D9 (a)): through a `**S` local, parameter, field, global, capture, a `: **S` call (generic
too), `**(pp + n)` and `***ppp`, `var t: S = **pp;` copies (it bound a handle to S's first field and faulted), a
1..7-byte `**pp` is read byte-exact, `**pp + a` dispatches `S_add`, `l = **pp` copies, `return **pp;` returns the
struct; `**pp = u` copies `sizeof(S)` bytes and `**pp OP= v` is refused (6.7.11's D4; it stored / operated on one
word); an if-expression branch `{ **pp }` copies at 8 B or less and is refused over 8 B. `*pp` reads as the `*S` for
a returned value (`return *pp;` returns the struct), an operator's operand and a by-value argument. It did NOT yet as
the WHOLE SOURCE of a copy through 6.7.14 (`l = *pp`, `G = *pp` and `*q = *pp` stored the pointer word into the first
field, and `var t: S = *pp;` bound a handle) — since 6.7.15 it does (ST-2, below).
6.7.15 — `*pp` AS THE WHOLE SOURCE OF A COPY (CHANGELOG [6.7.15] ST-2, ST-3; no decision needed — 6.7.13's D9 (a)
with 6.7.11's copy through a `*S`): `*pp` of a `**S` as the WHOLE source copies the struct it points at, at every
size, as `l = p` does — `l = *pp`, `G = *pp` (in a fn and at top level), `*q = *pp`, `**qq = *pp`, a for step, and in a
fn `var t: S = *pp;` / `(*pp)` (they bound a handle: a write through t changed the original). Into a `*S` or a handle,
`q = *pp` stays the rebind `q = p` is. A `**Q`'s `*qq` into an `S` (kept by 6.7.16's D53: `l = *qq`,
`var t: P3 = *qq`, `*p = *qq`, a top-level `GT = *GQQ`) is refused by name, "cannot copy 'qq' into a variable of a different struct/vector type: 'l'"
(it stored the pointer; since 6.7.16 the refusal names it as spelled, '*qq' — ST-12). A TOP-LEVEL DECLARATION
`var G: S = *GPP;` bound a handle and a struct-typed FIELD destination `o.s = *pp` stored the pointer word through 6.7.15;
since 6.7.16 both copy at every size (D17 (a), ZN-11 — struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710; ST-1 —
below). And the
`**pp OP= v` refusal names its operand as written (ST-3): "the struct '*pp' points at … (write `**pp = **pp + b`)" —
it called pp itself the struct; `***ppp *= 2` names '**ppp'.

6.7.13 — THE TYPED-POINTER WARNING AT EVERY `*T` SITE, QUIET ON `0` (the user's D18 (a), RC-11; a diagnostic only —
no binary changes): "assigning non-pointer to typed pointer" fires at every `*T` site — a declaration in a fn, after
the first top-level statement and in the declaration zone (replayed and baked, `var p: *i64 = 3;` reported at its
`;`), an assignment to a `*T` local or a `p: *T` parameter of a scalar `T` (`p = x;`, `p = load64(..);`), a
`*Struct` local, a `*T` global. The literal `0` ALONE never warns (the null idiom, as the kind-0 compare-mix warning
exempts 0): `var p: *T = 0;`, `p = 0;`, `G = 0;` are quiet at every site; `0 - 0` warns (`(0)` did too, through
6.7.15 — quiet since 6.7.16, FE-17, below). Through 6.7.12 the
zone and the local / parameter assignments were silent and `= 0` warned in a fn. (A string literal into a `*u8`
warned — a false positive; quiet since 6.7.14, below.)
6.7.14 (the user's D53, D26; CHANGELOG [6.7.14] RC-13, RC-10, VC-9):
  - A STRING LITERAL ALONE INTO A BYTE POINTER (`*u8` / `*i8`) NEVER WARNS, at any site — a declaration, an assignment,
    a parameter, a global, the declaration zone: `var s: *u8 = "abc";`, `t = "xyz";`. Into another `*T` (`*i64`, a
    one-byte struct's `*B1`) a string literal still warned in 6.7.14 (D53's text says "never warn for a string
    literal"; the lane exempted only the byte pointers the filed issue names), and so did a string EXPRESSION
    (`"abc" + 1`, `("xyz")`) — both settled in 6.7.15 (D59, below). `p = &a[1];` is refused without the warning beside
    it. A vector local's assignment never warns (VC-9).
  - A KNOWN `&f` CALLEE DECLARED `: *T` IS A `*T` (D26, RC-10): `fncall0(&gp) + 1` steps one element (it moved one
    byte), and `var w: *P2 = fncall0(&gp);` is silent. A callee held in a variable stays a word (`fncall0(fp)` into a
    `*P2` still warns). Since 6.7.15 its ELEMENT is read too (D31): `*fncall0(&bp)` of a `*u8` reads and writes one
    byte — pointer_subscripts_v678.
6.7.15 (the user's D59, D21, D11; CHANGELOG [6.7.15] RC-6, RC-7, VC-13, VC-3, GN-5, GN-14):
  - NO STRING LITERAL WARNS INTO ANY `*T` (D59 — item 18 of the 6.7.14 changes beyond the decisions' text, "Apply D53
    as worded"; RC-6): `var s: *i64 = "abcdefgh";`, `*f64`, `*i32`, a one-byte struct's `*B1`, a `*P2` global — in a fn,
    at top level (both zones) and in an assignment, as the literal `0` never does. Nor does the literal plus or minus
    ONE integer operand (an integer or char literal, or a single name), or the literal wrapped whole (RC-7; kept by
    6.7.16's D63):
    `"abc" + 1`, `"abc" - n`, `("xyz")`, `(("x"))`, `("abc" + 1)`, `("abc") + 1`. Warnings only, no binary changes. Still
    warning: any other string expression (`"abc" + x * 2`), `'a' + 0`, and `(0)` through 6.7.15 (the literal 0's
    exemption had the same one-token shape; quiet since 6.7.16, FE-17 — below).
  - `*(&p)` OF A TYPED POINTER IS p, ITS TYPE KEPT (D21 A, VC-13; its store and step kept by 6.7.16's D62) — p a local, parameter, capture or global, inside any
    parentheses: `**(&pb)` through a narrow pointer reads AND writes the element's width, as `*pb` does; `*(&p) + n`
    steps elements; `(*(&ps)).y` reads the field and `**(&ps)` is the struct (an operator dispatches `S_op`); with
    `p: *f64v2`, `**(&p)` is the vector at every vector destination. Through 6.7.14 `*(&p)` was the untyped word:
    `**(&pb)` of a `*u8` read and wrote 8 bytes, `*(&p) + 1` stepped one byte. Unchanged: `&p` itself (a byte pointer,
    6.7.11's D15), `*(&p + 0)` (the word at that address) and a handle's `*(&h)` (the slot's word). So `*(&x)` is x for
    every x the compiler types — a struct value (6.7.13), a vector (6.7.13), a typed pointer (6.7.15); only a handle's
    `*(&h)` is the slot's untyped word, and `&x + n` is always a byte pointer. Two exceptions stayed open through 6.7.15
    — the compound `*(&pi) += 1` stepped one byte, and `*(&GQ)` of a packed `i32` global read 8 bytes — both closed in
    6.7.16 (ST-10, D21 / ST-11 — below).
  - INSIDE A GENERIC: a `var t: *T` local is T's struct receiver in an instance, a scalar T stepping sizeof(T) (GN-5);
    and an INFERRED call over a `*i32` binds i32 (D11, GN-14), so `p + 1` steps 4 inside the generic (it ran the i64
    base and stepped 8) — generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310.
  - The pointer-to-pointer pin intern is hashed (VC-3): a unit's pins, up to 16383, intern in linear time; pin numbers
    and outputs unchanged.
6.7.16 (the user's D17 (a), D21, D70, D71, D72; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] ST-1, ST-6, ST-10 – ST-12, SS-13, SS-16, SS-17,
FE-17, FE-18, FF-21, FF-22, FF-24, ZN-11):
  - `*(&x)` IS x FOR EVERY x THE COMPILER TYPES. A narrow integer — an `i8` .. `i32` / `u8` .. `u32` local or packed
    global, wrapped, `*&x`, a for step, an argument — reads and writes x's width (D21, ST-11: `var y = *(&GQ);` read GQ
    and the next global, `*(&GQ) = 7` zeroed it); a narrow PARAMETER too, now that it is narrowed (D72, SS-16 —
    multi_width_types); an `f64` / `f32` / `bool` keeps its type (D70, SS-17: float reads, stores and `OP=`, an f32 at 4
    bytes, a bool's checks — `*(&b) = 5` / `*(&b) += 1` refused); an inline `Str` is t (D71, SS-13: a handle, its header
    passed, copied, rebound, its address into a `*Str`; `*(&t) = v` is `t = v`, `*(&t) OP= v` refused; a frame's t keeps
    every refusal). `*(&pi) OP= n` of a typed pointer steps as `pi OP= n` does (ST-10). Still the word: a closure's
    capture (its environment copy — ⚠ `*(&t) = v` of a captured inline `Str` writes one word, filed unplaced), `&x`
    itself and `*(&x + 0)` (byte pointers), `*(&n)` of an i64 and a handle's slot.
  - `o.s = *pp` INTO A STRUCT-TYPED FIELD copies the struct `*pp` points at, at 3 / 8 / 16 / 24 B byte-exact (ST-1; it
    stored the pointer word into the field's first word); another struct's `*qq` into a field is refused "cannot copy
    '*qq' into a struct field of a different struct type: 's'"; a `*S` field still takes the pointer.
  - A KNOWN `&f` CALLEE IS TYPED BY EVERY READER AS ITS DIRECT CALL (ST-6; 6.7.14's D26): `(fncall0(&gp)).b` /
    `(*callptr(&gp)).b` read the field (refused), `var t: P2 = *fncall0(&gp);` copies (it bound a handle on the first
    field and faulted), `l = fncall0(&gp)`, `o.s = fncall0(&gp)`, `m = *fncall0(&gp)` copy (they stored the pointer
    word), `take(*fncall0(&gp))` passes the struct (it faulted), `return *fncall0(&gp);` compiles. The `f` of `&f`
    resolves as the `&` operator does (a local named f is that variable); a callee held in a variable stays a word.
  - THE TYPED-POINTER WARNING (warnings only): `(0)` / `((0))` is the null literal at every `*T` site (FE-17; `(0) + 0`,
    `(1)` still warn); a call declared `: Str` into a `*Str` declaration or global is its pointer (FE-18; another struct's
    call keeps the warning); a `: Str` field, a `Str` name, a call result's `Str` field or method, and those wrapped
    whole, are a `*Str`'s value in every site (FF-22); into a `*T` local or parameter a value is exempt only when its
    declared type is T — `p = str_from(..);` into a `*P` warns now, as the declaration does (FF-21); a classic-for step
    into a `*T` warns as the statement does (FF-24).

[[entries]]
name = "method_chains_v6617"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.17 a method call's result takes `.field` and `.method(..)`, chained to any depth, as a value and as a bare statement — `s.clone().cat(t)`, `p.bump().bump().sum()`, `mkp(3).bump().sum()`. Before, the `.` after a method call was a syntax error in every position"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["methods", "chains", "call-result", "Str", "structs", "v6.6.17"]
---
    var u: Str = s.clone().cat(t);       # a Str-returning chain
    var n = p.big().back().bump().sum(); # struct results of every return class
    p.bump().mu();                       # statement form; #must_use applies to the LAST method

Each link's result is held the way a free call's result has been since 6.6.12 (a > 16 B struct
in its retptr temp, 9-16 B in a temp, <= 8 B inline, a `Str` in a pointer-mode slot), so the
receiver is never touched.

REFUSED BY NAME: at TOP LEVEL a chain on a struct-returning method (`'T_m' returns a struct by
value, and a struct result needs storage in a fn's frame`); a chain on a method that returns
neither a struct nor a `*T` (`cannot take a field of the result of 'T_m': it does not return a struct`).
Since 6.7.11 (ST-19, D43) a method returning `*T` CHAINS — `h.get().b`, `.q.b`, `.m()`, through a dyn and a
generic method; `h.get().b = 5` is refused as `get2(&h).b = 5` is (before, the false reason above).

THE 6.6.17 "STILL NOT SUPPORTED" LIST IS CLOSED: a method on an inline struct FIELD
(`b.v.sum()`, `self` the field's address) is a call since 6.7.0 (A5), a method on a `Str` field
(`h.name.len()`) since 6.7.15 (ST-10, below), compound assignment on a field (`h.n += 1`) since
6.7.5 (compound_assignment_every_lvalue_v675), and since 6.7.16 (ST-5) an UNTYPED `var k =
h.clone();` in a fn — a chain ending in a method declared `: Str` too (`w.s.clone()`,
`h.clone().clone()`, `h.sub(1, 3)`) — is a `Str` handle, as `var k = str_clone(h);` is:
`k.len()` / `k.cat(..)` compile (they were "no struct type in scope for 'k'"). ⚠ No decision
covers its overload half (on the filed keep-or-restore list): a call with a `_str` overload
now takes k — `println(k)` / `show(k)` reach `println_str` / `show_str` (they printed the
header's bytes as a C string). Top level keeps the word (`var K = H.clone(); K.len()` refused).
A method's 8 B struct result (6.7.0 A5) and a 16 / 24 B by-value result already typed the
local.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/method_chain.tcyr, tests/gates/frontend/call_result_field.sh C1-C3.
CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (b4).

6.7.14 (FE-23) — A DIAGNOSTIC NAMES A METHOD AS THE SOURCE SPELLS IT: every call-site message (the struct-type
and pointer refusals, arity, named arguments, `#pure`, `#deprecated`, `#must_use`, `private`, the destructure,
"undefined function") names a method `T.m` — never the mangled `T_m` / `T_Tr_m` — and a generic call `g<i32>`
(`g2<i32, i64>`, `gx<Box<i64>>`), never the instance `g$i32`: "… in a call to 'S.ri' - pass &b for its address",
"undefined function 'S.nope'". A method call's ARITY counts the arguments in its parentheses, without `self`:
`s.two(1, 2)` on `fn two(self, a)` is "'S.two' expects 1 argument, got 2" (it was "'S_two' expects 2 arguments, got
3"); the free spelling `S_two(&s, 1, 2)` is named `S.two` and counts its `&s`; a method declaring no parameter keeps
the receiver in its count (`g.zero()` on `fn zero()`: "'MG.zero' expects 0 arguments, got 1"). A free fn named
`S_x` (no impl) and a `mod` fn `mm_f` keep their names. Diagnostics only. The generics refusals naming a generic
METHOD stayed mangled through 6.7.14 (`generic 'P_pick' has no i64 ... instance`); since 6.7.15 (GN-24) they read
`generic 'P.pick' …` / `generic 'P.bm': its type argument does not implement …` — the free spelling `P_pick(p, 9)`
keeps `P_pick`. And a destination's struct refusal names a method result `Pt.dup` too (VC-10 —
struct_argument_type_checked_v673).

6.7.15 (ST-10) — A METHOD ON A `Str` FIELD: `w.s.len()`, `var k = w.s.clone();`, `w.s.eq(w.s)` with `s: Str` a
struct field parse (they were "expected ';', got '.'" on every target). A `Str` field is a HANDLE (one pointer slot
holding the header's address, 6.6.17), so the method's `self` is the field's VALUE — the header it holds, as
`str_len(w.s)` passes it — unlike a nested INLINE struct field (6.7.0 A5), whose `self` is the field's address. Every
receiver shape: a local, a `*W` local or parameter, two levels down (`o.w.s`), through a `*T` field (`o.pw.s`), a
call's or method's result (`sf_mk().s`, `w.me().s`), `self.s` in an impl, a global, a capture, a wrapped receiver
(`(w).s`, `(*p).s`), a statement (a write through `self` lands in the string w.s names), a chain on the result
(`w.s.clone().cat(w.s)`), top level.

6.7.16 (CHANGELOG [6.7.16] SB-1, SB-5, SB-6; no decision — a compiler failing valid cyrius): every pair / multi-value
lookahead takes a method ending a chain (SB-1) — `var c, d = w.p.m2();`, `w.p.rs(3)?`, a tuple capture, a dyn source
(ret2_rethi, stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555, tuples_v677, dyn_v678). The `.field` refusal on a method's
result names the method as the source spells it — "cannot take a field of the result of 'P.sc': it does not return a
struct" (it printed `T_m`, against FE-23 above) — and a multi-value one's hint spells the call: `var t: (i64, i64) =
w.p.m2(..);` (SB-6). A refused value's chain — an explicit list `.m<..>(..)`, `(..)`, `[..]` — is stepped whole, so the
refusal is the one error (SB-5).

[[entries]]
name = "nested_closure_capture_v6617"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.17 a closure nested inside another closure captures the variables of EVERY enclosing scope — the outermost fn's locals and params, the outer closure's params and locals, and what the outer closure itself captured — by value at construction, at any depth. The old guide rule `captured closures are flat` was a compiler limit written down as a rule, and is gone"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["closures", "capture", "nested", "scoping", "v6.6.17"]
---
    fn mk(b) {
        var g = |x| {
            var k = |y| { return y + b; };   # b: two levels out
            return callptr(k, x) + b;        # the outer keeps its own capture too
        };
        return g;
    }
    var add3 = |a| |b| |c| a + b + c;        # curried, three levels

- capture is BY VALUE at construction, as for one level (closures_v637_v638): a capture of a
  capture copies the word(s) out of the enclosing closure's env;
- a `var` declared in a block of a closure body (an `if`, a loop, a `for` header) hides an
  enclosing name only INSIDE that block — before 6.6.17 it hid it for the whole body, and a
  later read silently took a GLOBAL of the same name;
- a struct (by value or pointer-mode), a `cstring` and a `Str` capture keep their own type
  through every level, so dispatch on them is right;
- a nested `fn` inside a closure body is still refused by name (6.6.16) — define it at top level;
- 6.7.16: a capture of a capture shadows a same-named struct GLOBAL for `.field` / `.m()` too (D96, FB-1), an untyped
  capture's `.field` is the untyped local's "no struct type in scope" (GB-13), and a captured f64 binds `T = f64` (D3) —
  closures_v637_v638.

GATE: tests/tcyr/crossos/closure_nested_capture.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (b1). Compiler side:
the capture tables (`_cl_ncap`, `_cl_cap_slots`, `_cl_cap_w`) are per-closure state and are
saved / restored around each nested literal (`_cl_ctx_save` / `_cl_ctx_restore`).

6.7.11: the capture rule for names the body declares (a for-in variable, a local `const`, a destructure's later
names) and the captured-name write message are in closures_v637_v638.

[[entries]]
name = "nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617"
description = "Since cycc 6.6.17 type arguments nest wherever a type is written — `Vec<Box<i64>>` (the lexer's folded `>>` / `>>>` close two / three levels), `Box<Vec<i64>>`, `idv<Vec<i64>>(x)`, a field `items: Vec<Box<i64>>;` — `sizeof(Box<i32>)` is the instance's size in an expression and in `#assert`, and a multi-value return element takes type arguments, `(Box<i64>, i64)`"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["generics", "type-arguments", "sizeof", "multi-value-return", "v6.6.17"]
---
    struct Box<T> { v: T; w: T; }
    #assert sizeof(Box<i32>) == 8
    #assert sizeof(Box<Box<i32>>) == 16
    var x: Vec<Box<i64>> = vec_new();     # ran to end of file before 6.6.17
    fn two(a): (Box<i64>, i64) { return (a, 5); }   # was `expected ')', got '<'`

`sizeof(T<..>)` is exactly what `var b: T<..>` occupies (the instance, not the base generic).

STILL NOT SUPPORTED: a generic-struct FIELD `b: Box<i32>;` (`expected identifier, got '<'`);
`#derive` on a struct with a `Vec<Box<i64>>` field stops its field walk at the nested `<`.

GATE: tests/tcyr/frontend/nested_type_args.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.6.17] (b5). Compiler side: every
`<..>` skipper must count levels and be able to OWE a closer to the list around it, because the
lexer folds `>>` into one token.

6.7.14 — EVERY `Vec<..>` POSITION READS ITS ELEMENT (the user's D4 (a), D5 (a); CHANGELOG [6.7.14] GN-10):
6.7.11's D9 named the var / global / parameter / `sizeof` positions; since 6.7.14 a field's nested
`Vec<Vec<..>>`, a trait member's parameter and `: Vec<..>` return, a multi-value element, `Box<Vec<..>>`,
`idv<Vec<..>>(c)` and `sizeof(Box<Vec<..>>)` read it too. A GENERIC Vec element's own list is read as that
struct's annotation reads it, minting nothing: `Vec<Box<nosuch>>` and `Vec<Box<(i64, i64)>>` are refused, and
a struct FIELD `Vec<Box<Later>>` needs `Later` declared ABOVE the struct (as a field `Box<Later>` always did;
it compiled through 6.7.13 — kept by the user's 6.7.15 D41). A local's / parameter's does not. A NON-generic
element's list (`Vec<Pt<..>>`) was still skipped through 6.7.14; refused at the name since 6.7.15 (D6). Unchanged:
`Vec<i64>`, `Box<Vec<i64>>`, `Vec<Box<i64>>`, `(Box<Box<i32>>, i64, Vec<Box<i64>>)`. (The STILL NOT SUPPORTED field
`b: Box<i32>;` above is supported since 6.7.1 — trait_bounds_v671.)

6.7.15 (the user's D10 (a), D6 (a); CHANGELOG [6.7.15] GN-1, GN-2, GN-3, GN-13):
- AN OWED CLOSE BELONGS TO THE ENCLOSING LIST (GN-1): a nested argument whose closes lex as one `>>` / `>>>` and that is
  followed by `,` leaves the `,` to the enclosing list — `g2<P<P<R>>, i16>` binds B = i16 and `Two<P<P<R>>, i16>` is
  18 B (through 6.7.14 the next argument was read as the inner type's, silently: B = i64, 24 B); a doubly nested
  parameter that is not the last one (`fn f(a: Box<Box<Pt>>, b: i64)`) compiles (it was "unknown type 'b' as a type
  argument"). No `> >` spelling is needed.
- A VEC ELEMENT'S GENERIC STRUCT IS HELD TO ITS BOUNDS, at the element, nothing minted (D10 (a), GN-13): with `struct
  Box<T: Show>` and no `impl Show for Pt`, `var w: Vec<Box<Pt>> = 0;` is refused "generic 'Box': its type argument
  does not implement trait 'Show'" (its note naming the missing impl), once per (struct, arguments), as `var b:
  Box<Pt>;` is — at a local, a field, a parameter, a global, nested (`Vec<Two<Box<Pt>, i64>>`, `Vec<Vec<Box<Pt>>>`),
  and `Vec<Box<i64>>` written out; a bare `Vec<Box>` is judged as its i64 base (beyond D10's text, which named
  `Vec<Box<Pt>>` — kept by 6.7.16's D40); a generic's `Vec<Box<T>>` per instance. Through 6.7.14 it compiled (the list was read for names
  only). An element whose argument is itself an instance (`Vec<Box<Box<R>>>`) went unjudged through 6.7.15; refused
  since 6.7.16 (D10 — below).
- A `dyn` type argument (`Box<dyn Show>`) is ONE refusal (GN-2): no `Box$dyn Show` instance is minted to cascade into
  a second error at the annotation.
- A NON-generic element's list (`Vec<Pt<i64>>`) is refused at the name (D6, GN-3) —
  generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310.

6.7.16 (the user's D10, 2026-10-19; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] GS-9; a language change): AN ELEMENT WHOSE GENERIC STRUCT TAKES
ANOTHER INSTANCE IS HELD TO THE OUTER BOUND. With `struct Box<T: Show>` and `impl Show for R`, `var w: Vec<Box<Box<R>>> =
0;` is refused at the element, once, nothing minted — "generic 'Box': its type argument does not implement trait 'Show'"
with the note "the bound on type parameter 'T' needs `impl Show for Box$R`, and there is none" — as `var b:
Box<Box<R>>;` is: no impl can name an instance, so a bound on one never holds. A local, a field, a parameter, a global,
a generic's `Vec<Box<Box<T>>>` per instance, an outer-bounded / inner-unbounded `Vec<SB<UB<R>>>`; an unbounded outer
slot stays silent; `impl Show for Box` (the base) does not satisfy it; `Vec<Box<Box<i64>>>` (the inner `Box<i64>` is the
base) is judged as before; reported under `--syntax-only` too. Through 6.7.15 it compiled (the filed program exited 5).

[[entries]]
name = "closeout_language_changes_v6620"
description = "What cycc 6.6.20 (the v6.6.x closeout) changed for a cyrius author: refusals that used to be silent miscompiles, a bare struct to a `*T` parameter, last-definition binding, reserved intrinsic names, preprocessor caps and TAB separators, and the manifest keys the CLI now confines."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.6.20", "closeout", "preprocessor", "pointers", "tail-calls", "manifest", "security"]
---
Language / compiler (each was a SILENT wrong value or memory corruption before 6.6.20):
- `continue` binds to its own loop when a `while` sits between two `for`s (was the outer loop's step).
- A `: f64` fn returns its f64 through a tail call, a bare `return;` and a fall-off (stale xmm0 on x86 / PE).
- `#inline` / small generic fns keep a `*T` parameter's sizeof(T) step; a generic `: *T` result steps sizeof(T).
- A bare struct passed to a `p: *T` parameter (or a `*S` operator operand) takes its ADDRESS at every size;
  a struct-valued call result there goes through a frame temp, refused by name at top level.
- A redefined fn binds EVERY call to its LAST definition, as the duplicate-fn warning says.
- A fn / var / param / enum variant / `use` alias named `sizeof`, `mulh64` or `fncall0..8` is refused by name.
  (6.7.16: `sizeof` / `mulh64` are VALUES — `mulh64(a, b);` or `sizeof(T);` alone as a statement is refused by name,
  D11, FE-3; `fncall0..8(..);` stay calls — sizeof_operator, reserved_word_class_toknames.)
- A tail call into a fn with a `*T` parameter keeps its `jmp` unless a frame address can reach the callee
  (inside a loop it still diverts) — deep `*Node` list walks no longer overflow the stack.
- aarch64: calls with 262+ arguments and frames past 64 KiB are encoded correctly.

Preprocessor: `#if`/`#ifdef`/`#ifndef`/`#ifplat` nest at most 64 levels (per file); at most 16
function-like macros (main source only; a redefinition or an included one is refused); `#define`
names count once (a redefinition's latest value wins); a macro's parameter list / one invocation's
arguments are at most 511 bytes and the argument count must match; TAB separates a directive's
words; a stray `#else` / `#endif` or an unclosed block is an error; `#ifplat` is evaluated in
included files. `use` aliases: at most 64.

CLI / manifest (security, CYRIUS-2026-0029 … CYRIUS-2026-0035): `[package] cyrius` must be a version's shape (a leading
digit, `[0-9A-Za-z._-]`, no `..`); a manifest `[build] output` and `${file:PATH}` are confined to
the project by the `[embed]` path rules; `[deps.X] modules` entries may not leave the dep's tree;
project files are written through a symlink only to a file inside the project (never `.git`);
the crash-safe writers create their temp `O_EXCL|O_NOFOLLOW`; on Windows cmd.exe / certutil.exe
run by their System32 path.

GATES: see CHANGELOG [6.6.20] — each bullet names its test. Compiler side: the escape flag
`_fn_local_addr` + `_fnt_bvcp` (RPF-03), `_cont_vec` (RPF-01), `PP_PUSH_LEVEL` / `PP_MACRO_SLOT`.

[[entries]]
name = "traits_checked_v670"
description = "cyrius 6.7.0 traits: `trait` declarations with required and default methods, `impl X for T` checked against the trait, inherent `impl T {}`, the `T_Trait_m` qualified names and the collision rules, a typed `self` with arithmetic refused, and `var q: T = p` copying through a `*T`. Since 6.7.15 a member's signature reads its type arguments and is held to a fn's return set (D3, D7, D9), and an impl's return is checked against its member's (D4)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.0", "traits", "impl", "methods", "self", "dispatch", "ADR-007"]
---
DECLARE: `trait Show { fn show(self): i64; fn twice(self): i64 { return self.show() * 2; } }` — a
member ending in `;` is REQUIRED, one with a body is a DEFAULT. Only `fn` members. A trait or a member
declared twice is an error. Traits may be declared above or below their impls, in the declaration
section (before the first top-level statement), like `struct`.

IMPLEMENT: `impl Show for Point { fn show(self): i64 { .. } }` is CHECKED — an undeclared trait, a
method the trait does not declare, a different parameter count and a missing required method are
compile errors naming the trait. A default the impl does not define is instantiated for Point (parsed
from the trait's tokens with Point as the type; `self.show()` inside it calls Point's show).
Inherent methods with no trait: `impl Point { fn new(x, y): Point { .. } fn norm1(self) { .. } }`.

NAMES (static dispatch, ADR-004/ADR-007): a method is `Point_m`; every trait method is ALSO
`Point_Show_m`. Two traits giving Point an `m` make the plain `Point_m` / `p.m()` ambiguous — refused,
naming a qualified spelling; call `Point_A_m(p)`. Inside an impl/default of A, `self.m()` means A's.
An inherent `m` keeps `Point_m` (so `p.m()` picks it); the trait's is `Point_Trait_m`.

SELF: an untyped `self` in an impl is a `*T` — in either order, the impl above or below its struct (6.7.16, D6,
GS-13) — `self.x` reads and WRITES the receiver, and
`T_m(o)`, `T_m(&o)`, `o.m()` agree (before 6.7.0 `T_m(o)` on an 8-byte struct passed the value and
the method faulted). ⛔ Arithmetic on an untyped `self` (`self + 8`, `self[i]`, `self += n`) is
REFUSED: a `*T` steps sizeof(T), so the old `load64(self + 8)` idiom would have silently changed
meaning. Write `self.field`, or declare `self: *T` to step whole elements, or copy `var a = self;`
for a plain address.

COPY: `var q: T = p;` with `p: *T` copies `*p` at every size (it stored the pointer at <= 8 B and
aliased at > 8 B for a `*T` local); `var q: *T = p;` aliases.

CHAINS: `b.v.sum()`, `p.v.sum()`, `mk(3).v.sum()`, `c.w.v.scale(2);` — a method on a nested inline
field takes the field's address as `self`. `var u = s.clone();` types u from an 8-byte struct result.

SIGNATURES (6.7.8): every member's signature is parsed AT THE TRAIT, with fn-definition grammar and
words, whether or not an impl uses it — junk, an unknown type name or a stray token before the `;` is an
error even in a default every impl overrides ("expected ')', got '+'", "unknown type 'X' for parameter
'n'", "a trait method declaration ends with ';' (required) or a body (a default)"); a grammar error
reports under --syntax-only too. The impl check stayed the parameter count through 6.7.14; the full signature check
runs per dyn-formed pair (dyn_v678). Before 6.7.8 only the count was read. Since 6.7.15 the impl's RETURN is checked
too (D4 — below).

Trait objects: compiler-CHECKED `dyn` shipped in 6.7.8 (dyn_v678) — a 16-byte {data, vtable} struct, the
vtable built and verified from the impl. `lib/trait.cyr` stays as the unchecked library pattern.

DIAGNOSTICS NAME `T.m` (6.7.14, FE-23): a diagnostic names a method `Point.norm1` — never the mangled
`Point_norm1` or the trait-qualified `Point_Show_m` (that one is `Point.m` too) — and counts a method call's
arguments without `self`: `p.norm1(1)` on `fn norm1(self)` is "'Point.norm1' expects 0 arguments, got 1". The
symbol names above are unchanged; only what a message prints moved (method_chains_v6617).

6.7.15 — A MEMBER'S SIGNATURE IS A FN'S SIGNATURE (the user's D3 (a), D4 (a), D7 (a), D9 (a), 2026-10-18; CHANGELOG
[6.7.15] GN-9 .. GN-12, GN-12r). The trait-member reader is a second signature grammar beside the fn's, and every rule
a fn signature has must hold there, or the trait accepts what no impl can write:
  - TYPE ARGUMENTS READ (D7, GN-9): a member's parameter or return `fn a(self, b: Box<nosuch>): i64;` is refused
    "unknown type 'nosuch' as a type argument (...)", once, used or not, as a fn's own `m: Box<Zork>` is since 6.7.14
    (every type-argument refusal applies — `b: Box<(i64, i64)>` too); the member's own `<U>` is a name. It compiled as
    the base `Box`.
  - A MEMBER'S RETURN IS HELD TO A FN'S RETURN SET: `: Vec<..>` and a bare `: Vec` (D9, GN-10; the bare `: Vec` beyond
    D9's text, kept by 6.7.16's D38) are refused at the trait with a fn's words, "type 'Vec' cannot be a fn return type"; `: u128` (D3, GN-11)
    "`u128` is not supported as a fn return type yet" — also with a `: i64` impl, and under `--syntax-only`. Through
    6.7.14 each compiled though no impl could define it (and a `: i64` impl satisfied a `: u128` member). Use `*Vec<T>`
    / `*u128`. Unchanged: `: *Vec<i64>`, `: *u128`, a parameter `v: Vec<i64>`, a `*fn( .. ): u128` type's own return.
    `: u8` .. `: u64`, `: f32`, an enum and `: *fn(..)` member returns still compiled at the trait through 6.7.15;
    refused since 6.7.16 (D9 — below).
  - THE IMPL'S RETURN IS CHECKED AGAINST ITS MEMBER'S (D4, GN-12, GN-12r): `fn c(self): i64` implementing `fn c(self):
    f64;` is refused at the impl, "'P.c' returns i64, trait 'T' declares f64", at the method's return type (its name
    when it has none), once. 6.7.0 – 6.7.14 compared only the parameter COUNT, while dyn formation (6.7.8) compared
    types — so a mismatch was an error only when a dyn was formed, and a bounded generic caller `fn get<X: T>(o: X):
    f64 { return o.c(); }` read the integer bits (exit 2). The two returns are compared as forming a dyn compares them
    (a name by its resolved type, a type parameter as a type parameter), each side read AS AN ANNOTATION IS READ: a
    `>>` closes two lists and an all-`i64` list is its base struct, so `Box<i64>` / `Box` and `Box<Box<i64>>` /
    `Box<Box<i64> >` are one type. An OMITTED return is i64: a member with no return implemented `: bool` is refused (beyond D4's text — kept by
    6.7.16's D39).
    Parameter TYPES are not compared (D4 left them out; only the count). Forming a dyn from such an impl adds nothing.

6.7.16 (the user's D6, D9, D91, D92; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] GS-1 – GS-6, GS-11, GS-11r, GS-7r, GS-13, FF-30, FF-36,
FF-40 – FF-42):
  - A MEMBER'S RETURN IS HELD TO A FN'S RETURN SET, the rest of it (D9, GS-5): a member's `: u8` .. `: u64`, `: f32` or
    enum return is "type 'u8' cannot be a fn return type", and a `: *fn( .. )` / `: **fn( .. )` / bare `: *fn` return
    (a multi-value element too) is "a fn-pointer type `*fn( .. )` cannot be a fn return type" at its first `*`, once,
    with a fn's note (`**fn` beyond D9's text); under `--syntax-only` the names are silent and the `*fn` line is
    reported. Declare `: i64` or a pointer (`: *Color` is fine). A FN's fn-pointer return is that one line too (FF-40;
    it said "expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'fn'"), the body read on.
  - A MEMBER TAKES A FN'S PARAMETER GRAMMAR, the bare `*fn` / `**fn` included (D91, FF-41): `trait T { fn e(self, cb:
    *fn): i64; }` compiles (it was "expected '(', got ')'"); a member's own `<U>` reads after a `*fn(..)` (GS-3); a
    member's `cb: *fn(i64): u128` compiles — a fn pointer's own `: u128` is not the parameter's type (GS-4). A member's
    own third type parameter, `fn g<A, B, C>(..);`, is refused at its C (D92, FF-42).
  - THE IMPL CHECK REPORTS EVERY MISMATCH (GS-1): a return refusal no longer ends the check — a second method's
    mismatch, a parameter-count mismatch, an undeclared method and a missing required method report in the same
    compile. A side whose type its own reader refuses is that refusal alone: a member's D9 return, a `*fn( .. )` return
    on either side (GS-6), `Pt<i64>` where a reader reads the list (D7, GS-11, GS-11r), `Box<i64, Pt>` (D5, GS-7r).
    `cyrius lint` keeps the impl's "returns .., trait declares .." line where the member's own refusal is silent under
    lint (FF-36; restores 6.7.15's verdict).
  - AN IMPL BODY HOLDS ONLY `fn` DEFINITIONS (and directives / `pub`): a `struct`, `enum`, `var` or stray token there is
    "an impl body holds only `fn` definitions - in impl 'A'", as a trait body's non-`fn` member is (FF-30).
  - An impl ABOVE its struct: an untyped `self` is a `*T` (D6, GS-13) — `self.b` reads, `T_m(t)` passes `&t` as `t.m()`
    does (it faulted), and arithmetic on that `self` is refused as with the struct first (relaxed_fn_ordering).

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/traits_checked.sh, impl_self_typed.sh; tests/tcyr/crossos/traits_checked,
impl_self_typed, impl_inherent, struct_from_pointer_copies, method_on_nested_field. ADR-007.

[[entries]]
name = "trait_bounds_v671"
description = "cyrius 6.7.1 trait-bounded generics: `fn f<T: Show + Eq>` and `struct Box<T: Show>` — a bound is a contract checked at the definition, every instantiation (the i64 base included) needs the impl, any type (a scalar too) satisfies a bound through its impl; plus a struct type argument in either slot of a two-parameter generic and a `b: Box<i32>` struct field. Since 6.7.15 the contract covers a struct carrier's `T` field (D5) and a forward to a bounded generic is judged at any depth."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.1", "generics", "traits", "bounds", "C3", "ADR-007"]
---
BOUND: `fn describe<T: Show>(v: T): i64 { return v.show(); }`; several: `<T: Show + Eq>`; on a generic
struct: `struct Shown<T: Show> { v: T; n; }`. A bound names a declared trait, takes no type arguments,
sits on a generic's one or two type parameters, max 8 per parameter (through 6.7.15 a third parameter was
accepted and not recorded; since 6.7.16 it is refused itself — D8, D92, generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310).

CONTRACT (user decision 2026-10-07): on a bounded `T`-typed parameter or local (`v: T`, `p: *T`,
`var q: T`), `v.m()` must be a method of a bound's trait — refused at the DEFINITION otherwise (a
method in no bound, or in two). The call is THAT trait's method for T's impl: with Pt giving `size`
through Size AND Other, `<T: Size>` calls Size's. Fields (`v.x`) are not in a trait — per instance.
An UNBOUNDED T keeps per-instance resolution (`fn f<T>(v: T) { return v.show(); }` works).

CHECKED AT EVERY INSTANTIATION, naming the trait and the `impl` it needs: `describe(r)`,
`describe<R>(r)`, and the i64 base — `describe(5)` needs `impl Show for i64`; without one the base is
the dead stub and every scalar path (call, tail call, `&describe`, an unbounded generic forwarding
its T) is refused. A struct's instances too: `Shown<R>`, a written `Shown<i64>`, a bare `Shown`.
`fn mk<T>(x: T): Shown<T>` / `struct W<T> { s: Shown<T>; }` are fine; only their i64 base is refused,
where used (`mk(5)`, a bare `W`).

SCALARS: `impl Show for i64 { fn show(self): i64 { return self + 1; } }` — a bounded call on an i64
T passes the value; `describe<i32>(7)` needs `impl Show for i32`. `self.m()` inside a scalar impl (a
trait default included) calls that type's own m (before 6.7.1: "no struct type in scope for 'self'").

ALSO 6.7.1: `g<Pt, i64>(p, 2)`, `g(5, p)`, `g(p, q)` — a struct in either slot of a two-parameter
generic (refused 6.6.10..6.7.0); `struct H { a: i64; b: Box<i32>; c: i64; }` (packed: Box<i32> is
4 + 8 = 12 B) and `w: Box<T>` inside `W<T>`; a field naming its own struct is refused. `#derive`
reads nested type arguments (`xs: Vec<Box<i64>>`); an inline generic instance in a #derive'd struct
hits its layout check, like any non-derived inline struct.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/trait_bounds_checked.sh, generic_struct_field.sh,
generic_type_arg_unknown_refused.sh (K); tests/tcyr/crossos/trait_bounds, generic_two_param_struct,
generic_struct_field. ADR-007 decision 8. CHANGELOG [6.7.1].

6.7.14 (CHANGELOG [6.7.14] GN-14, GN-14r, GN-15):
  - A FORWARD TO A BOUNDED GENERIC: a generic that forwards its `T` to a bounded generic whose i64 base the bound
    refuses (`fn o<T>(x: T) { return gb(x); }`, `gb<T: Show>`, no `impl Show for i64`) has no i64 instance, and only
    that: `o(h)` with `h: f64` calls `o$f64`; `o(3)`, `&o` and a tail call are refused "generic 'o' has no i64
    instance: it forwards its type parameter to 'gb'" with one note naming the impl the chain's end needs ("the bound
    on type parameter 'T' of 'gb' needs `impl Show for i64`, and there is none"). Through 6.7.13 such a forwarder was
    a struct STUB, refused at every scalar call, `f64` included ("uses its type parameter as a struct"). It holds
    through `gb<T>(x)`, a generic method, a struct carrier, a generic instantiating a bounded struct and a cycle — up
    to 64 forwards through 6.7.14 (a deeper chain was refused at a definition); any number since 6.7.15 (below).
  - A BOUNDED CALL ON A CARRIER'S `T` FIELD is the bound's call, as on a `T`-typed name: `b.v.show()` with `b: Box<T>`
    (`v: T`, `T: Show`) — a `*Box<T>` carrier and a `var c: Box<T>` too — is a trait use, not a struct use, so `gs(bi)`
    with `bi: Box<i64>` runs when `impl Show for i64` exists (it was refused); at a scalar T the impl takes the field's
    value as `self`. A method the bound does not declare on such a field, and a `*T` field, kept their reading in
    6.7.14 (whether the bound's contract should hold there was filed, a question — answered by 6.7.15's D5, below).

6.7.15 (the user's D5 (a), 2026-10-18; CHANGELOG [6.7.15] GN-17 – GN-20, GN-20r):
  - THE CONTRACT COVERS A STRUCT CARRIER'S WHOLE `T` FIELD (D5, GN-18): under `fn gs<T: Show>(b: Box<T>)`, `b.v.other()`
    — a method no trait of the bound declares — is refused "method 'other' is not in the bound on type parameter 'T'"
    at the method's name, at the generic's DEFINITION, called or not, the note naming the field chain `'b.v'`; a method
    two bounds declare is ambiguous there. Through 6.7.14 it compiled and called the instance's own `other` (uncalled,
    the definition compiled). Unchanged: `b.v.show()`, an unbounded `Box<T>`, a field whose type parameter has no bound,
    and a `p: *T` field (outside the contract). The explicit spelling `v.other<i64>(1)` escaped the contract, and pass
    1's panic latch swallowed every contract refusal after the first, through 6.7.15 — both closed in 6.7.16 (D4, GB-3,
    GB-4 — below); their notes no longer print alone (GN-19).
  - ANY FORWARDING DEPTH (GN-20, GN-20r): the bounded-forward judgement is memoised per generic, so a forwarder to a
    bounded generic whose i64 base the bound refuses is judged at any depth (a 3000-deep chain costs what the same chain
    with no bound does); the 64-forward cap is gone. A CALLED chain was still held to the instance-nesting bound — about
    120 forwards — through 6.7.15; since 6.7.16 it is bounded by the compiler's stack, about 300 links (GB-2 — below).
  - A forwarder into a BOUNDED carrier generic whose base is bad for its bound (`fn gxb<T: Show>(b: Box<T>)`) is judged
    at its call — "generic 'outer' has no i64 instance: it forwards its type parameter to 'gxb'" — instead of refused at
    its definition (GN-17).
  - A Vec element's generic struct is held to its bounds (D10) — nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617.

6.7.16 (the user's D4, D8, D10, D102 — 2026-10-19 / 2026-10-20; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] GB-2 – GB-4, GB-13, GS-8, GS-9,
GS-15, FB-2):
  - THE EXPLICIT SPELLING IS PART OF THE CONTRACT (D4, GB-4): under `fn g<T: Show>(v: T)`, `v.other<i64>(1)` and
    `b.v.other<i64>(1)` (a carrier's T field) are refused at the definition, called or not — "method 'other' is not in
    the bound on type parameter 'T'". `<..>` after a method name on a bounded receiver is a type-argument list only when
    every name in it is a type: `h(v.y < a, c > (1))` keeps its two comparisons; a list naming no type at all
    (`v.other<Nope>(1)`) is no site — an uncalled such generic compiles, every call is refused "unknown type 'Nope' as a
    type argument". A bound's own generic member called with an explicit list (`v.show<i64>(1)`) is the bound's call, at
    a scalar T too (refused through 6.7.15).
  - EVERY CONTRACT REFUSAL OF A COMPILE PRINTS (GB-3; through 6.7.15 only the first did — the pre-scan's panic latch).
  - A CAPTURED RECEIVER (GB-13): a bounded T's member called on a captured receiver at a scalar T (`|| v.show()` at
    T = i64), and `self` captured in a trait default instantiated for a scalar, compile and call the bound's impl.
  - AN UNTYPED LOCAL OF A BOUNDED T IS A RECEIVER OF THE BOUND (D102, FB-2): `var y = x;` / `var y = id(x);` then
    `y.show()` calls the bound's impl at every T, i64 included (refused at the definition through 6.7.15).
  - THE FORWARDING CHAIN'S DEPTH (GB-2): the judgement takes any length; a CALLED chain is bounded by the compiler's
    stack — about 300 links on Linux, macOS and aarch64, 290 on Windows (each link's instance is parsed inside the call
    that reaches it); past it, one refusal, "nesting too deep (N generic instances, each parsed inside the call that
    reaches it, used over 6 MB of the compiler's stack)". Lexical nesting keeps its 256-level bound. The bounded-forward
    refusal and its notes name a generic method as written, `P.bk` (GS-15).
  - A THIRD TYPE PARAMETER is refused at the declaration, bounded or not (D8, GS-8; a trait member's own — D92); a Vec
    element whose generic struct takes another instance is held to the outer bound (D10, GS-9 —
    nested_generic_type_arguments_v6617).

[[entries]]
name = "const_and_const_fn_v672"
description = "cyrius 6.7.2 `const` (int / f64 / string, no storage, top level and in fns, any order) and `const fn` (runs at compile time in const contexts only, pure subset checked at the definition, bit-exact f64) — and the const contexts: array sizes, #assert, case labels, enum values."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.2", "v6.7.3", "const", "const fn", "comptime", "B1", "C1"]
---
CONST: `const LIMIT = 7;` `const PI = 3.14159;` `const NAME = "cyrius";` — NO STORAGE; each use is the
value (an int folds like an enum constant, an f64 is its literal, a string is its literal — passed to a
`Str` parameter it is wrapped like the literal). No type annotation (its type is its value's). `N = ..`,
`N += ..`, `&N` refused by name — and (6.7.3) a `for` step `for (..; ..; N = N + 1)`, which compiled in 6.7.2 and,
a top-level const's slot having size 0, stored over a neighbouring global (g0 7 → 100). Top-level consts: any order, forward use fine (a cycle is refused); one
top-level name is a const OR a global. Local consts: `const L = 3;` in a fn — visible to the rest of the
block, shadowed / shadowing by depth, a closure reads it by name (no frame slot, nothing captured).

CONST FN: `const fn fib(n) { .. }` is an ordinary fn; a CONST CONTEXT runs it at compile time, anywhere
else `fib(3)` is a plain runtime call (user decision: the disassembly shows the call you wrote). Body =
the pure subset, CHECKED AT THE DEFINITION even if never called: int and f64 arithmetic, comparisons,
&& / ||, shifts (`>>` logical, `>>>` arithmetic), `var x[: i64|i32|i16|i8|f64] = ..;` (narrow ones narrow
as at run time), local consts, `=` / `OP=`, if / elif / else, while, `for (;;)`-style with all three
clauses, `for i in a..b`, return / break / continue, const fn calls (recursion ok), exact f64 builtins
(f64_add..div, from, to, eq/lt/gt/le/ge, neg, abs, floor, ceil, trunc). NOT: load/store, arrays, `&x`,
fields, syscalls, globals, ordinary fn calls, transcendental builtins, generic const fns.

CONST CONTEXTS (any INTEGER const expression): a const's value, `var a[f(4)]` / `var a: i64[N * 2]` (top
level and in a fn), `#assert f(10) == 55 && N > 3` (any expression since 6.7.2), `case twice(N):`, enum
values `enum E { A = N; B = f(2); }`. ⚠ enum values and top-level array sizes are read in pass 1 in source
order: a const fn THEY call must be declared above them (a top-level const need not be).

SAME ANSWER AS RUN TIME: typing is the runtime's (f64 op iff the LEFT operand is f64; an int right
operand contributes its BITS). f64 is computed by an integer-only IEEE-754 module, bit-exact with x86-64
and AArch64. Refused by name: NaN (target-dependent bits), division by zero, f64_to out of range, a string
in arithmetic, > 10,000,000 steps, > 2,000 nested calls.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/const_checked.sh; tests/tcyr/crossos/const_values, const_contexts,
const_fn_f64 (compile-time vs run-time BITS). CHANGELOG [6.7.2].

6.7.11 (GF-11 .. GF-13, D12): a const context's call of a const fn is checked like any call — another file's
`private` const fn is refused (`'pk' is private to its file`) in every const context; a `#deprecated` one warns once
per call site. A const fn's arity in a const context is its RUN-TIME arity (the parser's own parameter walker): a
`Pair<i64, i64>` parameter is one parameter. A refused enum value moves past its expression (no cascade into the
member loop).
6.7.13 (MV-7): a refused const ANNOTATION — `const k: i64 = 3;`, "a const takes no type annotation: its type is its
value's" — is ONE error at the `:`, locally, in a closure or const fn body and at top level; the const is still bound
to its value. Through 6.7.12 the name was left unbound, so every read (and every const context it sized — an array
size, `#assert`, an enum value, a case label) added "undefined variable 'k'".
6.7.14 (CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-4, FE-6, FE-7, FE-8, FE-10, FE-16):
  - A CONST HAS NO FIELDS, AND A CONST FN NO FIELD ACCESS (FE-8): `C.x = 1` is "cannot assign to a field of const 'C' -
    a const has no storage", `C.x` "const 'C' has no fields - a const has no storage" (top-level and local consts, and
    `C.x` in any const context: a const's value, a const fn body, an array size); `a.x` in a const fn is "a field is not
    in a const fn: its body takes no memory (fields, arrays, load64, &x)". One error each — they were "expected ';',
    got '.'", a missing-include text or a false "undefined variable 'L'".
  - A BARE CONST OR ENUM-CONSTANT STATEMENT (`N;`, `N + 1;`, `GREEN;`, `Color.GREEN;`, a for step `N`) is "expected
    '='" — the syntax error a variable gets there; only `N = ..` / `N OP= ..` / `E.A = ..` is "cannot assign to const"
    (FE-10; it said "cannot assign" though nothing was assigned).
  - A CONST CONTEXT'S SKIPPED ARM is still a call path (FE-4): a short-circuit's right operand (`const X = 1 || pk();`)
    or an if-expression's untaken arm naming another file's `private` const fn is refused at the call (it compiled); a
    `#deprecated` one there warns.
  - A LOCAL `const` IN AN `#inline` OR GENERIC BODY is scoped to that replay (FE-6): `return w() + w();` with `#inline fn
    w(): i64 { const L = 3; return L; }` (and `gg(1) + gg(2)`) compiles — it was "'L' is already declared in this
    scope"; the const stays invisible to the caller.
  - AN INTEGER CONST FOLDS ON EITHER SIDE of a run-time operator (FE-16): `2 * C`, `1 + C`, `2 + C * 3`, `2 * E.A` are
    one `mov`, as `C * 2` was (no value changes; the code is smaller).
  - A const fn body's unknown name is reported once, by its definition check (FE-7).
6.7.15 (CHANGELOG [6.7.15] FE-9, FE-10, FE-11; diagnostics only — refused before and after):
  - A METHOD CALL OR A SUBSCRIPT IN A CONST FN is named once, at the `.` / `[` (FE-9): `a.m(..)` on a local, parameter
    or body const is "a method call is not in a const fn: it calls only const fns", `a[i]` "a subscript is not in a
    const fn: its body takes no memory (fields, arrays, load64, &x)" (they said "expected ';', got '.'" / "got '['" and
    the run-time body added a struct-scope error). A CONST HAS NO METHODS: `C.m(..)` on a top-level or local const — in
    code, the zone, a const's value, an array size — is "const 'C' has no methods - a const has no storage", beside
    6.7.14's "has no fields".
  - A refused const fn's definition check holds no cascade latch into its run-time body (FE-10): `const fn cf(): i64 {
    var a = 1; const k: i64 = 3; return k; }` no longer adds a false "undefined variable 'k'"; the body no longer
    repeats what the check said (a second "cannot assign to const 'k'", "unexpected ';'" beside "not a compile-time
    expression").
  - A refused const context evaluated in place no longer swallows the next one (FE-11): two bad enum values, two bad
    global array sizes, an enum value then an array size, or two `#assert`s print each refusal (only the first printed);
    an `#assert` after another declaration's latched error no longer prints "#assert failed" in place of its own
    refusal. A case label kept its statement's latch through 6.7.15 (closed in 6.7.16, FE-9 — below).
6.7.16 (CHANGELOG [6.7.16] FE-8, FE-9; diagnostics only — refused before and after):
  - A CONST HAS NO STORAGE, SO NO SUBSCRIPT (FE-8): `C[0]` on a top-level or local const — in code, a const fn, a const's
    value, an array size, a case label, `#assert`; stores and `OP=` too — is "cannot subscript const 'C' - a const has
    no storage", one error at the `[`, beside "has no fields" and "has no methods"; the next statement / size / member /
    `#assert` reports its own error (it was "expected ';', got '['", the generic subscript text or a FALSE "undefined
    variable 'L'"). A const fn's own locals and body consts keep "a subscript is not in a const fn". `a.m<T>(..)` /
    `C.m<T>(..)` in a const fn is a METHOD CALL ("a method call is not in a const fn" / "has no methods"), not a field
    (it added a false "undefined variable 'i64'").
  - EVERY REFUSED `case` LABEL REPORTS ITS OWN REFUSAL (FE-9): a second refused label is no longer "expected ':', got
    identifier 'ww'" — each label is evaluated with the latch clear and closed at its `:`, after a refused switch subject,
    in a nested switch and at top level.

[[entries]]
name = "declaration_zone_constant_initializers_v6714"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.14 a declaration-zone global whose initializer folds to an integer constant (a const / enum name, an expression of them, `= 0`) or is a bare float literal is its value FROM PROGRAM START and runs nothing at startup — `var R = INT_MIN % -1;` no longer traps; a named-field struct literal and a `**T` annotation work in the zone; a redeclared global's later folded constant is compared. Since 6.7.15 every constant redeclaration is the value from program start (D2), a named constant is compared, an all-constant struct / tuple literal is not re-stored at startup and a constant f64 expression is baked"
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "globals", "declaration-zone", "const", "constant-initializer", "startup", "kernel", "redeclaration"]
---
THE DECLARATION ZONE is the top-level `var` block before the first top-level statement. Pass 1 registers each
global; a CONSTANT initializer is baked into the image (6.6.16), anything else is a store replayed at startup
(EMIT_GVAR_INITS).

A CONSTANT INITIALIZER RUNS NOTHING AT STARTUP (the user's D11 (b); CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-15, FE-15r, FE-17):
    const C = 5;  enum E { A = 1; B = 2; }
    var X = C;                     # baked; no store at startup
    var R = INT_MIN % -1;          # 0 from program start — it TRAPPED at startup on x86_64 / cx / PE
    var Q = (0 - CF_MAX - 1) / (0 - 1);
    var G: f64 = 1.5;              # a bare float literal (f64, f32, untyped, `-1.5`) is baked too
  - An initializer that FOLDS TO AN INTEGER CONSTANT — a const or enum name, an expression of them, `= 0` — or a
    BARE FLOAT LITERAL is the global's value from program start. Since 6.6.16 `var X = C;` was baked AND stored again
    by the replay, and the replay re-ran the expression, so `var R = INT_MIN % -1;` trapped at startup (SIGFPE, exit
    136; on PE the divide exception) on x86_64 and cx while aarch64 ran. Now it starts at 0 on every target.
  - Consequences, as for an integer LITERAL global (a static init since v5.11.64): an x86 `kernel;` build reads a float
    literal global (it read 0 and warned "the initializer of 'G' runs after the top-level program"); an EARLIER
    initializer that reads it sees its value (`var b = load64(&G); var G: f64 = 1.5;` — b is 1.5); an earlier
    initializer's fn call that WRITES it keeps its write (`fn setg() { G = 9; } var r = setg(); var G = C;` → 9; the
    startup store reset it to C through 6.7.13). The float-literal half (a forward read sees the value, an earlier
    write is kept) goes beyond D11's text — kept by the user's 6.7.15 D46.
  - A REDECLARED NAME (6.7.14): over an earlier NONZERO literal the later constant was the value from program start
    (`var G = 3; var r = setg(); var G = C;` ended at setg's 9, as between two literals); over an earlier computed,
    `= 0` or const- / enum-NAMED constant declaration the order was kept (`var G = D; var r = setg(); var G = C;` gave
    5). Settled by 6.7.15's D2 (a) — below.
  - Still a store in 6.7.14: a computed initializer, a slot an array list covers, a bool global, an initializer naming
    another file's private const, an all-constant zone struct / tuple literal (baked AND stored), and in a `kernel;`
    build a float global whose constant is not a bare literal (`= CF`, `= (1.5)`, `= 1.5 + 1.0`) was left to the late
    replay. The last two are 6.7.15's — below.

THE ZONE TAKES WHAT A STATEMENT TAKES (CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-13, FE-14):
  - A NAMED-FIELD STRUCT LITERAL (`var p = P { a: 1, b: 2 };`, annotated or not) initialises a zone global — it was
    "undefined variable 'a'" + "unexpected ':'" (the guide's own Structs example failed as written); an all-constant
    one is baked like a positional one, so a `kernel;` build reads it. A bad named literal (an unknown, missing,
    repeated or nested field) gets the one error it gets after a statement.
  - A POINTER ANNOTATION WITH MORE THAN ONE `*` (`var P: **i64 = 0;`, `***i64`, `**B<i64>`) compiles in the zone — it
    was "expected '=', got identifier 'i64'" (the replay read one `*`).

A REDECLARED GLOBAL'S LATER FOLDED CONSTANT IS COMPARED (CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-18, FE-19; the user's D9 (a)):
  - `var a = 5; var a = 2 + 1;`, `= -5`, `= (7)` warn "duplicate symbol 'a' redefined with conflicting value (last
    definition wins)", as `= 3` always did (only an exact `= NUM ;` was compared). `true` / `false` fold to 1 / 0, so
    `var B: bool = false; var B: bool = true;` and `var a = 5; var a = true;` warn too (kept by the user's 6.7.15 D47).
    A same value stays silent. A NAMED constant on either side was not compared through 6.7.14; it is since 6.7.15
    (FE-14, below).
  - A global declared as an integer (untyped, integer-typed or a destructure target) AND as `bool` is "global 'G'
    redeclared with a different type or size (a global has one definition; rename one)" — bool_true_false_not_v673.

6.7.15 — EVERY CONSTANT REDECLARATION IS THE VALUE FROM PROGRAM START, AND MORE IS BAKED (the user's D2 (a), D46, D47;
CHANGELOG [6.7.15] FE-12 .. FE-16):
  - A REDECLARED NAME (D2 (a), FE-13): every constant redeclaration — a literal, `= 0`, a const or enum name, a
    replay-folded expression (`A + 1`) — is the global's value from program start, HOWEVER the earlier declaration is
    spelled, and an earlier constant declaration of the slot leaves no startup store. With `const C = 5; const D = 3;`:
    `var a = D; var b = a; var a = C;` gives b == 5 (it gave 3), `var G = 0; var b = G; var G = C;` 5 (it gave 0), `var
    a = E.A; var b = a; var a = E.B;` E.B; `var G = D;` (or `= 0`) then `var r = setg(); var G = C;` ends at setg's
    write (9; it reset to C). Beyond D2's text (measured, pinned; kept by 6.7.16's D45): an earlier COMPUTED initializer before a named
    constant still runs, its result discarded (`var a = f(); var b = a; var a = C;` gives b == C, as its literal twin
    already did), and a list before a named scalar is evaluated for its errors only. Kept: a bool global's startup
    store, a private-const reader's. A FLOAT literal redeclaration kept declaration order and was never compared through
    6.7.15 (D2 named integer constants); since 6.7.16 it is a constant redeclaration (D13 (a) — below).
  - A REDECLARED GLOBAL'S NAMED CONSTANT IS COMPARED (FE-14; warnings only): `var a = 3; var a = C;`, `var a = C; var a
    = 3;`, `var a = 3; var a = E.B;`, `var a = D; var a = C;`, `var a = 0; var a = C;` warn "duplicate symbol 'a'
    redefined with conflicting value (last definition wins)" once, at the later declaration — each later constant
    against the last constant before it (`3`, `C`, `4` warns twice). Same-value pairs and an earlier computed
    initializer stay silent. They compiled silently through 6.7.14 (pass 1 folds no name).
  - A REFUSED TYPE CHANGE IS ONE ERROR (FE-12): a global redeclared with another type or size reports only the type
    change; a reader between the two declarations reads the first, the code after the refused one reads it, and no
    conflicting-value warning rides on a refused pair.
  - AN ALL-CONSTANT ZONE STRUCT / TUPLE LITERAL IS NOT STORED AGAIN AT STARTUP (FE-15, kept by 6.7.16's D46; 6.7.14's D11
    (b) applied to the baked literal): `var p = P { 1, 2 };`, the named form, a tuple `(1, 2)` — every element a constant (nested, enum
    leaves, f64 / f32 fields, generic, wrapped) — were baked AND stored element by element; the stores are dropped when
    no earlier live writer of the slot exists and no later literal may bake other bytes over it, or the earlier writer
    is the same bytes. So an earlier initializer's call that writes it keeps its write (`var r = setp(); var p = P { 1,
    2 };` → p.a 9; it was 1). ⚠ A redeclared literal of OTHER bytes keeps declaration order (`var p = P{1,2}; var b =
    p.a; var p = P{3,4};` gave b == 1 through 6.7.15), unlike a scalar under D2 (a) — settled in 6.7.16: a constant
    redeclaration (D14 (a) — below).
  - A CONSTANT f64 EXPRESSION IS BAKED (FE-16, kept by 6.7.16's D47; the float-literal bake confirmed by 6.7.15's D46, and
    the same reading applied to a constant expression): an f64 const, a parenthesized literal and f64 arithmetic of them — `var G: f64 = CF;`, `=
    (1.5)`, `= 1.5 + 1.0`, `= -CF`, an untyped `var K = CF;`, an f32's `= CF` (binary32-rounded) — are in the image from
    program start, as a float literal is: an earlier read sees the value, an earlier call's write is kept, and an x86
    `kernel;` build reads it with no warning (it read 0 and was warned to "use a constant"). Still startup stores: a
    call, a NaN result (`0.0 / 0.0`), and through 6.7.15 arithmetic with an integer or enum operand (`1.5 + 1`, `1.5 *
    E.A` — warned as a mix, late in a `kernel;` build); an integer or enum RIGHT operand is baked since 6.7.16 (D15 (a) —
    below).
  Gates (6.7.15): tests/tcyr/crossos/global_constant_redeclaration_order.tcyr, zone_struct_literal_value.tcyr,
  zone_float_constant_initializer.tcyr; tests/gates/platform/kmode_enum_initializer_baked.sh.

6.7.16 — FLOATS, LITERALS AND LISTS ARE CONSTANT REDECLARATIONS TOO (the user's D13 (a), D14 (a), D15 (a), 2026-10-19;
D86 (a), 2026-10-20; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] ZN-1 – ZN-4, ZN-1r, ZN-8, SS-37):
  - A FLOAT CONSTANT REDECLARATION (D13 (a), ZN-2) — a float literal, `= CF`, constant f64 arithmetic, f32 too — holds
    the later constant from program start: `var G: f64 = 1.5; var b = load64(&G); var G: f64 = 2.5;` gives b 2.5's
    bits. A call between the declarations keeps its write; an earlier float constant leaves no startup store; an
    earlier computed initializer runs into a sink; a list before the float is evaluated for its errors only; a computed
    float redeclaration keeps its order. A differing float pair warns "conflicting value" at the later `=` (bits
    compared); a same-bits pair is silent.
  - A STRUCT OR TUPLE LITERAL OF CONSTANTS that redeclares a global (D14 (a), ZN-3): `var p = P{1,2}; var b = p.a; var
    p = P{3,4};` gives b 3; a call between two literals of other bytes keeps its write; an earlier literal leaves no
    startup store, in every spelling (tuple, named, generic, wrapped); an earlier computed initializer's result is
    discarded; a later computed literal runs in order.
  - f64 ARITHMETIC WITH AN INTEGER OR ENUM RIGHT OPERAND IS BAKED (D15 (a), ZN-4): `1.5 + 1`, `1.5 * E.A`, an integer
    const — computed as the run-time code computes it (the integer is its raw bits), the mix warning still printed once;
    an x86 `kernel;` build reads it with no late warning. An integer LEFT operand (`1 + 1.5`) and a NaN stay startup
    stores.
  - A LIST OVER A NAMED SCALAR CONSTANT KEEPS THE CONSTANT'S UNLISTED BYTES (ZN-1, ZN-1r; D86 (a), SS-37): the seed is the
    slot's LAST constant before the list, however it is spelled — a const or enum name, an integer, and a float's own
    bits (`var K = 1.5; var K[1] = {9};` is 0x3FF8000000000009; through 6.7.15 0x9). A computed initializer between is
    passed over, a const between two lists reaches the earlier one, and a constant after the float seeds.
  - The zone reports ONE ERROR PER DECLARATION (ZN-8), as a fn's statements do: the replay's panic latch swallowed every
    later declaration's error. ⚠ Filed unplaced at the close: a zone initializer's error is printed after a LATER
    line's pass-1 refusal (a day-plus redesign).
  Zone globals from generic calls, if-expressions and `*S` sources: struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711,
  struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710, f64_named_type_and_operators (D98).

See also field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml global_init_order_silent_zero (6.6.16's "what is a constant
initializer"). The guide: § Global Initializers, § Declaring a global twice. Gates:
tests/gates/frontend/const_init_bake_and_fold.sh, zone_named_struct_literal.sh, zone_pointer_annotation.sh,
global_redeclaration_one_definition.sh; tests/tcyr/crossos/const_fold_int_min_div.tcyr,
zone_named_struct_literal.tcyr, zone_float_scalar_baked.tcyr.

[[entries]]
name = "bool_true_false_not_v673"
description = "cyrius 6.7.3 `bool` / `true` / `false` / `!`: a WRITE into a bool (var, field, param, fn return, multi-return element, const, bool[N] list) takes only a boolean value; a bool READS as the integer 0 or 1; `!` is a real operator (it was silently dropped until 6.7.3)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.3", "bool", "true", "false", "!", "B2", "type check"]
---
VALUES: `true` = 1, `false` = 0, both reserved words; `bool` is an 8-byte word (sizeof 8, ADR-002).
`!e` = 1 when e's word is 0; binds like unary minus (its operand is a factor: `!x + 1` is `(!x) + 1`);
an f64 operand is tested by its raw bits (`!(-0.0)` is 0, as `if (-0.0)` is taken). ⚠ Until 6.7.3 the
lexer DROPPED a lone `!` — `!x` compiled as `x`, silently (the dropped-bang lexer bug).

WRITES ARE CHECKED (user decision 2026-10-07): a bool local / global / field / parameter / `fn g(): bool`
return / multi-return element / const takes only a BOOLEAN value — true, false, a comparison (incl. the
f64_eq/lt/gt/le/ge builtins), `!x`, `&&` / `||`, another bool (var, field, param, capture, const, a
bool fn's call), or one in parens. Everything else is refused by name, `0` and `1` INCLUDED:
`var b: bool = 1;` `b = n;` `b += 1;` (every compound op) `p.on = 2;` `f(1)` for `on: bool`,
`return 1;` / bare `return;` in a bool fn. Arithmetic over a bool is an int (`ok + 0`, `-ok`,
`(a < b) + 1` refused). Untyped vars stay ints (`var x = a < b;` then `var c: bool = x;` refused).
Write `x != 0` to make a bool. Raw memory (store64, `*p = v`, ret2) is unchecked.

READS: a bool is 0 / 1 everywhere an integer is (`n + ok`, `ok * 4`, an i64 parameter). Conditions
still take any integer. Off the end of a bool fn returns `false`.

ELSEWHERE: consts are bool iff their value is (`const ON = !OFF;`, a `: bool` const fn) and every const
context takes them as 0 / 1; a `bool[N]` list takes true / false / bool consts; `#derive(Serialize)`
writes JSON true / false, the decoders store 1 for `true` and 0 otherwise, `#derive(accessors)` gives
`fn S_f(p): bool` and `fn S_set_f(p, v: bool)`. Checks are off under --syntax-only, not under
CYRIUS_TYPE_CHECK=0.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/bool_checked.sh; tests/tcyr/crossos/bool_values.tcyr;
tests/tcyr/derive/derive_serialize_bool.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.7.3].

6.7.14 — BOOL-NESS IS PART OF A GLOBAL'S TYPE (the user's D9 (a); CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-19): a global declared once
as an integer (untyped, integer-typed or a destructure target) and again as `bool`, either way round, is refused
"global 'G' redeclared with a different type or size (a global has one definition; rename one)", once, at the second
declaration's `=`. Through 6.7.13 `var G = 5; var G: bool = G;` built a bool holding 5 (the bool mark is per slot and
was the last declaration's), and `var G = 5; var G: bool = true;`, `var G: bool = true; var G = 1;` and a destructure
over a bool global compiled. Two `bool` declarations of one global still fold. (A later folded `true` / `false` that
differs from an earlier one warns — declaration_zone_constant_initializers_v6714; kept by the user's 6.7.15 D47.)

6.7.15 — A KNOWN `&f` CALLEE'S `: bool` RESULT IS A BOOLEAN VALUE (the user's D30 (a); CHANGELOG [6.7.15] RC-9): the
boolean-value list gains a call to a `: bool` fn THROUGH `fncallN(&f, ..)` / `callptr(&f, ..)` with the callee
written `&f` — `var b: bool = fncall0(&isb);`, `callptr(&isb)`, an assignment, a wrap, `fncall1(&isb1, 3)`, `return
fncall0(&isb);` from a `: bool` fn, a bool parameter — as the direct `isb()` is. Through 6.7.14 each was refused "cannot
initialize bool 'b' with a value that is not a bool". It only accepts: a callee held in a variable (`var f = &isb;
fncall0(f)`, 6.7.11's D31), an `: i64` callee and `fncall0(&isb) + 0` stay refused.

6.7.16 — `*(&b)` OF A BOOL IS b (the user's D70, 2026-10-20; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] SS-17, SS-19): `*(&b)` is a boolean
value — a `b: bool` argument, a bool declaration, an if-expression's bool branch and a dyn source take it — and
`*(&b) = 5` / `*(&b) += 1` are refused naming b, "cannot assign a value that is not a bool to bool 'b'", as `b = 5` /
`b += 1` are (a local, a parameter or a scalar global; a for step; top level). Through 6.7.15 it was the untyped word. A
closure's capture keeps its environment word (typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617).

[[entries]]
name = "struct_argument_type_checked_v673"
description = "Since cycc 6.7.3 a struct ARGUMENT whose static struct type differs from its parameter's is refused by name: every argument form, a generic instance against its base both ways, a by-value struct of 8 B or less and a Str handle parameter, an operator's two operands and a method's self. A typed pointer (`u: *Pt`), `&p` and an untyped value convert freely. Before, only a struct-typed field into an address-passed parameter was checked; every other mismatch compiled clean and the callee read the argument with its own layout."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["structs", "arguments", "type-check", "generics", "operators", "methods", "v6.7.3"]
---
The filing from the 6.7.1 open, fixed in the 6.7.3 repair lane. The user's rule: an argument
whose static struct type differs from its parameter's is refused, as the receive path refuses
`var q: Q = p;` (since 6.6.6).

    struct Pt { x; y; }
    struct Q  { a; b; c; }
    fn bq(b: Q): i64 { return b.c; }
    fn go(): i64 { var p: Pt; p.x = 3; p.y = 4; return bq(p); }
    # error: cannot pass 'p' to a parameter of a different struct type in a call to 'bq'

MEASURED on 6.7.3's slot-open cycc; every program compiled clean:
  - bq(p) gave 0: it read past the 16-byte Pt.
  - bs(mk1(p)), a Box<Pt> into `fn bs(b: Box)`, gave 4 (b.v.y) where n was 8.
  - A global Pt into bq gave 97. `q + p` with `fn Q_add(a: Q, b: Q)` gave 1.
  - `q + mkp()`, `q + p.dup()` and `sl(p)` into `s: Str` SIGSEGV'd.

CHECKED — the argument has a static struct VALUE type: a named inline or pointer-mode local, a global
(in a fn and at top level), a closure capture, a field, a free call (named by the fn), a method or
operator result at every size (named as the source spells it since 6.7.14, `cannot pass 'Pt.dup' …` —
through 6.7.13 `'Pt_dup'`; a local that happens to be named `Pt_dup` keeps its name; VC-11) — through a
call, a method's arguments and a
tail call, into a by-value parameter of any size, a `p: *T` or a `s: Str`; an operator's two operands
(parameters 0 and 1 of the operator fn) and a method's self.

A generic INSTANCE is its own struct: a Box<Pt> passed to `b: Box` is refused, and the reverse. (This entry
said `gx(b)` for `fn gx<T>(b: Box<T>)` does not infer T through Box<T>: it does since 6.7.8, and since 6.7.11
through a generic call's result, a whole-argument wrap and a whole dereference too —
generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310.)

ACCEPTED: a TYPED POINTER of another struct (`u: *Pt` local, parameter, global, field or call result
into `*Q` or `Q`) converts freely, like `&p` and the receive path's `var q: *Q = u;` (user decision,
2026-10-08); an untyped variable; a literal; a call returning an integer or a pointer; a fn pointer.

6.7.10 (the user's decision 7): A POINTER INTO A BY-VALUE STRUCT PARAMETER PASSES THE STRUCT IT POINTS AT,
at every size. `bs1(&s)`, `bs1(sp)` with `sp: *S1`, `bs1(gp())` with `fn gp(): *S1`, `bs1(h.p)` with a `*S1`
field, `bs1(sp + i)`, and an operator's right operand (`o + sp`, `o + &s`) give `fn bs1(b: S1)` a COPY — a
`*T` local, parameter, global or capture, in a fn and at top level; the callee's writes never reach the
pointee. Over 8 B that is what the address-passed parameter always did; through 6.7.9 a struct of 8 B or
less got the POINTER as its one word and read the address as its fields, silently. A `*U` of another struct
still converts (read with the parameter's layout). An UNTYPED value is still the struct's word: an untyped
local holding an address (`var u = &s; bs1(u)`), an integer, an i8 / i16 / i32 value. Two edges stay the
word, by the compiler's own typing: an if-expression whose branches are pointers of DIFFERENT steps, and
`n + pb` with an i8..i32 `n` on the LEFT. A whole dereference (`take(*p)`, `a - *p`) is the struct too,
and a `*U` dereference into such a parameter is refused by name (`takeh(*pu)` crashed). Gate:
tests/tcyr/crossos/small_struct_pointer_arg.tcyr, struct_deref_whole_operand.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.7.10].

HOW (internals): SFPSID also records a by-value struct of 8 B or less and a Str handle parameter
(`_psid_plain`) — neither has a struct-mask bit, so every ABI reader tests `_pm_struct` before GFPSID.
Such an argument is consumed by `_sarg_byval_small` (the caller's own PCMPE + EPUSHR). A 9-16 B method
or operator result nothing stores is recorded with `_sc_tmp` -4; a whole field by the passive `_fpk_*`
record. Nothing emits: a 649-file differential corpus compiled byte-identical.

GATES: tests/gates/frontend/struct_arg_type_refused.sh (41 refusal rows, 12 acceptances,
mutation-proven) and tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_arg_type_accepted.tcyr (38 rows). CHANGELOG [6.7.3].

6.7.11 (ARG-5, ARG-6, ARG-8, D35 - D37): THE 8 B BY-VALUE CHECK IS SIZE-INDEPENDENT. A whole-argument wrap of
another struct's value (`bs1((t))`, D35 — the 6.7.6 "NOT YET") and a whole dereference of another struct's
pointer (`take8(*pu)`, D36; into a `Str` parameter too) are refused by name, as `bs1(t)` and the 16 B
`take(*pu)` were; `take8(pu)` (the POINTER) still converts. A wrapped struct as a tuple-literal element
(`((p), 1)`) is refused as `(p, 1)` is. The pointer rule (decision 7) also covers an if-expression whose
every branch is a pointer (any steps) and an integer on the LEFT of a struct pointer (`t1(m + pb)`,
`t1(n + pb)`) — the two edges named above are closed. A struct POINTER converts into any struct parameter;
its DEREFERENCE does not — `*pu` and `(t)` are values of their own struct.
A GENERIC STRUCT CARRIER binds T through a whole-argument wrap and a whole dereference as through the
plain spelling (`gw((w))` / `gw(*wp)` call `gw$i32`; a tuple literal binds nothing) —
generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (ARG-5, ARG-6, ARG-8).
6.7.13 — A STRUCT VALUE IS NEVER A WORD POINTER, NOR A VECTOR POINTER (the user's D5 (a), D33; ST-12, VC-9): a
struct VALUE (a local, a by-value parameter, a global, a capture, wrapped) passed to a `*i64` / `*u8` / `*f64` / `**T`
parameter is refused "cannot pass 's' to a parameter that is not a pointer to its struct type in a call to 'ri' -
pass &s for its address"; into a `*V` parameter (a local, a call, a method result, `*ps`, a global, a capture,
wrapped) "cannot pass 's' to a parameter that is a pointer to a vector type …". `&s` / a typed `*S` convert freely;
a handle and a `Str` already hold the address. (A struct FIELD, call or dereference argument into a word pointer, and
a struct field into `*V`, were not checked in 6.7.13 — both 6.7.14, below.)
6.7.14 — EVERY STRUCT VALUE INTO A POINTER OF ANOTHER KIND IS REFUSED (6.7.13's D5 (a) / D31, the user's D34;
CHANGELOG [6.7.14] ST-8, VC-12, VC-11): a struct-typed field `ri(w.s)`, a by-value call `ri(mk())`, a method or
operator result `ri(o.dup())` / `ri(a + b)`, a dereference `ri(*pp)` / `ri(**pp)` of a `*S` / `**S` — wrapped or not,
in a fn, a closure or at top level, into `p: *i64` / `*u8` / `**T` or any `*T` that records no struct (a generic's and
a method's pointer parameter too) — is refused once: "cannot pass 's' to a parameter that is not a pointer to its
struct type in a call to 'ri' - a struct value is no address" (naming the field, the callee, `Pt.dup`, or the
dereference with its `*`s). Through 6.7.13 each pushed the value's first word as the address (139 on x86_64 /
aarch64, a cx guest fault, PE 5). Still passed: an address `ri(&w + 0)`, a `*T` field, a `: *T` call or method,
`ri(*ppi)` of a `**i64`, a scalar field and a `Str` field. A STRUCT FIELD INTO `*V` (`rd(w.s)` into `p: *f64v2`) is
refused too (D34, VC-12) — pass `&w` (plus an offset) if the bytes are meant.
6.7.15 — METHOD RESULTS AND DEREFERENCES NAMED AS THE SOURCE SPELLS THEM (CHANGELOG [6.7.15] VC-10, VC-11, VC-12, ST-1;
diagnostics, no decision): the DESTINATION refusals name a method result `Pt.dup` / `Pt.t1` / `Pt.mq` too — the struct
assignment `w = s.dup();`, the small-struct `return s.t1();`, a field destination `h.q = s.dup();`, `var q: *Q =
s.dup();`, `var x: i64 = s.mq();` / `x = s.mq();`, a struct literal's element, the top-level "returns a struct by value"
refusal — where they printed `Pt_dup`; at top level an explicit instance is `mkg<i32>`, not `mkg$i32` (a free fn whose
name looks mangled keeps it). A refused `w = s.dup();` ends its statement: no "assigning non-pointer to typed pointer"
beside the refusal. A refused DEREFERENCE argument is quoted as written — '*gp(pb)', '*&b', '*h.pv', '**ppb', the
address-passed arm's '*gq(pq)' (it gave the operand's last name, one `*` short). And a FORWARD call of a fn whose
parameter names a struct declared below it is argument-checked since 6.7.15: a struct of another type there is
refused "cannot pass 'p' to a parameter of a different struct type in a call to 'g'" (relaxed_fn_ordering; kept by
6.7.16's D52).
6.7.16 — A POINTER TO ANOTHER STRUCT INTO A `*S` / `**S` PARAMETER IS REFUSED (the user's D97, 2026-10-20; CHANGELOG
[6.7.16] SS-43; a language change): `nc1(&y)` with `y: Box<Pt>` into `fn nc1(p: *Box<i64>)` read Pt's `y` as
Box<i64>'s `n`, silently, on every target; it is now "cannot pass 'y' to a parameter of a different struct type in a
call to 'nc1'", 6.7.3's words, at the argument, once — for every source the compiler types as a pointer to a struct (a
`*Qd` local, parameter, global, capture or field, a `: *Qd` call, method or generic call, `pq + 0`, `*ppq`, an
if-expression of such branches, `self`), every parameter path (a call, a tail call, a method's arguments, `Pt_m(&q)`, a
named argument, an operator's right operand `p + &q`), every depth (`**Qd` into `**Pt`, `***Qd` into `***Pt`), generic
instances both ways (`gb<i64>(&y)`, `gt<Pt>(&q)`), a callee below its call. Unchanged: a pointer to the declared struct,
an untyped pointer, `alloc()`, `0`, a generic's own binding (`gb(&y)`). So 6.7.3's "a typed pointer converts freely" now
holds into a BY-VALUE struct parameter only. ⚠ Filed unplaced at the 6.7.16 close (struct and pointer shapes beyond D97 /
D99): a typed pointer of another struct into a BY-VALUE parameter still copies through, and a pointer of another DEPTH
at a call or a declaration passes unchecked.

[[entries]]
name = "if_expression_v674"
description = "cyrius 6.7.4 B3: `if (c) { a } elif (d) { b } else { e }` is an expression wherever an expression is parsed — one expression per branch, else required, only the taken branch runs, every branch one kind (integer / f64 / f32 / one struct of <= 8 B), const contexts and array-list elements included."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.4", "if expression", "conditional", "ternary", "B3", "const context"]
---
SYNTAX: `var v = if (n > 0) { 1 } elif (n < 0) { 0 - 1 } else { 0 };` — each branch ONE expression in
braces (no statements, no `;`); `else` REQUIRED; only the taken branch runs. C's `c ? a : b` is not
cyrius: `?` is the Result propagation operator. At the START of a statement `if` is the if statement.
It is a FACTOR: `if (c) {a} else {b} + 1` is `(if ..) + 1`.

KINDS: every branch an integer (pointers, bools included), an f64, an f32, or ONE struct type of
<= 8 B (the result keeps its type: `var z: S = ..`, struct arguments, operator overloads see it).
Refused by name: a kind mix ("if expression branches differ in type: an integer here, an f64 before
it"), a struct over 8 B, a vector, u128, a `: stack` pair (branch on `&x`). All-boolean branches make
a bool (B2). An untyped var holding a float is an INTEGER (ADR-002) — declare it `: f64` — and
`var t = if (c) {1.5} else {2.5};` stays untyped like `var t = 1.5;`.

CONST CONTEXTS: const values, local consts, array sizes, #assert, case labels, enum values, const fn
bodies — the evaluator runs the taken arm and WALKS the rest (an untaken `1 / 0` never faults). There
a string is its own kind (`const B = if (D) {"dbg"} else {"rel"};`); a string/int mix is refused; at
run time a string literal is a pointer word, so `if (c) {"a"} else {0}` compiles. Since 6.7.4 every
GLOBAL ARRAY-LIST ELEMENT is a const context too (comparisons, && / ||, !, const fn calls, f64 const
arithmetic are legal; a bool[N] list takes `{1 < 2, !false}`).

LIMITS: a call in a branch is never a tail call (deep recursion through one grows the stack); an
explicit #inline fn holding one keeps the "control flow" warning.
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/if_expr_checked.sh; tests/tcyr/crossos/if_expr_values.tcyr. CHANGELOG [6.7.4].

6.7.13 (D1 (a), ST-6): a WHOLE DEREFERENCE of a `*S` as a branch — `{ *p }`, `{ (*p) }`, `{ *gp() }`,
`{ *(&t) }` — is a struct value. Of 8 B or less it joins `{ s }`, `{ *q }` and a call returning S and
COPIES (an untyped binding of it is the struct); over 8 B it is refused "an if expression branch cannot
be a struct value over 8 bytes … branch on a pointer" — write `if (c) { p } else { q }` into a pointer
and copy from it. Two different small structs are refused. Through 6.7.12 `{ *p }` was the struct's
first word. (A `*f64v2` pair was still an integer branch here; refused since 6.7.14 — below.)

6.7.14 (CHANGELOG [6.7.14] ST-9, ST-7; 6.7.13's D34 and D1 (a)):
  - A WHOLE DEREFERENCE OF A `*V` AS A BRANCH IS THE VECTOR, AND IS REFUSED as a vector name branch is —
    "an if expression branch cannot be a vector, u128 or slice value - branch on a pointer (&x)", once per
    branch: `{ *pv }`, `{ (*pv) }`, `{ *gpv() }`, `{ *&v }`, `{ *(&v) }`, `{ **ppv }`, a `*V` field and
    `*(pv + 0)`, at every vector width (f64v2, f32v4, f64v4, i32v4) and at top level. Through 6.7.13 it
    compiled to lane 0's word, silently (the filed repro gave 42 everywhere). Branch on the pointers, then
    dereference. A `*i64` dereference and the pointer words stay integers.
  - `{ **pp }` THROUGH A `**S` IS THE STRUCT `{ *p }` IS: of 8 B or less the destination copies it, over 8 B
    it is refused (it compiled and crashed — kept by the user's 6.7.15 D57).
  - 6.7.15 (D15, ST-9): an if-expression of `Str` HANDLES into an inline `Str` (`t = if (c) { a } else { b };`) keeps
    the one-word store, with the typed-pointer warning, by decision — an if-expression's value is untyped, as for every
    inline struct; assign in each branch (struct_parameter_is_a_copy_v6616).

6.7.16 (CHANGELOG [6.7.16] ZN-9, SS-35, FF-7):
  - AN UNTYPED TOP-LEVEL IF-EXPRESSION OF STRUCT BRANCHES BINDS THE STRUCT (the user's D16 (a), 2026-10-19: "G is the
    struct"; ZN-9): `var G = if (c) { B1 } else { B1 };` with branches of 8 bytes or less is that struct in the
    declaration zone and after the first statement, as in a fn — `G.a` reads it (it was "no struct type in scope for
    'G'"), `G + G` dispatches `P1_add`; a later branch of another type is refused once; a 16-byte branch is refused
    naming the struct, "an if expression branch cannot be a struct value over 8 bytes: struct 'P2'" (a 16-byte struct
    GLOBAL there was refused as "a vector, u128 or slice value"). In the zone, branches that are a generic call's or
    method's instance bind that instance (SS-35; `_gu_ie` declined every generic struct).
  - A BRANCH BODY IS ALWAYS BRACED (FF-7; refused before and after): `if (c) y = 1;`, `else y = 2;` are one "expected
    '{', got identifier 'y'", the code after parsed as written (it closed the enclosing body and invented "expected '}',
    got end of file").


[[entries]]
name = "loop_and_do_while_v675"
description = "cyrius 6.7.5 B5: `loop { … }` (contextual — a keyword only as `loop {` at a statement's start) and `do { … } while (c);` (`do` fully reserved); `continue` in a do goes to the condition, the do body is its own scope, both run in a const fn."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.5", "loop", "do while", "B5", "contextual keyword", "const fn"]
---
SYNTAX: `loop { … }` is `while (1) { … }` written plainly — NO condition is emitted or tested at its top
(a `while (1)` tests its constant every pass); `break` / `return` are its only exits. `do { … } while (c);`
runs the body once, then tests `c` after every pass; the trailing `;` is required. Both are statements:
no value, and `break` takes none (`break 5;` is a syntax error).

CONTINUE: in a `do` it goes to the CONDITION (as in C): `do { i += 1; if (i < 5) { continue; } } while (i < 3);`
stops at 3. In a `loop` it goes to the top.

SCOPE: the do body is its own scope (every braced body is, since 6.6.17) — the condition cannot see a `var`
declared inside: `do { var t = next(); } while (t != 0);` is "undefined variable 't'". Declare it before.

KEYWORDS: `do` is a full reserved word (`fn do()`, `var do`, a field `do` refused by name; 0 uses existed).
`loop` is CONTEXTUAL, like `kernel` (v5.8.45): only `loop {` at the start of a statement is the loop;
`var loop = 1; while (loop == 1) { … loop = 0; }`, a fn / field / struct named loop all compile (215 such
sites in 6 repos at 6.7.5). An identifier at statement start is never followed by `{`, so they never meet.
It is in neither reserved table; `[embed] loop` is a legal name.

PLACES: anywhere a `while` goes — fns, closures, generic instances, top level (entry code). A switch / match
inside is left by its own `break`. A tail call inside keeps its `jmp` unless the loop takes a frame address
(a do's condition included). An if-expression branch refuses either by name (one expression, not a statement).
CONST FN: both are in the pure subset, run under the 10,000,000-step budget (an endless `loop` in a const
context is refused by name).
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/loop_do_checked.sh (+ mutation ledger); tests/tcyr/crossos/loop_do_values.tcyr.
CHANGELOG [6.7.5].
6.7.16 (FF-7; refused before and after): a `loop` / `do` / `for` / `while` body is always braced — `do y = 1; while
(c);` and `for (..) y = i;` are one "expected '{', got identifier 'y'", the code after parsed as written (the head's `{`
step used to step one token whatever it was, so the enclosing body's `}` closed the block and an invented "expected
'}', got end of file" followed).

[[entries]]
name = "compound_assignment_every_lvalue_v675"
description = "cyrius 6.7.5 B8: `x OP= e` with eleven operators (`>>>=` new) on every lvalue — fields at any depth, slices, *T, self, captures, `*p`, classic-for steps; the address is taken ONCE, BEFORE the right-hand side; OP= on a struct value, a bool, a const or a call result's field is refused by name."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.5", "compound assignment", "+=", ">>>=", "field", "B8", "evaluation order"]
---
OPERATORS: `+= -= *= /= %= &= |= ^= <<= >>= >>>=` — `>>>=` (arithmetic shift) since 6.7.5, everywhere incl.
a const fn. LVALUES: a variable, `a[i]`, a field at any depth (`p.x`, `o.i.b`, `a.next.v` through a `*T`
field, `h.name.len` through a Str, a slice's `s.len`), through a `*T` local / param / global, `self: *T`, a
closure capture, and `*p`. Also a classic-`for` step (`for (h.n = 0; h.n < 5; h.n += 1)` — a field or `*p`
step takes plain `=` too, and a struct-valued field step copies the whole struct). Before 6.7.5 each of these
was `expected '=', got '+'`. Since 6.7.14 (ST-4) a step also stores through a `: *T` call's result —
`gp().n += 1`, `o.gp().n = v`, `gpg<H>().n += 1`, `fncall0(&gp).n += 1` — as the statement does
(field_chains_any_depth_and_call_result_fields_v6612); a by-value result is refused there as in the statement.

WIDTH / KIND: as reading the place — i8 / i16 / i32 load sign-extended and store at their width (wrap there);
u8 / u16 / u32 / f32 FIELDS are full words until B7; `+= -= *= /=` on f64 / f32 are float ops with
`x = x + e`'s warnings; `p += n` on a `*T` steps sizeof(T); `*p OP= v` is a WORD op like `*p`.

ORDER ⚠: a compound assignment computes the lvalue's address ONCE, BEFORE the right-hand side (as `a[i] OP= v`
always did); a plain `x.f = e` evaluates `e` first. They differ only when the RHS moves the lvalue:
`GP.n += repoint();` updates the OLD object; `GP.n = GP.n + repoint();` reads the old, writes the new.

REFUSED BY NAME: OP= on a STRUCT value (variable of any size, by-value param, global, struct-typed field) —
it used to integer-operate on the FIRST WORD and never call an overloaded `T_add` (13 vs `a = a + b`'s 33):
write `a = a + b`; a bool (its result is not a bool); a const / enum constant; a field of a call's result
(`mk(3).n += 1` — a temporary). Handles keep pointer arithmetic (`*T`, pointer-mode struct vars, Str).
Since 6.7.14 the struct refusal also covers the same variable THROUGH ITS ADDRESS, `*(&s) OP= v` / `*&s` /
`*((&s))` (the user's D17 (a), ST-10: "compound assignment to struct 's' is refused - a struct value is not
an integer or a float (write `a = a + b`)"; 6.7.13 left it the first-word op — `s.x` went 3 → 13), on a local,
a by-value parameter, a global, a capture, at top level and in a for step; and `**pp OP= v` through a `**S`
(ST-7; 6.7.11's D4 with 6.7.13's D9 (a)) — write the field through `var p: *S = *pp; p.x += v`. A handle's
`*(&h) OP= v` and a scalar's `*(&n) OP= v` keep the word op.
SINCE 6.7.6 (Break 1): `+=` / `-=` on a u128 carry across all 128 bits and every other u128 OP= is refused;
OP= on a SIMD vector, a typed array, a slice or a bare `var b[N]` is refused by name
(f32_writes_round_and_u128_arithmetic_v676, wide_value_writes_refused_v676).
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/field_compound_checked.sh (+ mutation ledger); tests/tcyr/crossos/field_compound_values.tcyr.
CHANGELOG [6.7.5].

6.7.11 (RET-10, ST-17): a captured name's `x = v;` / `x OP= v` in a closure is ONE error at `x`, before `v`
is parsed — "'x' is captured by value; a captured name is not assignable in a closure" (it was "undefined
variable"); copy it into a local (`var x_l = x;`). A captured struct's FIELD (`h.n += d`) writes the closure's
own copy. Since 6.7.14 (RC-1) the refusal holds WHETHER OR NOT A GLOBAL HAS THE NAME — through 6.7.13 a
same-named global silently took `y += d` (closures_v637_v638).

[[entries]]
name = "f32_writes_round_and_u128_arithmetic_v676"
description = "cyrius 6.7.6 (Break 1, user decisions 2026-10-08): every write of an f64 value into an `f32` ROUNDS to f32 (initializer, assignment, field, struct-literal field, `f32` argument); `u128` `+` / `-` carry and borrow across all 128 bits in both spellings, comparisons / truth tests / match / switch read all 128 bits (unsigned), a plain assignment takes the whole value, and every other u128 operator is refused by name."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.6", "f32", "u128", "rounding", "carry", "comparison", "match", "Break 1"]
---
F32 — EVERY WRITE ROUNDS. An f64 value written into an `f32` place is rounded to the nearest f32, ties
to even — what `f32_from(1.5)` gives, and what a 6.7.4 `f32[N]` list element already took:

    var x: f32 = 1.5;     # a local, a global (either side of the first statement), a for init
    x = 2.5;              # an assignment: a statement or a classic-for step, local or global
    p.a = 1.5;            # a field store (through a *T too); P { 1.5 } — a struct-literal field
    g(1.5);               # an argument to `v: f32` — a method, a fn defined below the call, and an
                          # operator overload's right operand (`r + 1.5` with fn R_add(self, b: f32))

"An f64 value" is one the compiler sees as f64: a float literal, f64 const arithmetic, an `f64`
variable / field / `: f64` fn, a float builtin's result. An f32 value is stored as it is; an integer
CONSTANT keeps its bits (6.6.10's warning); an untyped word (`load32(p)`) is the boxed idiom and is not
converted. Until 6.7.6 each of these stored the f64 BIT PATTERN — `x` read as 0.0, silently.
NOT ROUNDED: `*p = v` through `p: *f32` stays a raw word store (the `*p` rule); and an f32 OPERATOR
still reads its right operand's low 32 bits, so `x = x + 1.5` adds 1.5's bits and then stores an f32 —
write `x + f32_from(1.5)` (6.6.11's warning fires).

U128 `+` AND `-` CARRY, in both spellings — `b = b + x`, `b += x`, `b = b - x`, `b -= x` — with a u128
or an integer on either side, chains and parentheses included; unary `-b` is `0 - b`. An integer
operand is its 64-bit pattern ZERO-EXTENDED (the initializer / u128[N]-element rule): `b + (0 - 1)`
adds 2^64 - 1 — write `b - 1`.

    var b: u128 = 0 - 1;  # low 2^64 - 1, high 0
    b += 1;               # low 0, high 1: load64(&b + 8) == 1

COMPARISONS `== != < <= > >=` with a u128 on either side compare all 128 bits, UNSIGNED, against a
u128 or a zero-extended integer, wherever a comparison is written (a condition, an `&&` / `||`
operand, a value, an if-expression condition). A TRUTH TEST is a comparison with zero: `if (b)`,
`elif`, `while`, `do .. while`, a for condition, an `&&` / `||` operand and `!b` are true / false on
all 128 bits (`if (b)` is taken for b = 2^64, whose low word is 0). `match b` / `switch (b)` on a u128
subject compares each arm / case on all 128 bits, an integer arm zero-extended (`case -1:` is
2^64 - 1); the subject is read once. An INTEGER `match n` compares a u128 arm on 128 bits too.
WHOLE VALUE: `var a: u128 = c;` and `a = c;` copy all 16 bytes; `var a: u128 = 5;` and `a = 5;`
leave a high word of 0. Before 6.7.6 neither spelling carried, comparisons read the low words SIGNED,
truth tests and match / switch read the low word, `b = c` / `b = 5` kept the old high word, and a
LOCAL initializer stored its value into BOTH halves.

REFUSED BY NAME until implemented — every other operator with a u128 operand: `* / % << >> >>> & | ^ ~`,
`*% *| *? +| +? -| -?` and their `OP=`:

    error: `*` on u128 'b' is refused - it is not implemented for u128 yet (only `+` and `-` are;
    lib/bayan.cyr's bayan_u128_* helpers cover the rest)
    error: compound assignment `<<=` to u128 'b' is refused - ...

STILL THE LOW WORD: a u128 read where an integer is expected (`var n = b`, an argument, `return b`).
An if-expression branch that is a u128 `+` / `-` result is refused like a u128 name.
A u128 IS NOT A PARAMETER, RETURN OR FIELD TYPE YET (6.7.14 — the user's D10 (a); CHANGELOG [6.7.14] FE-26):
`fn big(v: u128)` is refused "`u128` is not supported as a parameter type yet - pass `*u128`" (a trait member's
parameter too); `fn mk(): u128` "... as a fn return type yet"; `struct S { w: u128; }` (a union's too) "... as a
field type yet" — once per token, `--syntax-only` too. Through 6.7.13 a `v: u128` parameter was ONE 8-byte slot
holding the low word, compared SIGNED (`big(0)` with `v > 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF` was 1), and `&v + 8` read the next
frame slot; the return and field said "expected identifier, got reserved keyword 'u128'". Pass `*u128` (`f(&b)`) or
the two halves. `*u128` and a `*fn( .. )`'s own list are unchanged. A CLOSURE'S CAPTURED u128 reads its full value
since 6.7.14 (`==`, `<`, `+` / `-` with carry, a truth test, `!`, `match`, a store, a nested capture) — through 6.7.13
the read was the env copy's ADDRESS (`c == 5` false for `c = 5`). Still not carried: u128 parameters / returns /
fields as 16-byte values (the roadmap_6 arc). A trait member's `: u128` RETURN type compiled through 6.7.14; refused
since 6.7.15 (D3 — traits_checked_v670), and a default on a bare `u128` parameter reports only the type's refusal
(FE-4 — default_and_named_arguments_v677).
BACKENDS: x86 add/adc and sub/sbb (PE and Mach-O share it), aarch64 adds/adc and subs/sbc, cx with an
unsigned compare.
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/silent_values_checked.sh (rows F W U C T S, + mutation ledger);
tests/tcyr/crossos/f32_scalar_init_rounds.tcyr, f32_writes_round.tcyr, u128_add_sub_carry.tcyr,
u128_compare_all_bits.tcyr, u128_assign_whole_value.tcyr, u128_compound_matches_long_form.tcyr.
CHANGELOG [6.7.6].

6.7.11 (RET-10): a refused u128 `OP=` does not parse its right-hand side (one error, no cascade).

6.7.15 — `*pu` THROUGH A TYPED `*u128` IS REFUSED BY NAME, as `pu[0]` is (the user's D20; CHANGELOG [6.7.15] ST-11; a
language change). A u128 element does not fit one register, and the dereference read and wrote its LOW 8 bytes,
silently: `var x: u128 = *pa;` dropped the high word, `*pa == a` compared the low word, a branch `{ *pa }` was the low
word, `*pa = b` left the old high word. Read or stored — `*pu`, `*pu = v`, `*pu OP= v`, a for step — through any operand
the compiler knows is a `*u128` (a name, a `*u128` field, a call or method declared `: *u128`, `p + n`, a wrap, a
parameter, a capture, `**pp` of a `**u128`, top level) it is now ONE error at the `*`:
    cannot dereference 'pu': a u128 element does not fit one register - use load64/store64 on its two halves
The halves are at byte offsets 0 and 8 through an UNTYPED address (`pu + 8` on a typed `*u128` steps 8 ELEMENTS). An
untyped `var p = &b;` (it reads and writes the low word), an untyped copy `var q = pa;`, `*&a`, and a `**u128`'s `*pp`
(the `*u128` itself) are unchanged.

[[entries]]
name = "wide_value_writes_refused_v676"
description = "cyrius 6.7.6 (Break 1, user decisions 2026-10-08): writes that kept only a FIRST WORD are refused by name — a top-level `var v = pair_fn();` (it kept the tag, dropped the payload), `OP=` on a SIMD vector, a typed array, a slice or a bare `var b[N]` / `stack var b[N]`, and a whole-array `a = v` on a typed array — as 6.7.5 refused `OP=` on a struct."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.6", "compound assignment", "pair bind", "stack enum", "typed array", "SIMD", "slice", "Break 1"]
---
TOP-LEVEL PAIR BIND. A `: stack` enum's fn returns two values; `var r = f();` in a fn body has been
refused since v6.5.67. At TOP LEVEL — in the declaration zone or after the first statement — it
compiled and kept the tag, dropping the payload silently (`Ok(42)` read as 0). Now the same words:

    error: a `: stack` enum returns two values — bind both: `var tag, val = f();`

`var t, v = f();` works at top level (since v6.6.0), and `var v = f()?;` consumes the pair.

COMPOUND ASSIGNMENT on a value that is not one integer or float — each operated on its FIRST WORD:

    var v: i64v2; v += w;     # added to lane 0 only        -> refused: use the packed-op builtins
    var a: i64[4]; a += 8;    # added to a[0] (f64[N]: bits) -> refused: index it, `a[i] += b`
    s += 1;                   # a slice: moved .ptr          -> refused: `s.ptr += 1` / `s.len -= 1`
    var b[16]; b += 8;        # a bare array's first word    -> refused: `store64(&b, load64(&b) + 8)`

    error: compound assignment to vector 'v' is refused - a vector value is not an integer or a
    float (write the long form with the packed-op builtins)
    error: compound assignment to array 'a' is refused - an array is not an integer or a float
    (index an element: `a[i] += b`)

Covered: a vector local or parameter; a typed array local, global or static (any element type); a
slice; each as a statement and as a for step — and a bare array local, global, static or
`stack var b[N]`. Every ELEMENT form and the slice fields keep working. On a u128 only `+=` / `-=` are legal, and they carry (see
f32_writes_round_and_u128_arithmetic_v676).

WHOLE-ARRAY ASSIGNMENT to a TYPED array is refused too: `var a: i64[4]; a = 8;` (or `a = b`) stored
into `a[0]`, silently:

    error: assignment to array 'a' is refused - an array is not an integer or a float (assign an
    element: `a[i] = v`)

A bare array's plain `b = v` still writes its first word (not decided; unchanged).
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/silent_values_checked.sh rows P, O, B (+ mutation ledger). CHANGELOG [6.7.6].

6.7.11 (RET-7, D22): a `: stack` pair as a STRUCT-LITERAL ELEMENT — `P { f(7), 3 }`, `P { a: f(7) }`, a
global's initialiser in either zone, a nested field's leaves — is refused with the same "bind both" words;
`P { f(7)?, 3 }` stores the payload. A plain ARGUMENT (`is_ok(f())`, `g(f())`) receives the TAG and an
operand (`f(7) == 0`) reads the tag — documented, not refused (the user's choice). See
stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555.

[[entries]]
name = "struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676"
description = "cyrius 6.7.6: a struct value is an operand on EITHER side — a call / method / operator result that returns a struct dispatches its own `T_op` as the left operand and is passed through a frame temporary as the right; parentheses around a whole struct source (argument, field, variable, declaration, for step, return value) are transparent; a `: Str` field into a struct value of another type is refused by name; a name intrinsic's result is untyped."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.6", "structs", "operator overloading", "parentheses", "Str", "fncall", "struct return", "Break 1"]
---
A STRUCT RESULT AS THE LEFT OPERAND dispatches from its own type, exactly as a struct variable there
does: `p.dup() + p` calls `P_add` (it integer-added the two first words: 2 where 6 is right),
`mk3(4) - s`, `(mk3(4)) - s`, `a + b + c` with a struct-returning `+` (it added an address to a first
word and crashed), `mk3(4) - s + 10` (it dispatched `P3_add` from the type `s` left behind). An
operand of 8 B or less is passed by value (to a `*T` parameter through a temporary), a larger one by
its temporary's address. A missing `T_op` is refused (`refusing to emit binary with 1 reachable
undefined function(s)`, naming `T_op` — `mkw(3) + 4` for an 8-byte W with no W_add too), and so is an
operator fn whose first parameter is another struct. A `Str` result is a heap handle and never
dispatches (`gs() + 8` is pointer arithmetic).

A STRUCT RESULT OVER 8 B AS THE RIGHT OPERAND of an operator whose parameter takes it by address (a
`*T`, or a by-value struct over 8 B) lands in a frame temporary whose address is passed: `s - mk3(4)`,
`s - s.dbl()`, `p + p.dup()` for a 16-byte p. Its first word used to be passed as the address: SIGSEGV.
At TOP LEVEL there is no frame, so an operand on either side that needs one (`G + G.dup()`,
`G + mk2(3)`) is refused by name, as a `*T` operand of 8 B or less already was.
The same expression works as an argument (`sz(mk3(1) + a)`), a declaration (`var r = mk3(4) - s;`,
`var q: P3 = mk3(1) + a;`) and a struct fn's return (`return mk3(1) + a;`) — the last two were
`expected ';', got '-'` (the receive took the call alone).

PARENTHESES AROUND A WHOLE STRUCT SOURCE are transparent, at every depth, for every source shape (a
name, a field, a call, a method or operator result), into every struct destination:

    struct P3 { x; y; z; }
    fn P3_sub(a: *P3, b: *P3): i64 { return a.z - b.z; }
    fn mk3(v): P3 { var t: P3 = P3 { v, v, v }; return t; }
    fn rd3(p: *P3): i64 { return p.z; }
    fn demo(): i64 {
        var s: P3 = P3 { 9, 9, 9 };
        var q: P3 = (s);                          # a copy, as `var q: P3 = s;`
        q.z = 4;
        return (s - mk3(4)) + rd3((q)) + s.z;     # 5 + 4 + 9 = 18
    }

An argument (`rd3((a))` SIGSEGV'd: it pushed the VALUE), a struct-typed field (`o.i = (z.k)`), a
variable (`w = (j)`), a declaration in a fn or at top level (`var q: P3 = (z.k);` SIGSEGV'd,
`var G: P3 = (A);`) and a for step each copied ONE word before 6.7.6. A struct fn's return value too:
`return (j);`, `return (mk3(v));`, `return ((j));`, `return (a + b);` and `return (a) + b;` work in
both return classes (a retptr struct over 16 B, a 9-16 B one in two registers) — they were refused.
Parentheses that are only PART of the source keep their meaning (`sz((a) + (b))` adds first); a
refused shape inside parentheses is refused as it is without them.

A `: Str` FIELD is a Str handle as a source: into a Str parameter / variable / field or an untyped one
it is the handle, as ever; into a struct VALUE of another type it is refused like a Str variable —
`bq(h.name)` for `fn bq(b: Q)`: `cannot pass 'name' to a parameter of a different struct type in a
call to 'bq'`; `var q: Q = h.name;`, `q = h.name`, `o.q = h.name`, `q + h.name` alike. They compiled
and read Q's fields out of the Str's 16-byte header and past it.

A NAME INTRINSIC'S RESULT — `mulh64(..)`, `fncall0`..`fncall8(..)`, `callptr(..)` — is an untyped word
whatever its last argument was: `fncall1(&f, n) + 1` with `n` a struct is 100, not `P_add`'s answer.
Kept: with the callee written `&f`, `f` declared `: f64` and an f64 last argument, the result is an
f64 (`fncall1(&dbl, d) + 1.0` is an f64 add). ⟳ Since 6.7.14 (D25, D26, RC-10) a call through `&f` is
typed as `f(..)` WHATEVER ITS ARGUMENTS — an f64 for a `: f64` f (the last-argument rule is gone), a
`*T` for a `: *T` f — below. Through a variable or a closure write
`f64_add(fncall1(fp, d), 1.0)` or bind it to a `var r: f64`.
NOT YET (backlog): a parenthesised argument to a by-value struct parameter of 8 B or less skips the
6.7.3 type check (`bs1((t))`); `var r = mk3(4) - s` types `r` as `P3` even when `P3_sub` returns an
integer (also `n - s + 10`, `-s + t`). FIXED in 6.7.10 (decision 11): a struct-typed FIELD or a `*T`
dereference as the LEFT operand dispatches (`h.p - s` subtracted the first words) — and an untyped
`var c = a + b;` of a struct-returning operator takes its struct type at every size (decision 12): see
struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710.
GATES: tests/gates/codegen/struct_value_codegen.sh; tests/tcyr/crossos/struct_value_codegen.tcyr.
CHANGELOG [6.7.6].

6.7.11 — PARENTHESES AND RECEIVES (RET-8, D24): an untyped `var x = (mk());` receives the struct as
`var x = mk();` does (over 8 B, a free or generic call). An annotation that names no struct and is no
pointer, receiving a struct over 8 B from a free or generic call, is refused — "cannot initialise 'x: i64'
from 'mkp', which returns the struct 'P2' - declare 'x: P2' or bind a field" — and keeps its annotation.
Through 6.7.12 a METHOD or OPERATOR call into a scalar annotation, a `*T` annotation and a wrapped 8 B call were
not judged; since 6.7.13 all three are (below). The 6.7.6 "NOT YET" items above are
fixed in 6.7.11: `bs1((t))` is checked (struct_argument_type_checked_v673), `var r = mk3(4) - s` with an
integer `P3_sub` is untyped, and `n - s + 10` / `-s + t` are integer arithmetic
(struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711). § Parentheses gains the literal element:
`H { 7, (mkq(4, 9)), 8 }` at every size (a wrapped 9-16 B METHOD / operator element was *unexpected '}'*
through 6.7.12; taken since 6.7.13 — below).
A CALL THROUGH A FN POINTER (6.7.11, ARG-1 / ARG-2, D31) knows its callee only when written `&f`:
`fncallN(&f, ..)` / `callptr(&f, ..)` with a struct-returning `f` carries the struct result as `f(..)` does
(the hidden pointer over 16 B, the pair at 9-16 B) and is a struct value in every destination — a
declaration (an untyped one infers the struct), an assignment, an argument, a field store, `return`, an
operator (it dispatches `S_add`), an 8 B global in the declaration zone; a vector-returning `f` likewise
(typed_simd_value_type_abi). At top level a result over 8 B is refused by name, as `f(..)`'s is. A callee
held in a VARIABLE (`var fp = &mk; callptr(fp, ..)`) is unknown: its result is one word, and a struct-typed
receive is a HANDLE (the user's D31, 2026-10-13) — right for a `: *S` callee, unsupported for a struct
returned by value (use the two-word receive at 9-16 B, or an out-pointer). A parenthesised callee
`callptr((&mk), ..)` counted as a variable through 6.7.12; since 6.7.13 it is the known callee (below). The
NAME INTRINSIC paragraph above holds for every other callee shape.
6.7.12 (D28) — THE SAME REFUSAL THROUGH A FN POINTER WRITTEN `&f`: `var x: i64 = fncall1(&mk3, 5);` /
`callptr(&mk3, 5)` with a struct over 8 B is "cannot initialise 'x: i64' from 'mk3', which returns the struct
'P3' - declare 'x: P3' or bind a field" (through 6.7.11 it read the first field). A callee in a variable stays
a word. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] FX-3.

6.7.13 — 6.7.11's D24 IS ONE RULE AT EVERY PATH (the user's D13, D14, D17; CHANGELOG [6.7.13] RC-1, RC-2, RC-3, RC-6, RC-7,
RC-10, ST-11): free call, generic, `&f` (wrapped too), method, operator, top level, and a pointer annotation (in a fn).
  - A METHOD or OPERATOR result over 8 B into an annotation that names no struct is refused with D24's words naming
    the fn, in a fn and at top level (D13; `var x: i64 = b.mk();`, `= a + c;` — through 6.7.12 it took the first word,
    narrowed). A refused declaration takes no struct behind its annotation.
  - A BY-VALUE STRUCT over 8 B into ANY `*T` annotation is refused in a fn — "… declare 'x: P2' and take `&x`" — at
    every path (direct, wrapped, generic, known `&f`, method, operator; D14); 8 B or less keeps the word and the
    typed-pointer warning. (Top level was not judged in 6.7.13; refused since 6.7.14 — the user's D28,
    struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711.)
  - AT TOP LEVEL (ST-11): `var G: i64 = mk2(5);`, `fncall1(&mk2, ..)` and `callptr(&mk2, ..)` with a 9-16 B struct —
    in the declaration zone or after the first statement, a redeclared global too — are refused with D24's words
    (once: a `: bool` gets no second "not a bool"); over 16 B keeps the no-frame refusal; 8 B or less and `Str` keep
    the word. 6.7.12's D28 is judged there too, `var x: i64 = fncall1((&mk3), 5);` included.
  - A WRAPPED 8 B RECEIVE (RC-2): `var x = (mk1());` binds the 8 B struct as `var x = mk1();` does (it was an untyped
    word — so `x + 1` now dispatches `P1_add`, and is refused "undefined function 'P1_add'" without one; annotate
    `: i64` for the word — kept by the user's 6.7.14 D52); `var x = (f());` with `f: f64` is an f64.
  - A PARENTHESISED `&f` CALLEE IS THE KNOWN CALLEE (RC-1): `callptr((&mk3), 5)` / `fncall1((&mk3), 5)` carry the
    struct / vector result to every fn destination, and at top level it is judged as `&f` unwrapped. `callptr((fp),
    ..)` is still a variable callee.
  - NESTED-LITERAL ELEMENTS (RC-3, RC-10): a wrapped 9-16 B method / operator element is taken, and a wrapped
    struct-typed FIELD `(h.q)` with it; a wrapped struct NAME `(q0)` as an element was still refused (6.7.14 — below).
    A wrapped dyn-returning call is a `dyn` element as the unwrapped call is (D17: `H { 7, (pass(o)), 8 }`); `var x =
    (pass(o));` stays refused (6.7.8 V4d).
  - 6.7.13's open list — the ASSIGNMENT `x = b.mk();` into a scalar, `var s = (mks());` with `: Str`, `var a = (f()) *
    2.0;`, `(s).a` / `(mk1()).a` — is closed in 6.7.14, below (the assignment: the user's D27,
    struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711).

6.7.14 — THE WRAP IS TRANSPARENT IN THE LAST POSITIONS, AND `&f` IS TYPED AS `f(..)` (the user's D24 – D26, D45, D46;
CHANGELOG [6.7.14] RC-5 .. RC-8, RC-10, RC-11, RC-20, RC-21):
  - `(e).field` / `(e).m(..)` (D24 (a), RC-11): `(s).a`, `((s)).a`, `(p).a` and `(*p).a` for a `*S`, `(s).m()`,
    `(mk1()).a`, `(mk3(5)).b` (24 B, through a temporary), a method / operator / known `&f` result, a `: *S` call, a
    `Str` call, chains, a struct destination (`var x: P2 = (b).mk();`), a top-level `var GPF = (GP1).a;`, and the
    statement `(s).m();` read exactly as without the wrap — each was "expected ';', got '.'". Refused by name: a wrap
    around a value that is no struct, `(x + 1).a` ("a field is read only from a struct value - the value in these
    parentheses is not a struct (or a pointer to one)"), and a store through it, `(s).a = v;` / `+= v` ("cannot assign
    to a field after parentheses - write the store without them (`s.a = v`)"). In 6.7.14 `(s).a;` as a statement was
    a discarded read and compiled, where the unwrapped `s.a;` is refused — beyond D24's text; the user's 6.7.15 D60
    refuses it as `s.a;` is (below).
  - A STRUCT NAME IN PARENTHESES AS A NESTED-STRUCT LITERAL ELEMENT (RC-7): `HE { 7, (q0), 8 }`, `((q0))`, a global
    `(G)` — the element the unwrapped name is (it was "unexpected '}'").
  - THE UNTYPED RECEIVE IN A FN: `var s = (mks());` with a `: Str` call is a `Str` (RC-5; it bound a word, so
    `println(s)` printed the header's bytes); `var a = (f()) * 2.0;` with a `: f64` call is an f64 (RC-6). At TOP LEVEL
    `var s = (mks());` was still a word through 6.7.14 (routed since 6.7.15, RC-4 — below).
  - THE DESTRUCTURE AND THE TUPLE CAPTURE (D45, RC-20): `var a, b = (f(7));`, `a, b = ((f()));`, a for step, both
    top-level zones, `var t: (i64, i64) = (two(3));` — ret2_rethi. `(f(7)) + 1` / `(f(7) + 1)` are not one call and
    stay refused.
  - THE `: stack` FIRST-CALL RULE (D46, RC-21): a store whose WHOLE value is parenthesised and starts with a pair call —
    `var v = (f(7) + 1);`, `h.a = (f(7) * 2);`, `((f(7)) + 1)` — is refused "bind both" as unwrapped
    (stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555).
  - A KNOWN `&f` CALLEE IS TYPED AS `f(..)` (D25 (a), D26 (a), RC-10): `fncallN(&f, ..)` / `callptr(&f, ..)` with `f`
    declared `: f64` is an f64 WHATEVER ITS ARGUMENTS (`var a = fncall0(&f);`, wrapped, `fncall0(&f) * 2.0` bind an
    f64; 6.7.6's last-argument rule is gone), and with `f` declared `: *T` a `*T` — `fncall0(&gp) + 1` steps one
    element, `var w: *P2 = fncall0(&gp);` is silent. A struct is 6.7.11's. A callee held in a VARIABLE is still a word
    (6.7.11's D31): copy the result untyped for byte arithmetic (`var q = fncall0(&gp); q + 1` steps one byte).
  - `callptr(&gp, 5).b = 40;` and `+= 40` store through a `: *T` result as statements, as `fncall1` does (RC-8;
    field_chains_any_depth_and_call_result_fields_v6612).

6.7.15 — PARENTHESISED DESTINATIONS AND STATEMENTS, AND THE KNOWN `&f` CALLEE'S BOOL AND ELEMENT (the user's D19, D30,
D31, D60; CHANGELOG [6.7.15] ST-12, ST-15, ST-16, RC-4, RC-9, RC-10):
  - A PARENTHESISED ASSIGNMENT DESTINATION IS REFUSED BY NAME (D19, ST-15; refused before and after): `(*pn) += 1;`,
    `(*pn) = 1;`, `(n) = 7;`, `((n)) = 7;`, `(s.a) = 3;`, `(buf[1]) = 3;`, `(n) <<= 1;`, a top-level `(Q) += 2;`,
    `(*(&s)) -= 1;` on a struct — "an assignment's destination is not parenthesised - write `*pn += 1`" (the statement
    quoted without its wraps), once, at the `(`; they were "unexpected '('". `(n + 1) = 7;`, `(f()) = 3;` and a tuple
    literal `(a, b) = ..` keep "unexpected '('"; `(s).a = v;` keeps 6.7.14's D24 words. `(pn)[0] = 1;` kept "unexpected
    '('" through 6.7.15; since 6.7.16 it is the read's words (D22, ST-7 — below).
  - A FIELD READ ALONE THROUGH PARENTHESES IS REFUSED, as `s.a;` is (D60 — item 19 of the 6.7.14 changes beyond the
    decisions' text; ST-16): `(s).a;`, `(p).a;`, `(*p).a;`, `(w).p.a;`, `((s)).a;`, `(*(&s)).a;`, `(p + 0).a;`, a
    top-level `(GS).a;` — "the field read 'a' is discarded - a statement is an assignment or a call", once, at the last
    field; `--syntax-only` too. They compiled in 6.7.14 only (and did nothing). A CALL anywhere keeps the statement, as
    without the parentheses: `(mk1()).a;`, `(s).m();`, `(s).me().a;`, `(gp()).a;`, `(*p).m();`, `(x + y).b;` with a
    struct operator.
  - `&` OF SOMETHING THAT IS NOT A NAME is one "expected identifier" (ST-12): `&(x)`, `&((x))`, `&5`, `&*q` no longer
    add a false "undefined variable 'fn'" (the program's first word). `&(x)` stays invalid syntax.
  - A KNOWN `&f` CALLEE'S `: bool` RESULT IS A BOOLEAN VALUE (D30, RC-9) — bool_true_false_not_v673; its NARROW `*T`
    RESULT CARRIES ITS ELEMENT (D31, RC-10; the stores kept by 6.7.16's D64): with `fn bp(): *u8`, `*fncall0(&bp)` / `*callptr(&bp)` read and write ONE
    byte, as `*bp()` does (through 6.7.14 the whole word, the stores too), `*(fncall0(&ip) + 1)` with `ip: *i32` reads
    4 bytes, a `*i8` callee sign-extends, and `fncall0(&bp)[1]` / `callptr(&bp)[1]` subscript and store (it was "it does
    not return a *T") — pointer_subscripts_v678. A callee held in a variable is still a word (6.7.11's D31): bind the
    call untyped (`var w = fncall0(&f);`) to keep one. `(fncall0(&gp)).b` / `(*fncall0(&gp)).b` with `gp: *P2` were
    refused "the value in these parentheses is not a struct" through 6.7.15; since 6.7.16 they read the field (ST-6 —
    typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617).
  - AT TOP LEVEL `var s = (mks());` WITH A `: Str` CALL IS ROUTED as `var s = mks();` is (RC-4): `println(s)` prints the
    string, `who(s)` reaches `who_str` (it took the cstring base and printed the header's address bytes). It is still a
    word — `s.len()` is refused at top level, wrapped or not. A `: Str` METHOD global was not routed through 6.7.15;
    since 6.7.16 it is (ZN-10 — below).

6.7.16 (the user's D22, 2026-10-19; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] ST-7, ZN-10, SB-2, SS-6):
  - A SUBSCRIPT STORE THROUGH A PARENTHESISED BASE (D22, ST-7; refused before and after): `(pn)[0] = 1;`,
    `(*(&pn))[1] = 2;`, `(pn)[0] += 1;`, `(pp)[0][1] = 1;`, `(mk())[0] = 1;`, top level, a generic (once) — is refused
    once, at the `(`, in the read's words: "a subscript's base is a name, a *T field or a call returning *T - name the
    pointer first (var q: *T = ..; q[i])"; the next statement parses normally; `(pn)[0];` keeps "unexpected '('".
    Dropping the parentheses is no fix: `*pn[0]` is `*(pn[0])`. A classic-for STEP with a parenthesised destination is
    refused in its statement's words too (SS-5; it said "expected '='"). `cyrius lint` refuses `(s).a = 1;` as the
    build does (SS-6).
  - A `: Str` METHOD GLOBAL IS ROUTED (ZN-10): `var s = BB.mks();` sends `println(s)` to `println_str` (it printed the
    header's bytes) — wrapped, at the end of a field chain (`W.b.mks()`), through a `*B` receiver, `.m<..>(..)`, in the
    zone and after the first statement, the method declared anywhere; the Str → `: cstring` warning reaches it.
  - A CHAIN ON A `callptr(&f, ..)` RESULT AS A BY-VALUE STRUCT ARGUMENT passes the struct its last step gives (SB-2):
    `take(callptr(&gw, &w).p.q16())`, `take(callptr(&gw, &w).q)`, wrapped — as `take(gw(&w).p.q16())` does (through
    6.7.15 a word was pushed as the address: SIGSEGV on x86_64 / aarch64, an access violation on PE, a guest fault on
    cx); a multi-value method there is refused "a multi-value call 'm2' is not a struct 'Q16' argument - bind it first"
    (it compiled and crashed).

[[entries]]
name = "tuples_v677"
description = "cyrius 6.7.7 B4 (user decisions 2026-10-08 / 2026-10-09): tuples — `(a, b)` builds an anonymous struct of 8-byte slots, `(i64, f64)` is its type (a var, a parameter, a struct field, a multi-value return), `t.0` / `t.1` read and write it; a tuple captures a multi-value call by its TYPE (`var t: (i64, i64) = f();`), `return t;` returns one, `a, b = f();` re-assigns existing variables; `var x = f();` keeps its first value; every open shape is refused by name."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.7", "tuples", "B4", "multi-value return", "anonymous struct", "t.0", "capture by type", "a, b = f()"]
---
SYNTAX:
    fn tuples(): i64 {
        var t = (5, 9);                  # (i64, i64): an un-annotated literal's elements are i64
        var p: (i64, f64) = (1, 2.5);    # a float element needs the type declared
        t.0 = t.0 + 1;                   # an element reads and writes like a field (OP= too)
        t.1 *= 2;
        t = (t.1, t.0);                  # every element is read before any is stored: a swap
        var u: (i64, i64) = t;           # a copy, by value
        if (p.1 > 2.0) { return t.0 * 10 + t.1; }   # 186
        return 0;
    }
The decision (user, 2026-10-08; roadmap.md § Spec, B4): a tuple bridges to the multi-value returns and is
captured by TYPE; a shape it leaves open is refused by name, never given a default meaning. The plan's forks
(user, 2026-10-09): a float element in an un-annotated literal is refused; the four lossy `: stack` pair
stores are refused; a closure captures a tuple under the struct rule.

TYPE: `(T1, T2, ..)` on a `var` (a local; a global in either zone), a parameter, a struct field, and a fn's
multi-value return `: (T1, T2[, T3])`. An anonymous struct of 8-byte slots, interned once per element list
under its spelling `(i64, f64)` (the name every diagnostic prints; `(` is never an identifier byte, so no user
struct or minted `Box$..` name collides), with fields named "0", "1", .. — `t.0` is an ordinary field access,
OP= included. Elements: i64, f64 (a typed float field, GFFK 1: `t.1 + t.1` is a float add), bool (a checked
bool field, GFBOOL — `ADDFIELDFK` kind 3, which records the bool map, not GFFK); 2..256 of them. Equal
element lists are ONE sid — `(i64,i64)` and `(i64, i64)` copy into each other — and a copy between any other
pair is the struct copy's type error. Every tuple is >= 16 B: always
inline, never pointer-mode, never MARK_SMALL_STRUCT. A tuple field is laid out inline (`struct H { a; p: (i64,
i64); b; }` is 32 B); a positional literal fills its leaves (`H { 1, 2, 3, 4 }`) or takes a whole tuple
(`H { 1, t, 4 }`).

REPRESENTATION: the struct table's caps hold — 1024 structs, 256 fields per struct, 8192 field-pool entries
(one struct entry + n pool entries per DISTINCT tuple type). No heap region, no layout change. A tuple return is
the multi-value convention (MULTIRET_SID; x86 / PE rax:rdx:r8, aarch64 x0:x2:x3, cx r0:r4:r5) — never the
struct-return retptr and never the 9-16 B E*_STRUCT_INT_PAIR class.

LITERAL: `(a, b)` / `(a, b, c)` .. (2..256 elements) in FIVE positions: a `var` initializer (a local; a global
in either zone — with constant elements a declaration-zone global is BAKED into the image as `var G = P { 3, 4 };`
is, so a `kernel;` build reads it before the late replay), `t = (..);` into a tuple variable (built in a frame
temporary first, so `t = (t.1, t.0)` swaps; a classic-for step too), `h.p = (..);` into a tuple field,
`take((..))` into a tuple parameter, and `return (a, b);`. In the first four `((a, b))` is the same literal (the
6.7.6 wrap rule); the return keeps its exact spelling (`return ((a, b));` is refused). Annotated, the elements
take the annotation's types with the struct literal's checks (an integer into an f64 element keeps its bits,
warned). Un-annotated, the literal is `(i64, .., i64)` — `var q = (1, 2);` is the spelled `(i64, i64)` — and a
float element is refused: declare the type (ADR-002: an untyped float is an integer). `secret var t = (..);` is
wiped whole at the exit.

PARAMETERS: `fn f(t: (i64, f64), k)` is a by-value struct parameter: the struct-mask bit (address-passed), the
prologue copy (the callee's writes never reach the caller), SLTYPE = -sid and the argument's sid check. Pass 1
(`_prescan_params_scan` -> `_tup_pscan`, interning quietly) and pass 2 (PARSE_FN_DEF -> `_tup_param`) both read
it, so a forward call is counted and address-passed like a backward one. Methods, trait methods (`_tr_arity`
counts it as one), Win64's 5th / 6th and every stack-passed argument take the struct path. A tail call that hands
over a tuple in its own frame, or a literal's temporary, becomes a real call; one handing over a global's keeps
its jmp.

CAPTURE (THE BRIDGE): `var t: (i64, f64) = f();`, `t = f();` (a for init / step too), `G = f();` at top level,
both global zones. The right-hand side is what `var a, b = f();` takes — ONE whole call `f(..)` / `g<T>(..)` /
`x.m(..)`, and since 6.7.14 `callptr(fp, ..)` and any parentheses around the whole call — and a callee declared
`: (T1, T2[, T3])` is checked element by element (f64 <- f64, bool <- a declared
bool, i64 <- any integer class); an undeclared / ret2 / `: stack` / Result / Option / Tagged / `: f64` callee is
unchecked (a bool element still needs a declared bool). Every value is read before any is stored, so
`t = f(t.1, t.0);` reads the old t and `var t: T = f(t.1);` an OUTER t. `var x = f();` is still the FIRST value
(the ret2 / rethi() idiom): nothing that compiled changed meaning.

RETURN AND RE-ASSIGN: `return t;` (a local, a by-value parameter, a global, `return (t);`) from a fn declared
`: (T1, T2[, T3])`, element classes exact, in the multi-value registers — a defer fn, a loop and an #inline
replay too. `a, b = f();` / `a, b, c = f();` re-assigns 2-3 plain variables (locals, parameters, globals, at top
level, f64 and bool targets, a for init / step) under the destructure's contract — the re-poll loop no longer
binds a fresh pair each pass:
    while (st != 0) { st, v = poll(); acc = acc * 10 + v; }

CLOSURES, DEFERS, ASYNC: a closure captures a tuple as it captures a struct — a by-value copy of the whole tuple,
taken when the closure is made. `t.0` in the body reads the copy, an element write stays in it (across the
closure's calls), `take(t)` passes the copy on; `t = ..` to the captured name is refused — "'t' is captured
by value; a captured name is not assignable in a closure" since 6.7.11 (it was "undefined variable"), as any
captured assignment is. A defer reads a tuple as it is at the exit; `return t;` takes the values before the
defers run. A tuple local across an `await` works (x86 coroutines; tuple_checked.sh AS1 / AS2); an `async fn`
tuple parameter and `return t;` from an `async fn` are refused by name.

NOT A NUMBER: a whole tuple — or a whole tuple FIELD — is not one value. `t == u`, `t + 1`, `!t`, `-t`, `if (t)`,
`while (t)`, `match t`, `switch (t)`, `for x in t`, `g(t)` into an untyped parameter, `load64(t)`,
`callptr(fp, t)`, `var x = t;`, an if-expression branch: "tuple 't' used as a value - take an element (`t.0`)
or copy it whole into a tuple" (the bare struct rule would read the first word). Nothing that is not a tuple
goes into one: `t = 5`, `t = g()` with a one-value g, `t = (g())`, `h.p = 5`, `var t: (i64, i64) = 5;` —
"cannot assign a value that is not a tuple to tuple 't'" (and the field / initialize forms). (A WRAPPED
multi-value call, `t = (f())`, captures since 6.7.14 — D45, below.)

LEXING: `t.0` lexes IDENT DOT IDENT"0": `_lex_dot` lexes the digit run after a `.` as a name when the token
BEFORE the DOT is an IDENT, `)` or `]`. `t.0.1` is two selectors (it was IDENT DOT FLOAT"0.1"); `0..10`, `a..5`,
`1.5`, `1.0.5` and `t. 0` (a space) lex as before. `p.0` on a named struct is "unknown field '0' on struct
'P'"; `t.2` past the arity, `t.01`, `t.0x1` and `t.1_0` are unknown fields of `(i64, i64)`.

REFUSED BY NAME (each extensible later): an element other than i64 / f64 / bool (narrow integers, u64, f32,
u128, a pointer, slice, vector, struct, a nested tuple); `()`, `(T)`, a trailing comma, an empty element, > 256;
a tuple as a pointer target (`*( .. )`, a parameter too), a slice / array element, a `Vec<( .. )>` struct field,
a sizeof / #assert sizeof operand, a type argument, a union field, a `#derive`d struct's field, a multi-value
return element, an `impl` target; a tuple type in a generic fn / struct, and a tuple binding a type parameter
(`first(t)`: "a tuple cannot bind a type parameter: 'first' is generic - pass an element (`t.0`), or take the
tuple in a fn that is not generic"); a tuple in a const context, a const fn's tuple local or parameter; a
literal outside its five positions ("a tuple `( .. )` is a value only as a `var` initializer, …"),
`return ((a, b));` ("a multi-value return is written `return (a, b);` - drop the extra parentheses"), a literal
assignment / field store / argument at top level (no frame for its temporary); a struct / tuple / u128 / vector
/ slice element, and a `: stack` pair element; methods (`t.m()`, `impl (i64, i64)`, and `x.0()` on ANY receiver:
"a method name cannot start with a digit"); `return t;` from a fn not declared with its values; a struct,
vector, mismatched or > 3-value callee captured or re-assigned ("cannot capture 'mk' into tuple (i64, i64) - it
returns struct 'P', not a tuple"; "cannot re-assign from 'mk' - it returns struct 'P', not multiple values"); a
multi-value call as a tuple argument or stored into a tuple field ("… - bind it first: `var t: (i64, i64) =
mk(..);`, then pass `t`"); `f().0` on a multi-value call; `a, b = ..` targets that are no plain variable, > 3,
duplicated, a const, an aggregate / u128 / f32, or a non-call right-hand side. `var v: Vec<(i64, i64)> = ..;`
compiles as before (the annotation's `<T>` is skipped). The declaration zone reads `name<A, B>(` by its tokens
(a generic declared below), and refuses a `<` comparison spelled like a type list inside a tuple literal there
("… - parenthesize the comparison").

`: STACK` PAIRS: every one-slot store of a `: stack` pair is refused since 6.7.7 — a field, an element, `*p`,
every OP=, a tuple-literal element (stack_enum_unboxed_payload_variants_v6555).
6.7.11: `return (a, b);` in a fn declared `: S` (a named struct) is refused — "a fn returning the struct `P` returns
that struct — `(a, b)` is a tuple, another type" (ST-15); a multi-value call is not a struct value at any size (RET-5,
struct_value_copies_and_result_type_checks_v6611); an undeclared fn whose returns are all `(a, b)` is counted, so
`var t: (i64, i64, i64) = dm(..)` is refused (RET-2).
6.7.13: the "bind it first" refusal of a multi-value call as a tuple ARGUMENT (R17) covers a callee counted by pass 1
(undeclared, every `return` `(a, b)`), a free fn or a method, not only a declared one (MV-1). A non-tuple word into
a tuple parameter (`take(5)`, `take(one(3))`, a scalar-declared multi-value callee) compiled and crashed through
6.7.13 — the user's 6.7.14 D44, below.
An element that is a whole dereference of a `*S` (`(*ps, 1)`, `(*gp(), 1)`, `(*(&s), 1)`) is refused "a tuple element
is i64, f64 or bool, not struct 'P8'", as `(s, 1)` is (D2 (a), ST-7) — it compiled as the struct's word; `(ps, 1)` and
`(ps.a, 1)` are i64s.
6.7.14 (the user's D44, D45; CHANGELOG [6.7.14] RC-18, RC-19, RC-20, RC-16):
  - A TUPLE PARAMETER TAKES THE TUPLE'S ADDRESS (D44): an untyped word is passed as it — `take(&t)`, a variable holding
    `&t`, a `: i64` call returning one (a tuple has no `*T` spelling, so the untyped word stays the address). An
    INTEGER LITERAL — `take(5)`, `take((5))`, a method's `x.take(5)`, an `#inline` callee's, at top level — is refused
    "cannot pass an integer to tuple parameter 't' (i64, i64) of 'take' - it takes a tuple"; a several-value call from
    a fn declared ONE scalar that pass 1 counted (`fn g2(a): i64 { return (a, a + 1); }`, its `ret2` form, a method, an
    overloaded operator, a `: f64` into `(f64, f64)`) is refused by R17's "a multi-value call 'g2' is not a tuple
    argument - bind it first". Each compiled and crashed (139 / 1 on cx / 5 on PE). A ONE-value call (`take(one(3))`)
    is still a word, by decision — your address, or a crash. `take(-5)`, `take(true)`, `take(5 + 0)` were still
    unrefused through 6.7.14 — refused since 6.7.15 (D36, below). Bind the values first: `var t: (i64, i64) = g2(3);
    take(t);`.
  - `callptr(fp, ..)` IS A WHOLE CALL TO THE CAPTURE as to the destructure (RC-19): `var t: (i64, i64) = callptr(fp,
    5);`, `t = callptr(..);` (a for step too), `G = callptr(..);`, a global in both zones — it was "cannot initialize
    tuple 't' with a value that is not a tuple"; the callee's count is never judged. `callptr(..) + 1` stays refused.
  - PARENTHESES AROUND THE WHOLE CALL are transparent to the capture (D45, RC-20): `var t: (i64, i64) = (two(3));`,
    `t = ((..));`, `G = (..);`; a wrapped `: stack` pair captures the tag and the payload, as unwrapped.
  - A fn left only through `ret2(..)` counts as 2 values, so a 3-tuple capture of it is refused (D42, RC-16;
    ret2_rethi).
6.7.15 (the user's D36 (a); CHANGELOG [6.7.15] RC-2, RC-3, RC-1):
  - AN ARGUMENT MADE ONLY OF LITERALS AND OPERATORS INTO A TUPLE PARAMETER IS REFUSED (D36, RC-3; a language change) —
    `take(-5)`, `take((-5))`, `take(5 + 0)`, `take(~0)`, `take((1 << 3))`, `take((1) + 2)`, a top-level `take('a' -
    1)`, the second argument of `take2(1 + 2, -5)`: "cannot pass an integer to tuple parameter 't' (i64, i64) of 'take'
    - it takes a tuple", as `take(5)` has been since 6.7.14; every literal `true` / `false` (`take(true)`,
    `x.take(!true)`) "cannot pass a bool to tuple parameter …". Through 6.7.14 each compiled and the callee read through
    the value as the tuple's address (139 on x86_64 / aarch64, 1 on cx, 5 on PE). Unchanged (D36): a NAME — a const too
    — `&t` and a call still pass their word as the address, unchecked (`take(C)` compiles, and faults unless C holds an
    address). Through 6.7.15 a float literal still compiled (`take(1.5)` faulted) and a comparison (`take(1 == 1)`,
    `take(!0)`) was refused in the integer words — both settled in 6.7.16 (D23 — below). Pass a tuple — `take((a, b))`,
    a tuple variable, or `&t`.
  - A WRAPPED multi-value call stored into a tuple FIELD (`h.p = (two(3));`, `((two(3)))`, a wrapped method, a for step)
    is refused ONCE, R17's "a multi-value call 'two' is not stored into a tuple field - bind it first" (RC-2; it added
    R12's "not a tuple" at the wrap).
  - A 3-tuple capture of a fn whose body ends in an infinite loop left only through `ret2(..);` is refused (RC-1;
    ret2_rethi).
6.7.16 (the user's D23, D104; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] RV-1 – RV-3, RV-2r, FB-4, GB-22, GB-23, SB-1, SB-1r2, SS-56):
  - THE REFUSAL NAMES THE ARGUMENT'S VALUE (D23, RV-1, RV-2, RV-2r), for a literal-only argument into a tuple parameter:
    "cannot pass a bool to tuple parameter …" for a boolean value — a comparison, `!x`, `&&` / `||`, or one in
    parentheses (`take(1 == 1)`, `take(!0)`, `take((1 != 0))`); "cannot pass an f64 …" for a value the compiler types
    f64 — a float literal, `-` / `~` of one, `+ - * /` with an f64 on EITHER side (`take(1.5)`, `take(-1.5)`,
    `take(1 + 1.5)`, `take(~1.5)`); "cannot pass an integer …" once an integer operator applies (`take((1 == 1) + 1)`,
    `take(1.5 & 1)`, and `take(2 +| 1.5)` — the saturating / checked operators give an integer, D104, FB-4). A FLOAT
    LITERAL is refused at the argument (it compiled, and the callee read the double's bits as the tuple's address: 139 /
    139 / 1 / 5). ⚠ Beyond D23's text (words only, on the filed list): a float-bearing argument the compiler types as an
    integer (`take(1.5 & 1)`, `take(-!1.5)`) is refused in the integer words. Unchanged (6.7.15's D36): a name, `&t` or a
    call is passed as the address. An argument that is no single expression (`take(1 < 2 < 3)`) gets the parse's error
    alone (RV-3).
  - A GENERIC'S MULTI-VALUE ELEMENT TYPED BY ITS TYPE PARAMETER IS THE INSTANCE'S (GB-22, GB-23): `fn mm2<V>(x: V): (V,
    i64)` at f64 returns `(f64, i64)` — `var a, b = mm2<f64>(h);` binds `a` as f64, the inferred method `p.mm(h)` too;
    `var t: (f64, i64) = mm2<f64>(h);` compiles, `var t: (i64, i64) = mm2<f64>(h);` / `mm2<Pt>(p)` into `(i64, i64)` are
    refused, as for a fn written with those values (⚠ no decision asked — on the filed list). Refusals spell the fn as
    written (`mt<f64>`, `Q.pair`, `(Box<Pt>, i64)`).
  - A CHAIN CAPTURES (SB-1, SB-1r2): the right-hand side may be a method ending a chain — `var t: (i64, i64) =
    w.p.m2();` (it was "not a tuple"), at top level `var t: (i64, i64) = (GW).p.mm(5);` — and a RE-assignment on a
    parenthesised head captures as the declaration does: `t = (*pw).p.m2();`, `t = (p).mm(5);`, a for step, a global
    `GT = (GW).p.mm(9);` (refused "cannot assign a value that is not a tuple" through 6.7.15). A wrong count is "cannot
    capture 'm3' into tuple (i64, i64) - it returns 3 values".
  - A REFUSED TUPLE ANNOTATION IS ITS DECLARATION'S ONE ERROR (SS-56): `var x: (f64v2, i64) = vf();` no longer adds a
    capture error that reads the refused element as i64.
TOOLS: `cyrius header` prints a tuple parameter as one `cyr_val` and, since 6.7.11, a two-value RETURN as `cyr_val2`
under the x86_64 SysV guard (aarch64 returns it in x0:x2) — three values are refused; the LSP's semantic tokens colour the
parameters after a tuple parameter and not its element types (both read a parameter list by paren depth).
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/tuple_checked.sh (L lexing, S shapes, R refusals, C compatibility, PW the
`_pwrap_k` callers, K1 kernel parity, AS async, SEC1, CL, ZG the zone's generic reading, X --syntax-only, T1 /
T2 tools, A the crossos file on x86 / IR=1 / IR=3 / DCE / aarch64 qemu / cx / PE wine; + mutation ledger);
tests/tcyr/crossos/tuple_values.tcyr (193 assertions; the release gate's ecb / ach / cass / pi leg);
stack_enum_lossy_context.sh axis 15. CHANGELOG [6.7.7].

[[entries]]
name = "default_and_named_arguments_v677"
description = "cyrius 6.7.7 B6 (user decisions 2026-10-08 / 2026-10-09): a parameter default is a compile-time constant on a TRAILING parameter, evaluated once at its definition; a direct call passes min..max arguments and the missing defaults are filled; named arguments `f(1, c: 3)` follow the positionals in any order, evaluated as written, placed in parameter order; fn pointers stay positional; every forward call is arity-checked; a trait method takes no default."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.7", "default arguments", "named arguments", "B6", "arity", "const", "forward call"]
---
SYNTAX:
    const LEVEL = 3;
    const fn twice(n): i64 { return n * 2; }
    fn tag(a, b = 2, c = twice(LEVEL)): i64 { return a * 100 + b * 10 + c; }
    tag(1)                  # 126 - b and c from their defaults
    tag(1, 5, 7)            # 157
    tag(1, c: 0)            # 120 - b from its default, c by name
    tag(c: 9, a: 4, b: 5)   # 459 - any order; placed as tag(4, 5, 9)
Typed parameters take defaults: `x: f64 = 1.5`, `y: f32 = 0.1`, `t: bool = true`, `u: u8 = 200`,
`p: *u8 = 0`, `s: cstring = "c"`. The decision: user, 2026-10-08 (roadmap.md § Spec, B6); the plan's forks F1
(a default in a trait's required signature is an error) and F2 (every forward call is arity-checked): user,
2026-10-09.

DEFAULTS: a default is a compile-time constant on a TRAILING parameter: whatever a top-level `const X = ..;`
takes (a literal, a const, an enum constant, sizeof, a const fn call, arithmetic — the 6.7.2 evaluator), minus a
block or an if-expression. Evaluated ONCE, at the end of pass 1 (`_pd_sweep`, after `_cst_sweep`, every fork), in
the top-level scope of the defining file, called or not: a caller's locals never reach it, a const / enum
declared later is fine, a `private` const or const fn of the defining file resolves from a call in another file.
A default naming a parameter or type parameter of its own fn is refused before evaluation (even with a global of
that name). Pass 1 records each definition's defaults (`_pd_record_at` from `_prescan_params_scan`) in one lazily
allocated row per defaulted fn (`_fnt_pdflt`; no heap region, nothing allocated without defaults); names are
never stored — the definition's tokens hold them. A generic instance shares its base's row; an async fn's
`f$impl` has none (its constructor takes every parameter).

TYPES: untyped: an integer, a bool, or a string (its literal's address). i8..i64 / u8..u64 / an enum type: an
integer in range, or a bool. bool: a bool. f64 / f32: a float, or 0 / an IEEE bit pattern (`_ic_val_ok`, the
`var x: f64 = n` rule); into an f32 it rounds (6.7.6). cstring: a string or 0. *T (and a Vec handle): an
integer. No default on Str (a literal is a heap wrap per call), a struct, a vector, u128, Result / Option /
Tagged, a tuple, a type parameter, or self. A float default on an untyped parameter is refused (ADR-002:
untyped, a float is its bits). A bare `u128` parameter is itself refused (6.7.14's D10 (a)), so since 6.7.15
(FE-4) a default on one reports only that refusal — "`u128` is not supported as a parameter type yet - pass
`*u128`", once, at the `u128` — and the default is not judged (through 6.7.14 the pass-1 default sweep printed
"… is a 128-bit integer: it takes no default" first and out of order). `fn f(b: *u128 = 0)` builds.

FILL AND ARITY: a call passes min..max arguments (min = the first defaulted parameter's index): "'f' expects 1
to 3 arguments, got 4"; a fn without defaults keeps the v6.5.1 text byte for byte ("'g' expects 2 arguments,
got 3"). The missing trailing defaults are pushed after the written arguments, in parameter order, each exactly
as its literal would be, so every convention sees one word per parameter (SysV 6 / Win64 4 / aarch64 8
registers, then the stack; cx). Fork F2: EVERY call to a fn pass 1 recorded is checked, a forward one included
— before 6.7.7 a call from a fn body to a fn defined later built with a surplus argument dropped or a missing
one unbound. Overloading by arity stays out.

NAMED: `f(1, c: 3)` — positionals first, then `name: value` in any order, each parameter at most once;
evaluated left to right AS WRITTEN, placed in PARAMETER order. In-order names cost nothing (a gap's defaults
pushed before the next argument — constants, so written order holds); an out-of-order call spills each
int-class argument to a hidden temporary (`_HTEMP`: a frame slot in a fn, a dead hidden global at top level)
and pushes every parameter in order. A named argument runs its parameter's gates (`_call_arg_one(S, fi, pi,
..)`: the cstring literal error, the bool check, the Str wrap, the struct address push, the f32 rounding); a
value-form vector is recorded at its parameter-order register (`_simd_ord_at`; by pointer on Win64). Methods:
`o.m(k: 1)` (self cannot be named), `T_m(self: &p, k: 1)`. Generics infer T through a name (`_call_arg_for`):
`gid(n: 2, x: p)` instantiates as `gid(p, 2)` does. A label inside a closure body is not a capture read.

DIRECT CALLS ONLY: fills and names reach `f(..)`, `o.m(..)`, `T_m(..)`, struct-returning calls (retptr and
register pair), the Win64 vector-retptr receives, generic instances (their base's row), an async fn's
constructor and await, defer bodies, closure bodies, forward calls — all through the one marshaller
(`_owncall_args_at`). fncallN / callptr pass exactly what they are given: a name there is refused ("named
arguments need a direct call - fncallN / callptr pass arguments by position"), and `fncall1(&f, 1)` /
`callptr(&f, 1)` short of a defaulted f's parameters is refused ("a call through a fn pointer passes every
argument: 'f' takes 3 (defaults fill only direct calls)"). Names are refused to builtins (two errors plus a
note), variadic fns, redefined fns, variant constructors, and a fn with a _str / _int / _ptr overload sibling (a
named call is never routed). A defaulted overload sibling is a routing target when the count is within its
min..max, and the call fills.

TAIL CALLS: a `return f(..)` that fills or names is an ordinary call, not a jmp (`_tc_args_divert` sends it to
PARSE_FNCALL, the one site that fills); a full-arity positional `return f(..)` keeps its jmp (objdump rows
T1 / T2; a 1,000,000-deep self tail recursion on every backend). `_CHECK_ARITY` mode 3 makes a defaulted callee
reaching the tail arm short of max a loud internal error, never a jmp with a parameter unset.

INLINE: a fn with defaults is never inlined; `#inline` on one warns "#inline ignored: fn has parameter
defaults". A named call to an inline-eligible fn takes the normal path (`_fnc_no_inline`: the replay binds by
position).

CONST CONTEXTS: `const N = cf(1);`, `const M = cf(1, c: 9);`, #assert, array sizes, enum values, case labels,
local consts and const fn bodies fill and bind names (`_ce_fill`, `_ce_call_named`). A default defined in terms
of itself is refused: "the default of parameter 'b' of 'cf' is defined in terms of itself".

METHODS / TRAITS: inherent `impl T` methods may declare defaults. A trait's methods may not — a required
signature or a default body (user, 2026-10-09: "Error, as built"): "trait 'Sh' method 'sh': a trait's methods
take no parameter defaults". Fork F1: a default in a REQUIRED signature compiled and was ignored before 6.7.7.
A method of `impl Trait for T` may not either ("… (the trait's signature is the contract)"). An operator fn with
a default is refused at the dispatch ("operator fn 'V2_add' declares a parameter default: an operator passes
exactly its two operands").

REFUSALS: definitions — a non-trailing default ("parameter 'b' of 'f' needs a default: it follows one that has
a default (defaults are trailing)"); a parameter / type parameter of its own fn ("a default must be a
compile-time constant: 'a' is a parameter of 'g'"); a non-constant (the evaluator's words); a block or
if-expression; a float on an untyped parameter; an integer on f64 / f32 ("… is its bit pattern - write 1.0");
the wrong kind for bool / an integer type / cstring / *T; out of a narrow type's range ("the default 300 does
not fit parameter 'x' of 'n' (': u8')"); a type that takes none; self; a variadic fn; a trait's methods; an
impl-Trait method; a fn defined twice; a default defined in terms of itself; a closure parameter ("a closure's
parameters take no defaults"); a default that is not one expression. Calls — outside min..max; an unknown name
("'f' has no parameter named 'd' - its parameters are: a, b, c"); named twice; by position and by name; a
positional after a named one; a required one missing ("missing argument for parameter 'b' of 'g' (it has no
default)"); through fncallN / callptr (a name, or short of a defaulted f); self: in the dot form; no declared
list; an overload-routed base; variadic; redefined; a reordered call in #naked. Each once, at its token; the
grammar ones under --syntax-only too.

TOOLS: `cyrius header`, api-surface, cyrdoc, cyrius-lsp and type-audit read a defaulted parameter list (`( [ {`
balanced, string / char literals skipped; since 6.7.11 a parameter's TYPE balances `<` `>` — `>>` / `>>>` closing
two / three — up to its depth-0 `=`, while in the default `<` stays the operator, so `p: Pair<i64, i64>` is one
parameter and `c = 1 < 2` is one too); api-surface records `name/MIN-MAX` for a defaulted fn.
`cyrius lint` takes a file that calls a sibling module's fn by name.
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/default_named_args_checked.sh (122 rows: refusals, objdump, two-file, generics,
lint, async, the tools, the S-R5 helper census; + mutation ledger M1-M23, MT1-MT9, MU1-MU4 — M21 has no killing
row and M20 is RED only with M18, both explained in the gate header);
tests/tcyr/crossos/default_named_args_values.tcyr (105 assertions, cx 103) on x86 plain / IR=3 / DCE=1,
qemu-aarch64, cx, wine, and on ecb / ach / cass / pi at the release gate. CHANGELOG [6.7.7].

6.7.11 (RET-10): D19 (a default on an operator fn) is reported ONCE, at the fn's first default with its
column, not per use; a direct call still fills it. A parameter whose type is refused keeps its default, so
short calls are not refused again. CHANGELOG [6.7.11].
6.7.13 (MV-5): a refused parameter DEFAULT (a value its type does not take, a default on a struct parameter, a trait
method implementation's default) keeps the fn's minimum argument count, so it is ONE error at the default — through
6.7.12 it set the minimum to the maximum and every short call was refused again ("'f' expects 1 argument, got 0"). A
fn defined twice with defaults still checks every argument. CHANGELOG [6.7.13].

[[entries]]
name = "dyn_v678"
description = "cyrius 6.7.8 checked trait objects (user decisions 2026-10-07 / 2026-10-10; ADR-007 §7): `o: dyn Tr` is an ordinary 16-byte {data, vtable} struct, FORMED automatically at a destination typed `dyn Tr` from a pointer the compiler knows, a declared scalar or a literal; the vtable is built and checked from `impl Tr for T` (full signatures, once per formed pair); `o.m(args)` is an indirect call through it; the two words are opaque; `dyn` is a reserved word and not a type argument. Static dispatch stays the default."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.8", "dyn", "trait objects", "vtable", "dynamic dispatch", "ADR-007", "reserved word", "opaque"]
---
SYNTAX:
    trait Area { fn area(self): i64; fn scaled(self, k): i64 { return self.area() * k; } }
    struct Sq { s; }
    impl Area for Sq { fn area(self): i64 { return self.s * self.s; } }
    impl Area for i64 { fn area(self): i64 { return self; } }
    fn total(o: dyn Area, k): i64 { return o.area() + o.scaled(k); }   # o.m(..): an indirect call
    fn main(): i64 {
        var q = Sq { 3 };
        var a: dyn Area = &q;             # FORMED from a pointer: the data word is &q
        var n: i64 = 5;
        var b: dyn Area = n;              # from a DECLARED scalar: the data word is the value 5
        return total(a, 2) + b.area();    # (9 + 18) + 5 = 32
    }
The decisions: the model (user, 2026-10-07): a dyn is an ordinary 16-byte struct, so ADR-002's one-word model
holds; the compiler builds and VERIFIES the vtable; the call is visible in the declared type; static dispatch
(ADR-004) stays the default. The eight questions (user, 2026-10-10): (1) forming is automatic from a known
pointer, a declared scalar or a literal — an untyped name is refused, no implicit `&p`; (2) a `self: T` of 8 B or
less is refused at forming, naming the method; (3) a generic member is allowed, CALLING it through a dyn is
refused; (4) full signatures are checked per formed (trait, type) pair, never-formed impls keep the count-only
check; (5) not a type argument; (6) a default's slot is T's own instance; (7) the words are opaque, `dyn A + B` is
refused; (8) lib/trait.cyr stays as it is.

TYPE: `dyn Tr` after a `:` — a local, a global in either zone, a parameter, a return, a struct field, a trait
member's signature. A struct of two 8-byte words, data and vtable, copied whole and checked by type like any
struct. `dyn` is reserved (token 173): `var dyn`, `fn dyn`, a field / method / `[embed]` name `dyn` is "expected
identifier, got reserved keyword 'dyn'".

FORMING (decision 1): at a destination typed `dyn Tr` — `var` (a local; a global after the first statement or in
the declaration zone), an assignment, a classic-for step, a field store (local, global, `*H`), an argument, a
`return` — the source is classified from its tokens before anything is emitted:
  - another dyn — a COPY;
  - a `*T` the compiler knows — the data word is the POINTER: `&p` of an inline local / by-value parameter /
    inline global, a `*T` local / parameter / global / field, a call or method returning `*T`, an untyped impl
    `self`, a pointer-mode handle;
  - a DECLARED scalar (an integer type, f64 / f32, bool, an enum, cstring; local, global or parameter) or a
    literal (`5`, `'A'`, `2.5`, `true`) — the data word is the VALUE, for a scalar impl;
  - since 6.7.13 (D15, RC-8) a call through `fncallN` / `callptr` whose callee is written `&f`, wrapped or not — read
    as the direct call: a dyn result is a COPY, a `*T` result forms; a callee held in a variable stays refused.
Parentheses around the whole source are transparent. A dyn ELEMENT of a struct literal takes a wrapped dyn-returning
call as it takes the unwrapped one since 6.7.13 (D17, RC-10: `H { 7, (pass(o)), 8 }`); `var x = (pass(o));` stays
refused (V4d). Refused by name, once, at the source: an untyped name
("cannot form dyn Show from 'x' - an untyped name may hold a pointer (ADR-002): …"), a bare inline struct ("… - no
implicit `&`: write `&p` (the dyn holds its address)"), `&` of a pointer / handle / scalar / dyn, a captured
name, any other expression, no impl ("no `impl Show for R` - a dyn Show is formed only from a type that
implements Show"), a FORM at top level into an assignment / field / argument ("a dyn formed at top level has no
frame to be built in - …"). A dyn holds `&p`, not a copy: one formed from `&local` and returned dangles.

THE CHECK (decisions 2, 4): at a pair's FIRST forming, every member's signature against the trait's — each
parameter after `self` and the return by resolved type (untyped = i64), generic-ness, `...` — and the `self` a dyn
can feed (untyped, `self: *T`, or `self: T` over 8 B for a struct; untyped or `self: T` for a scalar). A `self: T`
of 8 B or less is refused naming the method (it arrives in a register, not through the data word); a `#naked`
slot method is refused; a slot returning a `: stack` pair under a member that declares one value is refused. One
report per pair; each forming site also applies `&f`'s visibility / `#deprecated` check to every slot. Since 6.7.15
(the user's D4; traits_checked_v670) the impl's RETURN is checked against its member's at the `impl`, formed or not, and
forming a dyn from such an impl reports nothing more. Through 6.7.15 the two checks compared differently (the pair
check compared TOKEN BY TOKEN, so an impl returning `: Box` for a `: Box<i64>` member was refused here "the return type
differs"); since 6.7.16 the pair check reads each parameter and the return as an annotation reads it (GS-2 — below).

THE VTABLE (decision 6): a hidden global, one word per trait member in declaration order — T's method, T's default
instance, `T_Tr_m` when an inherent method holds the plain name, 0 for a member a dyn cannot call (generic, no
untyped `self`, variadic). FILLED AT EVERY FORMING SITE (no target relocates a data word: PIE, Mach-O arm64, PE; a
`kernel;` build never waits on the late replay). A slot method is address-taken as `&f` makes a fn, so
CYRIUS_DCE=1 keeps it even when its only forming site is dead code.

THE CALL (decisions 3, 7): `o.m(args)` loads slot k of the table, spills it to a frame slot BEFORE `self` and the
arguments, passes the data word as `self`, and calls through it (`call [rbp+disp32]` / `blr`, PE included), on
every receiver a method call takes (local, parameter, global, capture, `h.o.m()`, `hp.o.m()`, `self.o.m()`, a
call's result, a chain) and at top level. The trait member's signature governs arguments and result exactly as a
direct call's — by-value structs, a dyn formed in place, `: Str` literals, f64 / f32 / bool, vectors; a struct
result of any size (aarch64 X8 over 16 B), a dyn, a `*T` (which forms a dyn), an f64 typed f64. The result's value
class is the member's declaration: a `: Result` / `: Option` member returns its pair (`o.div(d)?`, a destructure
binds both, one name is refused "bind both"), any other one value. Refused: an undeclared member ("trait Show
declares no method 'nope' - …"), a generic member ("'pick' is generic - a call through a dyn Show has no instance
of it to run: call it on the concrete type"), no untyped `self` first, variadic, a multi-value / vector return,
named arguments ("named arguments need a direct call - a call through a dyn Show passes arguments by position"),
the wrong count ("'dyn Show.add' expects 1 argument, got 2" — since 6.7.14 a dyn call's arity counts the
arguments in its parentheses, without `self`; it was "expects 2 arguments, got 3", FE-23).

OPACITY (decision 7): no value use ("dyn Show 'o' used as a value - call one of its methods, or copy it whole into
a dyn"), no `&o`, no `.data` / `.vtable`, no destructure, no `return (a, b)` / `ret2` in a dyn fn, no struct
literal writing a dyn's words. A dyn RESULT goes only into a dyn: not into an untyped / integer / `*T` variable or
global, not under a scalar annotation, not a top-level `var G = mk();` after the first statement, not a tail
`return mk();` from a fn of another type. `load64(&w)` / `rethi()` still read raw words, as of any memory.

WHERE A DYN MAY NOT GO (decision 5), each refused by name: a type argument (`Vec<dyn S>`, `Box<dyn S>`,
`f<dyn S>`); a pointer (`*dyn S`); an array / slice / tuple / multi-value element; `dyn A + B`; `dyn S<..>`;
`sizeof(dyn S)`; `impl .. for dyn S`; a name that is no trait; a bare `dyn`; a generic, `async`, `const` or
`#naked` fn; a generic struct's field; a union member that is or holds one; `#derive`. RECORDS: a collection holds
pointers to records the program allocates — `struct Rec { o: dyn Area; }`, a `Vec` of `*Rec`.

INITIALISATION: struct locals are not zeroed, so `var o: dyn Tr;` and a struct local (or array) holding a dyn —
directly, nested, through a generic instance — without a value are refused ("declare dyn Show 'o' with its value -
struct locals are not zeroed, and a dyn's vtable is formed, never left to the frame"). Globals are zeroed.

lib/trait.cyr (decision 8): kept as it is — the unchecked library pattern; the two layouts do not interconvert.
lib/alloc.cyr's Allocator vtable cannot move to `dyn` while src/main.cyr includes it (cybs cannot parse `dyn`).
TOOLS: `cyrius header` prints a dyn parameter as one `cyr_val` and, since 6.7.11, a `dyn Tr` RETURN as `cyr_val2`
(data, vtable) under `#ifndef _WIN64`; cyrfmt, `cyrius lint`, cyrdoc, api-surface and
type-audit read `dyn Tr` as text (no change). Field notes: field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
checked_dyn_trait_objects_v678; field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml a_dyn_holds_an_address_or_a_value_v678.
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/dyn_checked.sh (234 rows + mutation ledger); tests/tcyr/crossos/dyn_objects.tcyr (81
assertions on x86 / IR=1 / IR=3 / DCE / qemu-aarch64 / cx / wine, and on ecb / ach / cass / pi at the release
gate). The guide's *Trait objects: `dyn` (6.7.8)* is the reference. CHANGELOG [6.7.8].

6.7.16 (CHANGELOG [6.7.16] GS-2, GS-5r, GS-7r, GS-7r2, FF-37, SS-19, SB-1; no new decision):
  - THE PAIR CHECK READS ANNOTATIONS (GS-2): each parameter and the return are compared as an annotation reads them, so
    `Box<i64>` / `Box`, `Box<Box<i64>>` / `Box<Box<i64> >` and `*Box<i64>` / `*Box` are one type and the dyn forms; the
    "(an untyped parameter is i64)" / "(no return type is i64)" hint prints only when a side is unannotated. A parameter
    or return type its own reader refuses (a member's `: u8`, `p: *Pt<i64>`, `b: *Box<i64, Pt>`, `n: Nope`, `p: [i64]`,
    `p: u128`) is that refusal alone at a formed dyn; the rest of the signature is still compared (GS-5r, GS-7r,
    GS-7r2).
  - Forming a dyn adds no "the parameter count differs" beside the impl's own parameter-count refusal (or 6.7.7's D13
    default line); a method the impl's check never reached keeps the site's line (FF-37).
  - SOURCES: `*(&x)` of a narrow integer / f64 / f32 / bool declared scalar is a dyn source of its type (`var o: dyn
    Num = *(&b);` takes bool's impl; SS-19); a `*T` method ending a chain forms a dyn (`var o: dyn Show =
    dw.d.me(1);` — it was "cannot form dyn Show from this expression"; SB-1).

[[entries]]
name = "bounds_checked_mode_v678"
description = "cyrius 6.7.8 C2 (user decisions 2026-10-10): the opt-in bounds-checked mode — `--bounds` > `CYRIUS_BOUNDS=1|0` > `[build] bounds` > a `#bounds` line in the entry file's leading block; it checks a typed array against its exact N, a slice against its length (reads and writes) and a `*T` for a negative index; a violation prints `bounds: index N out of [0, LEN) at <file>:<line>` and exits 134; a constant index out of range warns in every build and is an error under the mode; `@unsafe { }` turns checks off lexically. Off by default — not a byte when off."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.8", "C2", "bounds check", "#bounds", "CYRIUS_BOUNDS", "--bounds", "@unsafe", "subscript", "exit 134"]
---
SYNTAX:
    #bounds
    var a: u8[5];

    fn fill(v): i64 {
        for (var i = 0; i <= 5; i += 1) { a[i] = v; }   # <= : one element too many
        return 0;
    }

    fn main(): i64 {
        fill(7);
        return 0;
    }
stderr `bounds: index 5 out of [0, 5) at <source>:5`, exit 134. Without the mode the sixth store lands in the
array's spare slot bytes and the program exits 0. (The guide's example; frontend/bounds_mode_checked.sh TL4 runs
it for that exact line.)

THE SWITCH, highest first: `--bounds` (cyrius build / run / test / tests / fuzz; bench and the rest refuse it),
`CYRIUS_BOUNDS=1` / `=0` (unset or empty: no rung; any other value "CYRIUS_BOUNDS must be 0 or 1 (or unset): <v>"),
`[build] bounds = true` / `false`, a `#bounds` line. `CYRIUS_BOUNDS=0` and `bounds = false` turn a `#bounds`
program's mode off (cbt hands cycc `CYRIUS_BOUNDS=0` for a manifest false). Decided once per compile in the shared
PREPROCESS, before the first `#ifdef` (all seven forks); the predefine `CYRIUS_BOUNDS` is visible in every file —
`#ifdef CYRIUS_BOUNDS` — and a plain `#define CYRIUS_BOUNDS` switches nothing. `cyrius build --print-config` shows
`build.bounds`.

THE `#bounds` LINE belongs to the program, not a library: honoured only in the entry file's LEADING BLOCK (blank
lines, `#` comment lines, `include "…"` lines; a declaration, statement, `#define`, `#if*`, attribute or `#derive`
ends it). After code, inside an `#ifdef`, in an included file or a `[build] modules` file it is refused ("#bounds
must sit in the entry file's leading block (before any declaration, statement, #define or #if) - it switches the
whole program; or use --bounds / CYRIUS_BOUNDS=1 / [build] bounds"); `#bounds on` is "#bounds takes no argument";
inside a string it is data; `#boundsx` / `# bounds` are comments.

WHAT IS CHECKED, once the index is evaluated and before it is scaled — every subscript the language has:
  - `a[i]` on a typed array against its EXACT declared N (a `u8[5]` is 5, not its 8-byte slot) — local, global,
    static-storage local, capture, generic `T[N]`, f64 / f32 / bool elements; a redeclared global that folds keeps
    the FIRST declaration's N;
  - `s[i]` on a slice against its length — read, signed read, `=`, `OP=`;
  - `p[i]` on a `*T` (name, field, call) for a NEGATIVE index only: no extent exists for a `*T`; `slice<T>` is the
    checked spelling of a bounded pointer.
In a fn, at top level, in a closure, a `defer` body, an `#inline` replay, an `async fn`, a for step. NOT checked:
`load*` / `store*`, `*p`, `*(p - 1)`; vec and lib/bounds.cyr keep their exit 1.

THE VIOLATION: one stderr line, `bounds: index N out of [0, LEN) at <file>:<line>` (`[0, ...)` for a `*T`), the
file as every diagnostic names it (`<source>` the entry, an include by its path), the line the subscript is WRITTEN
on (an `#inline` body's own); exit 134 — the always-on slice check's code. Under the mode the line replaces a
slice's "slice bounds violation".

THE CONSTANT RULE (every build): an index of integer literals, `+ - *`, parentheses and names resolving to an
integer const / enum constant is judged at compile time — in range, no check is emitted; out of range, "warning:
index 5 is out of range [0, 5) for 'a' (an error under the bounds-checked mode)" / "index -1 is negative for 'p'
(…)", an ERROR under the mode outside `@unsafe`; once per token; silent under --syntax-only. A slice's constant
index >= 0 stays a run-time check.

`@unsafe { }` turns checks off LEXICALLY: a subscript whose `[` lies inside one is unchecked; a checked `#inline`
fn replayed inside one keeps its check. `#naked`: a checked subscript is refused under the mode ("the bounds check
is a call, which a #naked fn cannot make - wrap the access in @unsafe { }").

TARGETS: refused under the mode — a kernel / EFI image ("… (a violation has no stderr and no exit)") and a
`shared;` / `object;` build ("… (the checker would be defined in every object)"). Every hosted target lowers it.

HOW, AND THE COST: the preprocessor appends plain cyrius checkers (`__cyrius_bounds_chk` / `_chks` / `_fail` and
three helpers; the `__cyrius_` names are the compiler's) to the finished unit when the mode is on, and each site
is ONE CALL (the slice read's call pattern) — no backend changed. An in-range constant index costs nothing; a
program built without the mode carries not a byte of it. Measure a hot loop with the mode before shipping it on.
GATES: tests/gates/frontend/bounds_mode_checked.sh (153 rows + mutation ledger); fork_flag_parity.sh,
build_config_precedence.sh, build_print_config.sh, build_config_windows_arm.sh, manifest_key_inventory.sh;
tests/tcyr/crossos/bounds_mode_in_range.tcyr (39) and bounds_mode_violation.tcyr (20 spawned children, each its
exact line). The guide's *The bounds-checked mode (6.7.8)* is the reference. CHANGELOG [6.7.8].

[[entries]]
name = "pointer_subscripts_v678"
description = "cyrius 6.7.8 C2 (user decisions 2026-10-10): `p[i]` on a NAME, FIELD or CALL declared `*T` and `a[i]` on f64 / f32 / bool arrays — read, `=`, every `OP=`, the for step — each element typed as a declared T; and `*p` / `*p = v` / `*p OP= v` through a narrow `*T` act on the ELEMENT at its width (an approved change: before 6.7.8 `*p` was always the 8-byte word)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.8", "C2", "subscript", "typed pointer", "*p", "element width", "f64 array", "f32 array", "bool array", "behaviour-change"]
---
SYNTAX:
    struct Buf { len; data: *u8; }
    fn sum(p: *u8, n): i64 { var s = 0; for (var i = 0; i < n; i += 1) { s = s + p[i]; } return s; }
    fn first(b: *Buf): i64 { return b.data[0]; }                      # a field declared *T
    fn slen(s: *u8): i64 { var n = 0; while (*(s + n) != 0) { n = n + 1; } return n; }   # one byte
    fn main(): i64 {
        var raw: u8[8];
        var p: *u8 = &raw;
        p[0] = 200; p[1] = 50; p[2] = 0;
        var b = Buf { 3, p };
        *p += 60;                       # ONE byte: 200 + 60 wraps to 4 (6.7.7: a word add, 260)
        var f: f64[2];
        f[1] = 1.5;
        var g: f64 = f[1] * 2.0;        # an f64 element is an f64: a float multiply
        if (g == 3.0) { return sum(p, 2) + first(&b) + slen(p); }   # 54 + 4 + 2 = 60
        return 0;
    }

THE BASE: a NAME declared `*T` (a local, a `p: *T` parameter, a global, a capture, a generic instance's `p: *T`), a
FIELD declared `*T` (`h.p[i]`, `a.b.p[i]`, `self.p[i]`, `mk(4).p[i]`, through a `*H`), or a CALL declared `: *T`
(`f()[i]`, `g<i32>()[i]`, `o.m()[i]`). `p[i]` is the element at `p + i * sizeof(T)`, the pointer's VALUE the base.
The base is evaluated first (a call made once — `f()[i] += 1` calls f once), then the index; the element comes from
the base's DECLARATION, never from a pointer scale an argument left behind. `return f()[i];` is no tail call.

THE ELEMENT: an iN sign-extended, a uN zero-extended, an f64 an f64, an f32 its 4 bytes in single precision, a
bool a boolean value (`if (p[i])`). A store is checked as a store into a field of T: an f64 into an f32 element
rounds, a bool element takes only a boolean and refuses `OP=`, an integer constant into a float element warns, a
wider integer into a narrow element warns "narrowing".

REFUSED BY NAME, at the `[`, once: "cannot subscript 'x': it is not a typed array, a *T or a slice local - declare
`var a: T[N]` or `p: *T` (T an integer, f64, f32 or bool), or load / store at an explicit byte offset" (an untyped
name, a scalar, a pointer laundered through one: `var q = p; q[0]`); "… a bare `var a[N]` states no element width";
"… a subscript takes an integer, f64, f32 or bool element - step with p + i to an element of struct 'Pt'" (struct,
pointer, enum, string, vector elements); "cannot subscript field 'n': it is not a *T field"; "cannot subscript the
result of 'g': it does not return a *T" (a builtin, callptr, fncallN, a generic returning T); "a subscript's result
is an element, not an array - a second [..] is not supported"; "&a[i] is not supported - write &a + i * sizeof(T)
(or p + i)"; "a subscript's base is a name, a *T field or a call returning *T - name the pointer first (var q: *T
= ..; q[i])" (`(p + 1)[1]`, a literal). u128 elements as before — and since 6.7.15 (the user's D20, ST-11) `*pu`
through a typed `*u128` is refused as `pu[0]` is, "cannot dereference 'pu': a u128 element does not fit one register -
use load64/store64 on its two halves" (it read and wrote the low word, silently;
f32_writes_round_and_u128_arithmetic_v676).

`*p` AT THE ELEMENT'S WIDTH (approved; closes issues/archived/2026-10-08-deref-store-through-narrow-typed-pointer-is-word-store.md):
through `*u8`..`*u32`, `*i8`..`*i32`, `*f32`, `*p` is `p[0]`: read at T's width (sign- / zero-extended, an f32 typed
f32), `*p = v` / `*p OP= v` store T's width with `p[0] = v`'s checks (f32 rounding, the narrowing / int-into-float
warnings), statement, expression and for step alike. The operand must be KNOWN to be that pointer: a name, a `*T`
field, a `: *T` call, `p ± n`, `n + p` (a one-byte one too), parentheses, an if-expression of such — and since
6.7.13 `*pp` of a `pp: **T` (D9 (a), ST-19), so `**pp` through a narrow T is the element at its width and `*pp + n`
steps elements (it was on the word list below through 6.7.12). Anything else is the 8-byte word: an untyped address
(`var w = p; *w` is the explicit word access), an 8-byte pointee (`*i64`, `*u64`, `*f64`, `*bool` — `p[0]` is their
typed spelling), a bitwise / comparison / negation result, a call result's untyped field. Before 6.7.8: `*q = 5` through a `*i32` zeroed the next element; `*p = 1.5` through a
`*f32` stored the f64 bits as a word; `*s` on a `*u8` read eight characters.

NO BOUNDS CHECK outside the bounds-checked mode (bounds_checked_mode_v678); under it `p[i]` is checked for a
negative index, `a[i]` against its exact N. `load*` / `store*` stay valid and unchecked.
GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/pointer_subscript_values.tcyr (219), pointer_subscript_bases.tcyr (88),
deref_element_width.tcyr (145) — x86 / IR=1 / IR=3 / DCE, qemu-aarch64, cx, wine; tests/gates/frontend/
pointer_subscript_checked.sh (+ mutation ledger). Field note: field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml
typed_pointer_local_slot_v6616 (the typed-pointer history). CHANGELOG [6.7.8].

6.7.11: the plain-integer values gain `await f`, `+| +? -| -?` on a pointer and a narrow scalar local, and
`n + &x` / `n + pb` (a one-byte struct pointer) are byte pointers — typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617.

6.7.15 — A KNOWN `&f` CALLEE IS A BASE (the user's D31 (a); CHANGELOG [6.7.15] RC-10): a call whose callee is written
`&f` with `f` declared `: *T` — `fncall0(&bp)[1]`, `callptr(&bp)[1]`, wrapped — subscripts and stores the element, and
`*fncall0(&bp)` / `*callptr(&bp)` read and write it at its width, as `bp()[1]` / `*bp()` do (a `*i8` callee
sign-extends). Through 6.7.14 `fncall0(&bp)[1]` was refused "it does not return a *T" and the dereference read and
wrote the whole word. A `*S` callee's subscript refusal names the struct ("… its element is struct 'Pt'"). Still
refused "it does not return a *T": `fncallN` / `callptr` whose callee is NOT written `&f` (`fncall0(fp)[1]` — a callee
held in a variable is a word, 6.7.11's D31). And `**(&pb)` through a narrow pointer reads and writes the element (D21,
VC-13 — typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617).

[[entries]]
name = "struct_handle_line_and_struct_values_at_every_size_v6710"
description = "cyrius 6.7.10 (the user's decisions 1, 5, 6, 7, 11, 12): ONE storage rule at every size, for struct locals AND globals — declared from a struct VALUE of its type the variable is inline, from anything else (an address, `alloc`, `&x`, a `*T` or untyped call, an integer) it is a HANDLE, and from another handle it binds; a dereference of a `*T` in any spelling is a struct value; copies through a `*T` are size-independent; a pointer into a by-value struct parameter passes the struct; a struct-typed field or a `*T` dereference as an operator's LEFT operand dispatches `S_op`, and a whole dereference is a struct as an argument and a right operand; an untyped `var c = a + b;` of a struct-returning operator takes its struct type. Through 6.7.9 each held only over 8 bytes, or not at all."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.10", "structs", "handle", "inline", "copy", "dereference", "operator overloading", "by-value parameter", "B7", "decision 6"]
---
THE HANDLE LINE (decisions 1 and 5 — locals and globals, every size):

    struct S8 { v; }
    fn demo(): i64 {
        var h = alloc(8);
        store64(h, 7);
        var q: S8 = h;           # a handle: q.v reads h's word
        q.v = q.v + 30;          # writes the heap object
        var c: S8 = q;           # binds: c is a second handle to it
        var l: S8 = S8 { 1 };    # inline (a literal)
        l = q;                   # copies the struct q points at
        return load64(h) + c.v + l.v;   # 37 + 37 + 37 = 111
    }

  A STRUCT VALUE (the variable is inline; a copy): a literal; another inline struct of the type — a
  local, a by-value parameter, a global, a closure's captured copy, parenthesised or not; a struct-typed
  field (`b.v`); a `*T` name, or a dereference of a `*T` in ANY spelling — `*p`, `(*p)`, `*(p + 1)`,
  `*h.p`, `*gp()`, `*w.mp()` (copied through the pointer); a call, method, operator or if-expression
  returning the struct (generic, wrapped, parenthesised). For a global the same list, and a fn declared
  BELOW the global counts.
  ANYTHING ELSE (a handle): `alloc(..)`, `&x`, an untyped value (`*x` of an untyped x included), a call
  returning a `*T` or an untyped value, an integer — `var q: S8 = 0;` is a NULL handle, and
  `var a: Num = 1;` a handle to ADDRESS 1, so `a + b` is pointer arithmetic with no `Num_add` (declare it
  `Num { 1 }` or `mkn(1)` for the operator). From another handle (a captured one too) it BINDS: a second
  name for the same object. A struct value of ANOTHER type, a `*U` dereference included, is refused by
  name (`cannot copy 'pu' into a variable of a different struct/vector type: 'q'`).
  A HANDLE USED AS A VALUE is the struct it points at — a by-value argument, a `: S8` return, an
  operator's right operand, an if-expression branch, either side of a copy, in a fn and at top level; a
  closure captures the handle and reads through it. As an operator's LEFT operand it is pointer
  arithmetic (no dispatch), and `&q` is its slot.
  WAS (through 6.7.9) — the line held only over 8 B: a struct local or global of 8 B or less was inline
  whatever its initialiser, so `var q: S8 = h;` (or `= alloc(8)`, `= &s`, `= gp()`, `= 1`) held the
  ADDRESS as `q.v` and `q.v = 5` never reached the object, silently (`demo` read the address back). Over
  8 B `var q: P3 = *p;` was a handle holding the word `*p` read — its first field write crashed.
  A global declared from a `*T` GLOBAL's name (`var G: S8 = P;`) kept its 6.7.9 layout through 6.7.15 (inline holding
  the pointer word at 8 B or less; a handle bound to P's object over 8) — settled in 6.7.16: a copy at every size (D18
  (a), ZN-12 — below).

COPIES ARE SIZE-INDEPENDENT (decision 6): `l = p`, `G = p` and `q = r`-style copies through a `*T`, and
since 6.7.10 a `*T` global, a `: *T` call / method, a `*T` field and pointer arithmetic on a `*T` as
sources — the full table and the rebinds are in struct_assignment_handle_and_value_v6616 (its 6.7.10 known
defects are fixed in 6.7.11: struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711).

A POINTER INTO A BY-VALUE STRUCT PARAMETER (decision 7) passes the struct it points at, at every size:
`bs1(&s)`, `bs1(sp)`, `bs1(gp())`, `bs1(h.p)`, `bs1(sp + i)`, `o + sp` give `fn bs1(b: S1)` a copy; an
untyped value is still the struct's word — struct_argument_type_checked_v673. Since 6.7.14 (ST-6) `n + &x`
with a narrow `n` (`i8` / `i16` / `i32` / `u8`) is the pointer `&x + n` is (6.7.11's D15): `bs(n + &x)` into a
by-value struct of 8 B or less passes the struct at `&x + n` (it passed the address's bits as the struct: 78
where 77 is right, 14 on cx); `n + 5` stays the value.

A STRUCT-TYPED FIELD OR A `*T` DEREFERENCE AS AN OPERATOR'S LEFT OPERAND (decision 11) dispatches `S_add`
(`S_sub`, `S_mul`, `S_div`) as a struct variable there does, at every size:

    w.s + a     p.s + a (p: *W)     mk(1).s + a     self.s * a     o.w.s - a     (w.s) + a
    *p + a      (*p) + a            *gp() + a       *w.ps / a

  Into a `*S` parameter or over 8 B the field's own address (or the pointer) is passed — a write through
  it reaches the field — else its value. The result is a struct value: declared, assigned, passed,
  returned (`return w.s + a;`) and chained (`w.s + a + a`). A missing `S_op` is refused by name, as for a
  variable (`w.s + 1` with no `S_add`), as is an operator fn whose first parameter is another struct and,
  at top level, a 9-16 B result (`var G: H = GW.s + A;`). Through 6.7.9 the first WORDS were combined:
  `h.p - s` gave 250 where `P2_sub` gives 139, silently, at every size. Unchanged: a `*T` field is a
  pointer (`w.p + 1` steps it), a `Str` field a handle, and `-w.s + a` negates a word first.

A WHOLE DEREFERENCE IS A STRUCT VALUE AS AN ARGUMENT AND A RIGHT OPERAND: `take(*p)`, `take((*p))`,
`take(*gp())`, `take(*w.ps)`, `take(*(p + 1))`, `a - *p`, `a + (*p)` — to a by-value parameter over 8 B
(or an operator's) the pointer, which the callee copies; to a `*S` parameter of any size the pointee
itself, so a write reaches it. `take(*p + a)` passes the operator's result (at top level a 9-16 B one is
refused by name). A `*U` of another struct is refused (`cannot pass 'pu' to a parameter of a different
struct type`); an untyped `*x` is still the word it reads. Through 6.7.9: the word the dereference read
was passed as the struct's address — a crash on every target.
  A whole field of 3 / 5 / 6 / 7 B was still loaded as an 8-byte word on the way through 6.7.12 (an
  object ending exactly at the end of a mapping faulted); since 6.7.13 it is read byte-exact — see below.
  (A dereference of a 1-7 B `*S` reads exactly the struct since 6.7.11, ST-1.)

AN UNTYPED BINDING OF A STRUCT-RETURNING OPERATOR TAKES ITS STRUCT TYPE (decision 12): `var c = a + b;`
with `fn S_add(..): T` declares `c` an inline `T` at every size (as over 8 B, where the result's temporary
becomes `c`) — `c.f` reads and writes it, `c + c` dispatches again, `take(c)` passes it — whatever the
left operand (a variable, a field, a dereference, a call, wrapped, chained). The OPERATOR's return type
wins over a leading call's (`var c = mk(4) * b;` is `S_mul`'s type). An operator returning an integer
leaves the binding untyped; an untyped GLOBAL keeps the result's word at every size. Only `+ - * /` (and
`+% -% *%`) on `c` dispatch — `c & 0xFF`, `c | m`, `c ^ 1`, `c << 1`, `c % n` act on its word. Through
6.7.9 a result of 8 B or less was an untyped word (`c.f`: *no struct type in scope for 'c'*), and
6.7.10's packed fields shrank `struct C4 { r: u8; g: u8; b: u8; a: u8; }` from 32 B to 4, so
`var c = A + B; c.g = 1;` needed this to compile again. 6.7.11 (D42) extends the rule to EVERY whole struct
result — a method chain, an if-expression of struct branches, a 9-16 B result in parentheses (`var c = (a + b);`
was an untyped word through 6.7.10) — and a leading call types the binding only when it is the WHOLE initialiser
(`var r = mk3(4) - s;` with an integer `P3_sub` is untyped now; it was typed `P3`):
struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711.

6.7.13 — EXACT BYTES, ZERO-SIZE STRUCTS, `*(&p)`, AND NO STRUCT INTO A WORD POINTER (CHANGELOG [6.7.13] ST-1, ST-2,
ST-3, ST-5, ST-12; the user's D5 (a)):
  - A WHOLE STRUCT-TYPED FIELD of 3 / 5 / 6 / 7 B used as a value — an argument, `return w.s`, an operator operand,
    an untyped `var u = w.s`, a top-level `var G: S3 = GW.s` — is read byte-exact through its address, its padding
    0; a zero-size field reads no byte (ST-3).
  - EVERY WORD A STRUCT OF 8 B OR LESS TRAVELS IN HAS PADDING 0 — a by-value `self` from a `*S`, a `*S` into a
    by-value parameter, a handle or a captured handle passed by value, `return q;` of a `*S` parameter (ST-1). They
    carried frame garbage past `sizeof(S)` (255 after a frame-dirtying call).
  - A ZERO-SIZE STRUCT MOVES NO BYTE: into a struct-typed field from any source (a `*E` global, a `: *E` call, a
    by-value call, an untyped value); a zero-size `*p` value is the word 0, into a `*E` parameter the pointer, and
    `*p + l` dispatches (ST-2).
  - `*(&p)` / `*&p` JOINS THE HANDLE LINE: of an INLINE struct (a local, a by-value parameter, a value global, a
    captured copy) it is the struct value `p` is — `take(*(&p))`, `var q: S = *(&p);` and `l = *(&p)` copy the whole
    struct, `*(&p) + a` dispatches, `*(&u)` of another struct is refused by name. A handle's or a `*T`'s `&h` is its
    SLOT, so `*(&h)` is the slot's word. (`*(&s) OP= v` was still the first-word op in 6.7.13 — refused since
    6.7.14, below.)
  - A STRUCT VALUE INTO A WORD POINTER IS REFUSED (D5 (a), ST-12): `ri(s)` with `ri(p: *i64)` (or `*u8`, `*f64`,
    `**T`) is "cannot pass 's' to a parameter that is not a pointer to its struct type in a call to 'ri' - pass &s
    for its address". A handle, a `*T` and a `Str` already hold the address and pass. Into a `*V` the vectors lane's
    D33 refuses it in its own words (typed_simd_value_type_abi). (A field, call or dereference argument of struct
    type was not refused in 6.7.13 — since 6.7.14, below.)

6.7.14 (the user's D17 (a); CHANGELOG [6.7.14] ST-6, ST-8, ST-10):
  - `*(&s) OP= v` ON A STRUCT VALUE IS REFUSED AS `s OP= v` IS (D17 (a), ST-10): "compound assignment to struct 's' is
    refused - a struct value is not an integer or a float (write `a = a + b`)", once — a local, a by-value parameter, a
    global, a capture, at top level, in a for step, `*&s` and `*((&s))` too. Through 6.7.13 it was 6.7.5's word op on
    s's first word (s.x went 3 → 13). A handle's `*(&h)` and a scalar's `*(&n)` keep the word op (`&h` is the slot).
  - EVERY STRUCT VALUE INTO A WORD POINTER IS REFUSED, ONCE (ST-8; 6.7.13's D5 (a)): a struct-typed field `ri(w.s)`, a
    by-value call `ri(mk())`, a method or operator result, a `*S` / `**S` dereference — "… - a struct value is no
    address" (struct_argument_type_checked_v673). Still passed: an address, a `*T` field, a `: *T` call / method,
    a `Str` field.
  - `n + &x` with a narrow `n` is the pointer `&x + n` is, into a by-value struct of 8 B or less (ST-6; decision 7
    above).

6.7.15 (CHANGELOG [6.7.15] ST-2; no decision needed — 6.7.13's D9 (a) with the copy through a `*S`): `*pp` / `(*pp)` OF
A `**S` IN A FN IS ON THE STRUCT-VALUE SIDE OF THE LINE — `var t: S = *pp;` copies the struct `*pp` points at (it
bound a handle, so a write through t changed the original), and `l = *pp`, `G = *pp`, `*q = *pp`, `**qq = *pp` copy at
every size (typed_pointer_step_is_sizeof_t_v6617). A TOP-LEVEL declaration `var G: S = *GPP;` still bound a handle
through 6.7.15, as every top-level declaration from a `*S`-typed source did — settled in 6.7.16 (D17 (a) — below).

6.7.16 — A TOP-LEVEL DECLARATION FROM A `*S` SOURCE IS A COPY AT EVERY SIZE (the user's D17 (a), D18 (a), 2026-10-19;
D87 (a), 2026-10-20; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] ZN-11, ZN-12, SS-38, SS-39, SS-39r):
  - `var G: S = *GPP;` (GPP a `**S`) copies, in the declaration zone and after the first statement, as the same line in a
    fn has since 6.7.15 — `(*GPP)`, `*gpp()`, `*W.pp` too (D17 (a), ZN-11; G was a handle on the struct). `var G: S =
    P;` with P a `*S` global's name copies the struct P points at, `(P)` and a generic instance too (D18 (a), ZN-12; at
    8 B or less G held the POINTER WORD, over 8 B a handle). An untyped `var Q = P;` and `var R: *S = P;` stay the
    pointer — alias with `var G: *S = P;`.
  - THE SOURCE ABOVE AT EVERY SIZE (SS-38): a `*S` / `**S` source declared below G is refused by name — "cannot copy-init
    'N' from a global declared below it (declare the source first): 'GP'" / "... from a dereference of a global or fn
    declared below it (declare it first): 'GPP'" — at 8 B or less too (it faulted at startup, or read 0); a source the
    image holds from program start is read. ⚠ Beyond D17 / D18's text (on the filed keep-or-restore list): the
    declared-below refusals, and a `**Q`'s `*GQQ` into an `S` global, "cannot copy '*GQQ' into a variable of a different
    struct/vector type: 'Z'" (it read Q's bytes as an S).
  - A `*U` SOURCE OF ANOTHER STRUCT INTO AN `S` DECLARATION IS REFUSED AT EVERY SIZE (D87 (a), SS-39, SS-39r): a `*U`
    global's name, a `: *U` call or method, a `*U` field, pointer arithmetic, wrapped, a generic instance's — in a fn, the
    zone and after the first statement — "cannot copy 'GU' into a variable of a different struct/vector type: 't'"
    (named as spelled). It compiled: over 8 B a handle reading U's object as an S, at 8 B or less the address. A `*U`
    into a `U` keeps its handle bind; into a `*U` it aliases. (The call / method / field / arithmetic sources go beyond
    D87's text, which named the global's name — on the filed list.)

GATES: tests/gates/codegen/small_struct_handle_line.sh, tests/gates/codegen/struct_value_codegen.sh;
tests/tcyr/crossos/{small_struct_handle_line, small_struct_global_handle_line, small_struct_pointer_arg,
small_struct_pointer_copy, struct_pointer_source_copy, struct_field_left_operand,
struct_deref_whole_operand, struct_operator_result_binding}.tcyr. The guide: § Where a struct lives,
§ Parentheses, struct operands and `Str` fields, the struct-argument check. Field notes:
field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml struct_handles_and_values_since_6_7_10;
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml struct_values_at_every_size_and_packed_fields_v6710.
CHANGELOG [6.7.10] (B7-1, B7-1g, B7-1b, B7-1c, B7-1d, OPS-1 .. OPS-6).

[[entries]]
name = "struct_copies_returns_and_operator_results_v6711"
description = "cyrius 6.7.11 (the struct lane, the user's decisions D1 - D4, D38 - D44): `*p` of a struct under 8 B reads exactly the struct; every struct destination (a name, a struct-typed field, a declaration, a `*S` local / parameter / handle / GLOBAL, a store `*p = v`) takes every struct source the same way; a struct return is checked by its struct and a `*S` returns its pointee at every size; a tuple literal is not the struct a fn returns; integer operators give integers and unary minus negates a word; an untyped binding takes the struct of every whole struct result; a method's `*T` result chains; a parenthesised free struct call is a nested-literal element; the scanner decodes a nested derived struct. Each WAS a silent wrong value, a crash or a false refusal through 6.7.10. Since 6.7.14 a struct result over 8 B ASSIGNED to a scalar is refused as the declaration is, `return &q;` of a handle / `*T` is refused, and an untyped top-level `var x = mk1();` is the struct."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.11", "structs", "copy", "dereference", "*p = v", "struct return", "operator overloading", "untyped binding", "method chains", "derive", "Break 2"]
---
`*p` OF A STRUCT UNDER 8 B READS EXACTLY THE STRUCT (ST-1): every consumer — a global declared or assigned from
it, `l = *p`, `f(*p)`, `return *p`, `*p + a` — reads 1..7 bytes and the word's padding is 0.
  WAS: 8 bytes (SIGSEGV at a mapping's end, foreign bytes in the padding). A whole 3 / 5 / 6 / 7 B FIELD used as a
  value still read 8 bytes through 6.7.12; byte-exact since 6.7.13 (ST-3).

EVERY STRUCT DESTINATION TAKES EVERY STRUCT SOURCE THE SAME WAY (ST-2, ST-3, ST-4, ST-8, ST-9; D2, D4):

    destinations   a name (`l`, `G`), a struct-typed field (`o.s`), a declaration, a `*S` local / parameter /
                   handle (into the pointee), a typed `*S` GLOBAL (into `*GP`, D2), and a store `*p = v`
                   through a `*S` (D4)
    sources        a struct value; a whole dereference in any spelling (`*p`, `(*p)`, `*(p + n)`, `*gp()`,
                   `*w.p`); a `*S` global; a `: *S` call or method; a GENERIC call declared `: *T` with T
                   bound to the struct (explicit, or inferred when the arguments infer T = S); a `*S` field;
                   pointer arithmetic on a `*S`

  `*S` / handle sources into a `*S` / handle destination REBIND (`GP = q`, `GP = &x`); a `*U` of another struct is
  refused by name everywhere. `*p = v` copies exactly sizeof(S) bytes (0 for a zero-size struct); `*p OP= v` is
  refused (write the field); an untyped value through `*p` is stored at sizeof(S) bytes, at most 8 — `*p = 0` on a
  16-byte struct clears the first 8 only (D11 (a), kept and confirmed in 6.7.13, ST-18). Since 6.7.13 `*p = mk(..);`
  of a MULTI-VALUE call through a `*S` is refused "is not assigned to struct 'S' - bind it first", as `G = mk(..);`
  is (D26, MV-15; 6.7.11's D4: `*p = v` copies what `G = v` copies); a `*i64` takes the first value.
  WAS: `l = *p` and `q = *p` stored ONE word (into a `*S`, `q = *p` REBOUND q to the struct's first word — write
  `q = p.first` to load a pointer kept in the first field); `o.s = GP`, `o.s = gp()`, `o.s = w.p` stored the address;
  a generic `: *T` call was no source; `GP = l` stored l's first word into GP; `*p = v` wrote 8 bytes, overrunning a
  small struct.

A STRUCT RETURN IS CHECKED BY ITS STRUCT, AND A POINTER RETURNS ITS POINTEE, AT EVERY SIZE (ST-6 D3, ST-7 D1): at
8 B or less another struct's value, a `*U` and a handle of another struct are refused by name ("a fn returning the
struct `S8` takes a value of that struct, or a pointer to one — got `u` (a `U8`)"); `return E;` with E a `*S`
(local, parameter, global, `: *S` call / method, field, arithmetic, `*E`) returns a copy of the pointee in all three
return classes. WAS: an 8 B fn returned u's bytes, or the address.
  6.7.13 — `return &s;` IS `return s;` (D4 (a), ST-10, ST-10r): in a fn declared `: S`, at every size — a local,
  `&G`, a by-value parameter's `&p`, wrapped. `&u` of another struct is refused by name ("… got `&u` (a `*U`)");
  `return &w.s;` gets D3's message at every size, once. (`return &h;` of a handle, a `*S` local or an untyped `self`
  was still the SLOT's address in 6.7.13 — refused since 6.7.14, below.)
  6.7.14 — `return &q;` OF A HANDLE, A `*T` OR AN UNTYPED `self` IS REFUSED (the user's D19 (a), ST-11): in a fn
  returning a struct, at every size, once, at the `&` — q a handle or a `*T` (a local, a parameter — a typed `self: *S`
  too — or a global) or an untyped `self`: "a fn returning the struct `S8` takes a value of that struct, or a pointer to
  one — `&q` is the address of q's slot, not a struct — return q". `&q` is q's own slot (6.7.10's handle line), not a
  pointer to a struct. Through 6.7.13 a fn of 8 B or less returned the slot's address as the struct's word (the caller
  read a dead frame's address); over 8 B it was refused in other words. The same refusal holds for `&pu` of a `*U` and
  a handle of ANOTHER struct (kept by the user's 6.7.15 D58), whose advice since 6.7.15 (ST-13) ends ", and pu is a
  `*U8`" (a handle: ", and GUH is a `U8`") — "— return pu" pointed at `return pu;`, itself refused; the fn's own
  struct keeps "— return q". `return &s;` of an inline struct stays `return s;`.
  6.7.15 — `return &n;` WITH n HOLDING NO STRUCT IS REFUSED BY NAME AT EVERY SIZE (the user's D18; ST-14; a language
  change): `&n` of an integer, a `*i64` / `*f64v2`, a `**S`, an array, an inline vector, a `u128` / `f64` or a `Str`
  handle — a local, parameter or global — is "a fn returning the struct `S` takes a value of that struct, or a pointer
  to one — `&n` is the address of n's slot, not a struct" (a `Str` handle adds ", and t is a `Str`"), once, at the `&`,
  at 8 / 16 / 24 B. At 8 B or less it compiled and the caller read a dead frame's address as the struct's field (a
  different byte each run); over 8 B the classes' refusals named nothing. Beyond D18's text (kept by 6.7.16's D56): `&t` of an inline `Str` or
  a tuple is refused as `&u` of another struct ("… got `&t` (a `*Str`)"; at 8 B it compiled). A generic is judged per
  instance (`var n: T = x; return &n;` compiles at `<S8>`, refused at `<i32>`). Its i64 BASE went unjudged through
  6.7.15 (`hg(3)` returned the slot's address); since 6.7.16 a `&n` of a name typed by T makes the generic a stub —
  `hg(3)` is refused at the call, its note naming the `&n` (D1) — and a `&n` of a name typed with no type parameter is
  refused at the definition with D18's words, called or not, in a parsed or a stub's skipped base (D2) —
  generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310.
  6.7.14 — `return *pp;` / `return **pp;` with `pp: **S` in a fn returning S return the struct (ST-7): at 8 B `*pp`
  returned the pointer's bits; over 8 B both were refused.

A TUPLE LITERAL IS NOT THE STRUCT A FN RETURNS (ST-15, D39): `return (a, b[, c]);` in a fn declared `: S` (a named
struct) is refused in every class — "a fn returning the struct `P` returns that struct — `(a, b)` is a tuple,
another type". Build the struct (`var r: P = P { a, b }; return r;`). `ret2(a, b)` there is refused too since 6.7.13,
in every class (D22, MV-11): ret2_rethi.

IN A 9-16 B STRUCT FN A RETURNED CALL MUST END THE VALUE (ST-16, D40): `return mk2(1) == p;` is refused by name (it
returned a 16-byte struct from an 8-byte result); `return mk2(1) + p;` (a dispatch) and `return mk2(1);` are
unchanged.

INTEGER OPERATORS GIVE INTEGERS; UNARY MINUS NEGATES A STRUCT'S WORD (ST-12, ST-13, D38): `n - s` is first-word
arithmetic with an INTEGER result, so `n - s + 10` never dispatches `S_add`; `-s + t` is an integer add at every
size and `t + -s` passes the negated word; nothing is refused. `-` never dispatches. Every integer arm (`% << >> >>>
& | ^`, comparisons, `&& ||`, saturating / checked ops, `~`, unary `-`) clears the struct type it used to leak from
its right operand. WAS: `-s + t` called `S_add` with the minus dropped.
  6.7.13 — AN F64 RIGHT OPERAND TOO (D12 (a), RC-4): a comparison, `&& ||` and `& | ^ % << >> >>>` clear an f64 /
  f32 right operand's type as well — `(n < x) * 3` is 3 and `(n & x) * 3` multiplies integers with `x: f64`. Only
  `+ - * /` keep it (kind 2, 6.7.11's D41): they have f64 forms. (Through 6.7.12 the f64 type leaked: `(n < x) * 3`
  was an f64 multiply.)
  6.7.16 — AND THE SATURATING / CHECKED OPERATORS (the user's D104, 2026-10-20; FB-4): `+| +? -| -? *| *?` give an
  integer whatever their right operand, as `&` does — with `x: f64`, `(n +| x) * 3` is an integer multiply and
  `(n +| x) < 3` an integer comparison, with no warning (through 6.7.15 the result kept the f64 right operand's type:
  the f64 arm and "f64 arithmetic with a non-f64 right operand"). An operand's own mix still warns (`n +| x * 3`: the
  `x * 3`).

AN UNTYPED BINDING TAKES THE STRUCT OF EVERY WHOLE STRUCT RESULT (ST-18, D42; ST-10): an operator (6.7.10), a
method, a method chain, an if-expression of struct branches, any of them wrapped in parentheses — at every size —
so `c + 1` dispatches `S_add` (or is refused); write `c.v + 1`, or annotate `: i64` to keep the word. A LEADING call
types it only when the call is the WHOLE initialiser: `var r = mk3(4) - s;` with an integer `P3_sub` is untyped
(`r.x` is refused by name — *no struct type in scope for 'r'*). A chain ending in an integer method stays an
integer; an untyped global keeps the word — a whole free call excepted since 6.7.14 (D29, D30, below). A
leading `: f64` call types the binding f64 only while the WHOLE
initialiser is still an f64 (6.7.13, RC-5): `var x = f() * 2.0;` is f64; `var c = f() < 1.0;` is the integer 0 / 1
(it was typed f64, so `-c` flipped the sign bit).
6.7.12 — A PLAIN NAME TOO (D29 (a)): `var q = p;` / `var q = (p);` in a fn, with `p` an inline struct local, a
by-value struct parameter (`self: T` included) or a closure's captured copy, is `var q: P = p;` — `q.y` reads /
writes the COPY (in a closure too), `take(q)` passes the struct, `q + n` dispatches `P_add` (refused when there
is none). A `*T` source (an untyped impl `self`), a handle and every untyped global stay words; a bare `q` value
(`return q;`, `raw(q)`, `q == 3`) is still the first word. WAS (6.6.0 – 6.7.11): `q` was `p`'s first word — `q.y` "no
struct type in scope for 'q'" ("undefined variable 'q'" in a closure), `take(q)` faulted. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] FX-5.
6.7.13 — AND A STRUCT GLOBAL (D6 (b), ST-13): `var q = G;` in a fn, with G an INLINE struct global, is a copy of G
at every size — wrapped, inside a closure too. A handle G (a handle global, or a local / captured handle shadowing
G) keeps the word; top level is unchanged (`var q = G;` there is the word). Through 6.7.12 a struct global named in
a fn stayed a word and needed `var q: P = G;`.

A METHOD'S `*T` RESULT CHAINS (ST-19, D43): `h.get().b`, `.q.b`, `.m()`, through a dyn and a generic method. WAS:
*it does not return a struct* (the method arm missed what the free-call arm learned in 6.6.17).
  6.7.13 — A `: *T` CALL'S FIELD IS AN LVALUE (D8 (a), ST-15): `gp().n = 5;`, `gp().m += 5;` (the call runs once),
  `gpg<H>().n = 5;`, `o.gp().n = 5;` and `h.get().b = 5;` store through the returned pointer (6.7.11 refused the
  method form as `get2(&h).b = 5` was). A BY-VALUE result's field stays "the result is a temporary".

A PARENTHESISED FREE STRUCT CALL IS A NESTED-LITERAL ELEMENT (ST-20, D44): `H { 7, (mkq(4, 9)), 8 }` at every size,
with the unwrapped call's refusals (top level over 8 B; another struct by name — the one-leaf 8 B form that compiled
is refused now). A wrapped 9-16 B METHOD / operator element (*unexpected '}'* through 6.7.12) is taken since 6.7.13,
and a wrapped struct-typed FIELD `(h.q)` with it (RC-3); a wrapped struct NAME — `HE { 7, (q0), 8 }`, `((q0))`, a
global `(G)`, a parameter, a capture — is the element the unwrapped name is since 6.7.14 (RC-7; it was "unexpected
'}'": the wrapped name filled the nested struct's first leaf only); another struct's name is refused by name.

TOP-LEVEL COPY-INIT FROM A FIELD THROUGH A POINTER (ST-5): `var G: S = P.s;`, `gp().s`, `VV.p.s` (over 8 B) copy; a
source declared BELOW G in the leading block is refused by name, never the value store.

THE SCANNER DECODES A NESTED DERIVED STRUCT (ST-21): derive_str_fields. Since 6.7.13 it also walks a SCALAR field's
object / array / mismatched string whole (ST-4) — the scanner and the pairs form agree.

ONE int + f64 MIX WARNS ONCE (ST-11, D41), however far its result is carried; a captured name's write in a closure is
"'x' is captured by value; a captured name is not assignable in a closure" (ST-17) — since 6.7.13 its fix-it names
the local from the capture, `(var x_l = x;)` (RC-15).

6.7.14 — 6.7.11's D24 FAMILY: THE ASSIGNMENT, THE TOP-LEVEL `*T`, THE UNTYPED TOP-LEVEL RECEIVE (the user's D27 – D30;
CHANGELOG [6.7.14] RC-2, RC-9, RC-9r, RC-12, RC-14):
  - THE ASSIGNMENT IS JUDGED AS THE DECLARATION (D27 (a)): `x = mkp();`, `x = b.mk();`, `x = a + c;`, `x =
    fncall0(&mkp);` / `callptr(&mkp)` of a struct over 8 B into a variable, parameter or global declared with an
    annotation that names no struct (`i64`, `u8`, `f64`, `f32`, `i32`, `cstring`, an enum, `u128`; a classic-for
    header's `var x: u8` assigned in the loop body too), in a for init / step, wrapped, in a fn or at top level, is
    refused "cannot assign 'x: i64' from 'mkp', which returns the struct 'Q' - declare 'x: Q' or bind a field" (a method
    names 'Q_mk', an operator 'Q_add'). Through 6.7.13 it stored the first word, narrowed, silently. 8 B or less and
    `Str` keep the word; `: bool` keeps its own refusal. An UNTYPED destination (`var x = 0; x = mkp();`, an untyped
    global, a `for (var x = 0; ..)` shadowing an outer `x: i64`) and a `*T` local (`p = mkp();`) still took the first
    word in 6.7.14 — refused since 6.7.15 (D32, D33, below). Write the field, `x = mkp().a;`, or declare the struct.
    (The f32 / i32 / cstring / enum / u128 destinations go beyond D27's text — kept by the user's 6.7.15 D61.)
  - 6.7.13's D14 AT TOP LEVEL (D28 (a)): `var X: *P2 = mkp();` — a method, an operator, `fncall0(&mkp)` / `callptr`,
    wrapped, a generic, `*i64` / `*u8` / `**P2`, in the declaration zone and after the first statement — with a struct
    over 8 B is refused once in D14's words, "cannot initialise 'X: *P2' from 'mkp', which returns the struct 'P2' -
    declare 'X: P2' and take `&X`", with no typed-pointer warning. Through 6.7.13 it compiled under the warning and
    `X.b` faulted (139 / 14 on cx / 5 on PE).
  - AN UNTYPED TOP-LEVEL GLOBAL OF ONE WHOLE FREE CALL BINDS THE STRUCT (D29 (a), D30 (a)): `var x = mk1(30);`
    (wrapped too; a generic callee returning a plain struct; the zone or after the first statement) of 8 B or less is
    the struct, inline — `x.a` reads the field, `x + 1` dispatches `P1_add` (refused without one); annotate `: i64` for
    the word. Of 9 – 16 B it is refused as `var x: P2 = mk2(30);` is ("'mk2' returns a struct by value, and a struct
    result needs storage in a fn's frame — call it inside a fn"); through 6.7.13 x held the first word silently. A
    `Str` call, a method / operator / known `&f` result and a name kept the word at top level in 6.7.14 (a method /
    operator / known `&f` result binds since 6.7.15 — D34 / D35, below).
  - An UNKNOWN annotation type over a struct result is one error ("unknown type 'Foo' for variable 'x'"), not two (RC-2).
  - A call through `&f` is typed as `f(..)` — struct_value_operands_and_parentheses_v676 (D25, D26).

6.7.15 — THE REST OF THE D24 FAMILY: `*T` AND UNTYPED ASSIGNMENTS, THE TOP-LEVEL METHOD / OPERATOR / `&f` RECEIVE (the
user's D32 – D35, D61; CHANGELOG [6.7.15] RC-11 .. RC-14, RC-13r, RC-14r):
  - A STRUCT RESULT OVER 8 B ASSIGNED TO A `*T` (D32, RC-11; its reach kept by 6.7.16's D65): `*i64`, `*u8`, `**Q`, any `*T` but `*S` of the result's
    own struct — a local, parameter or global; the free call, a method, an operator, a known `&f`, a for step, wrapped,
    in a fn and at top level — is refused with 6.7.13 D14's words, "cannot assign 'p: *i64' from 'mkp', which returns
    the struct 'Q' - declare 'p: Q' and take `&p`", and no typed-pointer warning. Through 6.7.14 it stored the first
    word as the pointer (`*p` faulted: 139, PE 5). A `*Q` of the result's own struct keeps copying through p.
  - …AND TO AN UNTYPED VARIABLE (D33, RC-12; its reach kept by 6.7.16's D66): `var x = 0;`, an untyped parameter or global, a destructure's name, a
    `for (var x = 0; ..)` / `for x in ..` name, a closure's parameter — "cannot assign 'x' from 'mkp', which returns the
    struct 'Q' - declare 'x: Q' or bind a field" (D27's words minus the annotation). It silently held the first word. ⚠
    6.7.14's pinned acceptance — an untyped for-header `x` shadowing an outer `x: i64` kept the word — is REVERSED
    (refused, D33). Unchanged: 8 B or less and `Str` keep the word; a variable declared FROM the struct (`var x =
    mkp(); x = mkp();`) copies. Inside a generic at `T = i64` (its base) or `T = u8`, `var y: T = mkp();`, `y =
    mkp();` and `p = mkp();` into `p: *T` kept the first word through 6.7.15; since 6.7.16 each is a D1 stub line, so
    the scalar call is refused (generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310).
  - AT TOP LEVEL A WHOLE METHOD, OPERATOR OR KNOWN `&f` RESULT BINDS AS THE FREE CALL DOES (D34, D35, RC-13): `var G =
    B1.mk1();`, `var G = B1 + B1;`, `var G = fncall0(&mk1);`, `callptr(&mk1)`, wrapped, in either zone. Of 8 B or less G
    IS that struct (D34) — `G.a` reads the field, `G + G` dispatches `P1_add`, `G + 1` is refused without an operator
    fn; a method returning `G1<i32>` binds its instance. Of 9 – 16 B the line is refused once (D35), "'P2.mk' returns a
    struct by value, and a struct result needs storage in a fn's frame — call it inside a fn"; over 16 B the call's own
    refusal is the one error. Through 6.7.14 G held the first word (33; 2 for the operator). And a DECLARATION-ZONE
    generic call returning a generic struct's instance — `var x = mkg1(33);` with `fn mkg1<T>(v: T): G1<T>` — is that
    instance, as after the first statement (RC-14; `x.v` was "no struct type in scope for 'x'"), so `var y = x + 1;`
    dispatches `G1_add` (refused without one; through 6.7.14 the zone line added to the word). Still a word at top
    level through 6.7.15: an if-expression's struct result, a `Str`, a name; in the zone a GENERIC method's generic
    struct result, an expression argument (`mkg1(30 + 3)`), a struct carrier and a bounded `G1<T: Show>` — settled in
    6.7.16 (below), but a name, which keeps the word.

6.7.16 (the user's D1, D16 (a), D85 (a), D90, D99, D104; CHANGELOG [6.7.16] ZN-5 – ZN-7, ZN-9, ZN-10, SS-35, SS-41,
SS-46, SS-46r, FF-46, FF-52, SB-1, SB-3):
  - A STRUCT RESULT OVER 8 B STORED THROUGH A TYPED POINTER TO A SCALAR OR A POINTER SLOT IS REFUSED (D99, SS-46, SS-46r;
    a language change): `*p = mkp();` with `p: *i64` (`*u8`, `*f64`, `*bool`, a parameter, `*GP`, `*w.pi`, `*gpi()`,
    `*(p + 0)`, `**pp` of a `**i64`, `*pp` of a `**Pt`), `p[i] = mkp();` (beyond D99's text, on the filed list) — from a
    call, method, operator, known `&f`, wrapped, a for step — is "cannot assign '*p: i64' from 'mkp', which returns the
    struct 'Q' - store it through a '*Q' or bind a field", once (it stored Q's first word: the filed repro exited 77).
    `*(&x) = mkp()` is judged as `x = mkp()`. In a generic, a store through a name reading T is a D1 stub line. Unchanged:
    `*p = mk8()` (8 B), a `Str` result, `*p = mkp().a`, `*pq = mkp()` through a `*Q` (copies). ⚠ Filed unplaced at the
    close (struct and pointer shapes beyond D97 / D99): a scalar field `w.n = mkp();`, a local array element `buf[0] =
    mkp();`, an untyped pointer `*p = mkp();`, `*p += mkp()` / `x += mkp()` still take the first word, and a struct
    stored into a `*Q` slot reached through a dereference or a field (`*ppq = mkp();`, `w.pq = r;`) rebinds the pointer.
  - A STRUCT VALUE OVER 8 BYTES IS NO MULTI-VALUE ELEMENT (D90, FF-46, FF-52): `return (b, 5);` with a 16-byte `b` (an
    inline local or global, a by-value parameter or field, `(mk(), 5)`, `*p`) is "'mv' returns struct 'Pt' (16 bytes)
    as a multi-value element - return its pointer, or its fields as elements"; an 8-byte struct, a word, `&b`, `b.x`, a
    `Str` and a HANDLE named whole (its word is the address) stay elements (ret2_rethi). It handed back the struct's first
    word silently.
  - A CHAIN'S STRUCT RETURN (SB-3): `return w.p.q16();`, `return (*pw).p.q16();`, `return mk().p.q16();`, `return
    callptr(&gw, pw).p.q16();` from a 9-16 B struct fn and `return w.p.q24();` from a `: Q24` fn return as `return
    p.q16();` does (refused through 6.7.15); a multi-value method on a chain is refused into a struct field, a `: Q8`
    return and an async return, as on a single name (SB-1; they stored the first value).
  - AT TOP LEVEL AN UNTYPED GLOBAL BINDS MORE (no new decision but D16 (a) and D85 (a)): an if-expression of struct
    branches of 8 B or less is that struct in both zones (D16 (a), ZN-9 — if_expression_v674), its branches' generic
    instance too (SS-35); in the zone a GENERIC method's generic struct result (`b.mkg(P)` is `G1<Pt>`, an explicit
    `.m<..>(` too; ZN-7), an expression argument (`mkg1(30 + 3)`, `mkg1(-3)`, `mkg1(1.5)`), a struct carrier and a
    bounded `G1<T: Show>` bind their instance (ZN-5); a generic OPERATOR fn binds T as its call does, `b + p` calling
    `P1_add$Pt` (D85 (a), SS-41 — generics_monomorphization_v639_v6310); a `: Str` method result routes (ZN-10). A plain
    name keeps the word. ⚠ A ZONE untyped (or annotated) global from a generic call whose return is a NESTED list —
    `var x = mkn<i32>(33);` with `fn mkn<T>(v: T): G1<Box<T>>` — is refused "the declaration zone misread whether the
    initializer of 'x' is a struct value - please report it" (measured on 6.7.16's build/cycc); declared after the first
    top-level statement it binds `G1<Box<i32>>` and reads right.

GATES: tests/tcyr/crossos/{struct_pointer_source_copy, small_struct_pointer_copy, struct_operator_result_binding,
struct_deref_whole_operand}.tcyr; tests/gates/codegen/small_struct_handle_line.sh and the lane's ST rows. The guide:
§ Where a struct lives, § A store through a `*S` copies the struct, § Parentheses. Field notes:
field_notes/language/semantics_runtime.cyml struct_copies_and_operator_results_since_6_7_11;
field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml struct_destinations_peek_ends_and_replayed_returns_v6711. CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (ST-1 .. ST-21).

[[entries]]
name = "sys_sigprocmask_on_macos_v6712"
description = "cyrius 6.7.12: `sys_sigprocmask(how, set, oldset)` works on both Macs — it returns 0 and changes the CALLING THREAD's mask, as on Linux (through 6.7.11 it returned -78). It is Darwin's `__pthread_sigmask` (329) through the private alias 1329, never Darwin's process-wide `sigprocmask` (48); so the exec family empties an inherited mask on macOS too."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "macos", "darwin", "signals", "sigprocmask", "__pthread_sigmask", "alias band", "1329", "process.cyr", "exec"]
---
WHAT IT IS. Since 6.7.12 `sys_sigprocmask(how, set, oldset)` returns 0 on x86 and arm64 macOS and changes the CALLING
THREAD's mask, as Linux's `rt_sigprocmask` does. It is Darwin's `__pthread_sigmask` (329) — the call libSystem's
`pthread_sigmask` uses — reached through the private alias 1329 (1000 + the BSD number, routed on both Mach-O
backends; darwin_concurrency_syscall_wrappers_v6543 lists the band). It is NOT Darwin's `sigprocmask` (48), which XNU
applies to EVERY thread of the process, and it is not a renumber of rt_sigprocmask: Darwin's sigset is 32 bits, with no
sigsetsize argument.

THE SHIM. It passes the usual 8-byte set (Darwin reads the low half) and zeroes `oldset`'s upper word, so a caller's
8-byte buffers read the same on every target. `how` is the peer's SIG_BLOCK / SIG_UNBLOCK / SIG_SETMASK (1 / 2 / 3 on
Darwin).

WHAT FOLLOWS. `lib/process.cyr`'s exec family (`run`, `run_capture`, `spawn`, `exec_*`, `exec_cmd`) empties a mask
inherited from a launcher on macOS too, completing 6.7.9's rule: every exec-family child starts with SIGPIPE / SIGXFSZ
at SIG_DFL and an empty signal mask (Linux since 6.7.9, macOS since 6.7.12). A child that must keep a signal blocked
blocks it itself. Code that took the old -78 as "no signal mask on macOS, skip" now really blocks or unblocks.

Decisions: D3 (a public shim) and its scope, per-thread — both the user's, 2026-10-15 (the darwin lane's review
measured 48 changing another thread's mask: `thread_after=536870912` on ach and ecb, 0 with 329). Gate: a crossos row
asserting another thread's mask is unchanged, with `THREADS_CONCURRENT == 1` asserted on macOS so it cannot pass
vacuously; tests/tcyr/crossos/{sigprocmask_every_target, exec_child_signal_state}.tcyr. Field notes:
field_notes/language/platform_abi.cyml sys_sigprocmask_works_on_macos_per_thread_v6712; field_notes/compiler/gotchas.cyml
a_stated_cause_or_a_carried_number_is_not_evidence_seven_times (the brief's premise). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] DW-5, DW-5r.
